/* Quilio: past papers, recreated.
   Each question follows a real past paper question: the same chemistry and the same kind of task, written in new words
   with new numbers, so it can be practised and checked on this site. Every new number was recalculated.
   Questions that are almost entirely drawn structures (which cannot be checked here) are listed but not recreated. */
(function () {
  'use strict';
  const R = String.raw;
  window.OLY_PAST = [
  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2026, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Chlorine in pools · isocyanides and the Passerini reaction · tautomers in henna · carotenes and colour · turquoise and unit cells', qs: [
    { id: 'uk26-1', n: 1, t: 'Chlorine in the pool', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-11-4', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Swimming pools are disinfected with chlorine, which reacts with water: $\mathrm{Cl_2 + H_2O \rightarrow HOCl + HCl}$. Sodium carbonate is added to remove the acid, so that the chlorine does not re-form. In a badly run pool, chlorate(V) builds up. Urea from urine is hydrolysed to ammonia, which reacts with HOCl to give chloramines such as $\mathrm{NH_2Cl}$ and the volatile $\mathrm{NCl_3}$ that causes the “chlorine smell”.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the oxidation number of chlorine in $\mathrm{Cl_2}$, HOCl and HCl.`, h: 'Oxygen is −2 and hydrogen +1. What kind of redox reaction gives one higher and one lower product?', w: R`$\mathrm{Cl_2}$: 0; HOCl: +1; HCl: −1. Chlorine is both oxidised and reduced: a disproportionation.` },
        { q: R`Write an equation for sodium carbonate reacting with HOCl.`, h: 'HOCl is a weak acid; carbonate is a base.', w: R`$\mathrm{Na_2CO_3 + 2HOCl \rightarrow 2NaOCl + CO_2 + H_2O}$ (or, with less acid, $\mathrm{Na_2CO_3 + HOCl \rightarrow NaOCl + NaHCO_3}$).` },
        { q: R`Hypochlorite ions disproportionate into chlorate(V) and chloride ions. Write the ionic equation, and name the shape of $\mathrm{ClO_3^-}$.`, h: 'Balance the electrons: Cl goes from +1 to +5 in one ion and to −1 in two others.', w: R`$\mathrm{3OCl^- \rightarrow ClO_3^- + 2Cl^-}$. $\mathrm{ClO_3^-}$ has three bonding pairs and one lone pair on Cl: trigonal pyramidal.` },
        { q: R`Write an equation for the hydrolysis of urea, $\mathrm{(NH_2)_2CO}$, to ammonia and carbon dioxide.`, h: 'One water molecule is needed.', w: R`$\mathrm{(NH_2)_2CO + H_2O \rightarrow 2NH_3 + CO_2}$` },
        { q: R`Describe the dot-and-cross diagram of $\mathrm{NH_2Cl}$: the bonding pairs and lone pairs on N, and the lone pairs on Cl.`, h: 'N has five outer electrons and makes three bonds.', w: 'N: three bonding pairs (two N–H, one N–Cl) and one lone pair. Cl: one bonding pair and three lone pairs.' },
        { q: R`Henry’s law for $\mathrm{NCl_3}$ is $[\mathrm{NCl_3}] = k_H\,p$, with $k_H = 380\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}\ atm^{-1}}$. The pool water contains $3.2 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ of $\mathrm{NCl_3}$. Calculate the equilibrium partial pressure of $\mathrm{NCl_3}$ above the water.`, a: 8.42e-7, u: 'atm', tol: 0.02, h: 'Rearrange: p = [NCl₃] ÷ k_H.', w: R`$p = \dfrac{3.2 \times 10^{-4}}{380} = 8.4 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{atm}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk26-2', n: 2, t: 'Isocyanides', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 25, lec: ['ca-19-2', 'ca-15-1'],
      s: R`Isocyanides, R–N≡C, are isomers of nitriles, R–C≡N, and have famously foul smells. Nitriles form when halogenoalkanes react with sodium cyanide; with silver cyanide the isocyanide forms instead. In the Passerini reaction an isocyanide, a carboxylic acid and an aldehyde or ketone combine in one step to give an α-acyloxy amide: $\mathrm{R^1COO{-}C(R^2)(R^3){-}CONH{-}R^4}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`The usual structure is $\mathrm{R{-}N^+{\equiv}C^-}$. Give a second resonance structure with no formal charges, showing all lone pairs.`, h: 'Move the electrons of one π bond onto carbon.', w: 'R–N=C: with one lone pair on N and one lone pair on C (C then has only six outer electrons, like a carbene).' },
        { q: R`3-Bromopropene reacts with NaCN to give B, and with AgCN to give the isocyanide and a cream precipitate. Name B and the precipitate.`, h: 'Cyanide attacks through C; the leaving group ends up with silver.', w: R`B is but-3-enenitrile, $\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHCH_2C{\equiv}N}$. The cream precipitate is silver bromide, AgBr.` },
        { q: R`An isocyanide can be smelled at $6.5 \times 10^{-8}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ in air. How many molecules are needed to make a $42\ \mathrm{m^3}$ room smell of it?`, a: 1.64e21, u: 'molecules', tol: 0.02, h: '1 m³ = 1000 dm³.', w: R`$n = 6.5 \times 10^{-8} \times 42\,000 = 2.73 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times N_A = 1.64 \times 10^{21}$ molecules.` },
        { q: R`Isocyanides are made by dehydrating N-substituted amides C. Acid hydrolysis of an isocyanide gives the amine $\mathrm{R{-}NH_2}$ and an acid D that is 26.1% carbon by mass, whatever R is. Identify C and D.`, h: 'D has only one carbon atom.', w: R`D is methanoic acid, HCOOH ($12.01 \div 46.03 = 26.1\%$). So C is the formamide $\mathrm{R{-}NH{-}CHO}$.` },
        { q: R`In the carbylamine test, a primary amine is warmed with trichloromethane and KOH to give an isocyanide. Write the equation for butylamine, $\mathrm{C_4H_9NH_2}$, using molecular formulae.`, h: 'The CHCl₃ carbon becomes the isocyanide carbon; three HCl are removed by KOH.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_4H_{11}N + CHCl_3 + 3KOH \rightarrow C_5H_9N + 3KCl + 3H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'Which of these give a positive carbylamine test: ethylamine, dimethylamine, phenylamine, triethylamine, ethanamide, propan-2-amine?', h: 'The nitrogen needs two hydrogens.', w: 'Primary amines only: ethylamine, phenylamine and propan-2-amine. Ethanamide is an amide, not an amine.' },
        { q: 'Give the Passerini product of ethanoic acid, propanone and tert-butyl isocyanide.', h: 'Fit each piece into R¹COO–C(R²)(R³)–CONH–R⁴.', w: R`$\mathrm{CH_3COO{-}C(CH_3)_2{-}CONH{-}C(CH_3)_3}$` },
        { q: 'A student has 4 isocyanides, 3 carboxylic acids and 5 ketones. Each product uses one of each. How many different Passerini products can be made?', a: 60, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Multiply the choices.', w: R`$4 \times 3 \times 5 = 60$` },
        { q: 'Another student has 3 isocyanides, 2 carboxylic acids and 4 aldehydes, one of which is methanal. Counting each enantiomer separately, how many products are possible?', a: 42, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'The carbon from the aldehyde is a stereocentre only if its two groups differ. Methanal gives two H atoms.', w: R`Methanal: no stereocentre, $3 \times 2 \times 1 = 6$. The other three aldehydes give a stereocentre: $3 \times 2 \times 3 \times 2 = 36$. Total 42.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk26-3', n: 3, t: 'Tautomers in henna', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 25, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-17-1', 'ca-5-2'],
      s: R`Lawsone, the orange dye in henna, is 2-hydroxy-1,4-naphthoquinone. Like many carbonyl compounds it exists as tautomers: isomers that differ in where one hydrogen atom and one double bond sit, as in keto ⇌ enol.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`A simple ketone A is 99.9985% keto and 0.0015% enol B at equilibrium. Calculate $K_1 = [\mathrm{B}]/[\mathrm{A}]$.`, a: 1.5e-5, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Use the percentages as concentrations.', w: R`$K_1 = 0.0015 \div 99.9985 = 1.50 \times 10^{-5}$` },
        { q: R`For another keto C ⇌ enol D, $K_2 = 0.36$. What percentage of the mixture is D?`, a: 26.5, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Fraction of D = K ÷ (1 + K).', w: R`$0.36 \div 1.36 = 26.5\%$` },
        { q: 'Which of these can form an enol: propanone, benzaldehyde, 2,2-dimethylpropanal, cyclohexanone, ethanal, benzophenone?', h: 'An enol needs a hydrogen on the carbon next to C=O.', w: 'Propanone, cyclohexanone and ethanal. The others have no α-hydrogen.' },
        { q: 'Lawsone has two enol forms. Name them, and explain which is more stable.', h: 'Which OH can reach a C=O to form a hydrogen bond?', w: '2-hydroxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and 4-hydroxy-1,2-naphthoquinone. The 2-hydroxy form is more stable: its OH forms an intramolecular hydrogen bond to the neighbouring C=O.' },
        { q: 'Give both enol tautomers of butanone.', h: 'Either α-carbon can lose a hydrogen.', w: R`$\mathrm{CH_2{=}C(OH)CH_2CH_3}$ and $\mathrm{CH_3C(OH){=}CHCH_3}$ (which has E and Z forms).` },
        { q: R`Bond enthalpies ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): C–C 346, C=C 610, C–H 414, C–O 356, C=O 749, O–H 460. Calculate $\Delta_r H$ for a typical ketone turning into its enol, $\mathrm{CH_3{-}CO{-}R \rightarrow CH_2{=}C(OH){-}R}$.`, a: 83, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', abs: 0.5, h: 'The C–C single bond becomes C=C, C=O becomes C–O, and a C–H becomes O–H.', w: R`Broken: C–C + C=O + C–H $= 346 + 749 + 414 = 1509$. Made: C=C + C–O + O–H $= 610 + 356 + 460 = 1426$. $\Delta H = +83\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$: enols are usually less stable.` },
        { q: R`In the gas phase, lawsone’s triketone form turning into its preferred enol has $\Delta_r H^\circ = -30.40\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$ and $\Delta_r S^\circ = -9.60\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$. Calculate $K_{gas}$ at 298 K.`, a: 6.72e4, u: '', tol: 0.05, h: 'ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS°, then K = e^(−ΔG°/RT).', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -30.40 + 298 \times 0.00960 = -27.54\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$; $K = e^{27\,540/(8.314 \times 298)} = e^{11.12} = 6.7 \times 10^4$` },
        { q: R`Gibbs energies of solvation (gas → water): triketone $-122.5$, enol $-107.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Use a Gibbs energy cycle to find $K_{water}$ at 298 K.`, a: 129, u: '', tol: 0.05, h: 'Triketone(aq) → triketone(g) → enol(g) → enol(aq).', w: R`$\Delta G_{aq} = +122.5 - 27.54 - 107.0 = -12.04\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$; $K = e^{12\,040/(8.314 \times 298)} = 129$. Water stabilises the triketone more, so there is less enol than in the gas.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk26-4', n: 4, t: 'Carotenes and colour', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 3, m: 30, lec: ['ca-13-3', 'ca-14-2'],
      s: R`Carrots are orange because carotenes contain long chains of conjugated C=C bonds. In a simple model of a conjugated chain with $N$ p orbitals, the gap between the highest occupied and lowest unoccupied π orbitals is $\Delta E = 4\beta \sin\!\left(\dfrac{90^\circ}{N+1}\right)$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'β-Carotene has 40 carbon atoms, 11 C=C bonds and two rings. What is its molecular formula?', h: 'Each ring or C=C removes two H from CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.', w: R`DBE = 13, so H $= 82 - 26 = 56$: $\mathrm{C_{40}H_{56}}$.` },
        { q: 'Put the four π molecular orbitals of buta-1,3-diene in order of energy, and say how the number of nodes changes.', h: 'More nodes, higher energy.', w: 'ψ₁ (0 nodes) < ψ₂ (1) < ψ₃ (2) < ψ₄ (3). The four π electrons fill ψ₁ and ψ₂.' },
        { q: R`For hexa-1,3,5-triene take $\beta = 7.30 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$. Calculate $\Delta E$.`, a: 6.50e-19, u: 'J', tol: 0.01, h: 'N = 6 p orbitals, so the angle is 90° ÷ 7.', w: R`$\Delta E = 4 \times 7.30 \times 10^{-19} \times \sin 12.86^\circ = 6.50 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the wavelength of light absorbed, in nm, to the nearest whole number.', a: 306, u: 'nm', abs: 1.5, h: 'λ = hc ÷ ΔE.', w: R`$\lambda = \dfrac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \times 2.998 \times 10^8}{6.50 \times 10^{-19}} = 3.06 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{m} = 306\ \mathrm{nm}$` },
        { q: 'Does hexatriene absorb visible light (380–720 nm)? What happens to the absorbed wavelength as the conjugated chain gets longer?', h: 'Look at what happens to sin(90°/(N+1)) as N grows.', w: 'No: 306 nm is ultraviolet, so hexatriene is colourless. A longer chain gives a smaller gap and a longer wavelength, which is how carotenes reach the visible and look orange.' },
        { q: R`The dye tartrazine ($M = 534.4\ \mathrm{g\ mol^{-1}}$) can make skin temporarily see-through by raising the refractive index of its watery regions from 1.33 to match the fatty regions, 1.44. The rise is $\Delta n = 0.150\,c$ ($c$ in $\mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$). The watery volume is $7.20\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. What minimum mass of tartrazine is needed?`, a: 2.82, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Δn = 0.11, so c = 0.11 ÷ 0.150.', w: R`$c = 0.733\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$; $n = 0.733 \times 7.20 \times 10^{-3} = 5.28 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $m = 2.82\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'A dye absorbs most strongly at 450 nm, in the blue. What colour does it look?', h: 'We see the complementary colour.', w: 'Yellow to orange, the complementary colour of blue.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk26-5', n: 5, t: 'Turquoise and its cousins', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 3, m: 20, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Turquoise is $\mathrm{CuAl_6(PO_4)_4(OH)_x \cdot yH_2O}$, with copper present as $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ and $y$ between 2 and 8. Related minerals are libethenite, $\mathrm{Cu_2PO_4OH}$, and variscite, $\mathrm{AlPO_4 \cdot 2H_2O}$. Volumes of polyhedra with edge $a$: tetrahedron $a^3/(6\sqrt2)$; octahedron $(\sqrt2/3)a^3$. Each $\mathrm{H_2O}$ and each $\mathrm{OH^-}$ takes up $20\ \text{Å}^3$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find x, the number of hydroxide ions.', a: 8, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'The charges must add up to zero.', w: R`Positive: $+2 + 6 \times 3 = +20$. Phosphate: $4 \times (-3) = -12$. So $x = 8$.` },
        { q: R`Calculate the percentage of copper by mass in turquoise with $y = 4$, to 3 significant figures.`, a: 7.81, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'M = 741.4 for the anhydrous part; add four waters.', w: R`$M = 741.4 + 4 \times 18.02 = 813.4$; $63.55 \div 813.4 = 7.81\%$` },
        { q: 'Samples contain 0%, 7.6%, 12%, 19%, 44% and 53.2% copper. Which could be pure turquoise, and which pure libethenite?', h: 'Work out the range for y = 2 to 8, and the value for Cu₂PO₄OH.', w: 'Turquoise ranges from 7.18% (y = 8) to 8.17% (y = 2): the 7.6% sample. Libethenite is 53.2% copper.' },
        { q: R`The P–O bond is $1.53\ \text{Å}$. In a regular tetrahedron the edge is $a = \text{(P–O)} \times \sqrt{8/3}$. Calculate the volume of a $\mathrm{PO_4}$ tetrahedron in $\text{Å}^3$.`, a: 1.84, u: 'Å³', tol: 0.01, h: 'a = 2.498 Å.', w: R`$a = 2.498\ \text{Å}$; $V = 2.498^3 \div (6\sqrt2) = 1.84\ \text{Å}^3$` },
        { q: R`In variscite each Al sits in an $\mathrm{AlO_6}$ octahedron with Al–O $= 1.90\ \text{Å}$ (edge $= \sqrt2 \times$ Al–O). A unit cell contains 8 $\mathrm{AlPO_4 \cdot 2H_2O}$, and the polyhedra and water fill 50% of it. Calculate the unit cell volume in $\text{Å}^3$.`, a: 816, u: 'Å³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Per formula unit: one tetrahedron + one octahedron + two waters.', w: R`Octahedron: $a = 2.687\ \text{Å}$, $V = 9.15\ \text{Å}^3$. Per formula unit $1.84 + 9.15 + 40 = 50.98$; $\times 8 = 407.9$; $\div 0.50 = 816\ \text{Å}^3$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the density of variscite.', a: 2.57, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.015, h: 'Mass of 8 formula units ÷ cell volume (1 Å³ = 10⁻²⁴ cm³).', w: R`$M = 157.98$; mass $= 8 \times 157.98 \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 2.099 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{g}$; $\rho = 2.099 \times 10^{-21} \div 8.16 \times 10^{-22} = 2.57\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`Turquoise with $y = 6$ has one formula unit per cell, filled to 70%. Cu–O $= 2.25\ \text{Å}$. Calculate its density.`, a: 2.76, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.015, h: 'Add one CuO₆, six AlO₆, four PO₄, eight OH and six H₂O.', w: R`$\mathrm{CuO_6}$ $15.19$ + $6 \times 9.15$ + $4 \times 1.84$ + $14 \times 20 = 357.4\ \text{Å}^3$; cell $= 510.6\ \text{Å}^3$. $M = 849.5$; $\rho = 849.5 \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} \div 5.106 \times 10^{-22} = 2.76\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2025, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Cartridge propellant · astatine · epoxides · nanotubes and Raman spectra · iodination kinetics', skipped: 'Question 5 (the synthesis of agarospirol) is drawn almost entirely as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk25-1', n: 1, t: 'Cartridge propellant', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`Clay-pigeon cartridges use cellulose trinitrate (nitrocellulose) as the propellant. It is made by nitrating cellulose, $\mathrm{C_6H_7(OH)_3O_2}$, with concentrated nitric and sulfuric acids; the nitrating species is the nitronium ion, $\mathrm{NO_2^+}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the equation for cellulose reacting with nitric acid to give cellulose trinitrate, $\mathrm{C_6H_7(ONO_2)_3O_2}$.`, h: 'Each OH becomes ONO₂; what else forms?', w: R`$\mathrm{C_6H_7(OH)_3O_2 + 3HNO_3 \rightarrow C_6H_7(ONO_2)_3O_2 + 3H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`Show how $\mathrm{NO_2^+}$ forms: nitric acid accepts a proton from sulfuric acid, then loses water.`, h: 'Two steps.', w: R`$\mathrm{HNO_3 + H_2SO_4 \rightarrow H_2NO_3^+ + HSO_4^-}$, then $\mathrm{H_2NO_3^+ \rightarrow NO_2^+ + H_2O}$. $\mathrm{NO_2^+}$ is linear, O=N⁺=O.` },
        { q: R`Write the equation for the complete combustion of $\mathrm{C_6H_7N_3O_{11}}$ (the nitrogen leaves as $\mathrm{N_2}$).`, h: 'Products: 6CO₂, 3.5H₂O, 1.5N₂. Count the oxygen still needed.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_6H_7N_3O_{11} + 2.25O_2 \rightarrow 6CO_2 + 3.5H_2O + 1.5N_2}$` },
        { q: R`$\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): cellulose trinitrate $-668.4$; $\mathrm{CO_2}$ $-393.5$; $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ $-285.8$. Calculate its standard enthalpy of combustion.`, a: -2692.9, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants; O₂ and N₂ are zero.', w: R`$6(-393.5) + 3.5(-285.8) - (-668.4) = -2692.9\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'On firing there is no oxygen, and every product is a gas: CO, CO₂, H₂O and N₂. Write the equation.', h: 'H goes to H₂O and N to N₂ first; share the oxygen left over between CO and CO₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_6H_7N_3O_{11} \rightarrow 4.5CO + 1.5CO_2 + 3.5H_2O + 1.5N_2}$: 11 mol of gas per mole.` },
        { q: R`What volume of gas, in $\mathrm{m^3}$, does $4.20\ \mathrm{g}$ produce at 250 °C and 101 325 Pa?`, a: 6.67e-3, u: 'm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'M = 297.1. Multiply moles by 11.', w: R`$n = 4.20 \div 297.1 \times 11 = 0.1555\ \mathrm{mol}$; $V = \dfrac{0.1555 \times 8.314 \times 523}{101\,325} = 6.67 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m^3}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk25-2', n: 2, t: 'Astatine', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-1-2', 'ca-11-2'],
      s: R`Astatine is the rarest naturally occurring element: about $28\ \mathrm{g}$ exists in the Earth’s crust at any moment. It is made by bombarding bismuth with alpha particles. Bismuth is recovered from lead refining as an alloy A with calcium and magnesium.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Assuming all of it is $^{219}\mathrm{At}$, how many astatine atoms are on Earth?`, a: 7.70e22, u: 'atoms', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles = 28 ÷ 219.', w: R`$28 \div 219 \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 7.70 \times 10^{22}$` },
        { q: 'Alloy A is 9.59% Mg and 82.50% Bi by mass; the rest is calcium. Find its empirical formula.', h: 'Ca = 7.91%. Divide each by its Ar.', w: R`Ca $7.91 \div 40.08 = 0.197$; Mg $9.59 \div 24.31 = 0.394$; Bi $82.50 \div 208.98 = 0.395$: $\mathrm{CaMg_2Bi_2}$.` },
        { q: 'A reacts with chlorine to give bismuth metal and two ionic chlorides in a 2 : 1 ratio. Identify them.', h: 'Which metals are more reactive than bismuth?', w: R`$\mathrm{MgCl_2}$ and $\mathrm{CaCl_2}$: $\mathrm{CaMg_2Bi_2 + 3Cl_2 \rightarrow CaCl_2 + 2MgCl_2 + 2Bi}$.` },
        { q: R`$^{209}_{83}\mathrm{Bi} + \alpha \rightarrow \mathrm{At} + 3$ neutrons. What is the mass number of the astatine isotope?`, a: 210, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'An alpha particle is ⁴₂He; balance mass numbers.', w: R`$209 + 4 - 3 = 210$; atomic number $83 + 2 = 85$: $^{210}_{85}\mathrm{At}$.` },
        { q: R`Bismuth dissolves in concentrated nitric acid to give $\mathrm{Bi(NO_3)_3}$, water and a colourless gas of $M = 30.01$. Write the equation.`, h: 'The gas is a nitrogen oxide.', w: R`$\mathrm{Bi + 4HNO_3 \rightarrow Bi(NO_3)_3 + NO + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`An element D reacts with bromine to give only E, whose mass spectrum has two peaks of almost equal height at $m/z$ 205.8 and 207.8. E reacts with $\mathrm{At_2}$ to give BrAt and D. Identify D and E.`, h: 'Two equal peaks two units apart: one bromine atom. What is 206.8 − 79.9?', w: R`D is iodine, $\mathrm{I_2}$; E is IBr ($126.9 + 79.9$). $\mathrm{2IBr + At_2 \rightarrow 2BrAt + I_2}$.` },
        { q: R`$2.80\ \mathrm{g}$ of BrAt is made with $^{210}\mathrm{At}$ (half-life 486 minutes). How long, to the nearest hour, until only $2.10\ \mathrm{mg}$ remains?`, a: 84, u: 'h', abs: 0.6, h: 'Number of half-lives = log₂(2800 ÷ 2.10).', w: R`$\log_2(1333) = 10.38$ half-lives; $\times 486 = 5045\ \mathrm{min} = 84\ \mathrm{h}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk25-3', n: 3, t: 'Epoxides', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-13-4', 'ca-29-4', 'ca-14-2'],
      s: 'Epoxides are three-membered C–C–O rings. Their ring strain makes them open easily when a nucleophile attacks. They are made by oxidising alkenes with peroxyacids, which deliver the oxygen atom to one face of the C=C.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the general formula of an epoxide with n carbon atoms and no other functional groups.', h: 'One ring, like an alkene’s one C=C.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_nH_{2n}O}$` },
        { q: 'Roughly what is the C–C–O bond angle inside an epoxide ring, in degrees?', a: 60, u: '°', abs: 1, h: 'Three atoms in a ring.', w: 'About 60°, far below 109.5°: hence the ring strain. (In ethoxyethane the C–O–C angle is about 110°.)' },
        { q: 'Propene reacts with a peroxyacid. How many stereoisomers of the product form?', a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Does methyloxirane have a stereocentre? Are both faces of the alkene attacked equally?', w: 'Two: (R)- and (S)-methyloxirane, formed in equal amounts (a racemic mixture).' },
        { q: 'How many epoxide stereoisomers form from but-1-ene, (Z)-but-2-ene, (E)-but-2-ene and 2-methylpropene?', h: 'Count stereocentres, and look for meso forms.', w: 'But-1-ene: 2 (enantiomers). (Z)-but-2-ene: 1 (the cis epoxide is meso). (E)-but-2-ene: 2 (trans enantiomers). 2-Methylpropene: 1 (no stereocentre).' },
        { q: 'Oxirane reacts with (i) water and acid, (ii) ammonia, (iii) sodium methoxide, then acid. Name the products.', h: 'The nucleophile ends up on one carbon, OH on the other.', w: '(i) ethane-1,2-diol; (ii) 2-aminoethanol; (iii) 2-methoxyethanol.' },
        { q: 'Sodium 4-methylphenoxide opens the ring of epichlorohydrin, 2-(chloromethyl)oxirane, at its CH₂, giving an anion E⁻. E⁻ then closes a new ring by displacing chloride, giving F. Identify E⁻ and F.', h: 'The alkoxide made by ring opening is next to the CH₂Cl.', w: R`$\mathrm{E^-}$: $\mathrm{4{-}CH_3C_6H_4O{-}CH_2{-}CH(O^-){-}CH_2Cl}$. F: 2-[(4-methylphenoxy)methyl]oxirane, a glycidyl ether. This is how epoxy resins such as BADGE are built.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk25-4', n: 4, t: 'Nanotubes and Raman spectra', f: 'Analysis', lv: 3, m: 18, lec: ['ca-22-1', 'ca-14-1'],
      s: R`Burning ethyne in limited air gives carbon nanotubes as a side product. Raman spectroscopy measures how much energy light loses when it scatters off a vibrating molecule; the shift in wavenumber ($\tilde\nu = 1/\lambda$, with $\lambda$ in cm) equals the wavenumber of the vibration.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$36.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of ethyne at 298 K and 101 325 Pa gives $315\ \mathrm{mg}$ of nanotubes. What percentage of the carbon ends up in nanotubes?`, a: 0.891, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'n = pV/RT; each ethyne has two carbons.', w: R`$n = \dfrac{101\,325 \times 0.03600}{8.314 \times 298} = 1.472\ \mathrm{mol}$; carbon $= 2 \times 1.472 \times 12.01 = 35.36\ \mathrm{g}$; $0.315 \div 35.36 = 0.891\%$` },
        { q: R`Benzene is lit with a mercury line at $4046.6\ \text{Å}$ and gives Raman lines at $4618.3\ \text{Å}$ (line 1) and $4325.8\ \text{Å}$ (line 2). Calculate the wavenumber shift of line 1.`, a: 3059, u: 'cm⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: '1 Å = 10⁻⁸ cm.', w: R`$24\,712.1 - 21\,653.0 = 3059\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the frequency difference, in Hz, between line 2 and the incident light.', a: 4.78e13, u: 'Hz', tol: 0.01, h: 'Δf = c × Δν̃, with c in cm s⁻¹.', w: R`$\Delta\tilde\nu = 24\,712.1 - 23\,117.1 = 1595\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$; $\Delta f = 2.998 \times 10^{10} \times 1595 = 4.78 \times 10^{13}\ \mathrm{Hz}$` },
        { q: 'Which vibration causes each line?', h: 'Compare with IR bands just above 3000 and near 1600 cm⁻¹.', w: 'Line 1 (3059 cm⁻¹): C–H stretch. Line 2 (1595 cm⁻¹): stretching of the ring’s conjugated C=C bonds.' },
        { q: R`A nanotube’s breathing vibration has $\tilde\nu = A/d$, where $d$ is the diameter. With one laser, the scattered light was: radius 0.62 nm, $12\,538.9$; 0.70 nm, $12\,561.7$; 0.78 nm, $12\,579.9$; 0.90 nm, $12\,601.1\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. Find $A$ in $\mathrm{cm^{-1}\ nm}$.`, a: 248, u: 'cm⁻¹ nm', tol: 0.01, h: 'Careful: the table gives radius, not diameter. Scattered = laser − A/d.', w: R`Diameters 1.24 and 1.80 nm: $12\,601.1 - 12\,538.9 = 62.2 = A(1/1.24 - 1/1.80) = 0.2509A$, so $A = 248\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}\ nm}$.` },
        { q: 'What is the laser wavelength in nm?', a: 785, u: 'nm', tol: 0.003, h: 'Laser wavenumber = scattered + A/d.', w: R`$12\,538.9 + 248 \div 1.24 = 12\,738.9\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$; $\lambda = 785\ \mathrm{nm}$` },
        { q: R`What wavelength, in Å, would scatter from a nanotube of diameter 2.0 nm lit by the $4046.6\ \text{Å}$ line?`, a: 4067.0, u: 'Å', abs: 0.5, h: 'Shift = 248 ÷ 2.0 cm⁻¹.', w: R`$24\,712.1 - 124.0 = 24\,588.1\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, which is $4067.0\ \text{Å}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk25-6', n: 6, t: 'Iodinating a ketone', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 3, m: 20, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`A ketone A slowly forms its enol B, which reacts rapidly with iodine. Step 1: A → B, rate $k_1[\mathrm{A}]$. Step 2: B → A, rate $k_2[\mathrm{B}]$. Step 3: $\mathrm{B + I_2 \rightarrow C + HI}$, rate $k_3[\mathrm{B}][\mathrm{I_2}]$. $k_2 = 7.5\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $k_3 = 4.8 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. The steady state gives $[\mathrm{B}] = \dfrac{k_1[\mathrm{A}]}{k_2 + k_3[\mathrm{I_2}]}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'At high [I₂], which species appear in the rate equation for forming C?', h: 'If every enol is caught at once by iodine, which step limits the rate?', w: R`Only A: rate $= k_1[\mathrm{A}]$, zero order in iodine. Enolisation is rate-determining.` },
        { q: R`At high $[\mathrm{I_2}]$ with $[\mathrm{A}] = 0.150\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$, C forms at $6.9 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}\ s^{-1}}$. Calculate $k_1$.`, a: 4.6e-6, u: 's⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'rate = k₁[A].', w: R`$k_1 = 6.9 \times 10^{-7} \div 0.150 = 4.6 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'At low [I₂], steps 1 and 2 reach equilibrium. Write [B], and the rate of forming C.', h: 'k₁[A] = k₂[B].', w: R`$[\mathrm{B}] = k_1[\mathrm{A}]/k_2$; rate $= \dfrac{k_1k_3}{k_2}[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{I_2}]$, first order in iodine.` },
        { q: 'Write the rate of forming C at any [I₂], and check that it gives both limits.', h: 'rate = k₃[B][I₂] with the steady-state [B].', w: R`rate $= \dfrac{k_1k_3[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{I_2}]}{k_2 + k_3[\mathrm{I_2}]}$. Large $[\mathrm{I_2}]$: $k_1[\mathrm{A}]$. Small: $k_1k_3[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{I_2}]/k_2$.` },
        { q: R`The ketone can also form a second enol D, giving product E: $k_4 = 1.8 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $k_5 = 3.0 \times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $k_6 = 4.8 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. Which product dominates at high $[\mathrm{I_2}]$, and which at low $[\mathrm{I_2}]$?`, h: 'High: compare k₁ and k₄. Low: compare the equilibrium constants k₁/k₂ and k₄/k₅.', w: R`High $[\mathrm{I_2}]$: C, because $k_1 > k_4$. Low $[\mathrm{I_2}]$: E, because $k_4/k_5 = 6.0 \times 10^{-5}$ is far larger than $k_1/k_2 = 6.1 \times 10^{-7}$.` },
        { q: 'At what [I₂] do C and E form at the same rate?', a: 9.94e-6, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.02, h: 'Set k₁/(k₂ + kx) = k₄/(k₅ + kx), where k = k₃ = k₆.', w: R`$x = \dfrac{k_4k_2 - k_1k_5}{k(k_1 - k_4)} = \dfrac{1.35 \times 10^{-5} - 1.38 \times 10^{-7}}{4.8 \times 10^5 \times 2.8 \times 10^{-6}} = 9.9 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2024, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Silver and bronze unit cells · iodate minerals · strained rings as fuel · an MRI contrast agent · sulfur in the atmosphere', qs: [
    { id: 'uk24-1', n: 1, t: 'Silver and bronze', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-1-3', 'ca-3-3'],
      s: R`A football trophy has a mass of $5.20\ \mathrm{kg}$. Silver and bronze (an alloy of copper and tin) both have the face-centred cubic structure, with 4 atoms per unit cell.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the electron configuration of a silver atom and of a $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ ion.`, h: 'Ag is in the same group as Cu; 4s electrons are lost first.', w: R`Ag: [Kr]4d¹⁰5s¹. $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$: 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁹.` },
        { q: R`What would the trophy’s volume be if it were solid silver (density $10.5\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$)?`, a: 495, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'V = m ÷ ρ, with m in g.', w: R`$5200 \div 10.5 = 495\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: R`For bronze, density $= \dfrac{4[M(\mathrm{Cu}) + y(M(\mathrm{Sn}) - M(\mathrm{Cu}))]}{V N_A}$ with $V = 6.00 \times 10^{-23}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. For a bronze of density $8.05\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$, what percentage of the atoms are tin?`, a: 16.6, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'First find the average molar mass of an atom.', w: R`Average $M = 8.05 \times 6.00 \times 10^{-23} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} \div 4 = 72.72$; $y = (72.72 - 63.55) \div (118.71 - 63.55) = 0.166$: 16.6%.` },
        { q: 'A silver atom has radius 144 pm. In a face-centred cubic cell the face diagonal is 4r. Calculate the side length a, in cm.', a: 4.07e-8, u: 'cm', tol: 0.01, h: 'Face diagonal = a√2.', w: R`$a = 4r/\sqrt2 = 407.3\ \mathrm{pm} = 4.07 \times 10^{-8}\ \mathrm{cm}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the volume of the unit cell.', a: 6.76e-23, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'a³.', w: R`$(4.073 \times 10^{-8})^3 = 6.76 \times 10^{-23}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the density of silver.', a: 10.6, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Four atoms per cell.', w: R`$\rho = \dfrac{4 \times 107.87}{6.76 \times 10^{-23} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23}} = 10.6\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk24-2', n: 2, t: 'Iodate minerals', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ci-12-2'],
      s: R`Most iodine minerals contain iodate, $\mathrm{IO_3^-}$. Potassium iodate is stockpiled as an emergency tablet against radioactive iodine. One mineral has the formula $\mathrm{M(IO_3)Z}$: a metal ion, iodate and an anion Z in a 1 : 1 : 1 ratio, with $M = 255.46\ \mathrm{g\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the oxidation state of iodine in $\mathrm{IO_3^-}$, its shape and whether the O–I–O angle is above or below 109.5°.`, h: 'Count the lone pairs on iodine.', w: '+5. Three bonding regions and one lone pair: trigonal pyramidal, angle below 109.5°.' },
        { q: R`A child’s dose is equivalent to $65\ \mathrm{mg}$ of KI. What mass of $\mathrm{KIO_3}$ contains the same amount of iodine, in mg?`, a: 83.8, u: 'mg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Same moles of iodine: scale by molar masses.', w: R`$65 \div 166.0 \times 214.0 = 83.8\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
        { q: 'Write ionic equations for iodate reacting with iodide in acid, and for iodine reacting with thiosulfate.', h: 'I goes from +5 and −1 to 0.', w: R`$\mathrm{IO_3^- + 5I^- + 6H^+ \rightarrow 3I_2 + 3H_2O}$ (redox: a comproportionation). $\mathrm{I_2 + 2S_2O_3^{2-} \rightarrow 2I^- + S_4O_6^{2-}}$` },
        { q: R`$1.250\ \mathrm{g}$ of the mineral dissolves in nitric acid without fizzing and is made up to $250.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. $25.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ portions are treated with excess KI and titrated with $0.0800\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ thiosulfate. If all the iodine came from iodate, what would the titre be?`, a: 36.70, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.005, h: 'One IO₃⁻ gives 3I₂, which need 6 thiosulfate.', w: R`$n = 0.1250 \div 255.46 = 4.893 \times 10^{-4}$; thiosulfate $= 6n = 2.936 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\div 0.0800 = 36.70\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: R`The actual titre is $42.82\ \mathrm{cm^3}$, because the metal ion also oxidises iodide. How many moles of $\mathrm{I_2}$ does one mole of metal ion produce?`, a: 0.5, u: 'mol', abs: 0.02, h: 'Extra titre × concentration = extra thiosulfate; halve it for I₂.', w: R`Extra $6.12\ \mathrm{cm^3} \times 0.0800 = 4.90 \times 10^{-4}$ thiosulfate $\rightarrow 2.45 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{I_2}$, for $4.89 \times 10^{-4}$ metal ions: 0.5.` },
        { q: 'Identify the metal ion and Z, and name two anions that the lack of fizzing rules out.', h: 'Half an I₂ per ion: the metal gains one electron. Which ion does that with iodide? Then subtract masses.', w: R`$\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ (it becomes CuI). $255.46 - 63.55 - 174.90 = 17.01$: Z is $\mathrm{OH^-}$. The mineral is salesite, $\mathrm{Cu(IO_3)(OH)}$. No fizzing rules out carbonate and hydrogencarbonate (and sulfite).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk24-3', n: 3, t: 'Strained rings as fuel', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-14-1'],
      s: R`Engineered bacteria can make fuel molecules containing small carbon rings, which store extra energy as ring strain. Gas-phase data: $\Delta_c H$(cyclobutane) $= -2744$, $\Delta_c H$(cyclohexane) $= -3953$; $\Delta_f H$: $\mathrm{CO_2}$ $-393.5$, $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ $-285.8$ (all $\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the molecular formula of cyclobutane and the equation for its complete combustion.', h: 'A ring of four CH₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_4H_8 + 6O_2 \rightarrow 4CO_2 + 4H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the enthalpy of formation of cyclobutane.', a: 26.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔcH = Σ ΔfH(products) − ΔfH(cyclobutane).', w: R`$\Delta_f H = 4(-393.5) + 4(-285.8) + 2744 = +26.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the enthalpy of combustion per mole of carbon for cyclobutane.', a: -686, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Divide by 4.', w: R`$-2744 \div 4 = -686.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'And for cyclohexane?', a: -658.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Divide by 6.', w: R`$-3953 \div 6 = -658.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Treating cyclohexane as strain-free, estimate the ring strain energy of cyclobutane.', a: 109, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'Extra energy per CH₂ × 4.', w: R`$4 \times (686.0 - 658.8) = 109\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'A base removes an α-hydrogen from a ketone to give an enolate. Give its resonance form, and describe the roles when it reacts with iodomethane.', h: 'Where can the negative charge sit?', w: R`$\mathrm{^-CH_2{-}C(=O)R \leftrightarrow CH_2{=}C(O^-)R}$. The enolate is the nucleophile, iodomethane the electrophile (SN2 at carbon, iodide leaves). It is not a redox reaction.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk24-4', n: 4, t: 'An MRI contrast agent', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 3, m: 24, lec: ['ca-37-4', 'ca-28-1', 'ca-8-2'],
      s: R`Gadopiclenol, $\mathrm{C_{35}H_{54}GdN_7O_{15}}$, is a gadolinium(III) contrast agent for MRI. Water molecules bound to Gd swap with water around it, which shortens the relaxation time of nearby protons and brightens the image.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`A dose is $5.20\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.5000\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ solution. What mass of gadolinium is that, in mg?`, a: 409, u: 'mg', tol: 0.01, h: 'One Gd per molecule.', w: R`$5.20 \times 10^{-3} \times 0.5000 \times 157.25 = 0.409\ \mathrm{g} = 409\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
        { q: 'Which kind of radiation flips nuclear spins in MRI?', h: 'Same as NMR.', w: 'Radio waves.' },
        { q: R`After a pulse, $M = M_0(1 - e^{-t/\tau})$. What percentage of $M_0$ has returned at $t = 2.5\tau$?`, a: 91.8, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'e^(−2.5).', w: R`$1 - e^{-2.5} = 0.918$: 91.8%` },
        { q: R`Calculate the mole fraction $\chi = n(\text{agent})/n(\text{water})$ at $0.0350\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ in water of density $1.00\ \mathrm{kg\ dm^{-3}}$.`, a: 6.31e-4, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: '1 dm³ of water is 55.5 mol.', w: R`$0.0350 \div (1000 \div 18.02) = 6.31 \times 10^{-4}$` },
        { q: R`The relaxation time is $\tau = \tau_c + \tau_e$, where $\tau_e \propto 1/B^2$. Express $\tau_c$ in terms of $\tau_1$ (measured at field $B_1$) and $\tau_2$ (at $B_2$).`, h: 'Write τ₁ = τ_c + k/B₁² and τ₂ = τ_c + k/B₂², then eliminate k.', w: R`$\tau_c = \dfrac{\tau_1B_1^2 - \tau_2B_2^2}{B_1^2 - B_2^2}$` },
        { q: R`At low temperature $\tau_c = \dfrac{1}{q\chi r}$ with exchange rate $r = AT e^{-\Delta H/RT}$. $\tau_c = 1.628 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{s}$ at 278 K and $1.091 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{s}$ at 284 K. Find $Aq\chi$.`, a: 1.37e9, u: 'K⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.04, h: 'ln(1/(τ_c T)) = ln(Aqχ) − ΔH/(RT): a straight line against 1/T.', w: R`$y = \ln\dfrac{1}{\tau_c T}$: 3.0952 at 278 K, 3.4745 at 284 K. Gradient $= -\Delta H/R = -4990\ \mathrm{K}$ ($\Delta H = 41.5\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$). $\ln(Aq\chi) = 3.0952 + 4990/278 = 21.03$, so $Aq\chi = 1.4 \times 10^9\ \mathrm{K^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$.` },
        { q: R`High-temperature data give $A/(q\chi) = 8.722 \times 10^{14}\ \mathrm{K^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. How many water molecules, $q$, are bound to each Gd?`, a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.15, h: 'Divide one result by the other: you get (qχ)².', w: R`$(q\chi)^2 = 1.37 \times 10^9 \div 8.722 \times 10^{14} = 1.57 \times 10^{-6}$, so $q\chi = 1.25 \times 10^{-3}$ and $q = 1.25 \times 10^{-3} \div 6.31 \times 10^{-4} = 2$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk24-5', n: 5, t: 'Sulfur in the atmosphere', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 3, m: 20, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-12-1'],
      s: R`Dimethyl sulfide (DMS), $\mathrm{(CH_3)_2S}$, made by marine microbes, is slowly oxidised in the air. To find oxidation states from structures: give each shared pair to the more electronegative atom (H 2.20, C 2.55, S 2.58, O 3.44), then compare the electrons on S with a neutral S atom.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Oxidation state of S in DMS?', a: -2, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'S is slightly more electronegative than C.', w: 'S keeps both C–S pairs: 6 + 2 = 8 electrons, so −2.' },
        { q: R`In dimethyl sulfoxide, $\mathrm{(CH_3)_2S{=}O}$?`, a: 0, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'The S=O pairs go to O.', w: 'Gains 2 from the C–S bonds, loses 2 to the S=O: 0.' },
        { q: R`In methanesulfonic acid, $\mathrm{CH_3SO_2OH}$?`, a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'One C–S, two S=O, one S–OH.', w: 'C–S: −1. Two S=O: +4. S–O: +1. Total +4.' },
        { q: R`The final product is a gas G that dissolves to give a strong diprotic acid. $15.6\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of G at 101 kPa and 318 K dissolves in $1.000\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of water. What is the pH, to 2 decimal places, if the acid fully dissociates?`, a: 2.92, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'G is SO₃; each gives 2H⁺.', w: R`$n = \dfrac{101\,000 \times 15.6 \times 10^{-6}}{8.314 \times 318} = 5.96 \times 10^{-4}$; $[\mathrm{H^+}] = 1.19 \times 10^{-3}$; pH 2.92` },
        { q: R`A radical L• reacts with C (in large excess) to form J•: rate $= k[\mathrm{C}]^a[\mathrm{L•}]^b$. Experiment 1: $[\mathrm{C}]_0 = 800\ \mu\mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$; [L•] 1.00 → 0.50 (12 s) → 0.25 (24 s). Experiment 2: $[\mathrm{C}]_0 = 400$; [L•] 2.00 → 1.41 (12 s) → 1.00 (24 s). What is $a$? (b is 1, since L• falls with a constant half-life.)`, a: 1, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Compare the half-lives when [C] halves.', w: 'Half-life 12 s with [C] = 800, 24 s with [C] = 400: halving C halves the pseudo-first-order constant, so a = 1.' },
        { q: R`Calculate $k$ in $\mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$.`, a: 72.2, u: 'dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'k′ = ln 2 ÷ t½ = k[C].', w: R`$k' = 0.693 \div 12 = 0.0578\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$; $k = 0.0578 \div 8.00 \times 10^{-4} = 72.2\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`N• + $\mathrm{O_2}$ needs a third molecule X: rate $= k_3[\mathrm{N•}][\mathrm{O_2}][\mathrm{X}]$. At 298 K and 100 kPa, the measured rate $= k_2[\mathrm{N•}][\mathrm{O_2}]$ with $k_2 = 6.3 \times 10^{-12}\ \mathrm{cm^3\ molecule^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$, and the total gas concentration is $40.4\ \mathrm{mmol\ dm^{-3}}$. Calculate $k_3$ in $\mathrm{cm^6\ molecule^{-2}\ s^{-1}}$.`, a: 2.59e-31, u: 'cm⁶ molecule⁻² s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: '[X] = total gas, in molecules cm⁻³.', w: R`$[\mathrm{X}] = 40.4 \times 10^{-3} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} \div 1000 = 2.43 \times 10^{19}\ \mathrm{cm^{-3}}$; $k_3 = 6.3 \times 10^{-12} \div 2.43 \times 10^{19} = 2.6 \times 10^{-31}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2023, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Rocket fuels · electronegativity and crystal densities · amino acid complexes · vaping · holes in Swiss cheese', qs: [
    { id: 'uk23-1', n: 1, t: 'Rocket fuels', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: 'Rockets burn liquid hydrogen or liquid methane with liquid oxygen. On Mars, methane could be made from the CO₂-rich atmosphere by the Sabatier process.',
      p: [
        { q: R`Bond enthalpies: H–H 436, O–H 463 $\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. $\mathrm{H_2(g) + \tfrac12O_2(g) \rightarrow H_2O(g)}$ has $\Delta H = -242\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Calculate the O=O bond enthalpy.`, a: 496, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Broken: H–H + ½ O=O. Made: 2 O–H.', w: R`$-242 = 436 + \tfrac12x - 926$, so $x = 496\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Liquid hydrogen has density $0.0708\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$. How many moles of $\mathrm{H_2}$ are in $2.50\ \mathrm{dm^3}$?`, a: 87.8, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: '2500 cm³ × density.', w: R`$2500 \times 0.0708 = 177.0\ \mathrm{g}$; $\div 2.016 = 87.8\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'How much energy is released when it burns to steam?', a: 2.12e4, u: 'kJ', tol: 0.01, h: '242 kJ per mole of H₂.', w: R`$87.8 \times 242 = 2.12 \times 10^4\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Write the equation for the Sabatier process and give the oxidation numbers of C and H before and after.', h: 'CO₂ and hydrogen give methane and water.', w: R`$\mathrm{CO_2 + 4H_2 \rightarrow CH_4 + 2H_2O}$. C: +4 → −4. H: 0 → +1.` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{CH_4(g) + 2O_2(g) \rightarrow CO_2(g) + 2H_2O(g)}$, $\Delta H = -802.3\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Enthalpies of vaporisation: $\mathrm{CH_4}$ $+8.5$, $\mathrm{O_2}$ $+6.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Find $\Delta H$ when 1 mol of liquid methane burns in liquid oxygen to gaseous products.`, a: -780.2, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'First boil the liquids: that costs energy.', w: R`$-802.3 + 8.5 + 2 \times 6.8 = -780.2\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk23-2', n: 2, t: 'Electronegativity', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 18, lec: ['ca-3-1', 'ca-3-6', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Pauling: $\chi_A - \chi_B = 0.102\sqrt{D(\mathrm{AB}) - \tfrac12[D(\mathrm{AA}) + D(\mathrm{BB})]}$, with $D$ in $\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Mulliken: $\chi = 0.00197(E_i + E_{ea}) + 0.19$, energies in $\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$ (1 eV = 96.49 kJ mol⁻¹).`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$D$: H–H 436, Br–Br 193, H–Br 366. Calculate $\chi(\mathrm{Br}) - \chi(\mathrm{H})$.`, a: 0.732, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Average of 436 and 193 is 314.5.', w: R`$0.102\sqrt{366 - 314.5} = 0.102 \times 7.18 = 0.732$` },
        { q: R`With $\chi(\mathrm{H}) = 2.20$, what is $\chi(\mathrm{Br})$?`, a: 2.93, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Bromine is the more electronegative.', w: R`$2.20 + 0.73 = 2.93$` },
        { q: R`For oxygen, $E_i = 13.62\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $E_{ea} = 141.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Calculate its Mulliken electronegativity.`, a: 3.06, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Convert eV to kJ mol⁻¹ first.', w: R`$13.62 \times 96.49 = 1314.2$; $0.00197 \times 1455.2 + 0.19 = 3.06$` },
        { q: R`Van Arkel–Ketelaar triangles plot the mean electronegativity against the difference. Using Cs 0.79, F 3.98, Si 1.90, C 2.55: work out both for CsF and for SiC, and classify the bonding.`, h: 'Large difference = ionic; small difference with high mean = covalent; small difference with low mean = metallic.', w: 'CsF: mean 2.39, difference 3.19: ionic. SiC: mean 2.23, difference 0.65: covalent (a giant covalent network).' },
        { q: R`Boron phosphide has the zinc blende structure: cubic, $a = 4.538\ \text{Å}$, 4 BP units per cell. Calculate the cell volume in $\mathrm{cm^3}$.`, a: 9.35e-23, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: '1 Å = 10⁻⁸ cm.', w: R`$(4.538 \times 10^{-8})^3 = 9.35 \times 10^{-23}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the density of boron phosphide.', a: 2.97, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mass of 4 BP ÷ volume.', w: R`$\rho = \dfrac{4 \times 41.78}{9.35 \times 10^{-23} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23}} = 2.97\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk23-3', n: 3, t: 'Amino acid complexes', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-34-4', 'ca-28-4', 'ca-28-3'],
      s: R`Amino acids have an acidic –COOH and a basic $\mathrm{–NH_2}$. Phenylalanine ($\mathrm{R = CH_2C_6H_5}$) has $\mathrm{p}K_a$ values of 1.83 (the COOH of the cation) and 9.13 (the $\mathrm{NH_3^+}$). Deprotonated amino acids bind to metal ions through N and O.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the main form of phenylalanine at pH 1, pH 7 and pH 12.', h: 'Below pKa the group keeps its proton.', w: R`pH 1: $\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH(R){-}COOH}$. pH 7: the zwitterion $\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH(R){-}COO^-}$. pH 12: $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH(R){-}COO^-}$.` },
        { q: 'Calculate the isoelectric point.', a: 5.48, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Average of the two pKa values.', w: R`$(1.83 + 9.13) \div 2 = 5.48$` },
        { q: R`$50.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0250\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ phenylalanine hydrochloride is titrated with $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. What volume reaches the second equivalence point?`, a: 25.0, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'The cation has two acidic protons.', w: R`$2 \times 1.25 \times 10^{-3} = 2.50 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$ NaOH $= 25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: R`What is the pH after $18.75\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of NaOH?`, a: 9.13, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'Halfway between the first and second equivalence points.', w: 'Half of the zwitterion has been deprotonated: pH = pKa₂ = 9.13.' },
        { q: 'An octahedral complex M(Mt)₃ has three identical unsymmetrical N,O-bidentate ligands. How many stereoisomers are there, ignoring any chirality in the ligands themselves?', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'fac and mer, and are they chiral?', w: 'fac and mer isomers, each chiral: 4 stereoisomers.' },
        { q: R`Calculate the spin-only magnetic moment of the Cu(II) ion in an octahedral complex, $\mu = \sqrt{n(n+2)}$ BM.`, a: 1.73, u: 'BM', tol: 0.01, h: 'Cu²⁺ is d⁹.', w: R`One unpaired electron: $\sqrt3 = 1.73$ BM.` },
        { q: R`An Fe(III) complex has a measured moment of 5.75 BM. How many unpaired electrons does it have?`, a: 5, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Fe³⁺ is d⁵: compare 5.92 BM (high spin) with 1.73 BM (low spin).', w: 'Five: it is high spin.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk23-4', n: 4, t: 'Vaping', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-22-2', 'ca-37-4', 'ca-18-2'],
      s: R`Vitamin E acetate, $\mathrm{C_xH_{52}O_3}$, was found in vape liquids linked to lung injuries. It is harmless when swallowed, but when heated it releases a highly toxic gas.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'It contains a benzene ring, a cyclic ether ring, an ester C=O, and no other double bonds or rings. Find x.', a: 31, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'DBE = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6 = x − 52/2 + 1.', w: R`$6 = x - 26 + 1$, so $x = 31$: $\mathrm{C_{31}H_{52}O_3}$.` },
        { q: R`In the mass spectrum, an ion at $m/z$ 193.1 loses the toxic product to give $m/z$ 151.1. What is the $M_r$ of the toxic product?`, a: 42, u: '', abs: 0.2, h: 'Subtract.', w: '193.1 − 151.1 = 42.0' },
        { q: 'The toxic product has one ¹H NMR signal and two ¹³C signals, and comes from the acetate group. Identify it.', h: 'C₂H₂O.', w: R`Ketene, $\mathrm{CH_2{=}C{=}O}$.` },
        { q: 'In the Wittig reaction, an ylide with C=O next to the carbanion gives mainly the E alkene; otherwise mainly Z. Predict the products of Ph₃P=CHCO₂CH₃ with benzaldehyde, and of Ph₃P=CHCH₂CH₃ with ethanal.', h: 'Join the two carbons with C=C.', w: 'Methyl (E)-3-phenylprop-2-enoate (E, stabilised ylide); (Z)-pent-2-ene (Z, unstabilised ylide).' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk23-5', n: 5, t: 'Holes in Swiss cheese', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 22, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`Lactose is hydrolysed and fermented to lactic acid, $\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)COOH}$ ($K_a = 1.38 \times 10^{-4}$). In Emmental, bacteria then turn lactic acid into propanoic acid, ethanoic acid, $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and water. The $\mathrm{CO_2}$ makes the holes.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Calculate the pH of $0.0450\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ lactic acid (solve the quadratic).`, a: 2.62, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'x² ÷ (0.0450 − x) = 1.38 × 10⁻⁴.', w: R`$x = 2.42 \times 10^{-3}$, pH 2.62` },
        { q: 'Why is lactic acid stronger than propanoic acid?', h: 'Think about the OH next to the carboxylate.', w: 'The OH on the neighbouring carbon withdraws electrons and can hydrogen-bond to the carboxylate, stabilising the conjugate base.' },
        { q: 'Classify the breakdown of lactose, name its products, and write the overall equation for lactose giving lactic acid.', h: 'Water splits the disaccharide.', w: R`Hydrolysis, giving glucose and galactose. $\mathrm{C_{12}H_{22}O_{11} + H_2O \rightarrow 4C_3H_6O_3}$` },
        { q: 'Write the fermentation equation: lactic acid is the only reactant, and ethanoic acid and CO₂ form in equal amounts.', h: 'Try 3 lactic acid.', w: R`$\mathrm{3C_3H_6O_3 \rightarrow 2C_3H_6O_2 + C_2H_4O_2 + CO_2 + H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'A spherical hole has diameter 2.2 cm. What is its volume in m³?', a: 5.58e-6, u: 'm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'r = 0.011 m.', w: R`$\tfrac43\pi(0.011)^3 = 5.58 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m^3}$` },
        { q: 'If it holds pure CO₂ at 101 325 Pa and 20 °C, what mass of lactic acid was fermented, in mg?', a: 62.7, u: 'mg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Three lactic acid per CO₂.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{CO_2}) = \dfrac{101\,325 \times 5.58 \times 10^{-6}}{8.314 \times 293} = 2.32 \times 10^{-4}$; lactic acid $= 6.96 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} = 62.7\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{CO_2(aq) + H_2O \rightleftharpoons HCO_3^- + H^+}$, $K = 4.47 \times 10^{-7}$. At the end, $[\mathrm{CO_2(aq)}] + [\mathrm{HCO_3^-}] = 4.20 \times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ and the pH is 5.35. Find $[\mathrm{CO_2(aq)}]$.`, a: 3.82e-2, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: '[HCO₃⁻]/[CO₂] = K ÷ [H⁺].', w: R`Ratio $= 4.47 \times 10^{-7} \div 4.47 \times 10^{-6} = 0.100$; $[\mathrm{CO_2}] = 4.20 \times 10^{-2} \div 1.100 = 3.82 \times 10^{-2}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2022, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Ethanol in petrol · nitrous oxide · cubane · lateral flow tests · a tiny molecular knot · storing vaccines in silica', skipped: 'Question 3 is cut down to its NMR parts and question 5 to its analysis parts; their long syntheses are drawn as structures.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk22-1', n: 1, t: 'Ethanol in petrol', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-14-1', 'ca-16-1'],
      s: 'Petrol in the UK is sold as E5 or E10, where the number is the percentage of ethanol by volume; the rest is a mixture of octane isomers such as 2,2,4-trimethylpentane. The ethanol is a biofuel made by fermenting plant sugars.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the structural formula of 2,2,4-trimethylpentane.', h: 'Pentane chain; two methyls on C2, one on C4.', w: R`$\mathrm{(CH_3)_3C{-}CH_2{-}CH(CH_3)_2}$` },
        { q: 'Write equations for making glucose by photosynthesis, and for fermenting glucose to ethanol.', h: 'Fermentation also gives CO₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{6CO_2 + 6H_2O \rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6O_2}$; $\mathrm{C_6H_{12}O_6 \rightarrow 2C_2H_5OH + 2CO_2}$` },
        { q: R`Mean bond enthalpies ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): C–C 346, C–H 414, O=O 496, C=O 803, O–H 463. Estimate the enthalpy of combustion of an octane isomer, $\mathrm{C_8H_{18}}$, to gaseous products.`, a: -5108, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Any isomer has 7 C–C and 18 C–H. It needs 12.5 O₂ and makes 8 CO₂ and 9 H₂O.', w: R`Broken: $7(346) + 18(414) + 12.5(496) = 16\,074$. Made: $16(803) + 18(463) = 21\,182$. $\Delta H = -5108\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`1 L of E5 is 50 mL ethanol + 950 mL octane; 1 L of E15 is 150 mL ethanol + 850 mL octane. Densities: ethanol $0.789$, octane $0.703\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$. $\Delta_c H$: ethanol $-1270$, octane $-5108\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Taking E5 as 100%, how much energy does 1 L of E15 give?`, a: 96.9, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Find moles of each fuel in each litre.', w: R`E5: ethanol 0.856 mol, octane 5.847 mol: $30\,950\ \mathrm{kJ}$. E15: ethanol 2.569 mol, octane 5.231 mol: $29\,980\ \mathrm{kJ}$. $96.9\%$` },
        { q: 'Counting only the CO₂ from the octane (the ethanol is treated as carbon-neutral), what percentage of E5’s CO₂ does E15 release?', a: 89.5, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Same octane per mL in both.', w: R`$850 \div 950 = 89.5\%$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk22-2', n: 2, t: 'Nitrous oxide', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-12-1', 'ca-3-5'],
      s: R`Nitrous oxide, $\mathrm{N_2O}$, is used in whipped cream and as an anaesthetic. Two routes to it start from nitrous acid, $\mathrm{HNO_2}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Describe the bonding in $\mathrm{HNO_2}$.`, h: 'H–O–N=O.', w: 'H–O–N=O: N has one lone pair, the OH oxygen two, the double-bonded O two.' },
        { q: R`Route 1: $\mathrm{HNO_2}$ reacts with oxygen to give A, which is 76.17% O and 1.60% H by mass, the rest nitrogen. Identify A.`, h: 'Find the moles of each element.', w: R`N 22.23%. Ratio H : N : O = 1.59 : 1.59 : 4.76 = 1 : 1 : 3: $\mathrm{HNO_3}$.` },
        { q: 'A reacts with ammonia to give a salt that decomposes on heating to N₂O and water. Name the ions and give their shapes.', h: 'An acid and a base.', w: R`$\mathrm{NH_4^+}$, tetrahedral; $\mathrm{NO_3^-}$, trigonal planar. $\mathrm{NH_4NO_3 \rightarrow N_2O + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`What mass of that salt gives $5.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of $\mathrm{N_2O}$ at 298 K and 100 kPa?`, a: 16.2, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'n = pV/RT; one N₂O per formula unit.', w: R`$n = \dfrac{100\,000 \times 0.00500}{8.314 \times 298} = 0.202\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 80.04 = 16.2\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`Route 2: $\mathrm{2HNO_2 \rightarrow D + E + H_2O}$. E is a brown gas in equilibrium with a colourless gas X. D and E combine to F, which decomposes to $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{O_2}$. Identify D, E, F and X.`, h: 'Nitrogen oxides with N in +2 and +4.', w: R`D = NO, E = $\mathrm{NO_2}$, X = $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$, F = $\mathrm{N_2O_3}$ ($\mathrm{N_2O_3 \rightarrow N_2O + O_2}$).` },
        { q: R`Describe the structure of $\mathrm{N_2O}$ (linear, N in the middle).`, h: 'Two resonance forms.', w: R`$\mathrm{N{=}N^+{=}O^-}$ ↔ $\mathrm{N{\equiv}N^+{-}O^-}$: linear, central N positive.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk22-3', n: 3, t: 'Cubane', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-37-3', 'ca-13-4'],
      s: 'Cubane is a hydrocarbon whose eight carbon atoms sit at the corners of a cube, each bonded to one hydrogen. It is made from cubane-1,4-dicarboxylic acid.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the molecular formula of cubane.', h: 'Eight CH units.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_8H_8}$` },
        { q: 'How many ¹³C NMR signals does cubane have?', a: 1, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'All corners are alike.', w: 'One: all eight carbons are equivalent.' },
        { q: 'How many ¹³C signals does cubanecarboxylic acid have?', a: 5, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Count cube positions by their distance from the substituted corner, then add the COOH.', w: 'Substituted C; three neighbours; three face-diagonal carbons; the opposite corner; COOH: 5.' },
        { q: 'Cubane-1,2-dicarboxylic acid (adjacent corners)?', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Use the mirror planes through the C1–C2 edge.', w: 'The two substituted C; four carbons next to one of them (all equivalent); the last two; COOH: 4.' },
        { q: 'Cubane-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (opposite corners)?', a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Very symmetrical.', w: 'The two substituted C; the other six (all equivalent); COOH: 3.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk22-4', n: 4, t: 'Lateral flow tests', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 18, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-7-1'],
      s: R`A lateral flow test detects spike proteins (SP) of a virus. The swab goes into $1.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of extraction solution containing $8.0\ \mathrm{mmol\ dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{Na_2HPO_4}$ and $4.0\ \mathrm{mmol\ dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{KH_2PO_4}$. Gold nanoparticles (NP) coated with antibodies bind the spike protein: $\mathrm{SP + NP \rightleftharpoons SPNP}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$\mathrm{p}K_a(\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}) = 7.20$. What is the pH of the extraction solution, and what is it for?`, a: 7.50, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Henderson–Hasselbalch.', w: R`$7.20 + \log(8.0/4.0) = 7.50$. It is a buffer, keeping the proteins at a steady pH.` },
        { q: 'A sample has 5.4 × 10⁶ virus particles per cm³, each carrying 25 spike proteins. Calculate [SP] in mol dm⁻³.', a: 2.24e-13, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Particles per dm³ ÷ N_A.', w: R`$5.4 \times 10^6 \times 25 \times 1000 \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 2.24 \times 10^{-13}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`The nanoparticles are in large excess at $2.0 \times 10^{12}\ \mathrm{cm^{-3}}$, and $K = 1.5 \times 10^{10}\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$. What fraction of the spike protein carries a nanoparticle at equilibrium?`, a: 0.980, u: '', tol: 0.005, h: '[SPNP]/[SP] = K[NP]. Convert [NP] to mol dm⁻³ first.', w: R`$[\mathrm{NP}] = 3.32 \times 10^{-9}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$; $K[\mathrm{NP}] = 49.8$; fraction $= 49.8 \div 50.8 = 0.980$` },
        { q: R`The test line is $4.0\ \mathrm{mm} \times 1.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ with $1.0 \times 10^9$ antibodies per $\mathrm{mm^2}$. How many antibodies is that?`, a: 4.0e9, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Area × density.', w: R`$4.0 \times 10^9$` },
        { q: R`The line becomes visible at $3 \times 10^6$ nanoparticles per $\mathrm{mm^2}$. Assuming every spike protein that carries a nanoparticle is caught on the line, what is the minimum number of virus particles in the sample?`, a: 4.9e5, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'Nanoparticles needed ÷ (25 × fraction bound).', w: R`Needed: $3 \times 10^6 \times 4.0 = 1.2 \times 10^7$. Each virus gives $25 \times 0.980 = 24.5$: $4.9 \times 10^5$ virus particles.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk22-5', n: 5, t: 'A tiny molecular knot', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 3, m: 14, lec: ['ca-28-1', 'ca-37-4', 'ca-2-3'],
      s: 'Chemists tied the smallest “Chinese knot” molecule by wrapping organic strands around a 3 × 3 grid of metal ions, joining the strand ends, then removing the metals.',
      p: [
        { q: 'A by-product B (M = 139.1) is 51.8% C, 3.6% H and 10.1% N, the rest oxygen. Identify it.', h: 'Find the formula, then think of nitration of phenol.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_6H_5NO_3}$: 4-nitrophenol.` },
        { q: 'An amine RNH₂ gives these ¹H NMR signals in the product: 3.26 (2H), 1.77 (1H, multiplet), 1.63 (2H), 0.98 (6H, doublet). Identify the amine.', h: 'A 6H doublet means an isopropyl end.', w: R`3-Methylbutan-1-amine, $\mathrm{(CH_3)_2CHCH_2CH_2NH_2}$.` },
        { q: 'Each metal in the grid is bound by two tridentate strand sections. What is the geometry around the metal?', h: 'Six donor atoms.', w: 'Octahedral.' },
        { q: R`The metal salt is $\mathrm{MX_2 \cdot 6H_2O}$. With NaOH it gives a green precipitate that turns brown in air. $2.500\ \mathrm{g}$ loses $0.801\ \mathrm{g}$ of water on gentle heating. X⁻ is a tetrahedral ion of two elements, neither oxygen. Find the molar mass of the hydrated salt.`, a: 337.5, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: '0.801 g is six waters.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{H_2O}) = 0.801 \div 18.02 = 0.04445$; salt $= 0.007408\ \mathrm{mol}$; $M = 2.500 \div 0.007408 = 337.5$` },
        { q: 'Identify M²⁺ and X⁻.', h: 'Green then brown: iron(II) hydroxide. Subtract to get 2X.', w: R`$\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$. $337.5 - 108.1 - 55.85 = 173.6 = 2 \times 86.8$: $\mathrm{BF_4^-}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk22-6', n: 6, t: 'Storing vaccines in silica', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 18, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`“Ensilication” coats vaccine proteins in silica so they survive without a fridge. Tetraethyl orthosilicate is hydrolysed to $\mathrm{Si(OH)_4}$, which then condenses to $\mathrm{SiO_2}$ around the protein. The silica is later removed with fluoride: $\mathrm{SiO_2 + 6NaF + 6HCl \rightarrow H_2SiF_6 + 6NaCl + 2H_2O}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write equations for the hydrolysis of Si(OCH₂CH₃)₄ and the condensation of Si(OH)₄.', h: 'Four ethanol leave; then two water.', w: R`$\mathrm{Si(OC_2H_5)_4 + 4H_2O \rightarrow Si(OH)_4 + 4C_2H_5OH}$; $\mathrm{Si(OH)_4 \rightarrow SiO_2 + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`Use: $\mathrm{NaOH + HCl \rightarrow NaCl + H_2O}$, $-57.1$; $\mathrm{SiO_2 + 6HF \rightarrow H_2SiF_6 + 2H_2O}$, $-98.6$; $\mathrm{NaOH + HF \rightarrow NaF + H_2O}$, $-60.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Calculate $\Delta H$ for the silica removal.`, a: -76.4, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: '6 × (1) + (2) − 6 × (3).', w: R`$6(-57.1) - 98.6 + 6(60.8) = -76.4\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`For the condensation, $K = 1/[\mathrm{Si(OH)_4}]$ and $\ln[\mathrm{Si(OH)_4}] = -1720/T - 0.580$. Find $\Delta H^\circ$.`, a: -14.3, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'ln K = 1720/T + 0.580; compare with ln K = −ΔH/RT + ΔS/R.', w: R`$-\Delta H/R = 1720$, so $\Delta H = -14.3\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`And $\Delta S^\circ$?`, a: 4.82, u: 'J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔS/R = 0.580.', w: R`$8.314 \times 0.580 = +4.82\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Light scattering from coated (radius $r_1$) and free (radius $r_2$) protein gives $c = [1 - \tfrac{\Gamma t}{r_1} + \tfrac12(\tfrac{\Gamma t}{r_1})^2] + A[1 - \tfrac{\Gamma t}{r_2} + \tfrac12(\tfrac{\Gamma t}{r_2})^2]$, with $\Gamma = 0.170713\ \mathrm{nm\ \mu s^{-1}}$. At $t = 0$, $c_0 = 1.425000$. Find A.`, a: 0.425, u: '', tol: 0.002, h: 'At t = 0 each bracket is 1.', w: R`$c_0 = 1 + A$, so $A = 0.425$` },
        { q: R`$f = \dfrac{c - c_0}{\Gamma t} = at - b$, with $a = \tfrac12\Gamma(r_1^{-2} + Ar_2^{-2})$ and $b = r_1^{-1} + Ar_2^{-1}$. $c = 1.402238$ at $t = 1\ \mu\mathrm{s}$ and $1.380044$ at $t = 2\ \mu\mathrm{s}$. Find $b$, in $\mathrm{nm^{-1}}$.`, a: 0.135, u: 'nm⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Work out f at both times; the line through them gives a and b.', w: R`$f_1 = -0.133335$, $f_2 = -0.131671$; $a = 0.001664\ \mathrm{nm^{-1}\ \mu s^{-1}}$, $b = a - f_1 = 0.1350\ \mathrm{nm^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Find the radius of the free protein, r₂, in nm.', a: 5.0, u: 'nm', tol: 0.02, h: 'x₁ + Ax₂ = b and x₁² + Ax₂² = 2a/Γ: substitute x₁ = b − Ax₂.', w: R`$0.6056x_2^2 - 0.1148x_2 - 0.00128 = 0$ gives $x_2 = 0.200$: $r_2 = 5.0\ \mathrm{nm}$. Then $x_1 = 0.050$: $r_1 = 20\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the coated protein.` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2021, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Phosphine on Venus · carbon capture · levulinic acid · conformations of butane · xenon fluorides', skipped: 'Question 5 (the synthesis of hydroxychloroquine) is drawn almost entirely as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk21-1', n: 1, t: 'Phosphine on Venus', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`In 2020 a research paper claimed to detect phosphine, $\mathrm{PH_3}$, in the clouds of Venus. On Earth phosphine comes from living things or from industry, and with oxygen it is oxidised to phosphoric acid.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the shape of phosphine.', h: 'Three bonding pairs and one lone pair.', w: 'Trigonal pyramidal.' },
        { q: R`Give the oxidation state of P in $\mathrm{PH_3}$ and in $\mathrm{H_3PO_4}$, and the equation for phosphine burning in oxygen to phosphoric acid.`, h: 'Here H is +1.', w: R`−3 and +5. $\mathrm{PH_3 + 2O_2 \rightarrow H_3PO_4}$` },
        { q: R`Write equations for three non-biological routes: $\mathrm{PCl_3}$ with LiH; calcium phosphide with water; heating $\mathrm{H_3PO_3}$ (it disproportionates).`, h: 'In the last, P goes from +3 to −3 and +5.', w: R`$\mathrm{PCl_3 + 3LiH \rightarrow PH_3 + 3LiCl}$; $\mathrm{Ca_3P_2 + 6H_2O \rightarrow 2PH_3 + 3Ca(OH)_2}$; $\mathrm{4H_3PO_3 \rightarrow PH_3 + 3H_3PO_4}$` },
        { q: R`With hydrochloric acid, phosphine and methanal give a salt of molar mass $190.5\ \mathrm{g\ mol^{-1}}$ with a 1+ cation. Its ¹³C NMR has one signal; its ¹H NMR has two, one of which disappears with $\mathrm{D_2O}$. Identify the cation.`, h: 'Subtract Cl⁻; count how many CH₂OH groups P can carry.', w: R`$190.5 - 35.5 = 155.1$: $\mathrm{[P(CH_2OH)_4]^+}$, tetrakis(hydroxymethyl)phosphonium. One carbon environment; CH₂ and OH protons (OH exchanges with D₂O).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk21-2', n: 2, t: 'Carbon capture', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-10-1'],
      s: R`Calcium looping captures $\mathrm{CO_2}$ by cycling between CaO and $\mathrm{CaCO_3}$. Direct air capture instead uses KOH solution, which is regenerated with $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$. $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): CaO $-635.1$; $\mathrm{CaCO_3}$ $-1206.9$; $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$ $-986.1$; KOH $-425.9$; $\mathrm{K_2CO_3}$ $-1149.6$; $\mathrm{CO_2}$ $-393.5$; $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ $-285.8$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the equations in the carbonator and in the calciner.', h: 'One captures CO₂, the other releases it.', w: R`Carbonator: $\mathrm{CaO + CO_2 \rightarrow CaCO_3}$. Calciner: $\mathrm{CaCO_3 \rightarrow CaO + CO_2}$.` },
        { q: R`Flue gas contains $\mathrm{SO_2}$ from burning $\mathrm{H_2S}$, which reacts with $\mathrm{CaCO_3}$ and $\mathrm{O_2}$ to give two products. Write both equations.`, h: 'The calcium ends up as a sulfate.', w: R`$\mathrm{2H_2S + 3O_2 \rightarrow 2SO_2 + 2H_2O}$; $\mathrm{2SO_2 + 2CaCO_3 + O_2 \rightarrow 2CaSO_4 + 2CO_2}$` },
        { q: R`Calculate $\Delta H$ for the air contactor: $\mathrm{2KOH + CO_2 \rightarrow K_2CO_3 + H_2O}$.`, a: -190.1, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$(-1149.6 - 285.8) - (2(-425.9) - 393.5) = -190.1\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Calculate $\Delta H$ for the pellet reactor: $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2 + K_2CO_3 \rightarrow CaCO_3 + 2KOH}$.`, a: 77.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$(-1206.9 + 2(-425.9)) - (-986.1 - 1149.6) = +77.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk21-3', n: 3, t: 'Levulinic acid', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-18-1', 'ca-17-1', 'ca-28-4'],
      s: R`Levulinic acid, $\mathrm{CH_3COCH_2CH_2COOH}$, is made from sugars such as fructose and used to make medicines, plastics and fuels. Fructose loses three molecules of water, and the product is hydrolysed to levulinic acid and a by-product F.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Name the functional groups in levulinic acid and give its molecular formula.', h: 'Look at both ends.', w: R`Ketone and carboxylic acid; $\mathrm{C_5H_8O_3}$.` },
        { q: R`Overall, $\mathrm{C_6H_{12}O_6 \rightarrow C_5H_8O_3 + F + H_2O}$. Identify F.`, h: 'Balance the atoms.', w: R`F is methanoic acid, HCOOH.` },
        { q: 'What mass of levulinic acid can be made from 1.00 kg of fructose at 60% yield?', a: 387, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'One levulinic acid per fructose.', w: R`$1000 \div 180.16 = 5.551\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 0.60 \times 116.12 = 387\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`A ruthenium catalyst for the next step is $\mathrm{[RuH_2(CO)(PPh_3)_3]}$. What is the oxidation state of Ru?`, a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Hydride is H⁻; CO and PPh₃ are neutral.', w: '+2' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk21-4', n: 4, t: 'Conformations of butane', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-13-4'],
      s: 'Rotating about a C–C single bond gives conformers. Viewed down the C2–C3 bond of butane, the staggered conformers are anti (AP, the two methyls opposite) and two equivalent gauche forms, G1 and G2.',
      p: [
        { q: 'For ethane, at which dihedral angles between 0° and 360° is it eclipsed, and at which staggered? Which is lower in energy?', h: 'Every 60°.', w: 'Eclipsed at 0°, 120°, 240°, 360°; staggered at 60°, 180°, 300°. Staggered is lower in energy.' },
        { q: R`$\Delta G^\circ$ for $\mathrm{G1 \rightleftharpoons AP}$ is $-3.80\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$ at 298 K. Calculate K.`, a: 4.64, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'K = e^(−ΔG°/RT).', w: R`$K = e^{3800/(8.314 \times 298)} = 4.64$` },
        { q: R`What are $\Delta G^\circ$ and K for $\mathrm{G2 \rightleftharpoons G1}$, and K for $\mathrm{AP \rightleftharpoons G2}$?`, h: 'G1 and G2 are mirror images.', w: R`G2 ⇌ G1: $\Delta G^\circ = 0$, $K = 1$. AP ⇌ G2: $\Delta G^\circ = +3.80$, $K = 0.216$.` },
        { q: 'What percentage of the time is butane in the anti conformer?', a: 69.9, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'AP : G1 : G2 = 4.64 : 1 : 1.', w: R`$4.64 \div 6.64 = 69.9\%$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk21-6', n: 6, t: 'Xenon fluorides', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 3, m: 22, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-26-1', 'ca-8-2'],
      s: R`Xenon reacts with fluorine to give $\mathrm{XeF_2}$, $\mathrm{XeF_4}$ and $\mathrm{XeF_6}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the equation for making $\mathrm{XeF_4}$, and give the shapes of $\mathrm{XeF_4}$ and $\mathrm{XeF_2}$.`, h: 'Six and five electron pairs on Xe.', w: R`$\mathrm{Xe + 2F_2 \rightarrow XeF_4}$. $\mathrm{XeF_4}$: square planar (two lone pairs opposite each other). $\mathrm{XeF_2}$: linear (three lone pairs round the middle).` },
        { q: R`At 120 °C, in a fixed volume: 1.5 mol Xe + 4.0 mol $\mathrm{F_2}$, rate $2.7 \times 10^{-5}$; 1.5 mol Xe + 8.0 mol $\mathrm{F_2}$, rate $2.7 \times 10^{-5}$; 3.0 mol Xe + 8.0 mol $\mathrm{F_2}$, rate $5.4 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}\ s^{-1}}$. What is the order with respect to $\mathrm{F_2}$?`, a: 0, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Compare the first two runs.', w: R`Doubling $\mathrm{F_2}$ changes nothing: zero order. Doubling Xe doubles the rate: rate $= k[\mathrm{Xe}]$.` },
        { q: R`A nickel fluoride catalyst makes the reaction 11 times faster at 130 °C and 19 times faster at 105 °C. Assuming the A factors do not change with temperature, calculate $\Delta E = E_a - E_{cat}$.`, a: 27.7, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k_cat/k = (A_cat/A) e^(ΔE/RT); take the ratio at the two temperatures.', w: R`$\ln\dfrac{19}{11} = \dfrac{\Delta E}{R}\left(\dfrac{1}{378} - \dfrac{1}{403}\right)$; $0.5465 = \dfrac{\Delta E}{8.314} \times 1.641 \times 10^{-4}$; $\Delta E = 27.7\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`For $\mathrm{F + Xe \rightarrow XeF}$, $k = C\sqrt{T/\mu}\,e^{-E_a/RT}$. Data: 50 °C, $2.88 \times 10^{-10}$; 110 °C, $5.31 \times 10^{-8}$; 170 °C, $2.41 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. Find $E_a$.`, a: 88.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Plot ln(k/√T) against 1/T, or use the two end points.', w: R`$\ln\dfrac{k_2/\sqrt{443}}{k_1/\sqrt{323}} = 9.032 - 0.158 = 8.874 = \dfrac{E_a}{R}(8.387 \times 10^{-4})$; $E_a = 88.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`What would $k$ be at 110 °C if xenon’s molar mass were 40 instead of 131.29 (F = 19.00), all else unchanged? Use $\dfrac{1}{\mu} = \dfrac{1}{M_{Xe}} + \dfrac{1}{M_F}$.`, a: 6.03e-8, u: 'dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k ∝ μ^(−1/2).', w: R`$\mu$: 16.60 (real) and 12.88 (if 40). $k = 5.31 \times 10^{-8} \times \sqrt{16.60/12.88} = 6.03 \times 10^{-8}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2020, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Calcium carbide · hydrogen as a fuel · sun cream molecules · silicates · colourful conjugated molecules · anammox bacteria', skipped: 'The long syntheses in questions 3 and 6 are drawn as structures, so those questions keep only their other parts.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk20-1', n: 1, t: 'Calcium carbide', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 14, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Calcium carbide, $\mathrm{CaC_2}$, is made by heating calcium oxide with carbon; carbon monoxide is the by-product. With water it releases ethyne, once burned in miners’ lamps.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the equation for making $\mathrm{CaC_2}$, and describe the bonding in the carbide ion, $\mathrm{C_2^{2-}}$.`, h: 'Three carbons per CaO.', w: R`$\mathrm{CaO + 3C \rightarrow CaC_2 + CO}$. $\mathrm{C_2^{2-}}$: a C≡C triple bond with one lone pair on each carbon (isoelectronic with $\mathrm{N_2}$).` },
        { q: 'Write the equation for calcium carbide reacting with water.', h: 'Calcium hydroxide forms too.', w: R`$\mathrm{CaC_2 + 2H_2O \rightarrow Ca(OH)_2 + C_2H_2}$` },
        { q: R`$0.845\ \mathrm{g}$ of impure $\mathrm{CaC_2}$ reacts fully with $100.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of water. $25.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of the mixture needs $30.85\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ HCl. The impurities do not react. Calculate the percentage purity.`, a: 93.6, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'HCl : Ca(OH)₂ = 2 : 1, and only a quarter of the mixture was titrated.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{HCl}) = 6.17 \times 10^{-3}$; $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2} = 3.085 \times 10^{-3}$ in 25 cm³, so $0.01234\ \mathrm{mol}$ in total $= 0.791\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{CaC_2}$: 93.6%` },
        { q: 'In the tetragonal unit cell, Ca²⁺ ions sit at the corners and the centre, and each carries one C₂²⁻ unit. How many formula units of CaC₂ are in one cell?', a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: '8 corners × 1/8, plus the centre.', w: '1 + 1 = 2 Ca, so 2 CaC₂ (4 carbon atoms).' },
        { q: R`The density is $2.22\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$ and the cell sides x and y are both $3.89\ \text{Å}$. Find the third side, z, in Å.`, a: 6.34, u: 'Å', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mass of 2 CaC₂ ÷ density = cell volume.', w: R`$m = 2 \times 64.10 \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 2.129 \times 10^{-22}\ \mathrm{g}$; $V = 95.9\ \text{Å}^3$; $z = 95.9 \div 3.89^2 = 6.34\ \text{Å}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk20-2', n: 2, t: 'Hydrogen as a fuel', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-24-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`Hydrogen can be made by reforming methane, $\mathrm{CH_4(g) + 2H_2O(l) \rightarrow CO_2(g) + 4H_2(g)}$, or by electrolysing water. $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): $\mathrm{CH_4}$ $-74.4$; $\mathrm{CO_2}$ $-393.5$; $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ $-285.8$. The standard entropy of formation of $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ is $-163.3\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Calculate ΔH for the reforming reaction.', a: 252.5, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants; H₂ is zero.', w: R`$-393.5 - (-74.4 + 2(-285.8)) = +252.5\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`In electrolysis, which half-reaction happens at the cathode: $\mathrm{2H_2O \rightarrow O_2 + 4H^+ + 4e^-}$ or $\mathrm{2H^+ + 2e^- \rightarrow H_2}$?`, h: 'Reduction happens at the cathode.', w: R`$\mathrm{2H^+ + 2e^- \rightarrow H_2}$ (reduction).` },
        { q: R`Calculate the cell potential for $\mathrm{2H_2O(l) \rightarrow 2H_2(g) + O_2(g)}$ at 298 K.`, a: -1.229, u: 'V', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔH = +571.6; ΔS = +326.6 J K⁻¹; n = 4.', w: R`$\Delta G = 571.6 - 298 \times 0.3266 = 474.3\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $E = -474\,300 \div (4 \times 96\,485) = -1.229\ \mathrm{V}$` },
        { q: 'Assuming ΔH and ΔS do not change, what is the cell potential at 350 K?', a: -1.185, u: 'V', tol: 0.005, h: 'Same method, new T.', w: R`$\Delta G = 571.6 - 350 \times 0.3266 = 457.3\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $E = -1.185\ \mathrm{V}$: electrolysis gets easier when hot.` },
        { q: R`The heat a burner gives per second is proportional to $\Delta_c H / \sqrt{M_r}$ at the same gas pressure. Find the ratio for hydrogen compared with methane.`, a: 0.905, u: '', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔcH(CH₄) = −890.7; ΔcH(H₂) = −285.8.', w: R`$\dfrac{285.8/\sqrt{2.016}}{890.7/\sqrt{16.04}} = \dfrac{201.3}{222.4} = 0.905$: a gas grid switched to hydrogen would deliver a little less heat.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk20-3', n: 3, t: 'Sun cream molecules', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-29-4', 'ca-13-4', 'ca-17-1'],
      s: 'Two UV filters in sun creams worry marine scientists: oxybenzone (2-hydroxy-4-methoxybenzophenone) and octinoxate (2-ethylhexyl (E)-3-(4-methoxyphenyl)prop-2-enoate).',
      p: [
        { q: 'Which functional groups does oxybenzone contain?', h: 'Hydroxy on the ring, methoxy on the ring, C=O between two rings.', w: 'Ketone, ether and phenol.' },
        { q: 'Oxybenzone has an intramolecular hydrogen bond. Between which atoms?', h: 'The OH is next to the C=O.', w: 'The H of the 2-hydroxy group and the oxygen of the ketone C=O (a six-membered ring).' },
        { q: 'Octinoxate has a C=C and a stereocentre in its 2-ethylhexyl group. How many stereoisomers are possible?', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'E/Z × R/S.', w: 'E or Z, each R or S: 4.' },
        { q: 'Under UV light octinoxate forms cyclobutane dimers. Which of these do all the dimers share: the same molecular formula, the same melting point, the same effect on plane-polarised light?', h: 'Isomers differ in physical properties.', w: 'Only the same molecular formula.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk20-4', n: 4, t: 'Silicates', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-9-2', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Silicates are built from $\mathrm{SiO_4}$ tetrahedra joined at their corners. Heating $\mathrm{SiO_2}$ with sodium carbonate in a 1 : 2 ratio at 1500 °C gives a salt X with a tetrahedral anion containing only Si and O, and a colourless gas Y.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Identify X and Y and write the equation.', h: 'Two carbonates give two CO₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{SiO_2 + 2Na_2CO_3 \rightarrow Na_4SiO_4 + 2CO_2}$. X is $\mathrm{Na_4SiO_4}$ (anion $\mathrm{SiO_4^{4-}}$); Y is $\mathrm{CO_2}$.` },
        { q: R`Write the equation for $\mathrm{CaSiO_3}$ with dilute HCl giving $\mathrm{SiO_2 \cdot 11H_2O}$.`, h: 'Balance H and O with water.', w: R`$\mathrm{CaSiO_3 + 2HCl + 10H_2O \rightarrow SiO_2 \cdot 11H_2O + CaCl_2}$` },
        { q: 'In quartz, how many other tetrahedra is each SiO₄ tetrahedron joined to?', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Every oxygen is shared.', w: 'Four: every corner oxygen is shared, giving SiO₂.' },
        { q: R`Give the charges of the ring anions $\mathrm{[Si_3O_9]}$ and $\mathrm{[Si_6O_{18}]}$, and the formula of the sheet anion in talc, $\mathrm{Mg_3Si_4O_{10}(OH)_2}$.`, h: 'Each Si is +4, each O −2.', w: R`$\mathrm{[Si_3O_9]^{6-}}$, $\mathrm{[Si_6O_{18}]^{12-}}$; talc contains $\mathrm{[Si_4O_{10}]^{4-}}$.` },
        { q: R`A rock contains only talc, $\mathrm{Mg_3Si_4O_{10}(OH)_2}$, and chrysotile, $\mathrm{Mg_3Si_2O_5(OH)_4}$. It is 23.31% Mg and 24.24% Si by mass. What percentage of the formula units are talc?`, a: 35, u: '%', abs: 0.6, h: 'Both have 3 Mg, so the Si : Mg mole ratio tells you the mixture.', w: R`$\mathrm{Si : Mg} = 0.8630 : 0.9589 = 0.900$, so 2.70 Si per 3 Mg. $4t + 2(1 - t) = 2.70$ gives $t = 0.35$: 35% talc.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk20-5', n: 5, t: 'Colourful molecules', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 3, m: 18, lec: ['ca-28-3', 'ca-8-2'],
      s: R`Coloured molecules have long chains of alternating single and double bonds. In the particle-in-a-box model, a chain of length $L$ with $n$ double bonds absorbs at $\Delta E = \dfrac{(2n+1)h^2}{8m_eL^2}$, where $\Delta E = hc/\lambda$. For porphine-type rings, $\lambda = \varepsilon d^2$, where $d$ is the distance between opposite N atoms.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Hexa-1,3,5-triene ($n = 3$) absorbs at 258 nm. Calculate $\Delta E$ in J.`, a: 7.70e-19, u: 'J', tol: 0.01, h: 'hc/λ.', w: R`$6.626 \times 10^{-34} \times 2.998 \times 10^8 \div 258 \times 10^{-9} = 7.70 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate the box length L in Å.', a: 7.40, u: 'Å', tol: 0.01, h: 'L² = 7h² ÷ (8 m_e ΔE).', w: R`$L = \sqrt{\dfrac{7 \times (6.626 \times 10^{-34})^2}{8 \times 9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 7.70 \times 10^{-19}}} = 7.40 \times 10^{-10}\ \mathrm{m}$` },
        { q: R`A magnesium porphyrin absorbs at 577.0 nm with N–Mg $= 2.060\ \text{Å}$, so $d = 2 \times$ N–Mg. Calculate $\varepsilon$ (nm Å⁻²) to 4 significant figures.`, a: 33.99, u: 'nm Å⁻²', tol: 0.002, h: 'd = 4.120 Å.', w: R`$\varepsilon = 577.0 \div 4.120^2 = 33.99$` },
        { q: 'The zinc complex absorbs at 572.0 nm. Find the N–Zn bond length in Å, to 4 significant figures.', a: 2.051, u: 'Å', tol: 0.002, h: 'd = √(λ/ε); bond = d/2.', w: R`$d = \sqrt{572.0 \div 33.99} = 4.102\ \text{Å}$; N–Zn $= 2.051\ \text{Å}$` },
        { q: 'Porphine itself has d = 4.130 Å. Predict its absorption wavelength.', a: 579.8, u: 'nm', tol: 0.002, h: 'λ = εd².', w: R`$33.99 \times 4.130^2 = 579.8\ \mathrm{nm}$` },
        { q: R`Proton transfer inside porphycene has $k = 1.55 \times 10^{12}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at 400 K and $3.60 \times 10^{12}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at 500 K. Find $E_a$.`, a: 14.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Two-point Arrhenius.', w: R`$E_a = \dfrac{8.314 \ln(3.60/1.55)}{1/400 - 1/500} = 14.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk20-6', n: 6, t: 'Anammox bacteria', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-12-1'],
      s: R`Anammox bacteria turn ammonium and nitrite into nitrogen gas, in three acidic steps: nitrite is reduced to hydroxylamine, $\mathrm{NH_2OH}$; hydroxylamine and ammonium combine to hydrazine, $\mathrm{N_2H_4}$; hydrazine is oxidised to $\mathrm{N_2}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the oxidation state of N in $\mathrm{N_2}$, $\mathrm{NH_4^+}$, $\mathrm{NO_2^-}$, $\mathrm{N_2H_4}$ and $\mathrm{NH_2OH}$.`, h: 'H is +1 and O −2 here.', w: '0, −3, +3, −2, −1.' },
        { q: 'Write the three steps as balanced equations or half-equations.', h: 'Balance with H⁺, H₂O and electrons.', w: R`$\mathrm{NO_2^- + 5H^+ + 4e^- \rightarrow NH_2OH + H_2O}$; $\mathrm{NH_4^+ + NH_2OH \rightarrow N_2H_4 + H_2O + H^+}$; $\mathrm{N_2H_4 \rightarrow N_2 + 4H^+ + 4e^-}$` },
        { q: 'Combine them into the overall equation.', h: 'The electrons and H⁺ cancel.', w: R`$\mathrm{NH_4^+ + NO_2^- \rightarrow N_2 + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`A treatment plant removes $3.60\ \mathrm{kg}$ of $\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ a day by anammox. What volume of $\mathrm{N_2}$, in $\mathrm{m^3}$, is released per day at 298 K and 101 kPa?`, a: 4.90, u: 'm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'One N₂ per NH₄⁺.', w: R`$n = 3600 \div 18.04 = 199.6\ \mathrm{mol}$; $V = 199.6 \times 8.314 \times 298 \div 101\,000 = 4.90\ \mathrm{m^3}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2019, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Carbon dioxide in fizzy drinks · refining precious metals and ¹⁹⁵Pt NMR · treating nerve-agent poisoning · a biodegradable plastic', skipped: 'Question 4 (the synthesis of the pesticide thiamethoxam) is drawn almost entirely as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk19-1', n: 1, t: 'Carbon dioxide in drinks', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 1, m: 16, lec: ['ca-7-1', 'ca-5-2', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`Fizzy drinks hold dissolved $\mathrm{CO_2}$. By Henry’s law, $[\mathrm{CO_2(aq)}] = k_H\,p$, with $k_H = 3.4 \times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}\ atm^{-1}}$ at 25 °C. Much of the $\mathrm{CO_2}$ used in food comes from making hydrogen for ammonia, via the shift reaction $\mathrm{CO + H_2O \rightleftharpoons CO_2 + H_2}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Describe the bonding in CO₂ and CO, and give the difference in the oxidation state of carbon.', h: 'CO has a triple bond.', w: 'O=C=O, two lone pairs on each O. C≡O, one lone pair on each atom. Carbon is +4 in CO₂ and +2 in CO: a difference of 2.' },
        { q: R`A $330\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ can has $\mathrm{CO_2}$ at 2.6 atm above the drink at 25 °C. What is $[\mathrm{CO_2}]$ in the drink?`, a: 0.0884, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Multiply.', w: R`$3.4 \times 10^{-2} \times 2.6 = 0.0884\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: 'What mass of CO₂ is dissolved in the 330 cm³ of drink?', a: 1.28, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles = c × V.', w: R`$0.0884 \times 0.330 = 0.02917\ \mathrm{mol} = 1.28\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`If that $\mathrm{CO_2}$ were a gas filling the $330\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ at 25 °C, what would its pressure be, in atm (1 atm = 101 325 Pa)?`, a: 2.16, u: 'atm', tol: 0.01, h: 'p = nRT/V.', w: R`$p = 0.02917 \times 8.314 \times 298 \div 3.30 \times 10^{-4} = 2.19 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{Pa} = 2.16\ \mathrm{atm}$` },
        { q: 'Under what conditions is CO₂ most soluble in water?', h: 'Henry’s law, and gases dissolve exothermically.', w: 'High pressure and low temperature.' },
        { q: R`The shift reactor is fed 50 mol CO, 30 mol steam, 10 mol $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and 10 mol $\mathrm{H_2}$; $K_p = 0.80$. How many moles of $\mathrm{H_2}$ leave at equilibrium?`, a: 22.7, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'Equal moles of gas on each side: use moles directly. Check Q first to find the direction.', w: R`$Q = 100 \div 1500 < 0.80$, so it moves right. $\dfrac{(10+x)^2}{(50-x)(30-x)} = 0.80$ gives $0.2x^2 + 84x - 1100 = 0$, $x = 12.7$: $\mathrm{H_2} = 22.7\ \mathrm{mol}$ (CO 37.3, steam 17.3, $\mathrm{CO_2}$ 22.7).` },
        { q: R`$\Delta_f H$: CO $-110.5$, $\mathrm{CO_2}$ $-393.5$, $\mathrm{H_2O(g)}$ $-241.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Calculate $\Delta H$ for the shift reaction.`, a: -41.2, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$-393.5 - (-110.5 - 241.8) = -41.2\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk19-2', n: 2, t: 'Refining precious metals', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 22, lec: ['ca-28-1', 'ca-22-2', 'ca-37-4'],
      s: R`When nickel and copper are refined by electrolysis, a sludge of precious metals collects under the anode. Aqua regia (concentrated HCl + $\mathrm{HNO_3}$) dissolves gold, palladium and platinum as chloro complexes, which are then separated.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Identify the metal: (i) [Kr]4d¹⁰; (ii) [Xe]4f¹⁴5d⁹6s¹; (iii) [Xe]4f¹⁴5d⁷6s².', h: 'Count across the d block.', w: 'Pd, Pt, Ir.' },
        { q: R`Write equations for gold giving $\mathrm{HAuCl_4}$ and platinum giving $\mathrm{H_2PtCl_6}$ in aqua regia, with $\mathrm{NO_2}$ as the other product.`, h: 'Each NO₂ takes one electron.', w: R`$\mathrm{Au + 4HCl + 3HNO_3 \rightarrow HAuCl_4 + 3NO_2 + 3H_2O}$; $\mathrm{Pt + 6HCl + 4HNO_3 \rightarrow H_2PtCl_6 + 4NO_2 + 4H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`Take chlorine as 76% $^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$ and 24% $^{37}\mathrm{Cl}$ ($^{197}\mathrm{Au}$ is 100%). What percentage of $\mathrm{AuCl_4^-}$ ions appear at $m/z$ 339?`, a: 42.1, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '339 means three ³⁵Cl and one ³⁷Cl, in any of 4 positions.', w: R`$4 \times 0.76^3 \times 0.24 = 0.421$: 42.1%, the biggest peak (337 is 33.4%).` },
        { q: R`Iron(II) chloride reduces chloroauric acid to gold. Write the equation.`, h: 'Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺, Au(III) → Au.', w: R`$\mathrm{HAuCl_4 + 3FeCl_2 \rightarrow Au + 3FeCl_3 + HCl}$` },
        { q: 'A palladium compound Y, square planar, is 50.3% Pd, 33.5% Cl, 13.3% N and 2.9% H. Identify it and describe its isomers.', h: 'Find the ratio; ammonia is a ligand.', w: R`$\mathrm{Pd(NH_3)_2Cl_2}$, which has cis and trans isomers. $\mathrm{H_2PdCl_4 + 4NH_3 \rightarrow Pd(NH_3)_2Cl_2 + 2NH_4Cl}$` },
        { q: R`In ¹⁹⁵Pt NMR, coupling to N equivalent nuclei of spin I gives $2NI + 1$ lines. How many lines for $\mathrm{[Pt(^{14}NH_3)_3Cl]^+}$ ($^{14}\mathrm{N}$: I = 1)?`, a: 7, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'N = 3, I = 1.', w: R`$2 \times 3 \times 1 + 1 = 7$ lines, with intensities 1 : 3 : 6 : 7 : 6 : 3 : 1 (the coefficients of $(x^2 + xy + y^2)^3$).` },
        { q: R`And for cis-$\mathrm{[Pt(^{15}NH_3)_2Cl_2]}$ ($^{15}\mathrm{N}$: I = ½)?`, a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'N = 2, I = ½.', w: '2 × 2 × ½ + 1 = 3 lines, 1 : 2 : 1.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk19-3', n: 3, t: 'Treating nerve-agent poisoning', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`Nerve agents block the enzyme acetylcholinesterase. Oxime drugs such as pralidoxime chloride (PAM, $\mathrm{C_7H_9ClN_2O}$) bind to the blocked enzyme and then reactivate it: $k_{obs} = \dfrac{k_2[\mathrm{PAM}]}{[\mathrm{PAM}] + 1/K_c}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Calculate the molar mass of PAM.', a: 172.6, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Add up the atoms.', w: R`$7(12.01) + 9(1.008) + 35.45 + 2(14.01) + 16.00 = 172.6$` },
        { q: 'A patient of 70.0 kg receives 0.0500 mmol of PAM per kg per hour. What mass is needed for 24 hours?', a: 14.5, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'mmol × kg × hours.', w: R`$0.0500 \times 70.0 \times 24 = 84.0\ \mathrm{mmol} = 14.5\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'What is the order in PAM at very low [PAM], and at very high [PAM]?', h: 'Compare [PAM] with 1/K_c.', w: R`Low: first order ($k_{obs} \approx k_2K_c[\mathrm{PAM}]$). High: zero order ($k_{obs} \approx k_2$, the enzyme is saturated).` },
        { q: R`$k_{obs} = 0.0714\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ at $[\mathrm{PAM}] = 1.0 \times 10^{-4}$ and $0.1667\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ at $5.0 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$. Find $k_2$.`, a: 0.25, u: 'min⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'Invert: 1/k_obs = 1/k₂ + (1/(k₂K_c))(1/[PAM]). A straight line.', w: R`Points (10 000, 14.0) and (2000, 6.0): gradient $0.00100$, intercept $4.0 = 1/k_2$, so $k_2 = 0.25\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$.` },
        { q: R`Find $K_c$.`, a: 4000, u: 'dm³ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'Gradient = 1/(k₂K_c).', w: R`$K_c = 1 \div (0.25 \times 0.00100) = 4.0 \times 10^3\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk19-5', n: 5, t: 'A biodegradable plastic', f: 'Polymers', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-35-1', 'ca-35-3', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: R`Poly(lactic acid), PLA, is a biodegradable polymer of lactic acid, $\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)COOH}$. Each chain is $\mathrm{H{-}[OCH(CH_3)CO]_n{-}OH}$, with one carboxylic acid end.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many chiral centres does lactic acid have, and what type of polymerisation makes PLA?', h: 'Water is lost.', w: 'One (C2). Condensation polymerisation.' },
        { q: R`PLA can also be made from A, $\mathrm{C_6H_8O_4}$. Identify A.`, h: 'Two lactic acids joined head-to-tail into a ring.', w: 'Lactide: the cyclic diester of two lactic acid molecules.' },
        { q: R`$0.2085\ \mathrm{g}$ of PLA dissolved in benzyl alcohol needs $4.62\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0500\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ KOH. Calculate the average molar mass of the chains.`, a: 903, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'One COOH per chain.', w: R`$n = 2.31 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $M = 0.2085 \div 2.31 \times 10^{-4} = 903\ \mathrm{g\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'What is the average number of lactic acid units per chain?', a: 12.3, u: '', abs: 0.2, h: 'Repeat unit C₃H₄O₂ = 72.06; add one H₂O for the ends.', w: R`$(903 - 18.0) \div 72.06 = 12.3$` },
        { q: 'The world makes 320 000 tonnes of PLA a year. What mass of NaOH would turn it all into sodium lactate (ignore end groups), in tonnes?', a: 1.78e5, u: 't', tol: 0.01, h: 'One NaOH per repeat unit.', w: R`$3.20 \times 10^{11} \div 72.06 = 4.44 \times 10^9\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 40.00\ \mathrm{g} = 1.78 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{t}$` },
        { q: R`An enzyme breaks PLA into lactic acid and its dimer B ($\mathrm{C_6H_{10}O_5}$, one ester and one acid group). $1.250\ \mathrm{g}$ of a degraded sample is made up to $250.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. $25.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ needs $15.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0800\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ NaOH to neutralise it. Another $25.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ is boiled with $40.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of the NaOH; the excess needs $23.75\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0800\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ HCl. Find the mass of lactic acid in the sample.`, a: 0.991, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'The first titration counts acid groups; the extra NaOH used on boiling counts ester groups (one per B).', w: R`Per $25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$: acid $= 1.20 \times 10^{-3}$; NaOH used on boiling $= 3.20 \times 10^{-3} - 1.90 \times 10^{-3} = 1.30 \times 10^{-3}$, so B $= 1.0 \times 10^{-4}$ and lactic acid $= 1.10 \times 10^{-3}$. In the sample: lactic acid $0.0110\ \mathrm{mol} = 0.991\ \mathrm{g}$.` },
        { q: 'And the mass of the dimer B?', a: 0.162, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'M(B) = 162.1.', w: R`$1.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol} \times 162.1 = 0.162\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
  ] },
  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2018, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Lithium batteries and oxygen candles · ammonia from lithium · enriched uranium · a cough medicine · a helium compound', skipped: 'Question 4’s long synthesis is drawn as structures, so that question keeps only its calculation.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk18-1', n: 1, t: 'Lithium and oxygen candles', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 14, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-24-2', 'ca-2-3'],
      s: R`In a lithium-ion cell the positive electrode is $\mathrm{CoO_2}$, which takes in lithium ions on discharge: $\mathrm{CoO_2 + Li^+ + e^- \rightarrow LiCoO_2}$. The other electrode is graphite holding lithium atoms.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the oxidation state of Co in $\mathrm{CoO_2}$ and in $\mathrm{LiCoO_2}$.`, h: 'O is −2, Li +1.', w: '+4 and +3.' },
        { q: 'If the cell is overcharged, lithium metal can form. At which electrode?', h: 'Charging pushes Li⁺ back where it came from, and reduces it there.', w: 'At the graphite electrode, where Li⁺ is reduced: the cathode while charging.' },
        { q: R`Lithium burns in air to give $\mathrm{Li_2O}$ and $\mathrm{Li_2O_2}$ in a 1 : 1 ratio. Write the equation, and the equation for $\mathrm{Li_2O_2}$ absorbing $\mathrm{CO_2}$.`, h: 'The peroxide releases oxygen as it absorbs CO₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{8Li + 3O_2 \rightarrow 2Li_2O + 2Li_2O_2}$; $\mathrm{2Li_2O_2 + 2CO_2 \rightarrow 2Li_2CO_3 + O_2}$` },
        { q: 'Spacecraft “oxygen candles” use compound A, which is 21.60% Na and 33.31% Cl by mass, the rest oxygen. Find its formula, and the shape of its anion.', h: 'O is 45.09%.', w: R`Na : Cl : O = 0.940 : 0.940 : 2.818 = 1 : 1 : 3: $\mathrm{NaClO_3}$. $\mathrm{ClO_3^-}$ is trigonal pyramidal (three bonds, one lone pair).` },
        { q: 'Write the equation for A decomposing to release all its oxygen as O₂.', h: 'The other product is the chloride.', w: R`$\mathrm{2NaClO_3 \rightarrow 2NaCl + 3O_2}$` },
        { q: R`A has density $2.49\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$. How many $\mathrm{cm^3}$ of $\mathrm{O_2}$ (at room temperature and pressure, $24.0\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$) does $1\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of A release?`, a: 842, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of A in 2.49 g, × 1.5 for O₂.', w: R`$2.49 \div 106.44 = 0.02339\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\mathrm{O_2} = 0.03509\ \mathrm{mol} = 842\ \mathrm{cm^3}$: over 800 times its own volume.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk18-2', n: 2, t: 'Ammonia from lithium', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 2, m: 18, lec: ['ca-24-1', 'ca-24-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`A proposed alternative to the Haber process cycles lithium. Step 1: electrolysis of molten LiOH, $\mathrm{4LiOH \rightarrow 4Li + 2H_2O + O_2}$. Step 2: lithium reacts with nitrogen. Step 3: the product reacts with water to give LiOH and $\mathrm{NH_3}$. Data at 800 K: $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): LiOH $-445$; Li $+16.2$; $\mathrm{H_2O}$ $-266$; $\mathrm{O_2}$ $+16.9$. $S$ ($\mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$): LiOH 131; Li 65.0; $\mathrm{H_2O}$ 226; $\mathrm{O_2}$ 238. $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the two half-equations for step 1.', h: 'Li⁺ is reduced; OH⁻ is oxidised.', w: R`$\mathrm{Li^+ + e^- \rightarrow Li}$ (×4); $\mathrm{4OH^- \rightarrow 2H_2O + O_2 + 4e^-}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate ΔH for step 1 at 800 K.', a: 1329.7, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Products minus reactants, with the coefficients.', w: R`$4(16.2) + 2(-266) + 16.9 - 4(-445) = +1329.7\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate ΔS for step 1.', a: 426, u: 'J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Same pattern.', w: R`$4(65.0) + 2(226) + 238 - 4(131) = +426\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate ΔG for step 1 at 800 K.', a: 988.9, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔG = ΔH − TΔS (ΔS in kJ).', w: R`$1329.7 - 800 \times 0.426 = 988.9\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Electrolysis needs 0.60 V more than the cell potential. What is the minimum voltage to apply?', a: 3.16, u: 'V', tol: 0.01, h: 'E = −ΔG/(nF) with n = 4.', w: R`$E = -988\,900 \div (4 \times 96\,500) = -2.56\ \mathrm{V}$; apply at least $2.56 + 0.60 = 3.16\ \mathrm{V}$` },
        { q: 'Write steps 2 and 3. How many Li are needed per NH₃?', a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Lithium nitride is Li₃N.', w: R`$\mathrm{6Li + N_2 \rightarrow 2Li_3N}$; $\mathrm{Li_3N + 3H_2O \rightarrow 3LiOH + NH_3}$: 3 Li per $\mathrm{NH_3}$.` },
        { q: R`$0.250\ \mathrm{A}$ flows for 1200 s and the lithium yield is 91.0% (steps 2 and 3 go fully). What volume of $\mathrm{NH_3}$ forms, in $\mathrm{cm^3}$ at RTP ($24.0\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$)?`, a: 22.6, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Q = It; one electron per Li; three Li per NH₃.', w: R`$Q = 300\ \mathrm{C}$; Li $= 300 \div 96\,500 \times 0.910 = 2.83 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\mathrm{NH_3} = 9.43 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} = 22.6\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: 'A farm of 150 acres needs 0.0650 tonnes of NH₃ per acre each year. If the LiOH were never recycled, how many tonnes of lithium would a year need?', a: 11.9, u: 't', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of NH₃ × 3 × 6.94 g.', w: R`$\mathrm{NH_3} = 9.75 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{g} \div 17.03 = 5.73 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{mol}$; Li $= 1.72 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{mol} = 11.9\ \mathrm{t}$. Recycling is essential.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk18-3', n: 3, t: 'Enriched uranium', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-1-2', 'ca-3-5'],
      s: R`Uranium-235 undergoes fission; natural uranium is mostly uranium-238. Uranium is enriched as gaseous $\mathrm{UF_6}$ in centrifuges. Accurate masses: $^{235}\mathrm{U}$ 235.0439, $^{238}\mathrm{U}$ 238.0507. 1 kiloton $= 4.184 \times 10^{12}\ \mathrm{J}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Complete fission of $1.00\ \mathrm{kg}$ of $^{235}\mathrm{U}$ releases 17.6 kilotons. Calculate the energy released in $\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$.`, a: 1.73e10, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles in 1000 g.', w: R`$17.6 \times 4.184 \times 10^{12} = 7.364 \times 10^{13}\ \mathrm{J}$ for $4.255\ \mathrm{mol}$: $1.73 \times 10^{13}\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}} = 1.73 \times 10^{10}\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`A sample of depleted uranium has $A_r = 238.0412$. Assuming it contains only the two isotopes, what percentage is $^{235}\mathrm{U}$?`, a: 0.316, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '235.0439x + 238.0507(1 − x) = 238.0412.', w: R`$x = \dfrac{238.0507 - 238.0412}{238.0507 - 235.0439} = 0.00316$: 0.316%` },
        { q: R`Why is fluorine so useful for separating $^{235}\mathrm{UF_6}$ from $^{238}\mathrm{UF_6}$? Is $\mathrm{UF_6}$ polar?`, h: 'What if the halogen had isotopes of its own?', w: 'Fluorine has only one natural isotope, so any mass difference comes from uranium alone. Octahedral UF₆ is non-polar.' },
        { q: R`By Graham’s law, lighter molecules effuse faster: rate ∝ $1/\sqrt{M}$. Using integer masses, by what percentage does $^{235}\mathrm{UF_6}$ effuse faster than $^{238}\mathrm{UF_6}$?`, a: 0.429, u: '%', tol: 0.02, h: 'M = 349 and 352; the rate ratio is √(352/349).', w: R`$\sqrt{352/349} = 1.00429$: just 0.429% faster, which is why thousands of centrifuge stages are needed.` },
        { q: R`Write equations for $\mathrm{UF_6}$ with hydrogen giving $\mathrm{UF_4}$, and $\mathrm{UF_4}$ with magnesium giving uranium metal.`, h: 'HF and MgF₂ form.', w: R`$\mathrm{UF_6 + H_2 \rightarrow UF_4 + 2HF}$; $\mathrm{UF_4 + 2Mg \rightarrow U + 2MgF_2}$` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{U_3O_8}$ contains uranium in two oxidation states. What are they if they differ by one, and if they differ by two?`, h: 'The three U add up to +16.', w: 'Differ by one: +5, +5, +6. Differ by two: +4, +6, +6.' },
        { q: R`Assuming the Earth formed with equal amounts of both isotopes, estimate its age. Today $^{235}\mathrm{U}$ is 0.720% of natural uranium; half-lives: $^{235}\mathrm{U}$ $7.04 \times 10^8$ years, $^{238}\mathrm{U}$ $4.47 \times 10^9$ years.`, a: 5.94e9, u: 'years', tol: 0.01, h: 'N₂₃₈/N₂₃₅ = e^((λ₂₃₅ − λ₂₃₈)t).', w: R`$\lambda_{235} = 9.85 \times 10^{-10}$, $\lambda_{238} = 1.55 \times 10^{-10}\ \mathrm{yr^{-1}}$; $t = \ln(99.28/0.720) \div 8.30 \times 10^{-10} = 5.9 \times 10^9$ years (an overestimate: the isotopes did not start equal).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk18-4', n: 4, t: 'A cough medicine', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 6, lec: ['ca-34-1', 'ca-2-1'],
      s: R`Dextromethorphan, $\mathrm{C_{18}H_{25}NO}$, is a cough suppressant. It contains a tertiary amine and a methoxy group, and is sold as its hydrobromide monohydrate.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Which atom is protonated in the hydrobromide salt?', h: 'The basic atom.', w: 'The nitrogen of the tertiary amine.' },
        { q: R`Calculate the molar mass of the salt, $\mathrm{C_{18}H_{25}NO \cdot HBr \cdot H_2O}$.`, a: 370.3, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Add HBr and H₂O to 271.4.', w: R`$271.4 + 80.9 + 18.0 = 370.3$` },
        { q: 'A dose is 15.0 mg of the salt. How many mg of dextromethorphan itself is that?', a: 11.0, u: 'mg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Scale by 271.4 / 370.3.', w: R`$15.0 \times 271.4 \div 370.3 = 11.0\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk18-5', n: 5, t: 'A compound of helium', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Helium is needed for superconducting magnets, so its use in party balloons has been questioned. Under 300 GPa, helium and sodium form a compound X. Take the molar volume of a gas at RTP as $24.0\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Air contains $0.905\ \mathrm{mg\ m^{-3}}$ of helium, and the atmosphere has a volume of $4.0 \times 10^9\ \mathrm{km^3}$. How many moles of helium is that?`, a: 9.04e14, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: '1 km³ = 10⁹ m³.', w: R`$0.905 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{g\ m^{-3}} \times 4.0 \times 10^{18}\ \mathrm{m^3} \div 4.003 = 9.04 \times 10^{14}\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'How many balloons of radius 15 cm could all that helium fill at RTP?', a: 1.54e15, u: 'balloons', tol: 0.01, h: 'V = 4/3 πr³ in dm³.', w: R`$V = 14.14\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ = 0.589 mol each; $9.04 \times 10^{14} \div 0.589 = 1.54 \times 10^{15}$` },
        { q: 'In the cubic unit cell of X, He atoms sit at the corners and face centres, and 8 Na atoms sit inside. Give the formula of X.', h: 'Corners 8 × 1/8, faces 6 × 1/2.', w: R`4 He and 8 Na: $\mathrm{Na_2He}$.` },
        { q: 'The cell edge is 3.95 Å. Calculate the density of X.', a: 5.39, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mass of 8 Na + 4 He ÷ a³.', w: R`$m = (8 \times 22.99 + 4 \times 4.003) \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 3.32 \times 10^{-22}\ \mathrm{g}$; $V = 6.16 \times 10^{-23}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$; $\rho = 5.39\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: 'In X the sodium is present as Na⁺ and the electrons are paired and trapped in gaps in the lattice. Is X metallic, ionic or covalent, and does it conduct? Write its decomposition equation.', h: 'Electrons that are not free cannot carry a current.', w: R`An ionic solid (an electride) and an insulator. $\mathrm{Na_2He \rightarrow 2Na + He}$` },
      ] },
  ] },


  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2017, mins: 120,
    topics: 'A green swimming pool · hydrogen sulfide in the air · titanium–gold alloys · adamantane and twistane · superbases', skipped: 'The twistane synthesis in question 4 is drawn as structures, so that question keeps only its other parts.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk17-1', n: 1, t: 'The green pool', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-11-4', 'ca-6-1', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: 'A diving pool at a sporting event turned green. Pools are chlorinated with sodium hypochlorite, NaClO; suggested culprits included algae, copper(II) salts, and hydrogen peroxide added by mistake, which destroys the hypochlorite.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the oxidation state of Cl in NaClO and the acid–base equilibrium of ClO⁻ in water.', h: 'ClO⁻ is the conjugate base of a weak acid.', w: R`+1. $\mathrm{ClO^- + H_2O \rightleftharpoons HClO + OH^-}$` },
        { q: 'Write equations for: hypochlorite with hydrochloric acid giving chlorine; hydrogen peroxide destroying hypochlorite.', h: 'H₂O₂ is oxidised to O₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{ClO^- + Cl^- + 2H^+ \rightarrow Cl_2 + H_2O}$; $\mathrm{H_2O_2 + ClO^- \rightarrow Cl^- + H_2O + O_2}$` },
        { q: 'Write equations for ammonia with hypochlorite giving NCl₃, and giving hydrazine. Give the shape and approximate bond angle of NCl₃.', h: 'NCl₃ needs three ClO⁻; hydrazine needs two NH₃ per ClO⁻.', w: R`$\mathrm{NH_3 + 3ClO^- \rightarrow NCl_3 + 3OH^-}$; $\mathrm{2NH_3 + ClO^- \rightarrow N_2H_4 + Cl^- + H_2O}$. NCl₃ is trigonal pyramidal, about 107°.` },
        { q: R`HClO has $\mathrm{p}K_a = 7.53$. What percentage of the free chlorine is present as HClO (the better disinfectant) at pH 7.20?`, a: 68.1, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '[ClO⁻]/[HClO] = 10^(pH − pKa).', w: R`Ratio $= 10^{-0.33} = 0.468$; HClO $= 1 \div 1.468 = 68.1\%$` },
        { q: R`The pool holds $2.50 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ and should contain $3.0 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ of hypochlorite. What mass of NaClO is needed, in kg?`, a: 5.58, u: 'kg', tol: 0.01, h: 'M(NaClO) = 74.44.', w: R`$3.0 \times 10^{-5} \times 2.50 \times 10^6 = 75.0\ \mathrm{mol} = 5.58\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
        { q: 'A swimmer’s hair turned green after shampoo with a high pH. Suggest the formula of the blue precipitate.', h: 'Copper(II) ions at high pH.', w: R`$\mathrm{Cu(OH)_2}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk17-2', n: 2, t: 'Hydrogen sulfide in the air', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-12-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{H_2S}$ is removed from the air mainly by the hydroxyl radical: $\mathrm{H_2S + {\cdot}OH \rightarrow {\cdot}SH + H_2O}$, rate $= k[\mathrm{H_2S}][\mathrm{OH}]$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the lab preparation of H₂S from iron(II) sulfide, and the equations for H₂S burning to SO₂ and for H₂S reacting with SO₂ to give sulfur.', h: 'The last is a comproportionation.', w: R`$\mathrm{FeS + 2HCl \rightarrow FeCl_2 + H_2S}$; $\mathrm{2H_2S + 3O_2 \rightarrow 2SO_2 + 2H_2O}$; $\mathrm{2H_2S + SO_2 \rightarrow 3S + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`Near a volcano $\mathrm{H_2S}$ is emitted at $6.2 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{molecules\ cm^{-3}\ s^{-1}}$; $[\mathrm{OH}] = 1.3 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{cm^{-3}}$ and $k = 4.4 \times 10^{-12}\ \mathrm{cm^3\ molecule^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. At steady state, what is $[\mathrm{H_2S}]$?`, a: 1.08e11, u: 'molecules cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Production = k[H₂S][OH].', w: R`$[\mathrm{H_2S}] = \dfrac{6.2 \times 10^5}{4.4 \times 10^{-12} \times 1.3 \times 10^6} = 1.08 \times 10^{11}\ \mathrm{cm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`Convert that to $\mu\mathrm{g\ m^{-3}}$.`, a: 6.13, u: 'μg m⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: '1 m³ = 10⁶ cm³; M = 34.08.', w: R`$1.08 \times 10^{17}\ \mathrm{m^{-3}} \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} \times 34.08 = 6.13 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{g} = 6.13\ \mu\mathrm{g\ m^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`1,1,1-Trichloroethane is removed only by OH, with $k = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}\ \mathrm{cm^3\ molecule^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$, and has an atmospheric half-life of 4.8 years. Estimate the global average $[\mathrm{OH}]$ (1 year = 365.25 days).`, a: 4.58e5, u: 'molecules cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 't½ = ln 2 ÷ (k[OH]).', w: R`$t_{1/2} = 1.515 \times 10^8\ \mathrm{s}$; $[\mathrm{OH}] = 0.693 \div (1.515 \times 10^8 \times 10^{-14}) = 4.6 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{cm^{-3}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk17-3', n: 3, t: 'Titanium–gold alloys', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 3, m: 22, lec: ['ca-3-3', 'ca-4-2', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{Ti_3Au}$ is about four times harder than titanium and safe in the body. Gold-coloured TiN films are made by $a\,\mathrm{TiCl_4} + b\,\mathrm{NH_3} \rightarrow c\,\mathrm{TiN} + d\,\mathrm{HCl} + e\,\mathrm{N_2}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the oxidation state of Ti in TiN, which elements are oxidised and reduced, and balance the equation.', h: 'Some N goes from −3 to 0.', w: R`Ti +3. N is oxidised, Ti reduced. $\mathrm{6TiCl_4 + 8NH_3 \rightarrow 6TiN + 24HCl + N_2}$` },
        { q: R`What mass of titanium is needed to make $25.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ of $\mathrm{Ti_3Au}$?`, a: 10.54, u: 'kg', tol: 0.005, h: 'Mass fraction of Ti in Ti₃Au.', w: R`$M = 340.58$; Ti fraction $= 143.61 \div 340.58 = 0.4217$; $10.54\ \mathrm{kg}$ Ti and $14.46\ \mathrm{kg}$ Au.` },
        { q: R`In α-$\mathrm{Ti_3Au}$, Au is at the cube corners and Ti at the face centres, cube edge $4.10\ \text{Å}$. How many Ti surround each Au, and what is the Ti–Au distance?`, a: 2.90, u: 'Å', tol: 0.005, h: 'A corner and a face centre are half a face diagonal apart.', w: R`12 Ti around each Au (each Ti has 4 Au and 8 Ti neighbours). Distance $= a/\sqrt2 = 2.90\ \text{Å}$, the same as the shortest Ti–Ti distance.` },
        { q: R`In β-$\mathrm{Ti_3Au}$, Au is at the corners and body centre (edge $5.12\ \text{Å}$), and Ti atoms sit in pairs on each face, half an edge apart. Ti is at positions like $(\tfrac14, 0, \tfrac12)$. Calculate the Ti–Au distance.`, a: 2.86, u: 'Å', tol: 0.005, h: 'From (¼, 0, ½) to the body centre (½, ½, ½).', w: R`$\Delta = (\tfrac14, \tfrac12, 0)a$, so $d = a\sqrt5/4 = 2.86\ \text{Å}$` },
        { q: 'And the distance from a Ti to its next-nearest Ti neighbours, such as (0, ½, ¼)?', a: 3.14, u: 'Å', tol: 0.005, h: 'Δ = (¼, ½, ¼)a.', w: R`$a\sqrt6/4 = 3.14\ \text{Å}$ (the nearest Ti, its pair partner, is $a/2 = 2.56\ \text{Å}$ away).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk17-4', n: 4, t: 'Adamantane and twistane', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-13-3', 'ca-37-3', 'ca-13-4'],
      s: 'Cyclohexane prefers the chair shape, with near-tetrahedral angles; the twist-boat is higher in energy. Adamantane has all its rings locked as chairs; its isomer twistane has all its rings locked as twist-boats.',
      p: [
        { q: 'By how many degrees does the ideal C–C–C angle in the chair differ from the angle in a flat regular hexagon?', a: 10.5, u: '°', abs: 0.2, h: '109.5° against 120°.', w: '120 − 109.5 = 10.5°' },
        { q: 'How many ¹³C NMR signals does adamantane give?', a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Four CH at the bridgeheads, six CH₂ between them.', w: 'Two: the 4 CH are equivalent, and so are the 6 CH₂.' },
        { q: 'Give the molecular formula shared by adamantane and twistane.', h: 'Three rings, ten carbons.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_{10}H_{16}}$ (DBE 3: three rings).` },
        { q: 'Twistane has no plane of symmetry but three C₂ axes. Is it superimposable on its mirror image?', h: 'No mirror plane and no centre of symmetry.', w: 'No: it is chiral, and exists as two enantiomers.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk17-5', n: 5, t: 'Superbases', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-1-4', 'ca-37-3'],
      s: R`The proton affinity, PA($\mathrm{X^-}$), is the enthalpy change for $\mathrm{HX \rightarrow H^+ + X^-}$ in the gas phase: the bigger it is, the stronger the base. Ionising a hydrogen atom takes $2.18 \times 10^{-18}\ \mathrm{J}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Using the N–H bond enthalpy in ammonia (450 kJ mol⁻¹) and the electron affinity of $\mathrm{NH_2}$ (−74.4 kJ mol⁻¹ for $\mathrm{NH_2 + e^- \rightarrow NH_2^-}$), calculate PA($\mathrm{NH_2^-}$).`, a: 1688, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Three steps: break N–H, ionise H, add the electron to NH₂.', w: R`$450 + 2.18 \times 10^{-18} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} \div 1000 - 74.4 = 450 + 1312.8 - 74.4 = 1688\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`The lithium monoxide anion, $\mathrm{LiO^-}$, has PA = 1778 kJ mol⁻¹. It is made from $\mathrm{LiC_2O_4^-}$ by losing neutral molecules of mass 44, then 28. Identify them.`, h: 'Oxalate breaks up into two carbon oxides.', w: R`$\mathrm{CO_2}$ (44) then CO (28): $\mathrm{LiC_2O_4^- \rightarrow LiO^- + CO_2 + CO}$. It is a stronger base than $\mathrm{NH_2^-}$.` },
        { q: R`Compound A, $\mathrm{C_{12}H_6O_4}$, is a benzene with two identical groups R, and fizzes with sodium hydrogencarbonate. Identify R.`, h: 'C₆H₄R₂: R = C₃HO₂, an acid with a triple bond.', w: R`R = $\mathrm{{-}C{\equiv}C{-}COOH}$.` },
        { q: 'How many ¹³C NMR signals would the para isomer of A give?', a: 5, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Ring carbons by symmetry, plus the three chain carbons.', w: 'Para: 2 ring + 3 chain = 5. Meta gives 7 and ortho 6; A shows 6, so it is the ortho isomer.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2016, mins: 120,
    topics: 'The sulfur–iodine cycle · tungsten and molybdenum · double bond equivalents · dating King Richard III', skipped: 'Question 4 (the synthesis of flibanserin) is drawn almost entirely as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk16-1', n: 1, t: 'The sulfur–iodine cycle', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-2'],
      s: R`A cycle of three gas-phase reactions can store energy as hydrogen: (1) $\mathrm{I_2 + SO_2 + 2H_2O \rightleftharpoons H_2SO_4 + 2HI}$; (2) $\mathrm{2H_2SO_4 \rightleftharpoons 2SO_2 + 2H_2O + O_2}$; (3) $\mathrm{2HI \rightleftharpoons H_2 + I_2}$. Data at 298 K: $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): HI 26.0; $\mathrm{I_2(g)}$ 62.0; $\mathrm{H_2O(g)}$ $-241.8$. $S$ ($\mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$): HI 206.6; $\mathrm{H_2}$ 130.7; $\mathrm{I_2(g)}$ 260.7.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'For reaction (3), calculate ΔH°.', a: 10.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'H₂ is zero.', w: R`$62.0 - 2(26.0) = +10.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Calculate ΔS° for reaction (3).', a: -21.8, u: 'J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$130.7 + 260.7 - 2(206.6) = -21.8$` },
        { q: 'Calculate K for reaction (3) at 298 K.', a: 1.28e-3, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, then K = e^(−ΔG/RT).', w: R`$\Delta G = 10.0 + 298 \times 0.0218 = 16.50\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = e^{-6.658} = 1.28 \times 10^{-3}$` },
        { q: 'And K at 723 K, assuming ΔH and ΔS stay the same?', a: 0.0138, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'Same method.', w: R`$\Delta G = 10.0 + 723 \times 0.0218 = 25.76\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = e^{-4.286} = 0.0138$` },
        { q: 'Write the overall equation for one turn of the cycle.', h: '2 × (1) + (2) + 2 × (3).', w: R`$\mathrm{2H_2O \rightarrow 2H_2 + O_2}$` },
        { q: 'ΔH° for reaction (2) is +442 kJ mol⁻¹. Calculate ΔH° for reaction (1).', a: 10.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'The overall reaction has ΔH = +483.6.', w: R`$483.6 = 2\Delta H_1 + 442 + 2(10.0)$, so $\Delta H_1 = +10.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'How much energy does one turn store per mole of sulfur atoms?', a: 241.8, u: 'kJ', tol: 0.005, h: 'Two S per turn.', w: R`$483.6 \div 2 = 241.8\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk16-2', n: 2, t: 'Tungsten and molybdenum', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-4-1', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Tungsten, like sulfur, reaches +6 with oxygen and fluorine. Its ore scheelite, $\mathrm{CaWO_4}$, contains the tetrahedral tungstate ion $\mathrm{WO_4^{2-}}$; powellite, $\mathrm{CaMoO_4}$, has the same structure. Lithium can be pushed into $\mathrm{WO_3}$: $\mathrm{WO_3 + xLi^+ + xe^- \rightarrow Li_xWO_3}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the O–W–O angle in $\mathrm{WO_4^{2-}}$, and equations for $\mathrm{WO_3}$ reduced by hydrogen and for $\mathrm{WF_6}$ hydrolysed by water.`, h: 'Tetrahedral. HF is a product of hydrolysis.', w: R`109.5°. $\mathrm{WO_3 + 3H_2 \rightarrow W + 3H_2O}$; $\mathrm{WF_6 + 3H_2O \rightarrow WO_3 + 6HF}$` },
        { q: R`What is the average oxidation state of W in $\mathrm{Li_{0.45}WO_3}$?`, a: 5.55, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Li is +1, O −2.', w: R`$6 - 0.45 = +5.55$` },
        { q: R`Taking air as 80% $\mathrm{N_2}$ and 20% $\mathrm{O_2}$, how many times denser than air is $\mathrm{WF_6}$ gas?`, a: 10.3, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Compare molar masses.', w: R`$M(\text{air}) = 28.82$; $297.8 \div 28.82 = 10.3$` },
        { q: R`Calculate the density of $\mathrm{WF_6(g)}$ at 310 K and $1.0 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{Pa}$, in $\mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$.`, a: 0.01156, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'ρ = pM/RT, then g m⁻³ to g cm⁻³.', w: R`$\rho = 10^5 \times 297.8 \div (8.314 \times 310) = 1.156 \times 10^4\ \mathrm{g\ m^{-3}} = 0.0116\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`$\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): $\mathrm{SF_6}$ $-1220.5$; $\mathrm{H_2O(g)}$ $-241.8$; $\mathrm{H_2SO_4(g)}$ $-740.6$; HF $-273.3$. Calculate $\Delta H$ for $\mathrm{SF_6 + 4H_2O \rightarrow H_2SO_4 + 6HF}$. Why does $\mathrm{SF_6}$ survive in water?`, a: -192.7, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$-740.6 + 6(-273.3) - (-1220.5 + 4(-241.8)) = -192.7\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Thermodynamically unstable but kinetically stable: crowded S cannot be attacked.` },
        { q: R`Powellite’s tetragonal cell is $0.522 \times 0.522 \times 1.142\ \mathrm{nm}$ and holds 4 $\mathrm{CaMoO_4}$. Calculate its density.`, a: 4.27, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: '1 nm³ = 10⁻²¹ cm³.', w: R`$V = 0.3112\ \mathrm{nm^3}$; mass $= 4 \times 200.0 \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 1.329 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{g}$; $\rho = 4.27\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk16-3', n: 3, t: 'Double bond equivalents', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-13-1', 'ca-37-3'],
      s: R`The double bond equivalent (DBE) of a hydrocarbon $\mathrm{C_nH_x}$ counts rings plus π bonds: $\mathrm{DBE} = \tfrac12(2n + 2 - x)$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give general formulae for an alkyne, a cycloalkene and a dialkyne.', h: 'Each DBE removes two H from CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.', w: R`Alkyne $\mathrm{C_nH_{2n-2}}$; cycloalkene $\mathrm{C_nH_{2n-2}}$; dialkyne $\mathrm{C_nH_{2n-6}}$` },
        { q: R`DBE of $\mathrm{C_7H_6}$?`, a: 5, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: '½(16 − 6).', w: '5' },
        { q: R`DBE of $\mathrm{C_{20}H_{24}}$?`, a: 9, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: '½(42 − 24).', w: '9' },
        { q: R`DBE of the fullerene $\mathrm{C_{70}}$?`, a: 71, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'x = 0.', w: R`$\tfrac12(142) = 71$` },
        { q: R`Three isomers of $\mathrm{C_8H_8}$ (DBE 5) give these ¹³C spectra: A, one signal at 132 (CH); B, one signal at 47 (CH); E, signals at 147 (C), 138, 131, 127 (CH, 2C each) and 112 (CH₂). Identify A, B and E.`, h: '132 is alkene, 47 is sp³. E has a CH₂= group and a benzene ring.', w: 'A: cyclooctatetraene (four C=C, one ring). B: cubane (six rings, no double bonds; eight equivalent CH). E: styrene (phenylethene).' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk16-5', n: 5, t: 'Dating King Richard III', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-1-2', 'ca-26-1'],
      s: R`Bones found under a car park were radiocarbon dated. Bone is about 62% hydroxyapatite, $\mathrm{Ca_5(PO_4)_3OH}$, by mass; the rest is mostly collagen, treated here as polyglycine. Natural carbon is 98.93% $^{12}\mathrm{C}$ and $1.215 \times 10^{-10}\%$ $^{14}\mathrm{C}$; take the half-life of $^{14}\mathrm{C}$ as 5730 years.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the repeat unit of polyglycine and the equations for burning it and for reducing CO₂ to graphite with hydrogen.', h: 'Glycine loses water when it polymerises.', w: R`$\mathrm{{-}NH{-}CH_2{-}CO{-}}$. $\mathrm{(C_2H_3NO)_n + \tfrac94nO_2 \rightarrow 2nCO_2 + \tfrac32nH_2O + \tfrac12nN_2}$; $\mathrm{CO_2 + 2H_2 \rightarrow C + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'What mass of graphite comes from a 1.5 g bone sample?', a: 0.240, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Collagen = 38% of 1.5 g; carbon is 24.02/57.05 of the repeat unit.', w: R`$1.5 \times 0.38 = 0.570\ \mathrm{g}$; $\times 24.02 \div 57.05 = 0.240\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`For a 3.0 mg carbon sample at the time of death, how many $^{14}\mathrm{C}$ atoms decay per day?`, a: 60.5, u: 'per day', tol: 0.02, h: 'Number of ¹⁴C atoms × ln 2 ÷ t½ (in days).', w: R`C atoms $= 3.0 \times 10^{-3} \div 12.011 \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 1.504 \times 10^{20}$; $^{14}\mathrm{C} = 1.83 \times 10^8$; $\lambda = 3.31 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{day^{-1}}$: about 60 per day.` },
        { q: R`The bone’s measured $^{14}\mathrm{C} : {}^{12}\mathrm{C}$ ratio is $1.150 \times 10^{-12}$. How old is the bone, in years?`, a: 543, u: 'years', tol: 0.02, h: 'Living ratio = 1.215 × 10⁻¹² ÷ 0.9893.', w: R`Living ratio $= 1.228 \times 10^{-12}$; $t = 5730 \times \log_2(1.228/1.150) = 543$ years.` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2015, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Indium tin oxide · molecules in space · Ritalin · hangovers · “green” jet fuel', skipped: 'The Ritalin synthesis in question 3 is drawn as structures, so that question keeps only its calculation and IR parts.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk15-1', n: 1, t: 'Touchscreens', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Touchscreens are coated with indium tin oxide (ITO), a transparent conductor that here is 92% $\mathrm{In_2O_3}$ and 8% $\mathrm{SnO_2}$ by mass.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the equation for heating indium(III) hydroxide to indium(III) oxide.', h: 'Water is lost.', w: R`$\mathrm{2In(OH)_3 \rightarrow In_2O_3 + 3H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'A tablet screen carries 32 mg of ITO. What mass of indium is that, in mg?', a: 24.4, u: 'mg', tol: 0.01, h: 'In is 229.64/277.64 of In₂O₃.', w: R`$32 \times 0.92 \times 0.8271 = 24.4\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
        { q: R`The coating holds $650\ \mathrm{mg}$ of indium per $\mathrm{m^2}$, and ITO has density $7.12\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$. How thick is the coating, in nm?`, a: 120, u: 'nm', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mass of ITO per m², then volume ÷ area (1 m² = 10⁴ cm²).', w: R`ITO $= 650 \div (0.92 \times 0.8271) = 854\ \mathrm{mg\ m^{-2}}$; $0.854 \div 7.12 = 0.120\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ over $10^4\ \mathrm{cm^2}$: $1.20 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{cm} = 120\ \mathrm{nm}$` },
        { q: R`In $\mathrm{In_2O_3}$ the indium ions form a face-centred cubic arrangement. How many In ions per cell, and so how many oxide ions inside it?`, a: 6, u: 'oxide ions', abs: 0.01, h: '8 × ⅛ + 6 × ½ indium; In : O = 2 : 3.', w: '4 indium ions, so 6 oxide ions.' },
        { q: R`$3.00\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{In_2O_3}$ loses $0.346\ \mathrm{g}$ when heated to 700 °C. What is the product?`, h: '11.5% of 277.6 is lost.', w: R`$277.6 \times 0.885 = 245.7 = 2 \times 114.8 + 16.0$: $\mathrm{In_2O}$.` },
        { q: 'With ammonia at 630 °C, In₂O₃ gives water and a semiconductor. What is it?', h: 'N replaces O.', w: R`InN: $\mathrm{In_2O_3 + 2NH_3 \rightarrow 2InN + 3H_2O}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk15-2', n: 2, t: 'Molecules in space', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 18, lec: ['ca-13-4', 'ca-1-3'],
      s: R`Radio telescopes identify molecules in space from rotational transitions. Rotational energy levels are $E_J = hBJ(J+1)$, so the transition $J \rightarrow J - 1$ absorbs or emits at frequency $2BJ$. Also $B = \dfrac{h}{8\pi^2\mu r^2}$, with reduced mass $\mu = \dfrac{m_1m_2}{m_1 + m_2}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Name and give the structures of the nitriles with formula C₅H₉N.', h: 'Four: put the C≡N on each possible C₄ skeleton.', w: 'Pentanenitrile, 2-methylbutanenitrile, 3-methylbutanenitrile, 2,2-dimethylpropanenitrile.' },
        { q: R`The cyanopolyyne $\mathrm{HC_{11}N}$ emits at 13 863.10 MHz for $J = 41 \rightarrow 40$. Calculate B in MHz.`, a: 169.06, u: 'MHz', tol: 0.001, h: 'f = 2BJ with J = 41.', w: R`$B = 13\,863.10 \div 82 = 169.06\ \mathrm{MHz}$` },
        { q: R`At what frequency is $J = 40 \rightarrow 39$?`, a: 13525.0, u: 'MHz', tol: 0.0005, h: '2B × 40.', w: R`$80 \times 169.062 = 13\,525.0\ \mathrm{MHz}$` },
        { q: R`Calculate the reduced mass of $^{12}\mathrm{C}^{16}\mathrm{O}$ in kg.`, a: 1.139e-26, u: 'kg', tol: 0.003, h: 'Atom masses = 12.00 g ÷ N_A and 16.00 g ÷ N_A, in kg.', w: R`$m_C = 1.993 \times 10^{-26}$, $m_O = 2.657 \times 10^{-26}\ \mathrm{kg}$; $\mu = 1.139 \times 10^{-26}\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
        { q: R`CO emits at 230 538.000 MHz for $J = 2 \rightarrow 1$. Calculate the C–O bond length in Å.`, a: 1.131, u: 'Å', tol: 0.003, h: 'B = f ÷ 4; convert MHz to Hz.', w: R`$B = 5.763 \times 10^{10}\ \mathrm{Hz}$; $r = \sqrt{\dfrac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{8\pi^2 \times 1.139 \times 10^{-26} \times 5.763 \times 10^{10}}} = 1.131 \times 10^{-10}\ \mathrm{m}$` },
        { q: R`Another CO line is at 691 473.076 MHz. Which J does the transition start from?`, a: 6, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Divide by 2B.', w: R`$691\,473 \div 115\,269 = 6.0$: $J = 6 \rightarrow 5$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk15-3', n: 3, t: 'Ritalin', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-22-1', 'ca-34-1'],
      s: 'Ritalin (methylphenidate) is a secondary amine and an ester, given as its hydrochloride salt.',
      p: [
        { q: R`$12.00\ \mathrm{mg}$ of the hydrochloride contains $10.376\ \mathrm{mg}$ of Ritalin. Calculate the molar mass of Ritalin.`, a: 233.0, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'The extra 1.624 mg is HCl, in the same number of moles.', w: R`$n = 1.624 \div 36.46\ \mathrm{mmol}$; $M = 10.376 \times 36.46 \div 1.624 = 233.0$` },
        { q: 'Which bonds cause IR bands at 1740 cm⁻¹ (sharp), 2260 cm⁻¹, 3000 cm⁻¹ (very broad) and 3180 + 3390 cm⁻¹ (two sharp)?', h: 'Ester, nitrile, carboxylic acid, primary amide.', w: 'C=O of an ester; C≡N of a nitrile; O–H of a carboxylic acid; the two N–H of an NH₂ group in a primary amide.' },
        { q: 'Ritalin has two stereocentres. How many stereoisomers, and how many enantiomer pairs?', a: 4, u: 'stereoisomers', abs: 0.01, h: '2².', w: '4 stereoisomers: two pairs of enantiomers.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk15-4', n: 4, t: 'Hangovers', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-16-1'],
      s: R`Alcohol dehydrogenase (AD) oxidises ethanol with $\mathrm{NAD^+}$: rate $= \dfrac{k_{cat}[\mathrm{AD}][\mathrm{C_2H_5OH}]}{K_M + [\mathrm{C_2H_5OH}]}$, with $k_{cat} = 1.40\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $K_M = 1.00 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What happens to NAD⁺ in the reaction?', h: 'Ethanol is oxidised.', w: 'It is reduced (to NADH).' },
        { q: 'A drink-drive limit is 50 mg of ethanol per 100 cm³ of blood. Convert this to mol dm⁻³.', a: 0.01085, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: '0.50 g dm⁻³ ÷ 46.07.', w: R`$0.50 \div 46.07 = 1.09 \times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: 'What is the order with respect to ethanol at this level, and why?', h: 'Compare with K_M.', w: R`Zero order: $[\mathrm{C_2H_5OH}] \gg K_M$, so rate $\approx k_{cat}[\mathrm{AD}]$.` },
        { q: 'Blood alcohol falls by 18 mg per 100 cm³ each hour. Calculate the concentration of AD.', a: 7.75e-7, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Rate in mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ = k_cat[AD].', w: R`rate $= 0.18 \div 46.07 \div 3600 = 1.09 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}\ s^{-1}}$; $[\mathrm{AD}] = 7.8 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: 'During this zero-order phase, does the half-life increase, stay the same or decrease as the alcohol falls?', h: 'For zero order, t½ = [A]₀ ÷ 2k.', w: 'It decreases.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk15-5', n: 5, t: '“Green” jet fuel', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 20, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`Sunlight heats ceria, $\mathrm{CeO_2 \rightarrow CeO_{2-\delta} + \tfrac{\delta}{2}O_2}$; the reduced ceria then takes O from $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and $\mathrm{H_2O}$, making syngas (CO + $\mathrm{H_2}$), which Fischer–Tropsch turns into alkanes. Molar volume at RTP: $24.0\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the general Fischer–Tropsch equation for an alkane $\mathrm{C_nH_{2n+2}}$, and the ideal CO : $\mathrm{H_2}$ ratio for decane.`, h: 'Oxygen leaves as water.', w: R`$\mathrm{nCO + (2n+1)H_2 \rightarrow C_nH_{2n+2} + nH_2O}$. Decane: 10 : 21.` },
        { q: R`$150\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{CeO_2}$ releases $410\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $\mathrm{O_2}$ at RTP. Calculate δ.`, a: 0.0392, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'O atoms lost ÷ moles of CeO₂.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{O_2}) = 0.01708$; O atoms $= 0.03417$; $\mathrm{CeO_2} = 0.8715\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\delta = 0.0392$` },
        { q: 'What volume of syngas could step 2 make, in cm³ at RTP?', a: 820, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Each O atom put back makes one CO or one H₂.', w: R`$0.03417\ \mathrm{mol} \times 24\,000 = 820\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: R`In fact $780\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ formed, with $\mathrm{H_2 : CO} = 1.85 : 1$. How many moles of $\mathrm{H_2}$?`, a: 0.0211, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'Total moles × 1.85/2.85.', w: R`$0.0325 \times 1.85 \div 2.85 = 0.0211\ \mathrm{mol}$ (CO $0.0114\ \mathrm{mol}$)` },
        { q: 'Solar input: 24 min at 3.80 kW, then 30 min at 0.90 kW. ΔcH: CO −283, H₂ −286 kJ mol⁻¹. What percentage of the solar energy is stored in the syngas?', a: 0.131, u: '%', tol: 0.02, h: 'Energy out ÷ energy in.', w: R`In: $5.472 + 1.620 = 7.09 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{J}$. Out: $0.0211 \times 286 + 0.0114 \times 283 = 9.26\ \mathrm{kJ}$. Efficiency $0.13\%$.` },
        { q: 'ΔcH: hexane −4163, heptane −4817 kJ mol⁻¹. Estimate ΔcH for decane.', a: -6779, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Each extra CH₂ adds the same amount.', w: R`$\Delta$ per $\mathrm{CH_2} = -654$; decane $= -4817 + 3(-654) = -6779\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Use a Hess cycle to find $\Delta H$ for $\mathrm{10CO + 21H_2 \rightarrow C_{10}H_{22} + 10H_2O(l)}$.`, a: -2057, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Combustion route: reactants minus products.', w: R`$10(-283) + 21(-286) - (-6779) = -2057\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2014, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Controlling phosphate with lanthanum · a lithium lamp and ionisation energy · acne cream · bombardier beetles · methane hydrates', skipped: 'The syntheses in questions 3 and 4 are drawn as structures, so those questions keep only their calculations and deductions.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk14-1', n: 1, t: 'Controlling phosphate', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ci-12-2'],
      s: R`Too much phosphate in the blood is harmful. Lanthanum carbonate tablets react with stomach acid, and the $\mathrm{La^{3+}}$ ions then precipitate phosphate as insoluble $\mathrm{LaPO_4}$. Tablet strengths give the mass of $\mathrm{La^{3+}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write equations for lanthanum carbonate with hydrochloric acid, and for the precipitation of lanthanum phosphate.', h: 'La is +3 throughout.', w: R`$\mathrm{La_2(CO_3)_3 + 6HCl \rightarrow 2LaCl_3 + 3H_2O + 3CO_2}$; $\mathrm{La^{3+} + PO_4^{3-} \rightarrow LaPO_4}$` },
        { q: 'Write equations for making lanthanum carbonate from lanthanum nitrate and sodium carbonate, and from lanthanum chloride and ammonium hydrogencarbonate (CO₂ is given off).', h: 'Precipitation; balance the carbonate.', w: R`$\mathrm{2La(NO_3)_3 + 3Na_2CO_3 \rightarrow La_2(CO_3)_3 + 6NaNO_3}$; $\mathrm{2LaCl_3 + 6NH_4HCO_3 \rightarrow La_2(CO_3)_3 + 6NH_4Cl + 3CO_2 + 3H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`A tablet contains $750\ \mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{La^{3+}}$. What mass of $\mathrm{La_2(CO_3)_3 \cdot 2H_2O}$ is that, in mg?`, a: 1333, u: 'mg', tol: 0.005, h: 'Two La per formula unit; M = 493.9.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{La}) = 750 \div 138.91 = 5.399\ \mathrm{mmol}$; salt $= 2.700\ \mathrm{mmol} \times 493.9 = 1333\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
        { q: 'What mass of phosphate ion can one such tablet remove, in mg?', a: 513, u: 'mg', tol: 0.005, h: 'La³⁺ : PO₄³⁻ = 1 : 1.', w: R`$5.399\ \mathrm{mmol} \times 94.97 = 513\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk14-2', n: 2, t: 'A lithium lamp', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-1-3', 'ca-1-4'],
      s: R`A lithium lamp glows red from the $2p \rightarrow 2s$ transition at 670.8 nm. Lithium also emits lines from excited $nd$ levels down to $2p$: $n = 3$, 610.4 nm; $n = 4$, 460.3 nm; $n = 5$, 413.3 nm; $n = 6$, 391.5 nm. The ionisation energy of an $nd$ electron is close to $k/n^2$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the electron configuration of lithium and its valence orbital.', h: 'Three electrons.', w: '1s² 2s¹; the 2s electron.' },
        { q: 'Calculate the energy of one 670.8 nm photon, in J.', a: 2.961e-19, u: 'J', tol: 0.005, h: 'E = hc/λ.', w: R`$6.626 \times 10^{-34} \times 2.998 \times 10^8 \div 670.8 \times 10^{-9} = 2.961 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$` },
        { q: 'And per mole, in kJ mol⁻¹.', a: 178.3, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: '× N_A.', w: R`$2.961 \times 10^{-19} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 178.3\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Each $nd \rightarrow 2p$ line has energy I.E.(2p) − $k/n^2$, so a graph of $1/\lambda$ against $1/n^2$ is a straight line whose intercept gives I.E.(2p). Using $n = 3$ and $n = 6$, find I.E.(2p) in J.`, a: 5.68e-19, u: 'J', tol: 0.01, h: 'Gradient from the two points, then extrapolate to 1/n² = 0.', w: R`$1/\lambda$: $1.6383 \times 10^6$ and $2.5543 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$ at $1/n^2 = 0.1111$ and $0.02778$. Gradient $-1.099 \times 10^7$; intercept $2.860 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$; $\times hc = 5.68 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$` },
        { q: 'Hence find the first ionisation energy of lithium in kJ mol⁻¹.', a: 520, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Ground state = 2p ionisation energy + the 2s→2p gap.', w: R`$(5.68 + 2.96) \times 10^{-19} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 520\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk14-3', n: 3, t: 'Acne cream', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 6, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-33-2'],
      s: R`Tazarotene ($M = 351.46$) is used in a 0.10% (by mass) cream of density $0.92\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$. In the body it becomes an active form with $M = 323.41$ and two fewer ¹³C NMR signals.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Calculate the concentration of tazarotene in the cream, in mol dm⁻³.', a: 2.62e-3, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: '1 dm³ of cream has mass 920 g.', w: R`$920 \times 0.0010 = 0.92\ \mathrm{g\ dm^{-3}}$; $\div 351.46 = 2.62 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: 'Tazarotene is an ethyl ester. Explain the active form.', h: 'What has mass 28.05 and two carbons?', w: R`$351.46 - 323.41 = 28.05 = \mathrm{C_2H_4}$: the ethyl ester is hydrolysed to the carboxylic acid, losing the two ethyl carbons from the spectrum.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk14-4', n: 4, t: 'Bombardier beetles', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-5-1', 'ca-37-4'],
      s: R`A bombardier beetle mixes equal volumes of hydrogen peroxide solution and a solution of compound A. Catalase decomposes the peroxide; A is oxidised to B: overall $\mathrm{A + H_2O_2 \rightarrow B + 2H_2O}$, $\Delta H = -202\ \mathrm{kJ}$ per mole of A. The mixture behaves like water: $4.18\ \mathrm{J\ g^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$, $1.00\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the equation for catalase decomposing H₂O₂, and classify the reaction.', h: 'O goes from −1 to 0 and −2.', w: R`$\mathrm{2H_2O_2 \rightarrow 2H_2O + O_2}$: disproportionation.` },
        { q: R`How much energy heats $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of the mixture from 25 °C to 100 °C, in kJ?`, a: 313.5, u: 'kJ', tol: 0.005, h: 'q = mcΔT.', w: R`$1000 \times 4.18 \times 75 = 313\,500\ \mathrm{J} = 313.5\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'What is the minimum concentration of H₂O₂ in the beetle’s peroxide chamber?', a: 3.10, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'One H₂O₂ per A; the peroxide is diluted 2× on mixing.', w: R`$313.5 \div 202 = 1.55\ \mathrm{mol}$ per dm³ of mixture, so $3.10\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ before mixing.` },
        { q: 'How many different 1,2,4-trisubstituted benzenes are there if all three groups are different, and if two are the same?', a: 6, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Arrange three different labels on three positions.', w: '6 if all different (3!), 3 if two are identical.' },
        { q: 'A contains C, H and O, has two OH groups (exchange with D₂O) and seven ¹³C signals. Removing one substituent gives C, whose ¹H NMR has two signals. Identify A and B.', h: 'C is benzene-1,4-diol.', w: 'A is 2-methylbenzene-1,4-diol; B is 2-methyl-1,4-benzoquinone, the hot irritant the beetle sprays.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk14-5', n: 5, t: 'Fire and ice', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 3, m: 22, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-4-1', 'ca-5-2'],
      s: R`Methane hydrates are ice-like cages of water holding methane, $\mathrm{(CH_4)_x(H_2O)_y}$. Take $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$ and 1 atm $= 1.0 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{Pa}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$150\ \mathrm{g}$ of a hydrate is burned in excess oxygen. The products give $175.39\ \mathrm{g}$ of water and, with limewater, $127.09\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{CaCO_3}$. Find the ratio of $\mathrm{H_2O}$ to $\mathrm{CH_4}$ in the hydrate.`, a: 5.67, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of CH₄ = moles of CaCO₃. Burning each CH₄ also makes 2 H₂O: subtract those.', w: R`$\mathrm{CH_4} = 1.2698\ \mathrm{mol}$; all water $= 9.735\ \mathrm{mol}$, of which $2.540$ came from burning, so $7.195\ \mathrm{mol}$ in the cage. Ratio $5.67 = 17/3$: empirical formula $\mathrm{(CH_4)_3(H_2O)_{17}}$.` },
        { q: 'Its molar mass is 2835.18 g mol⁻¹. Give the molecular formula.', h: 'Divide by the empirical formula mass, 354.4.', w: R`$2835.18 \div 354.4 = 8$: $\mathrm{(CH_4)_{24}(H_2O)_{136}}$` },
        { q: R`A lake bed holds $5.80 \times 10^{11}\ \mathrm{kg}$ of methane. What volume would it occupy at −15 °C and 1 atm, in $\mathrm{m^3}$?`, a: 7.75e11, u: 'm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'n = m ÷ 16.04.', w: R`$n = 3.616 \times 10^{13}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $V = 3.616 \times 10^{13} \times 8.31 \times 258 \div 10^5 = 7.75 \times 10^{11}\ \mathrm{m^3}$` },
        { q: R`The common hydrate is $\mathrm{(CH_4)_8(H_2O)_{46}}$ ($M = 957.07$). What mass of hydrate holds that methane, in kg?`, a: 4.33e12, u: 'kg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Methane is 13.41% of the hydrate by mass.', w: R`$5.80 \times 10^{11} \div 0.1341 = 4.33 \times 10^{12}\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
        { q: R`One unit cell contains one $\mathrm{(CH_4)_8(H_2O)_{46}}$, and the crystal density is $0.93\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$. Calculate the cell edge in nm.`, a: 1.196, u: 'nm', tol: 0.005, h: 'Cell mass ÷ density = a³.', w: R`$m = 957.07 \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 1.589 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{g}$; $V = 1.709 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$; $a = 1.196 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{cm}$` },
        { q: 'Treating water as spheres of radius 0.14 nm and methane as 0.21 nm, what percentage of the cell do the molecules fill?', a: 49.1, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Add 8 methane spheres and 46 water spheres.', w: R`$8 \times 0.0388 + 46 \times 0.01149 = 0.839\ \mathrm{nm^3}$; $0.839 \div 1.709 = 49.1\%$` },
        { q: R`$\Delta_c H$ of $\mathrm{(CH_4)_8(H_2O)_{46}}$ is $-6705.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. $\Delta_f H$: $\mathrm{CH_4}$ $-74.8$, $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ $-285.8$, $\mathrm{CO_2}$ $-393.5$. Calculate $\Delta H$ for $\mathrm{8CH_4(g) + 46H_2O(l) \rightarrow (CH_4)_8(H_2O)_{46}}$.`, a: -417.4, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'The caged water does not burn: compare with burning 8 free CH₄.', w: R`$\Delta_c H(\mathrm{CH_4}) = -890.3$; $8(-890.3) - (-6705.0) = -417.4\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2013, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Hydrazine rocket fuels · medals and bike tyres · colours of compounds · benzodiazepines · creatine', skipped: 'The benzodiazepine syntheses in question 4 are drawn as structures, so that question keeps only its calculation.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk13-1', n: 1, t: 'Hydrazine rockets', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 14, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Hydrazine, $\mathrm{N_2H_4}$, decomposes over a catalyst to give thrust; ammonia forms on the way. Wartime rocket planes burned a hydrazine–methanol mixture with hydrogen peroxide.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the equation for hydrazine decomposing to ammonia and nitrogen.', h: 'Three hydrazines.', w: R`$\mathrm{3N_2H_4 \rightarrow 4NH_3 + N_2}$` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{2NH_3(g) + H_2O_2(l) \rightarrow N_2H_4(l) + 2H_2O(l)}$, $\Delta H = -240.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. $\Delta_f H$: $\mathrm{NH_3}$ $-45.9$, $\mathrm{H_2O_2}$ $-187.8$, $\mathrm{H_2O}$ $-285.8$. Find $\Delta H$ for hydrazine decomposing into its elements.`, a: -52.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'First find ΔfH(N₂H₄).', w: R`$\Delta_f H(\mathrm{N_2H_4}) = -240.0 + 2(-45.9) - 187.8 + 2(285.8) = +52.0$, so decomposition is $-52.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Give the oxidation numbers of N and O before and after in $\mathrm{N_2H_4 + 2H_2O_2 \rightarrow N_2 + 4H_2O}$, and write the equation for $\mathrm{H_2O_2}$ oxidising methanol.`, h: 'Methanol goes to CO₂.', w: R`N: −2 → 0; O: −1 → −2. $\mathrm{CH_3OH + 3H_2O_2 \rightarrow CO_2 + 5H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`A plane carries 200 L of hydrazine (density $1.021\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$, $\Delta_c H = -622.2$) and 800 L of methanol (density $0.7918$, $\Delta_c H = -726.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$). How much heat is released if both burn completely, in kJ?`, a: 1.83e7, u: 'kJ', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mass → moles → heat for each.', w: R`Hydrazine: $204.2\ \mathrm{kg} = 6371\ \mathrm{mol} \to 3.96 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{kJ}$. Methanol: $633.4\ \mathrm{kg} = 19\,770\ \mathrm{mol} \to 1.435 \times 10^7\ \mathrm{kJ}$. Total $1.83 \times 10^7\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: R`Hydrazine with $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$ ignites on contact. Suggest the products, and the brown gas formed when $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$ is warmed. A derivative $\mathrm{C_2H_8N_2}$ has two different N and two equivalent C: give its structure.`, h: 'N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{N_2}$ and $\mathrm{H_2O}$; $\mathrm{NO_2}$; $\mathrm{(CH_3)_2N{-}NH_2}$ (UDMH).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk13-2', n: 2, t: 'Medals and bike tyres', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 18, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`Olympic medals are analysed and racing tyres are filled with lighter gases. Iodometry: $\mathrm{2Cu^{2+} + 4I^- \rightarrow 2CuI + I_2}$; $\mathrm{I_2 + 2S_2O_3^{2-} \rightarrow 2I^- + S_4O_6^{2-}}$. $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$0.650\ \mathrm{g}$ of a bronze medal is dissolved, treated with excess KI and made up to $100.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. $20.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ needs $23.95\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0800\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ thiosulfate. Find the percentage of copper.`, a: 93.7, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Cu : S₂O₃²⁻ = 1 : 1; scale by 5.', w: R`$1.916 \times 10^{-3} \times 5 = 9.58 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol} = 0.609\ \mathrm{g}$: 93.7%` },
        { q: R`A silver medal is a disc 80 mm across and 8 mm thick, 92.5% Ag and 7.5% Cu by mass (Ag $10.49$, Cu $8.96\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$; take the alloy density as the mass-weighted average). Find its mass.`, a: 417, u: 'g', tol: 0.005, h: 'V = πr²h in cm³.', w: R`$V = \pi \times 4.0^2 \times 0.8 = 40.2\ \mathrm{cm^3}$; $\rho = 10.375$; $m = 417\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`$4.000\ \mathrm{g}$ of a gold medal with concentrated nitric acid leaves $0.052\ \mathrm{g}$ undissolved; adding HCl to the solution precipitates $4.912\ \mathrm{g}$ of AgCl. Find the percentage of silver.`, a: 92.4, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Gold does not dissolve; Ag is 107.87/143.32 of AgCl.', w: R`Ag $= 4.912 \times 0.7527 = 3.697\ \mathrm{g}$: 92.4% Ag, 1.3% Au, 6.3% Cu` },
        { q: R`A tyre is a torus, $V = \pi^2 r d^2/2$, with $r = 0.35\ \mathrm{m}$ (wheel radius) and tube diameter $d = 25\ \mathrm{mm}$. At 100 psi (1 psi = 6895 Pa) and 25 °C, how many moles of gas are in one tyre?`, a: 0.301, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'V = 1.08 × 10⁻³ m³.', w: R`$V = \pi^2 \times 0.35 \times 0.025^2 \div 2 = 1.080 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m^3}$; $n = \dfrac{689\,500 \times 1.080 \times 10^{-3}}{8.31 \times 298} = 0.301\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'How many grams lighter are both tyres with helium instead of air (80% N₂, 20% O₂)?', a: 14.9, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'M(air) = 28.82.', w: R`$2 \times 0.301 \times (28.82 - 4.00) = 14.9\ \mathrm{g}$ (with $\mathrm{SF_6}$ they would be 70.5 g heavier).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk13-3', n: 3, t: 'Colours of compounds', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-28-3', 'ca-11-3'],
      s: 'Chemists often identify compounds by colour.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the colours of: CuO, Cu₂O, anhydrous CuSO₄, K₂Cr₂O₇, FeSO₄·7H₂O, ZnCl₂.', h: 'Copper oxides differ; anhydrous copper sulfate is not blue.', w: 'CuO black; Cu₂O red-brown; anhydrous CuSO₄ white; K₂Cr₂O₇ orange; FeSO₄·7H₂O pale green; ZnCl₂ white.' },
        { q: 'Seven solutions are mixed in pairs: barium chloride, chlorine water, iron(II) sulfate, lead(II) nitrate, silver nitrate, sodium carbonate, sodium iodide. Which pair gives a bright yellow precipitate, which a brown solution, and which a dirty green precipitate that turns brown?', h: 'Think PbI₂, I₂, Fe(OH)₂.', w: 'Lead(II) nitrate + sodium iodide: yellow PbI₂. Chlorine water + sodium iodide: brown iodine. Iron(II) sulfate + sodium carbonate: green iron(II) carbonate/hydroxide, oxidised to brown in air.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk13-4', n: 4, t: 'Benzodiazepines', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 6, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-36-1'],
      s: R`Diazepam ($M = 284.74$) is made from 4-chloroaniline ($M = 127.57$) in several steps, one molecule of each.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'A patient took 2 mg of diazepam three times a day for 2 years (730 days). If the overall yield of the synthesis is 23.0%, what mass of 4-chloroaniline was needed?', a: 8.53, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Total diazepam → moles → ÷ yield → mass of starting material.', w: R`$2 \times 3 \times 730 = 4380\ \mathrm{mg}$; $0.01538\ \mathrm{mol} \div 0.230 = 0.06688\ \mathrm{mol} = 8.53\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk13-5', n: 5, t: 'Creatine', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-34-4', 'ca-37-4'],
      s: R`Creatine, $\mathrm{H_2N{-}C({=}NH){-}N(CH_3){-}CH_2{-}COOH}$, is sold as its monohydrate. In the body it forms phosphocreatine; it also cyclises to creatinine, a waste product.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the molecular formula of creatine monohydrate and its percentage of nitrogen by mass.', a: 28.2, u: '% N', tol: 0.005, h: 'C₄H₉N₃O₂ · H₂O.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_4H_{11}N_3O_3}$, $M = 149.15$; N $= 42.03 \div 149.15 = 28.2\%$` },
        { q: 'Give the main form of creatine at pH 1, pH 7 and pH 12.', h: 'The guanidine group is very basic.', w: 'pH 1: guanidinium cation with COOH (charge +1). pH 7: zwitterion, guanidinium⁺ and COO⁻. pH 12: neutral guanidine with COO⁻ (charge −1).' },
        { q: 'Suggest how creatinine forms.', h: 'An intramolecular condensation.', w: 'The COOH condenses with the guanidine NH₂, losing water to give a five-membered ring (a cyclic amide).' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2012, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Rings of sulfur and S₄N₄ · perming hair · chemistry general knowledge', skipped: 'Questions 4 (the Lipitor synthesis) and 5 (a molecular car) are drawn almost entirely as structures, so they are not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk12-1', n: 1, t: 'Rings of sulfur', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-7-1'],
      s: R`Sulfur forms rings: $\mathrm{S_8}$ is most stable, but $\mathrm{S_6}$, $\mathrm{S_7}$ and others exist. In solution, $\mathrm{8S_7 \rightleftharpoons 7S_8}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`The S–S bond enthalpy is 259.0 in $\mathrm{S_7}$ and 264.0 kJ mol⁻¹ in $\mathrm{S_8}$. Calculate ΔH for $\mathrm{8S_7 \rightarrow 7S_8}$.`, a: -280, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Each ring of n atoms has n bonds.', w: R`Broken: $8 \times 7 \times 259.0 = 14\,504$. Made: $7 \times 8 \times 264.0 = 14\,784$. $\Delta H = -280\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`2.00 g of sulfur in $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of solvent is 0.40% $\mathrm{S_6}$, 0.90% $\mathrm{S_7}$ and 98.70% $\mathrm{S_8}$ by mass. Calculate $\log_{10} K_c$ for $\mathrm{8S_7 \rightleftharpoons 7S_8}$.`, a: 17.97, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'K = [S₈]⁷/[S₇]⁸. Work in logs.', w: R`$[\mathrm{S_7}] = 8.02 \times 10^{-5}$, $[\mathrm{S_8}] = 7.70 \times 10^{-3}$; $\log K = 7(-2.114) - 8(-4.096) = 17.97$` },
        { q: 'Combustion enthalpies: orthorhombic S₈ −296.83, monoclinic S₈ −297.16 kJ mol⁻¹ (per mole of S). Find ΔH for orthorhombic → monoclinic, and say which is more stable.', a: 0.33, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'Hess: subtract the combustion values.', w: R`$-296.83 - (-297.16) = +0.33\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$: orthorhombic is lower in energy, so more stable at 298 K.` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{S_4N_4}$ is an eight-membered ring of alternating S and N; each N makes three bonds, two S make two bonds and two make four. Describe a structure. Then describe poly(sulfur nitride).`, h: 'Put double bonds to fit the valencies.', w: R`Ring S–N=S=N–S–N=S=N (with S=N double bonds to the four-bonded sulfurs). Poly(sulfur nitride): $\mathrm{({-}S{-}N{=}S{=}N{-})_n}$ chains, alternating S–N and S=N.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk12-2', n: 2, t: 'Perming hair', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-34-4', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`Hair keratin is rich in cysteine, linked by S–S bridges. Perms break them with thioglycolic acid, $\mathrm{HSCH_2COOH}$ ($\mathrm{p}K_a$ 3.67 and 10.31). Sulfur in hair is measured by oxidising it to sulfate and weighing $\mathrm{BaSO_4}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$1.20\ \mathrm{g}$ of hair gives $300\ \mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{BaSO_4}$ from $3.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of solution. Find the percentage of sulfur, ignoring any loss.`, a: 3.43, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'S is 32.06/233.38 of BaSO₄.', w: R`$300 \times 0.1374 = 41.2\ \mathrm{mg}$; $41.2 \div 1200 = 3.43\%$` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{BaSO_4}$ dissolves to $2.40\ \mathrm{mg\ dm^{-3}}$. Correct the percentage of sulfur.`, a: 3.52, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Add back 3.00 × 2.40 mg.', w: R`$307.2\ \mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{BaSO_4}$ → $42.2\ \mathrm{mg}$ S: 3.52%` },
        { q: 'Is joining two cysteines into cystine an oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis or dehydration?', h: 'Two S–H become S–S.', w: 'Oxidation (hydrogen is removed).' },
        { q: 'At pH 9.5, what percentage of the thioglycolate is the dianion (assume only mono- and dianion are present)?', a: 13.4, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '[A²⁻]/[HA⁻] = 10^(pH − pKa₂).', w: R`$10^{-0.81} = 0.155$; $0.155 \div 1.155 = 13.4\%$` },
        { q: R`A perm solution is $0.18\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ ammonium thioglycolate, $\mathrm{HSCH_2COONH_4}$, in $400\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ bottles. What mass is in each bottle?`, a: 7.86, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'M = 109.15.', w: R`$0.18 \times 0.400 = 0.072\ \mathrm{mol} = 7.86\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk12-3', n: 3, t: 'General knowledge', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-3-6'],
      s: 'A round of chemical general knowledge.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Order these by boiling point, lowest first: C₂H₅OC₂H₅, C₂H₅OH, HOCH₂CH₂OH, CH₃CHO, C₄H₁₀, H₂O.', h: 'More hydrogen bonding, higher boiling point.', w: 'C₄H₁₀ < CH₃CHO < C₂H₅OC₂H₅ < C₂H₅OH < H₂O < HOCH₂CH₂OH' },
        { q: 'Give formulae for fool’s gold, Epsom salts and laughing gas, and systematic names for acetone, toluene and sodium hypochlorite.', h: 'Iron sulfide; magnesium sulfate hydrate; a nitrogen oxide.', w: 'FeS₂, MgSO₄·7H₂O, N₂O; propanone, methylbenzene, sodium chlorate(I).' },
        { q: 'What colour change is seen when zinc oxide is heated?', h: 'It reverses on cooling.', w: 'White to yellow (white again when cool).' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2011, mins: 120,
    topics: 'A snack food · calcium carbide · explosives and oxygen balance · a spectroscopy puzzle · phenobarbital', skipped: 'The phenobarbital synthesis in question 5 is drawn as structures, so that question keeps only its analysis.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk11-1', n: 1, t: 'A snack food', f: 'Analysis', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-5-1', 'ci-12-2'],
      s: R`A bag of salt-and-vinegar corn snack holds $25\ \mathrm{g}$. Its energy is measured by burning a sample under a can of water ($4.18\ \mathrm{J\ g^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$; 1 kJ = 0.239 dietary calories). Its salt is measured by titrating chloride with silver nitrate, using potassium chromate(VI) as indicator.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$1.5\ \mathrm{g}$ of snack heats $400\ \mathrm{g}$ of water by 19.6 °C. How many dietary calories are in a bag?`, a: 130.5, u: 'calories', tol: 0.01, h: 'q = mcΔT for 1.5 g, then scale to 25 g.', w: R`$400 \times 4.18 \times 19.6 = 32.77\ \mathrm{kJ}$ for 1.5 g; $546.2\ \mathrm{kJ}$ per bag $\times 0.239 = 131$ calories` },
        { q: R`A whole bag is washed and the solution made up to $200.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. $20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ needs $17.40\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0500\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$. What mass of NaCl is in the bag?`, a: 0.508, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl; scale by 10.', w: R`$8.70 \times 10^{-4} \times 10 = 8.70 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol} = 0.508\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'Give formulae for silver nitrate(V) and potassium chromate(VI), and the number of chiral centres in citric acid, HOOC–CH₂–C(OH)(COOH)–CH₂–COOH.', h: 'Are the two CH₂COOH groups different?', w: R`$\mathrm{AgNO_3}$, $\mathrm{K_2CrO_4}$. Citric acid has no chiral centre: the central carbon carries two identical $\mathrm{CH_2COOH}$ groups.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk11-2', n: 2, t: 'Calcium carbide', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: R`Calcium carbide is made from lime and coke at about 2000 °C, releasing a toxic gas A. With water it gives a hydrocarbon C. Heating calcium phosphate with coke gives A and a compound B, which reacts with water to give a garlic-smelling gas.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Name two common diatomic gases isoelectronic with $\mathrm{C_2^{2-}}$, and write the equation for making $\mathrm{CaC_2}$.`, h: 'Count electrons: 14.', w: R`$\mathrm{N_2}$ and CO. $\mathrm{CaO + 3C \rightarrow CaC_2 + CO}$; A is CO.` },
        { q: 'C is 92.3% carbon and has Mr 26. Give its formula and the equation for CaC₂ with water.', h: 'Empirical formula CH.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_2H_2}$. $\mathrm{CaC_2 + 2H_2O \rightarrow Ca(OH)_2 + C_2H_2}$` },
        { q: R`What mass of ethyne does $10.0\ \mathrm{g}$ of 80.0% pure calcium carbide give?`, a: 3.25, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles CaC₂ = moles C₂H₂.', w: R`$8.00 \div 64.10 = 0.1248\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 26.04 = 3.25\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'Write the equation for calcium phosphate with coke, and name the smelly gas.', h: 'B is calcium phosphide.', w: R`$\mathrm{Ca_3(PO_4)_2 + 8C \rightarrow Ca_3P_2 + 8CO}$; $\mathrm{Ca_3P_2}$ with water gives phosphine, $\mathrm{PH_3}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk11-3', n: 3, t: 'Explosives', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`An explosive $\mathrm{C_aH_bN_cO_d}$ explodes fastest when it holds just enough oxygen to turn all its C into $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and H into $\mathrm{H_2O}$. The oxygen balance is $\mathrm{OB\% = \dfrac{-1600\,(2a + b/2 - d)}{M}}$. Molar volume at RTP: $24\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'For complete decomposition to CO₂, H₂O and N₂, what must d be?', h: 'Count the O atoms needed.', w: R`$d = 2a + \tfrac{b}{2}$` },
        { q: R`Calculate the oxygen balance of PETN, $\mathrm{C_5H_8N_4O_{12}}$ ($M = 316.14$).`, a: -10.1, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '2a + b/2 − d = 10 + 4 − 12.', w: R`$-1600 \times 2 \div 316.14 = -10.1\%$` },
        { q: 'PETN explodes to CO₂, CO, H₂O and N₂ (H takes O first, then C). Write the equation.', h: '8 O are left for 5 C.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_5H_8N_4O_{12} \rightarrow 3CO_2 + 2CO + 4H_2O + 2N_2}$: 11 mol of gas.` },
        { q: R`What mass of PETN gives $2.0\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of gas at RTP?`, a: 2.40, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: '11 mol of gas per mol of PETN.', w: R`$2.0 \div 24 = 0.0833\ \mathrm{mol}$ gas; $\div 11 = 7.58 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 316.14 = 2.40\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk11-4', n: 4, t: 'A spectroscopy puzzle', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-37-4', 'ca-37-3', 'ca-22-1'],
      s: R`Seven compounds 1–7 all have formula $\mathrm{C_4H_{10}O}$. Compounds 1–4 show a broad IR band near 3300 cm⁻¹.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Compound 4 is an alcohol with only two ¹H NMR signals. Identify it.', h: 'Very symmetrical.', w: '2-Methylpropan-2-ol.' },
        { q: 'Compound 3 is an alcohol with ¹H signals in the ratio 2 : 1 : 1 : 6. Identify it.', h: 'A 6H signal means two equivalent CH₃.', w: R`2-Methylpropan-1-ol, $\mathrm{(CH_3)_2CHCH_2OH}$.` },
        { q: 'Compound 5 has only two ¹H signals, a 3H triplet and a 2H quartet. Identify it.', h: 'Symmetrical ether.', w: 'Ethoxyethane.' },
        { q: 'Ethers 6 and 7 have four and three ¹³C signals. Identify them.', h: 'The other two ethers.', w: '6: 1-methoxypropane. 7: 2-methoxypropane. (Compounds 1 and 2 are butan-1-ol and butan-2-ol.)' },
        { q: R`How many structural isomers of $\mathrm{C_4H_{10}O}$ are there in all?`, a: 7, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Four alcohols, three ethers.', w: '7' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk11-5', n: 5, t: 'A metabolite', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ci-12-2'],
      s: R`Compound A ($M_r = 60$) is found in urine. $0.300\ \mathrm{g}$ of A burns to $0.220\ \mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and $0.180\ \mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{H_2O}$. Another $0.300\ \mathrm{g}$ releases ammonia that neutralises $49.95\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ HCl. The rest is oxygen.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many moles of N are in 0.300 g of A?', a: 9.99e-3, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'NH₃ : HCl = 1 : 1.', w: R`$0.04995 \times 0.200 = 9.99 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'Find the molecular formula of A and name it.', h: 'Find C, H, N, then O by difference.', w: R`C $4.99 \times 10^{-3}$; H $0.0200$; N $9.99 \times 10^{-3}$; O by difference $0.0799\ \mathrm{g} = 4.99 \times 10^{-3}$. Ratio 1 : 4 : 2 : 1: $\mathrm{CH_4N_2O}$, urea (M = 60).` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2010, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Ambrox · a copper complex from the IChO practical · halogen fluorides · salting roads · osmium tetroxide · gold leaf', skipped: 'The structures in questions 1 and 5 are not recreated; those questions keep their calculations.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk10-1', n: 1, t: 'Ambrox', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-2-4'],
      s: R`Ambrox ($\mathrm{C_{16}H_{28}O}$, $M = 236.39$), a perfume ingredient, is made from (−)-drimenol ($M = 222.37$), one molecule from one, in steps with yields of 82% and 88%. Drimenol is 0.40% of the bark it is extracted from.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many moles is 12 tonnes of Ambrox?', a: 5.08e4, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: '1 tonne = 10⁶ g.', w: R`$1.2 \times 10^7 \div 236.39 = 5.08 \times 10^4\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'What mass of drimenol is needed each year, in tonnes?', a: 15.6, u: 't', tol: 0.01, h: 'Overall yield = 0.82 × 0.88.', w: R`$5.08 \times 10^4 \div 0.7216 = 7.03 \times 10^4\ \mathrm{mol} \times 222.37 = 15.6\ \mathrm{t}$` },
        { q: 'And what mass of bark, in tonnes?', a: 3910, u: 't', tol: 0.01, h: 'Drimenol is 0.40% of the bark.', w: R`$15.64 \div 0.0040 = 3.91 \times 10^3\ \mathrm{t}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk10-2', n: 2, t: 'A copper complex', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-28-5', 'ca-2-3'],
      s: R`Compound X contains tetramethylammonium cations, $\mathrm{(CH_3)_4N^+}$, and a complex anion made of copper, chlorine and oxygen. Copper is found with EDTA ($\mathrm{Cu^{2+} + EDTA^{4-} \rightarrow [CuEDTA]^{2-}}$), chloride with silver nitrate. Combustion analysis: C 20.86%, H 5.25%, N 6.08%.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$0.1200\ \mathrm{g}$ of X needs $20.84\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.02500\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ EDTA. Find the percentage of copper.`, a: 27.6, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: '1 : 1.', w: R`$5.21 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} \times 63.55 = 0.03311\ \mathrm{g}$: 27.6%` },
        { q: R`$0.1500\ \mathrm{g}$ of X needs $20.35\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0800\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$. Find the percentage of chlorine.`, a: 38.5, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl.', w: R`$1.628 \times 10^{-3} \times 35.45 = 0.05771\ \mathrm{g}$: 38.5%` },
        { q: 'Find the formula of the anion.', h: 'Divide each % by Ar, then by the copper value. Oxygen is what is left.', w: R`Per Cu: Cl 2.5, C 4, H 12, N 1, O 0.25. Four Cu: $\mathrm{[(CH_3)_4N]_4[Cu_4OCl_{10}]}$; the anion is $\mathrm{[Cu_4OCl_{10}]^{4-}}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk10-3', n: 3, t: 'Halogen fluorides', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-11-2', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{ClF_3}$ is a ferocious fluorinating agent: $\mathrm{2AgCl + 2ClF_3 \rightarrow 2AgF_2 + Cl_2 + 2ClF}$. Iodine forms IF, $\mathrm{IF_3}$, $\mathrm{IF_5}$ and $\mathrm{IF_7}$, with $\Delta_f H$ = −95.0, −485, −840 and −960 kJ mol⁻¹.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the equation for uranium with $\mathrm{ClF_3}$ giving $\mathrm{UF_6}$ and ClF, and say what is oxidised when AgCl reacts.`, h: 'Ag goes from +1 to +2.', w: R`$\mathrm{U + 3ClF_3 \rightarrow UF_6 + 3ClF}$. In the AgCl reaction, $\mathrm{Ag^+}$ and $\mathrm{Cl^-}$ are oxidised.` },
        { q: R`Calculate ΔH for $\mathrm{5IF \rightarrow 2I_2 + IF_5}$.`, a: -365, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'I₂ is zero.', w: R`$-840 - 5(-95.0) = -365\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: R`Calculate ΔH for $\mathrm{5IF_3 \rightarrow I_2 + 3IF_5}$.`, a: -95, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$3(-840) - 5(-485) = -95\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: R`Calculate ΔH for $\mathrm{7IF_5 \rightarrow I_2 + 5IF_7}$. Which fluoride does not disproportionate?`, a: 1080, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$5(-960) - 7(-840) = +1080\ \mathrm{kJ}$: $\mathrm{IF_5}$ is stable to disproportionation.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk10-4', n: 4, t: 'Salting roads', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 16, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ci-3-3'],
      s: R`Dissolved ions lower the freezing point of water by $\Delta T = \dfrac{RT_m^2\,x}{\Delta_m H}$, where $x$ is the mole fraction of dissolved particles, $T_m = 273\ \mathrm{K}$ and $\Delta_m H = 6.01\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Take 1 dm³ of solution to contain 1 dm³ of water.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What is the concentration of pure water, in mol dm⁻³?', a: 55.5, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.005, h: '1000 g ÷ 18.02.', w: R`$55.5\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`For $2.50\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ NaCl, find the mole fraction of ions.`, a: 0.0826, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Each NaCl gives 2 ions.', w: R`$5.00 \div (55.5 + 5.00) = 0.0826$` },
        { q: 'What is its freezing point, in °C?', a: -8.5, u: '°C', abs: 0.1, h: 'ΔT = RT²x/ΔH.', w: R`$\Delta T = 8.314 \times 273^2 \times 0.0826 \div 6010 = 8.5\ \mathrm{K}$: −8.5 °C` },
        { q: R`In $2.00\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{CaCl_2}$, each $\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}$ ties up 9 water molecules, which no longer count as solvent. What is the freezing point, in °C?`, a: -14.2, u: '°C', abs: 0.15, h: '6 mol of ions; free water = 55.5 − 18.', w: R`$x = 6 \div (37.5 + 6) = 0.138$; $\Delta T = 14.2\ \mathrm{K}$: −14.2 °C` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk10-5', n: 5, t: 'Osmium tetroxide', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 8, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-14-2', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: R`Osmium tetroxide turns alkenes into diols by adding two OH groups to the same face of the C=C, through a cyclic osmate ester. It is used in tiny catalytic amounts because it is toxic and costly.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the oxidation number of Os in $\mathrm{OsO_2}$, $\mathrm{OsO_4}$ and $\mathrm{OsO_4^{2-}}$.`, h: 'O is −2.', w: '+4, +8 and +6.' },
        { q: 'What type of reaction is alkene → diol, and what is the oxidation number of Os in the cyclic osmate ester (OsO₄ joined to two carbons through two O atoms)?', a: 6, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Osmium gains two electrons.', w: 'An oxidation of the alkene; osmium is reduced from +8 to +6.' },
        { q: '(Z)-But-2-ene and (E)-but-2-ene each react. Which gives the meso diol?', h: 'Both OH go on the same face (syn addition).', w: '(Z)-But-2-ene gives meso-butane-2,3-diol; (E)-but-2-ene gives the pair of enantiomers.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk10-6', n: 6, t: 'Gold leaf', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-3-3', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Gold ($A_r = 197$, density $19.3\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$) is face-centred cubic. A dome is a hemisphere 20.0 m across, covered with 60 kg of gold. $N_A = 6.02 \times 10^{23}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Calculate the mass of one gold atom, in g.', a: 3.27e-22, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: '197 ÷ N_A.', w: R`$3.27 \times 10^{-22}\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'Find the radius of a gold atom, in pm.', a: 144, u: 'pm', tol: 0.01, h: '4 atoms per cell; a = 2√2 r.', w: R`$a^3 = 4 \times 3.27 \times 10^{-22} \div 19.3 = 6.78 \times 10^{-23}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$, $a = 408\ \mathrm{pm}$, $r = 144\ \mathrm{pm}$` },
        { q: 'What fraction of the cell do the atoms fill?', a: 0.740, u: '', tol: 0.003, h: '4 × (4/3)πr³ ÷ (2√2 r)³.', w: R`$\pi/(3\sqrt2) = 0.740$` },
        { q: 'How thick is the gold on the dome, on average, in cm?', a: 4.95e-4, u: 'cm', tol: 0.01, h: 'Volume ÷ curved area 2πr².', w: R`$V = 60\,000 \div 19.3 = 3109\ \mathrm{cm^3}$; area $= 2\pi(1000)^2 = 6.283 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{cm^2}$; $4.95 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}$` },
        { q: 'Layers of atoms stack a√3/3 apart. About how many layers thick is the gold?', a: 21000, u: 'layers', tol: 0.02, h: 'Layer spacing = 2.35 × 10⁻⁸ cm.', w: R`$4.95 \times 10^{-4} \div 2.354 \times 10^{-8} = 2.1 \times 10^4$ layers` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2009, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Greener fireworks · propane heaters and the Olympic flame · matches · arsenic in water · a hidden painting found with X-rays', skipped: 'Question 4 (the loperamide synthesis) is drawn almost entirely as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk09-1', n: 1, t: 'Greener fireworks', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-2-3'],
      s: R`Nitrogen-rich fuels such as 3,6-dihydrazino-1,2,4,5-tetrazine, $\mathrm{C_2H_6N_8}$, and octanitrocubane, $\mathrm{C_8(NO_2)_8}$, could replace toxic metal salts and perchlorates in fireworks.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the equation for $\mathrm{C_2H_6N_8}$ burning in excess oxygen, and say how many ¹⁵N NMR signals it gives.`, h: 'Ring N are all alike; the NH and NH₂ nitrogens differ.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_2H_6N_8 + 3.5O_2 \rightarrow 2CO_2 + 3H_2O + 4N_2}$. Three signals: ring N, NH and $\mathrm{NH_2}$.` },
        { q: 'By how many degrees is the C–C–C angle in cubane smaller than in a straight-chain alkane?', a: 19.5, u: '°', abs: 0.2, h: '90° against 109.5°.', w: '109.5 − 90 = 19.5°' },
        { q: 'Show that octanitrocubane needs no extra oxygen to burn.', h: 'Count the O atoms.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_8(NO_2)_8 \rightarrow 8CO_2 + 4N_2}$: its 16 O atoms exactly oxidise its 8 C.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk09-2', n: 2, t: 'Propane heaters', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 14, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-4-1', 'ca-5-1'],
      s: R`A patio heater burns propane at 12 kW from a cylinder holding 15 kg of propane ($\Delta_c H = -2220\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$). Molar volume of a gas: $24\ \mathrm{dm^3\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many moles of propane are in the cylinder?', a: 340, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'M = 44.09.', w: R`$15\,000 \div 44.09 = 340\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'What mass of CO₂ does burning it all release, in kg?', a: 44.9, u: 'kg', tol: 0.01, h: 'C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O.', w: R`$3 \times 340 \times 44.01 = 44.9\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
        { q: 'At 12 kW, how many cm³ of propane gas leave the cylinder each second?', a: 130, u: 'cm³ s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: '12 kJ s⁻¹ ÷ 2220 kJ mol⁻¹.', w: R`$5.41 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\ s^{-1}} \times 24\,000 = 130\ \mathrm{cm^3\ s^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`The odorant ethanethiol, $\mathrm{C_2H_5SH}$, is added at 0.05 molecules per $10^9$ propane molecules. What mass goes into the cylinder?`, a: 1.06e-6, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles × 5 × 10⁻¹¹ × 62.13.', w: R`$340 \times 5 \times 10^{-11} \times 62.13 = 1.06 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{g}$. Its C–S–H angle is about 95–100° (bent, two lone pairs on S).` },
        { q: 'An Olympic flame burns 5000 m³ of methane an hour for 17 days. What mass of CO₂ is produced, in tonnes?', a: 3741, u: 't', tol: 0.01, h: 'Volume ÷ 0.024 m³ mol⁻¹.', w: R`$2.04 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{m^3} \div 0.024 = 8.5 \times 10^7\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 44.01 = 3.74 \times 10^3\ \mathrm{t}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk09-3', n: 3, t: 'Matches', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-37-3'],
      s: R`Strike-anywhere match heads contain $\mathrm{P_4S_3}$ and $\mathrm{KClO_3}$. $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): KCl $-436.5$; $\mathrm{KClO_3}$ $-397.7$; $\mathrm{SO_2}$ $-296.8$; $\mathrm{P_4S_3}$ $-151.0$; $\mathrm{P_4O_{10}}$ $-2984$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write a single equation for $\mathrm{P_4S_3}$ reacting with $\mathrm{KClO_3}$ to give $\mathrm{P_4O_{10}}$, $\mathrm{SO_2}$ and KCl.`, h: 'Combine P₄S₃ + 8O₂ with 2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂.', w: R`$\mathrm{3P_4S_3 + 16KClO_3 \rightarrow 3P_4O_{10} + 9SO_2 + 16KCl}$` },
        { q: R`What is the mass ratio $\mathrm{P_4S_3 : KClO_3}$ for exact reaction?`, a: 0.337, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: '3 × 220.1 : 16 × 122.55.', w: R`$660.3 \div 1960.8 = 0.337$` },
        { q: 'Calculate ΔH for the equation above.', a: -11791, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$3(-2984) + 9(-296.8) + 16(-436.5) - [3(-151.0) + 16(-397.7)] = -11\,791\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{P_4S_3}$ has two ³¹P environments. Suggest a structure for an isomer of $\mathrm{P_4S_4}$ with only one.`, h: 'Make every P equivalent.', w: 'A P₄ square (or tetrahedron-derived cage) with S bridging every P–P edge equally, e.g. a P₄ ring with four S, each P bonded to two S and one P, all equivalent.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk09-5', n: 5, t: 'Arsenic', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-3-5', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Arsenic in groundwater is removed by adsorbing hydrogenarsenate, $\mathrm{HAsO_4^{2-}}$, onto iron(III) hydroxide: $[\mathrm{HAsO_4^{2-}}]_t = [\mathrm{HAsO_4^{2-}}]_0e^{-kt}$. The safe limit is $10\ \mu\mathrm{g\ dm^{-3}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the shape of arsine, $\mathrm{AsH_3}$, the Marsh-test equations (arsine burning to $\mathrm{As_2O_3}$; $\mathrm{As_2O_3}$ with zinc and sulfuric acid), and the oxidation state of As in $\mathrm{HAsO_4^{2-}}$.`, h: 'Zinc reduces As(III) to AsH₃.', w: R`Trigonal pyramidal. $\mathrm{2AsH_3 + 3O_2 \rightarrow As_2O_3 + 3H_2O}$; $\mathrm{As_2O_3 + 6Zn + 6H_2SO_4 \rightarrow 2AsH_3 + 6ZnSO_4 + 3H_2O}$. +5.` },
        { q: R`At 40 °C the arsenate falls from 160 to $20\ \mu\mathrm{g\ dm^{-3}}$ in 45 minutes. Find k in $\mathrm{min^{-1}}$.`, a: 0.0462, u: 'min⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: '160 → 20 is three half-lives.', w: R`$t_{1/2} = 15\ \mathrm{min}$; $k = 0.693 \div 15 = 0.0462\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Another sample reaches the safe limit after 50 minutes. What was its starting concentration?', a: 101, u: 'μg dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'C₀ = 10 e^(kt).', w: R`$10 \times e^{0.0462 \times 50} = 101\ \mu\mathrm{g\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`At 20 °C the adsorption reaches equilibrium with $K = [\text{adsorbed}]/[\text{dissolved}] = 150$. Starting from $25\ \mu\mathrm{g\ dm^{-3}}$, what concentration stays dissolved?`, a: 0.166, u: 'μg dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Dissolved fraction = 1/(1 + K).', w: R`$25 \div 151 = 0.166\ \mu\mathrm{g\ dm^{-3}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk09-6', n: 6, t: 'A hidden painting', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-1-3', 'ca-1-4'],
      s: R`X-ray fluorescence revealed a portrait painted beneath a landscape. Electron energies follow $E_n = -\dfrac{(Z - S)^2 R_H}{n^2}$ with $R_H = 13.6\ \mathrm{eV}$; for Kα lines ($n = 2 \rightarrow 1$) take $S = 1$, and for Lα lines ($n = 3 \rightarrow 2$) $S = 7.4$. 1 eV = 96.485 kJ mol⁻¹.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Calculate the ionisation energy of hydrogen in kJ mol⁻¹.', a: 1312, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'E₁ = −13.6 eV.', w: R`$13.6 \times 96.485 = 1312\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Potassium’s first ionisation energy is $418.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$ ($n = 4$). Find the effective nuclear charge felt by its outer electron.`, a: 2.26, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'IE = Z_eff² × 13.6 ÷ 16 (in eV).', w: R`$418.8 \div 96.485 = 4.341\ \mathrm{eV}$; $Z_{eff}^2 = 4.341 \times 16 \div 13.6 = 5.11$; $Z_{eff} = 2.26$` },
        { q: 'A Kα peak at 8000 eV belongs to which element (atomic number)?', a: 29, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'E = 13.6(Z − 1)²(1 − ¼).', w: R`$(Z-1)^2 = 8000 \div 10.2 = 784$; $Z = 29$: copper.` },
        { q: 'An Lα peak at 9956 eV comes from the red pigment of the lips. Find Z, and suggest the pigment.', a: 80, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'E = 13.6(Z − 7.4)²(¼ − 1/9).', w: R`$(Z - 7.4)^2 = 9956 \div 1.889 = 5271$; $Z = 80$: mercury. The red sulfide pigment is vermilion, HgS.` },
        { q: 'Estimate the Kα energy of tin (Z = 50), in eV.', a: 24490, u: 'eV', tol: 0.005, h: '10.2 × 49².', w: R`$10.2 \times 49^2 = 24\,490\ \mathrm{eV}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2008, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Pure silicon for solar cells · vitamin D in mushrooms · mercury fulminate · aluminium compounds · peptide toxins · fexofenadine', skipped: 'The structures in questions 2, 4 and 6 are not recreated; those questions keep their calculations.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk08-1', n: 1, t: 'Pure silicon', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-9-2', 'ca-3-5'],
      s: R`Silicon for solar cells is made from quartz and purified by zone refining. As a molten zone passes along a bar, the impurity in the solid at fraction $f$ of the way along is $C_s = kC_0(1 - f)^{k-1}$, where $k$ is the distribution coefficient and $C_0$ the starting concentration.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write equations for: quartz + carbon → silicon; trichlorosilane + hydrogen → silicon; silane igniting in air.', h: 'CO, HCl and SiO₂ form.', w: R`$\mathrm{SiO_2 + 2C \rightarrow Si + 2CO}$; $\mathrm{HSiCl_3 + H_2 \rightarrow Si + 3HCl}$; $\mathrm{SiH_4 + 2O_2 \rightarrow SiO_2 + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`Triethoxysilane, $\mathrm{HSi(OC_2H_5)_3}$, disproportionates to silane and tetraethoxysilane. Write the equation.`, h: 'Four molecules.', w: R`$\mathrm{4HSi(OC_2H_5)_3 \rightarrow SiH_4 + 3Si(OC_2H_5)_4}$` },
        { q: R`Aluminium has $k = 2.0 \times 10^{-3}$ and $C_0 = 2800$ ppm. After one pass, the last 4% of the bar is cut off. What is the highest Al concentration left, in ppm?`, a: 139, u: 'ppm', tol: 0.01, h: 'Worst point: f = 0.96.', w: R`$2.0 \times 10^{-3} \times 2800 \times 0.04^{-0.998} = 139\ \mathrm{ppm}$` },
        { q: R`Iron has $k = 8.0 \times 10^{-6}$ and $C_0 = 1500$ ppm. What minimum percentage of the bar must be cut off for Fe to stay below 10 ppm?`, a: 0.12, u: '%', tol: 0.02, h: 'C_s ≈ kC₀/(1 − f).', w: R`$0.012 \div (1 - f) < 10$, so $1 - f > 1.2 \times 10^{-3}$: cut off 0.12%.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk08-2', n: 2, t: 'Vitamin D in mushrooms', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 1, m: 6, lec: ['ca-8-2', 'ca-26-1'],
      s: 'UV light turns ergosterol in mushrooms into vitamin D₂.',
      p: [
        { q: 'The rate constant is 2.5 times larger at 32 °C than at 20 °C. Calculate the activation energy.', a: 56.7, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ln(k₂/k₁) = (Ea/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂).', w: R`$E_a = 8.314 \ln 2.5 \div (1/293 - 1/305) = 56.7\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk08-3', n: 3, t: 'Mercury fulminate', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: R`Mercury(II) fulminate, $\mathrm{Hg(CNO)_2}$, is a detonator. It detonates to mercury, carbon monoxide and nitrogen. Its fulminate ion is C–N–O; the isomeric cyanate ion is O–C–N.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the detonation equation.', h: 'Three products.', w: R`$\mathrm{Hg(CNO)_2 \rightarrow Hg + 2CO + N_2}$` },
        { q: R`$\Delta_f H$: $\mathrm{Hg(CNO)_2}$ $+300$, CO $-110.5\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Calculate ΔH of detonation.`, a: -521, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Hg and N₂ are elements.', w: R`$2(-110.5) - 300 = -521\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Draw (describe) the fulminate and cyanate ions with sensible bonding.', h: 'Formal charges.', w: R`Fulminate: $\mathrm{^-C{\equiv}N^+{-}O^-}$ (bonded through C to Hg). Cyanate: $\mathrm{^-O{-}C{\equiv}N \leftrightarrow O{=}C{=}N^-}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk08-4', n: 4, t: 'Aluminium compounds', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-3-4', 'ca-9-2'],
      s: 'Aluminium halides are electron-deficient. Aluminium phosphide is used as a rat poison because it releases a toxic gas with water.',
      p: [
        { q: 'How many electrons surround Al in monomeric AlBr₃?', a: 6, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Three bonding pairs.', w: 'Six: it is electron-deficient.' },
        { q: 'In the vapour, aluminium bromide forms molecules with Mr 533.4. Give their formula and how Al completes its octet.', h: 'Compare with AlBr₃ = 266.7.', w: R`$\mathrm{Al_2Br_6}$: two bromines bridge, each donating a lone pair, so each Al has 8 electrons.` },
        { q: 'Write the equation for aluminium phosphide reacting with water.', h: 'A toxic hydride of phosphorus forms.', w: R`$\mathrm{AlP + 3H_2O \rightarrow Al(OH)_3 + PH_3}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk08-5', n: 5, t: 'Peptide toxins', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 8, lec: ['ca-34-4', 'ca-22-2'],
      s: 'Cone snails make polypeptide toxins. Using integer masses (C 12, H 1, N 14, O 16): glycine 75, alanine 89, valine 117; each peptide bond releases one water.',
      p: [
        { q: 'What is the mass of the tripeptide Ala–Val–Gly?', a: 245, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Add the amino acids, subtract two waters.', w: R`$89 + 117 + 75 - 36 = 245$` },
        { q: 'A peptide has 8 residues: 2 cysteine, 2 glycine and one each of four others. How many different sequences are possible?', a: 10080, u: '', abs: 0.5, h: '8! ÷ (2! 2!).', w: R`$40\,320 \div 4 = 10\,080$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk08-6', n: 6, t: 'Fexofenadine', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 6, lec: ['ca-2-1', 'ca-34-1'],
      s: 'The antihistamine fexofenadine is sold as its hydrochloride: a tablet with 180.00 mg of fexofenadine contains 193.08 mg of the salt.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Calculate the molar mass of fexofenadine.', a: 501.7, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'The extra mass is HCl, in the same number of moles.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{HCl}) = 13.08 \div 36.46\ \mathrm{mmol}$; $M = 180.00 \times 36.46 \div 13.08 = 501.7$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2007, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Rocket propellants · shapes of phosphorus compounds · phosphate in blood · a flame retardant · Tamiflu · chlorine dioxide', skipped: 'The stereocontrolled Tamiflu synthesis (question 5) is drawn as structures, so only its calculations are kept.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk07-1', n: 1, t: 'Rocket propellants', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-5-2'],
      s: R`A launch vehicle’s external tank weighs 30 t empty and 760 t when full of liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen in exactly the ratio needed to make water. Densities: liquid $\mathrm{H_2}$ $0.0708\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$, liquid $\mathrm{O_2}$ $1.141\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What mass of hydrogen is in the full tank, in tonnes?', a: 81.7, u: 't', tol: 0.01, h: 'H₂ : O₂ = 2.016 : 16.00 by mass.', w: R`$730 \times 2.016 \div 18.016 = 81.7\ \mathrm{t}$; so $\mathrm{O_2} = 648\ \mathrm{t}$` },
        { q: 'What total tank volume is needed, in m³?', a: 1722, u: 'm³', tol: 0.01, h: '1 g cm⁻³ = 1 t m⁻³.', w: R`$81.7 \div 0.0708 = 1154\ \mathrm{m^3}$; $648 \div 1.141 = 568\ \mathrm{m^3}$; total $1722\ \mathrm{m^3}$` },
        { q: R`The solid boosters react $\mathrm{10Al + 6NH_4ClO_4 \rightarrow 4Al_2O_3 + 2AlCl_3 + 12H_2O(l) + 3N_2}$. $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): $\mathrm{NH_4ClO_4}$ $-295.3$; $\mathrm{Al_2O_3}$ $-1675.7$; $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$ $-704.2$; $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ $-285.8$. Find ΔH for the equation.`, a: -9769, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.002, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$4(-1675.7) + 2(-704.2) + 12(-285.8) - 6(-295.3) = -9769\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'The boosters hold 500 t of propellant, 18% of it aluminium (the limiting reagent). How much energy is released, in kJ?', a: 3.26e9, u: 'kJ', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of Al ÷ 10 × 9769.', w: R`$9.0 \times 10^7 \div 26.98 = 3.34 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\div 10 \times 9769 = 3.26 \times 10^9\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk07-2', n: 2, t: 'Shapes in phosphorus chemistry', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-4', 'ca-12-1'],
      s: R`White phosphorus is $\mathrm{P_4}$, a tetrahedron of P atoms. Its oxides $\mathrm{P_4O_6}$ and $\mathrm{P_4O_{10}}$ keep the tetrahedron, with an O inserted along every edge. Phosphate is detected as a yellow molybdophosphate, $[\mathrm{PMo_{12}O_{40}}]^{n-}$, in which a central $\mathrm{PO_4}$ sits inside a cage of twelve Mo atoms at the corners of a cuboctahedron.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many P–O–P bridges are in P₄O₆?', a: 6, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'One per tetrahedron edge.', w: R`A tetrahedron has 6 edges, so 6 bridging O. $\mathrm{P_4O_{10}}$ adds one terminal $\mathrm{P{=}O}$ on each P.` },
        { q: R`Write the equation for $\mathrm{P_4O_{10}}$ with water.`, h: 'Phosphoric(V) acid forms.', w: R`$\mathrm{P_4O_{10} + 6H_2O \rightarrow 4H_3PO_4}$` },
        { q: 'How many edges does a cuboctahedron have?', a: 24, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: '12 vertices, each meeting 4 edges.', w: R`$12 \times 4 \div 2 = 24$` },
        { q: R`The tungsten analogue with silicon at the centre is $[\mathrm{SiW_{12}O_{40}}]^{n-}$, with W in the +6 state. Find n.`, a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Sum the oxidation states.', w: R`$4 + 12(6) - 40(2) = -4$, so $n = 4$ (the phosphorus version is $3-$, giving $\mathrm{(NH_4)_3PMo_{12}O_{40}}$).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk07-3', n: 3, t: 'Phosphate in blood', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`Phosphate in serum is precipitated as ammonium molybdophosphate, which is then titrated: 23 mol of NaOH react with each mole. $K_{a2}(\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}) = 6.2 \times 10^{-8}$; $K_{sp}(\mathrm{LaPO_4}) = 7.08 \times 10^{-27}$; $K_{sp}(\mathrm{AlPO_4}) = 9.84 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{mol^2\ dm^{-6}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`A 15.0 cm³ serum sample needs 21.85 cm³ of 0.0120 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. Find the phosphate concentration.`, a: 7.6e-4, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles NaOH ÷ 23.', w: R`$2.622 \times 10^{-4} \div 23 = 1.14 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\div 0.0150 = 7.60 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`At pH 7.6, what is the ratio $[\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}]/[\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}]$?`, a: 2.47, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Ka₂ ÷ [H⁺].', w: R`$6.2 \times 10^{-8} \div 2.51 \times 10^{-8} = 2.47$: $\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}$ dominates.` },
        { q: R`Lanthanum carbonate is a phosphate binder. Write its reaction with stomach acid, then find the concentration of a saturated $\mathrm{AlPO_4}$ solution.`, a: 9.92e-11, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 's = √Ksp.', w: R`$\mathrm{La_2(CO_3)_3 + 6HCl \rightarrow 2LaCl_3 + 3CO_2 + 3H_2O}$. $s = \sqrt{9.84 \times 10^{-21}} = 9.92 \times 10^{-11}$. $\mathrm{LaPO_4}$ ($s = 8.4 \times 10^{-14}$) is less soluble, so it precipitates first from a mixture.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk07-4', n: 4, t: 'A flame retardant', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-37-3', 'ca-1-2'],
      s: R`Phosphine reacts with methanal in acid to give a salt whose cation $\mathrm{X^+}$ is $\mathrm{[P(CH_2OH)_4]^+}$. Its mass spectrum shows $\mathrm{X^+}$, then repeated losses of 30 mass units. (C 12, H 1, O 16, P 31, D 2.)`,
      p: [
        { q: R`What is the m/z of $\mathrm{X^+}$?`, a: 155, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'C₄H₁₂O₄P⁺.', w: R`$48 + 12 + 64 + 31 = 155$` },
        { q: R`The sample is shaken with $\mathrm{D_2O}$. Where does the $\mathrm{X^+}$ peak move to?`, a: 159, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Only the O–H hydrogens exchange.', w: R`Four OH → OD: $155 + 4 = 159$` },
        { q: 'The ³¹P signal is split by the CH₂ protons. How many lines does it show?', a: 9, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'n + 1 rule.', w: 'P couples to 8 equivalent CH₂ protons: 9 lines. The lost fragment of mass 30 is CH₂O, and the ion at m/z 65 is [PH₃(CH₂OH)]⁺.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk07-5', n: 5, t: 'Tamiflu', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 6, lec: ['ca-13-4', 'ca-2-1'],
      s: R`Oseltamivir, $\mathrm{C_{16}H_{28}N_2O_4}$ ($M = 312.4$), has three chiral centres and is sold as its phosphate salt (one $\mathrm{H_3PO_4}$, $M = 98.0$, per molecule).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many stereoisomers are possible?', a: 8, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: '2ⁿ.', w: R`$2^3 = 8$` },
        { q: 'What mass of the phosphate salt delivers a 45 mg dose of oseltamivir?', a: 59.1, u: 'mg', tol: 0.005, h: 'Scale by 410.4/312.4.', w: R`$45 \times 410.4 \div 312.4 = 59.1\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk07-6', n: 6, t: 'Chlorine dioxide', f: 'Redox', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: R`Chlorine dioxide, $\mathrm{ClO_2}$, is a bent, odd-electron molecule used to bleach paper pulp and treat water.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the oxidation state of Cl in $\mathrm{ClO_2}$, $\mathrm{HClO_3}$ and $\mathrm{HClO_4}$, and write the disproportionation of $\mathrm{HClO_3}$ into $\mathrm{ClO_2}$ and $\mathrm{HClO_4}$.`, h: 'Balance by electron changes: +5 → +4 and +5 → +7.', w: R`+4, +5, +7. $\mathrm{3HClO_3 \rightarrow 2ClO_2 + HClO_4 + H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`Write equations for making $\mathrm{ClO_2}$ from $\mathrm{NaClO_3}$ with $\mathrm{SO_2}$ in sulfuric acid, and from $\mathrm{NaClO_2}$ with chlorine.`, h: 'SO₂ becomes sulfate.', w: R`$\mathrm{2NaClO_3 + SO_2 \rightarrow 2ClO_2 + Na_2SO_4}$; $\mathrm{2NaClO_2 + Cl_2 \rightarrow 2ClO_2 + 2NaCl}$` },
        { q: R`What mass of $\mathrm{NaClO_2}$ makes 10.0 kg of $\mathrm{ClO_2}$ by the chlorine route?`, a: 13.4, u: 'kg', tol: 0.01, h: '1 : 1 mole ratio.', w: R`$10.0 \div 67.45 \times 90.44 = 13.4\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2006, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Sherbet sweets · exhaust pollution · acyl chlorides and oxochlorides · heating copper sulfate · emergency oxygen · isotopes in halocarbons', skipped: 'The sildenafil synthesis (question 6) is drawn as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk06-1', n: 1, t: 'Sherbet sweets', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-5-2', 'ca-13-4'],
      s: R`A boiled sweet has a sucrose shell ($\mathrm{C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}}$, $\Delta_c H = -5644\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$) round a sherbet of sodium hydrogencarbonate and tartaric acid, $\mathrm{HOOC{-}CH(OH){-}CH(OH){-}COOH}$. 1 kJ = 0.239 dietary Calories; molar gas volume 24.0 dm³.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How much energy does one sweet containing 5.80 g of sucrose release?', a: 95.6, u: 'kJ', tol: 0.005, h: 'M(sucrose) = 342.3.', w: R`$5.80 \div 342.3 \times 5644 = 95.6\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'How many sweets would supply 2000 dietary Calories?', a: 87.5, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: '2000 ÷ 0.239 kJ.', w: R`$8368\ \mathrm{kJ} \div 95.6 = 87.5$, so about 88 sweets.` },
        { q: R`One sweet fizzes out 7.20 cm³ of $\mathrm{CO_2}$. What minimum mass of tartaric acid does it contain?`, a: 0.0225, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Tartaric acid is diprotic: 1 mol gives 2 CO₂ with NaHCO₃.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{CO_2}) = 3.00 \times 10^{-4}$; tartaric acid $1.50 \times 10^{-4} \times 150.09 = 0.0225\ \mathrm{g}$ (and $\mathrm{NaHCO_3}$ $0.0252\ \mathrm{g}$).` },
        { q: 'How many stereoisomers does tartaric acid have?', a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Two chiral centres, but one isomer has a mirror plane.', w: 'Three: a pair of enantiomers and the meso form. (Citric acid has no chiral centre.)' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk06-2', n: 2, t: 'Exhaust pollution', f: 'Redox', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-12-1'],
      s: 'Catalytic converters remove CO, NO and unburnt fuel. Diesel engines also inject urea, which hydrolyses to ammonia; the ammonia then reduces NO to nitrogen.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Write equations for: CO + NO; urea + water; NH₃ + NO.', h: 'N₂, CO₂ and H₂O are the products.', w: R`$\mathrm{2CO + 2NO \rightarrow 2CO_2 + N_2}$; $\mathrm{CO(NH_2)_2 + H_2O \rightarrow CO_2 + 2NH_3}$; $\mathrm{4NH_3 + 6NO \rightarrow 5N_2 + 6H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'What mass of urea is needed to remove 1.00 kg of NO?', a: 667, u: 'g', tol: 0.005, h: '1 urea → 2 NH₃, which remove 3 NO.', w: R`$1000 \div 30.01 = 33.3\ \mathrm{mol}$ NO; urea $= 11.1\ \mathrm{mol} \times 60.06 = 667\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk06-3', n: 3, t: 'Acyl chlorides and oxochlorides', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-4', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: R`Acyl chlorides, $\mathrm{POCl_3}$ and $\mathrm{SOCl_2}$ all react rapidly with water and alcohols, giving HCl.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write equations for ethanoyl chloride + water, and thionyl chloride + water.`, h: 'SO₂ is released.', w: R`$\mathrm{CH_3COCl + H_2O \rightarrow CH_3COOH + HCl}$; $\mathrm{SOCl_2 + H_2O \rightarrow SO_2 + 2HCl}$` },
        { q: R`Give the shapes of $\mathrm{POCl_3}$ and $\mathrm{SOCl_2}$ and the oxidation states of P and S.`, h: 'S has a lone pair.', w: R`$\mathrm{POCl_3}$: tetrahedral, P +5. $\mathrm{SOCl_2}$: trigonal pyramidal, S +4.` },
        { q: R`$\mathrm{POCl_3}$ and $\mathrm{H_3PO_4}$ give an acid B. Its sodium salt contains 34.6% Na and 23.3% P by mass (rest O). Find the number of P atoms per formula of the salt.`, a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Find the empirical formula.', w: R`Na : P : O $= 1.505 : 0.752 : 2.631 = 4 : 2 : 7$: $\mathrm{Na_4P_2O_7}$. So B is $\mathrm{H_4P_2O_7}$: $\mathrm{5H_3PO_4 + POCl_3 \rightarrow 3H_4P_2O_7 + 3HCl}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk06-4', n: 4, t: 'Heating copper sulfate', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`A 2.500 g sample of $\mathrm{CuSO_4{\cdot}5H_2O}$ is heated slowly. Its mass levels off at 2.139 g (A), 1.778 g (B), 1.598 g (C), 1.197 g (D), 0.796 g (E) and finally 0.716 g (F).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many water molecules per CuSO₄ remain in B?', a: 1, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Each H₂O lost is 0.1804 g here.', w: R`$n = 0.01001\ \mathrm{mol}$; $2.500 - 1.778 = 0.722\ \mathrm{g} = 4$ waters lost: B is $\mathrm{CuSO_4{\cdot}H_2O}$ (A is the trihydrate, C anhydrous).` },
        { q: 'Identify E and F, and write the equation for E → F.', h: 'Molar masses per Cu: 79.5 and 71.5.', w: R`E: CuO; F: $\mathrm{Cu_2O}$. $\mathrm{4CuO \rightarrow 2Cu_2O + O_2}$` },
        { q: 'D forms when half of the CuSO₄ has decomposed to CuO. What is its molar mass per two Cu atoms?', a: 239.2, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'D = CuO·CuSO₄.', w: R`$1.197 \div 0.01001 \times 2 = 239.2$: $\mathrm{Cu_2SO_5}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk06-5', n: 5, t: 'Emergency oxygen', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Aircraft oxygen masks run on candles of sodium chlorate; rebreathers use potassium superoxide, $\mathrm{KO_2}$, which also absorbs $\mathrm{CO_2}$. Molar gas volume 24.0 dm³.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`What mass of $\mathrm{NaClO_3}$ releases 72 dm³ of oxygen?`, a: 212.9, u: 'g', tol: 0.005, h: 'NaClO₃ → NaCl + 1½O₂.', w: R`$3.00\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{O_2}$ needs $2.00\ \mathrm{mol} \times 106.44 = 213\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`Give the oxidation state of O in $\mathrm{CO_2}$, $\mathrm{BaO_2}$ and $\mathrm{KO_2}$, and write the reaction of $\mathrm{KO_2}$ with water.`, h: 'Oxide, peroxide, superoxide.', w: R`−2, −1, −½. $\mathrm{4KO_2 + 2H_2O \rightarrow 4KOH + 3O_2}$, then $\mathrm{2KOH + CO_2 \rightarrow K_2CO_3 + H_2O}$.` },
        { q: R`What mass of $\mathrm{KO_2}$ releases the same 72 dm³ of oxygen?`, a: 284.4, u: 'g', tol: 0.005, h: '4 KO₂ give 3 O₂.', w: R`$4.00\ \mathrm{mol} \times 71.10 = 284\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk06-7', n: 7, t: 'Isotopes in halocarbons', f: 'Analysis', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-1-2', 'ca-37-3'],
      s: R`Chlorine is 75% ³⁵Cl and 25% ³⁷Cl; bromine is 50% ⁷⁹Br and 50% ⁸¹Br. Take C = 12, H = 1, D = 2, F = 19.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`$\mathrm{CCl_3F}$ gives molecular ion peaks at m/z 136, 138, 140 and 142. Give the intensity of the 138 peak relative to the 142 peak.`, a: 27, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Binomial (3 : 1)³.', w: R`Ratios 27 : 27 : 9 : 1, so 27.` },
        { q: R`Bromochloromethane, $\mathrm{CH_2BrCl}$: which peak is tallest, and what are the m/z values?`, h: 'Combine 1 : 1 (Br) with 3 : 1 (Cl).', w: 'm/z 128, 130, 132 in the ratio 3 : 4 : 1; 130 is the tallest.' },
        { q: R`A sample of $\mathrm{CH_2Cl_2}$ has half its hydrogen replaced by deuterium at random. Its molecular ions run from m/z 84 to 90. What is the intensity of m/z 86 as a percentage of the tallest peak?`, a: 83.3, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'H/D gives 1 : 2 : 1; Cl₂ gives 9 : 6 : 1. Combine them.', w: R`84–90: 9 : 18 : 15 : 12 : 7 : 2 : 1. Tallest is 85; $15 \div 18 = 83.3\%$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2005, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Carbon oxides · iodine pentoxide · ant stings · NMR of a molecular dancer · the breathalyser', skipped: 'The rimonabant synthesis (question 6) is drawn as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk05-1', n: 1, t: 'Carbon oxides', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-3-4', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: 'Unusual oxides of carbon are made by dehydrating acids: CO from methanoic acid, carbon suboxide C₃O₂ from propanedioic acid, and C₁₂O₉ from benzenehexacarboxylic acid.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the dehydration equations for methanoic acid and for propanedioic acid.', h: 'CH₂(COOH)₂ loses two waters.', w: R`$\mathrm{HCOOH \rightarrow CO + H_2O}$; $\mathrm{CH_2(COOH)_2 \rightarrow O{=}C{=}C{=}C{=}O + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'What mass of C₃O₂ can be made from 10.4 g of propanedioic acid?', a: 6.80, u: 'g', tol: 0.005, h: 'M = 104.06 and 68.03.', w: R`$10.4 \div 104.06 \times 68.03 = 6.80\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'Describe the bonding in CO.', h: 'Isoelectronic with N₂.', w: 'A triple bond (two shared pairs from C and O plus one dative pair from O), with a lone pair on each atom.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk05-2', n: 2, t: 'Iodine pentoxide', f: 'Redox', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{I_2O_5}$ oxidises carbon monoxide quantitatively; the iodine formed is titrated with thiosulfate ($\mathrm{I_2 + 2S_2O_3^{2-} \rightarrow 2I^- + S_4O_6^{2-}}$). Molar gas volume 24.0 dm³.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the equation for $\mathrm{I_2O_5}$ with CO, and give the oxidation state of iodine in $\mathrm{I_2O_5}$.`, h: 'CO₂ forms.', w: R`$\mathrm{I_2O_5 + 5CO \rightarrow I_2 + 5CO_2}$; iodine +5.` },
        { q: R`A 200 cm³ gas sample releases iodine needing 6.40 cm³ of 0.150 mol dm⁻³ thiosulfate. What percentage by volume is CO?`, a: 28.8, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: '1 I₂ ↔ 5 CO.', w: R`$9.60 \times 10^{-4} \div 2 = 4.80 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{I_2}$; CO $= 2.40 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol} = 57.6\ \mathrm{cm^3}$; $28.8\%$` },
        { q: R`A 2.000 g sample of a hydrate $\mathrm{HI_3O_8}$ loses 0.0353 g of water on heating to $\mathrm{I_2O_5}$. Write the equation, and show it fits.`, h: '2HI₃O₈ → 3I₂O₅ + H₂O.', w: R`$\mathrm{2HI_3O_8 \rightarrow 3I_2O_5 + H_2O}$: $18.02 \div (2 \times 509.7) = 1.77\%$; $2.000 \times 0.0177 = 0.0353\ \mathrm{g}$ ✓. The parent acid is $\mathrm{HIO_3}$: $\mathrm{I_2O_5 + H_2O \rightarrow 2HIO_3}$; and $\mathrm{I_2 + 5Cl_2 + 6H_2O \rightarrow 2HIO_3 + 10HCl}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk05-3', n: 3, t: 'Ant stings', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 16, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`An ant injects 75% of its venom, $5.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of a solution that is 60% methanoic acid by volume. Methanoic acid: density $1.22\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$, $M = 46.03$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many ants would be needed to collect 1.0 dm³ of pure methanoic acid?', a: 250000, u: 'ants', tol: 0.01, h: 'Total venom per ant = 5.0×10⁻³ ÷ 0.75.', w: R`$6.67 \times 10^{-3} \times 0.60 = 4.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ per ant; $1000 \div 4.0 \times 10^{-3} = 2.5 \times 10^5$ ants` },
        { q: 'What mass of NaHCO₃ neutralises one sting?', a: 6.68e-3, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles injected = volume × 0.60 × 1.22 ÷ 46.03.', w: R`$7.95 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol} \times 84.01 = 6.68 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'The acid spreads into 2.0 cm³ of body water and the pH there is 2.59. What percentage of the acid is ionised?', a: 6.47, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'c = 7.95×10⁻⁵ ÷ 0.0020.', w: R`$c = 0.0398\ \mathrm{mol\ dm^{-3}}$; $[\mathrm{H^+}] = 2.57 \times 10^{-3}$; $6.47\%$` },
        { q: 'Calculate Ka for methanoic acid.', a: 1.78e-4, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.02, h: 'Ka = [H⁺]² ÷ (c − [H⁺]).', w: R`$(2.57 \times 10^{-3})^2 \div 0.0372 = 1.78 \times 10^{-4}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk05-4', n: 4, t: 'NMR of a molecular dancer', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 8, lec: ['ca-37-3', 'ca-37-4'],
      s: 'NanoPutians are molecules built to look like stick figures; their NMR spectra are assigned using equivalence and the n + 1 rule (coupling within three bonds).',
      p: [
        { q: 'A CH proton is coupled to the six equivalent protons of two CH₃ groups. How many peaks does it show?', a: 7, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'n + 1.', w: 'Seven, in the ratio 1 : 6 : 15 : 20 : 15 : 6 : 1 (Pascal’s triangle row 6).' },
        { q: 'A tert-butyl group gives one ¹H signal. How many H does it integrate to, and how many ¹³C signals does the group give?', h: 'Quaternary C plus three equivalent CH₃.', w: '9 H, as a singlet. Two ¹³C signals: the quaternary carbon and the CH₃ carbons.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk05-5', n: 5, t: 'The breathalyser', f: 'Redox', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`Acidified dichromate oxidises ethanol in breath to ethanoic acid, turning from orange to green. Blood holds 2300 times as much alcohol as the same volume of breath. One driving limit is 50 mg of alcohol per 100 cm³ of blood.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the overall equation for dichromate oxidising ethanol to ethanoic acid.', h: 'Ethanol loses 4 e⁻; dichromate gains 6 e⁻.', w: R`$\mathrm{3C_2H_5OH + 2Cr_2O_7^{2-} + 16H^+ \rightarrow 3CH_3COOH + 4Cr^{3+} + 11H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'At the limit, how much ethanol is in an 800 cm³ breath sample, in μg?', a: 174, u: 'μg', tol: 0.01, h: '50 mg ÷ 2300 per 100 cm³ of breath.', w: R`$0.02174\ \mathrm{mg} \times 8 = 0.174\ \mathrm{mg} = 174\ \mu\mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`The tube must cope with four times the limit. What minimum mass of $\mathrm{K_2Cr_2O_7}$ must it contain, in mg?`, a: 2.96, u: 'mg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of ethanol × 2/3.', w: R`$0.696\ \mathrm{mg} \div 46.07 = 1.51 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times \tfrac{2}{3} \times 294.2 = 2.96\ \mathrm{mg}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2004, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Half-equations · acid rain and marble · dissolved oxygen · tin pest · X-rays from a supernova', skipped: 'The Agent Orange and asparagus questions (4 and 7) are drawn as structures, so they are not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk04-1', n: 1, t: 'Half-equations', f: 'Redox', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-6-1'],
      s: 'Write half-equations in acidic solution, and say whether each is an oxidation or a reduction.',
      p: [
        { q: R`(i) $\mathrm{Co^{3+} \rightarrow Co^{2+}}$ (ii) $\mathrm{VO^{2+} \rightarrow VO_2^+}$ (iii) $\mathrm{S_2O_3^{2-} \rightarrow S_4O_6^{2-}}$ (iv) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- \rightarrow MnO_2}$`, h: 'Balance O with H₂O, then H with H⁺, then charge with e⁻.', w: R`(i) $\mathrm{Co^{3+} + e^- \rightarrow Co^{2+}}$, reduction. (ii) $\mathrm{VO^{2+} + H_2O \rightarrow VO_2^+ + 2H^+ + e^-}$, oxidation. (iii) $\mathrm{2S_2O_3^{2-} \rightarrow S_4O_6^{2-} + 2e^-}$, oxidation. (iv) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + 4H^+ + 3e^- \rightarrow MnO_2 + 2H_2O}$, reduction.` },
        { q: R`(v) $\mathrm{NO_3^- \rightarrow NO}$ (vi) $\mathrm{H_2O_2 \rightarrow H_2O}$ (vii) $\mathrm{ClO_3^- \rightarrow Cl^-}$`, h: 'Count the change in oxidation state first.', w: R`(v) $\mathrm{NO_3^- + 4H^+ + 3e^- \rightarrow NO + 2H_2O}$ (vi) $\mathrm{H_2O_2 + 2H^+ + 2e^- \rightarrow 2H_2O}$ (vii) $\mathrm{ClO_3^- + 6H^+ + 6e^- \rightarrow Cl^- + 3H_2O}$: all reductions.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk04-2', n: 2, t: 'Acid rain and marble', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-12-1', 'ca-10-1'],
      s: 'Marble monuments (CaCO₃) are attacked by acid rain. Conservators soak the stone in barium hydroxide, which absorbs CO₂ to lay down barium carbonate.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Write equations for CaCO₃ with carbonic acid and with sulfuric acid, and for CO₂ with Ba(OH)₂.', h: 'Hydrogencarbonate is soluble.', w: R`$\mathrm{CaCO_3 + H_2CO_3 \rightarrow Ca(HCO_3)_2}$; $\mathrm{CaCO_3 + H_2SO_4 \rightarrow CaSO_4 + CO_2 + H_2O}$; $\mathrm{Ba(OH)_2 + CO_2 \rightarrow BaCO_3 + H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'Why does the barium treatment slow further damage? Include an equation.', h: 'Compare the solubilities of the sulfates.', w: R`$\mathrm{2BaCO_3 + 2SO_2 + O_2 \rightarrow 2BaSO_4 + 2CO_2}$: the $\mathrm{SO_2}$ is trapped as very insoluble $\mathrm{BaSO_4}$ (far less soluble than $\mathrm{CaSO_4}$), so less sulfuric acid reaches the marble.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk04-3', n: 3, t: 'Dissolved oxygen', f: 'Redox', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`In the Winkler method, dissolved $\mathrm{O_2}$ oxidises $\mathrm{Mn(OH)_2}$ to $\mathrm{Mn(OH)_3}$; acidified iodide then turns the Mn(III) back to Mn(II), releasing iodine, which is titrated with thiosulfate.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the equations for the first two steps.', h: 'O₂ takes 4 electrons.', w: R`$\mathrm{4Mn(OH)_2 + O_2 + 2H_2O \rightarrow 4Mn(OH)_3}$; $\mathrm{2Mn(OH)_3 + 2I^- + 6H^+ \rightarrow 2Mn^{2+} + I_2 + 6H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`A 50.0 cm³ water sample needs 17.6 cm³ of 0.00200 mol dm⁻³ thiosulfate. Find the dissolved oxygen in mg dm⁻³.`, a: 5.63, u: 'mg dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: '1 O₂ → 2 I₂ → 4 S₂O₃²⁻.', w: R`$3.52 \times 10^{-5} \div 4 = 8.80 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{O_2} = 0.282\ \mathrm{mg}$; $\div 0.0500 = 5.63\ \mathrm{mg\ dm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`Nitrite interferes, making NO from iodide. Write that equation.`, h: 'N goes +3 → +2.', w: R`$\mathrm{2NO_2^- + 2I^- + 4H^+ \rightarrow 2NO + I_2 + 2H_2O}$ (and NO is reoxidised by air, so the error grows).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk04-5', n: 5, t: 'Tin pest', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-2'],
      s: R`Metallic white tin slowly crumbles into powdery grey tin in the cold. For white → grey: $\Delta H = -2.09\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$; $S^\circ$: white 51.4, grey $44.1\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}\ mol^{-1}}$. Densities: white $7.31$, grey $5.75\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Calculate ΔG for white → grey at 250 K, in J mol⁻¹.', a: -265, u: 'J mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔS = −7.30 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', w: R`$-2090 - 250(-7.30) = -265\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}}$: spontaneous.` },
        { q: 'Above what temperature is white tin the stable form?', a: 286, u: 'K', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔG = 0.', w: R`$2090 \div 7.30 = 286\ \mathrm{K}$ (13 °C)` },
        { q: 'A 1.00 kg tin organ pipe turns completely to grey tin. By how many cm³ does its volume grow?', a: 37.1, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Volume = mass ÷ density.', w: R`$1000 \div 5.75 - 1000 \div 7.31 = 173.9 - 136.8 = 37.1\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ (+27%). The denser white tin has the higher coordination number.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk04-6', n: 6, t: 'X-rays from a supernova', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-1-3', 'ca-1-4'],
      s: R`For a one-electron atom, $E_n = -\dfrac{Z^2 R_H}{n^2}$ with $R_H = 2.179 \times 10^{-18}\ \mathrm{J}$; $h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\ \mathrm{J\ s}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What is the energy of the n = 3 level of hydrogen?', a: -2.42e-19, u: 'J', tol: 0.01, h: 'Divide by 9.', w: R`$-2.179 \times 10^{-18} \div 9 = -2.42 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$` },
        { q: 'What frequency of light excites hydrogen from n = 1 to n = 3?', a: 2.92e15, u: 'Hz', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔE = R_H(1 − 1/9).', w: R`$\Delta E = 1.937 \times 10^{-18}\ \mathrm{J}$; $f = \Delta E/h = 2.92 \times 10^{15}\ \mathrm{Hz}$` },
        { q: 'A supernova emits X-rays of 3.55 × 10¹⁷ Hz from the n = 2 → 1 transition of a one-electron ion. Find Z.', a: 12, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Z² = hf ÷ (0.75 R_H).', w: R`$hf = 2.352 \times 10^{-16}\ \mathrm{J}$; $Z^2 = 2.352 \times 10^{-16} \div 1.634 \times 10^{-18} = 144$; $Z = 12$: magnesium ($\mathrm{Mg^{11+}}$).` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'ukcho', comp: 'UK Chemistry Olympiad', round: 'Round 1', y: 2003, mins: 120,
    topics: 'Self-heating cans · Reinecke-type salts · atom economy · balancing redox · old combining weights · hydroxylamine · polonium', skipped: 'The flunitrazepam synthesis (question 7) is drawn as structures, so it is not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'uk03-1', n: 1, t: 'A self-heating can', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-5-1'],
      s: R`A self-heating can warms coffee by adding water to calcium oxide. $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$): $\mathrm{CaO}$ $-634.9$; $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ $-285.8$; $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$ $-986.1$. Coffee: $c = 4.18\ \mathrm{J\ g^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Calculate ΔH for $\mathrm{CaO + H_2O \rightarrow Ca(OH)_2}$.`, a: -65.4, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Products minus reactants.', w: R`$-986.1 - (-634.9 - 285.8) = -65.4\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'What minimum mass of CaO heats 180 g of coffee by 45 °C?', a: 29.0, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'q = mcΔT.', w: R`$q = 4.18 \times 180 \times 45 = 33.9\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $33.9 \div 65.4 = 0.518\ \mathrm{mol} \times 56.08 = 29.0\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk03-2', n: 2, t: 'A Reinecke-type salt', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-28-1', 'ca-28-3'],
      s: R`The salt $\mathrm{K[Cr(SCN)_x(NH_3)_y]}$ has an octahedral chromium ($x + y = 6$) and contains 14.5% Cr by mass. (SCN = 58.08, NH₃ = 17.03, K = 39.10, Cr = 52.00.)`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find x.', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'M = 52.00 ÷ 0.145; express M in terms of x.', w: R`$M = 357$; $39.10 + 52.00 + 58.08x + 17.03(6 - x) = 357$ gives $x = 4$, $y = 2$` },
        { q: 'What is the oxidation state of Cr, and how many geometric isomers are there?', h: 'Two NH₃ on an octahedron.', w: 'Cr(III): 4(−1) + 3 = −1 overall. Two isomers: cis and trans, with the two NH₃ adjacent or opposite.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk03-3', n: 3, t: 'Atom economy', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-2-4'],
      s: 'Atom economy = mass of the wanted product ÷ total mass of reactants × 100%.',
      p: [
        { q: R`The old chlorohydrin route to epoxypropane is $\mathrm{C_3H_6 + Cl_2 + Ca(OH)_2 \rightarrow C_3H_6O + CaCl_2 + H_2O}$. What is its atom economy?`, a: 31.0, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'M: 42.08, 70.90, 74.09; product 58.08.', w: R`$58.08 \div 187.07 = 31.0\%$` },
        { q: R`Maleic anhydride, $\mathrm{C_4H_2O_3}$, can be made from butane: $\mathrm{C_4H_{10} + 3\tfrac{1}{2}O_2 \rightarrow C_4H_2O_3 + 4H_2O}$. Atom economy?`, a: 57.6, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'M(product) = 98.06.', w: R`$98.06 \div (58.12 + 112.0) = 57.6\%$` },
        { q: R`The older route uses benzene: $\mathrm{C_6H_6 + 4\tfrac{1}{2}O_2 \rightarrow C_4H_2O_3 + 2CO_2 + 2H_2O}$. Atom economy?`, a: 44.1, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Benzene 78.11.', w: R`$98.06 \div 222.1 = 44.1\%$: the butane route wastes less (and avoids benzene). An addition reaction has 100% atom economy.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk03-4', n: 4, t: 'Balancing redox equations', f: 'Redox', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-6-1'],
      s: 'Balance these ionic equations in acidic solution.',
      p: [
        { q: R`(i) $\mathrm{Fe + Ag^+}$ (ii) $\mathrm{Cl_2 + I^-}$ (iii) $\mathrm{Al + H^+}$ (iv) $\mathrm{PbO_2 + H^+ + Cl^-}$ (gives $\mathrm{Pb^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Cl_2}$)`, h: 'Electrons lost = electrons gained.', w: R`(i) $\mathrm{Fe + 2Ag^+ \rightarrow Fe^{2+} + 2Ag}$ (ii) $\mathrm{Cl_2 + 2I^- \rightarrow 2Cl^- + I_2}$ (iii) $\mathrm{2Al + 6H^+ \rightarrow 2Al^{3+} + 3H_2}$ (iv) $\mathrm{PbO_2 + 4H^+ + 2Cl^- \rightarrow Pb^{2+} + Cl_2 + 2H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`(v) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + H_2C_2O_4}$ (vi) $\mathrm{Cr_2O_7^{2-} + Fe^{2+}}$ (vii) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + Fe^{2+} + C_2O_4^{2-}}$ (all Fe → Fe³⁺, all C → CO₂)`, h: 'In (vii), FeC₂O₄ loses 3 electrons in total.', w: R`(v) $\mathrm{2MnO_4^- + 5H_2C_2O_4 + 6H^+ \rightarrow 2Mn^{2+} + 10CO_2 + 8H_2O}$ (vi) $\mathrm{Cr_2O_7^{2-} + 6Fe^{2+} + 14H^+ \rightarrow 2Cr^{3+} + 6Fe^{3+} + 7H_2O}$ (vii) $\mathrm{3MnO_4^- + 5Fe^{2+} + 5C_2O_4^{2-} + 24H^+ \rightarrow 3Mn^{2+} + 5Fe^{3+} + 10CO_2 + 12H_2O}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk03-5', n: 5, t: 'Old combining weights', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-2-3'],
      s: 'Early chemists weighed in grains. Use modern atomic masses: Sn 118.71, Zn 65.38, O 16.00.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Heating 15 grains of tin gives 19.04 grains of oxide. What is the ratio O : Sn in the oxide?', a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Moles of each.', w: R`Sn $0.1264$, O $4.04 \div 16 = 0.2525$: ratio 2.00, so $\mathrm{SnO_2}$` },
        { q: '50 grains of zinc are heated in air and the solid then weighs 58 grains. How many grains of zinc are left unreacted?', a: 17.3, u: 'grains', tol: 0.01, h: 'The gain is oxygen.', w: R`8 grains O $= 0.5$ "grain-moles"; Zn reacted $= 0.5 \times 65.38 = 32.7$; unreacted $= 17.3$ grains (and 40.7 grains of ZnO formed).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk03-6', n: 6, t: 'Hydroxylamine and iron(III)', f: 'Redox', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`1.500 g of hydroxylammonium chloride, $\mathrm{NH_3OH^+Cl^-}$ ($M = 69.49$), is made up to 250 cm³. A 20.0 cm³ portion is boiled with excess iron(III); the iron(II) formed needs 27.6 cm³ of 0.0250 mol dm⁻³ $\mathrm{KMnO_4}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`How many moles of $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ form per mole of $\mathrm{NH_3OH^+}$?`, a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'MnO₄⁻ takes 5 Fe²⁺.', w: R`$\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} = 5 \times 6.90 \times 10^{-4} = 3.45 \times 10^{-3}$; $\mathrm{NH_3OH^+} = 1.727 \times 10^{-3}$; ratio 2.00` },
        { q: 'What is the oxidation state of N in the nitrogen product, and what is the product?', a: 1, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'N starts at −1 and loses 2 electrons.', w: R`$-1 + 2 = +1$: $\mathrm{N_2O}$. $\mathrm{2NH_3OH^+ + 4Fe^{3+} \rightarrow N_2O + 4Fe^{2+} + H_2O + 6H^+}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'uk03-8', n: 8, t: 'Polonium', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-3-6'],
      s: R`Polonium-210 is an α-emitter with a half-life of 138 days; fresh $^{210}\mathrm{Po}$ gives out $141\ \mathrm{W\ g^{-1}}$. Solid polonium is simple cubic, density $9.20\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What does ²¹⁰Po decay to, and what power does 1 g give after 200 days?', a: 51.6, u: 'W', tol: 0.01, h: 'P = P₀(½)^(t/t½).', w: R`$^{210}_{84}\mathrm{Po} \rightarrow {}^{206}_{82}\mathrm{Pb} + {}^4_2\alpha$. $141 \times 0.5^{200/138} = 51.6\ \mathrm{W}$` },
        { q: 'A space power source loses 5.0% of its output in 6.0 years. What half-life does its isotope have?', a: 81.1, u: 'years', tol: 0.01, h: 't½ = t ln2 ÷ ln(P₀/P).', w: R`$6.0 \times 0.693 \div \ln(1/0.95) = 81.1$ years (Po-210 would be useless here).` },
        { q: 'Estimate the radius of a polonium atom, in pm.', a: 168, u: 'pm', tol: 0.01, h: 'One atom per simple cubic cell; touching along the edge.', w: R`$a^3 = 209.98 \div (9.20 \times 6.022 \times 10^{23}) = 3.79 \times 10^{-23}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$; $a = 336\ \mathrm{pm}$; $r = a/2 = 168\ \mathrm{pm}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'c3l6', comp: 'Cambridge Chemistry Challenge (C3L6)', round: 'Lower Sixth', y: 2024, mins: 90,
    topics: 'Arsenic and its oxides · a copper arsenite pigment · “forever chemicals”: PTFE, C–F bonds and oxygen-carrying fluorocarbons', skipped: 'The parts that need a printed graph or a drawing grid (polarizability, the helix path) are left out.', qs: [
    { id: 'c3l624-1', n: 1, t: 'Arsenic', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 40, lec: ['ca-1-4', 'ca-6-1', 'ca-2-3', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: R`Arsenic (Group 15) is found in minerals such as arsenopyrite, FeAsS. In the gas phase it forms $\mathrm{As_2}$ and tetrahedral $\mathrm{As_4}$. Its oxide exists as molecules of $M_r = 395.68$, which split above 800 °C into molecules of half that mass.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the electron configuration of As and its highest and lowest likely oxidation states, with a binary compound for each.', h: 'Five outer electrons.', w: R`$\mathrm{[Ar]\,3d^{10}4s^24p^3}$. Highest +5, e.g. $\mathrm{AsF_5}$ or $\mathrm{As_2O_5}$; lowest −3, e.g. $\mathrm{Na_3As}$ or $\mathrm{GaAs}$ (with a Group 1, 2 or 13 metal).` },
        { q: R`The oxide with $M_r = 395.68$ has every As–O bond equivalent. How many As–O bonds does each molecule have?`, a: 12, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'It is As₄O₆: an O bridges each edge of the As₄ tetrahedron.', w: R`$\mathrm{As_4O_6}$: 6 bridging O, each with 2 bonds = 12. Above 800 °C it gives $\mathrm{As_2O_3}$ ($M_r = 197.84$), $\mathrm{O{=}As{-}O{-}As{=}O}$.` },
        { q: R`In FeAsS, As and S have the same oxidation state. Give it, and write equations for heating FeAsS with no air (gives As), in limited air (FeAsS : O₂ = 1 : 0.75) and in excess air (1 : 2.5).`, h: 'Fe is +2.', w: R`As and S are −1. $\mathrm{FeAsS \rightarrow FeS + As}$; $\mathrm{4FeAsS + 3O_2 \rightarrow 4FeS + 2As_2O_3}$; $\mathrm{2FeAsS + 5O_2 \rightarrow Fe_2O_3 + As_2O_3 + 2SO_2}$` },
        { q: R`Balance the oxidation of $\mathrm{As_2O_3}$ to $\mathrm{H_3AsO_4}$ by concentrated nitric acid, which gives NO. How many mol of $\mathrm{HNO_3}$ per mol of $\mathrm{As_2O_3}$?`, a: 1.333, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'As loses 2 e⁻ each; N gains 3.', w: R`$\mathrm{3As_2O_3 + 4HNO_3 + 7H_2O \rightarrow 6H_3AsO_4 + 4NO}$: $4/3 = 1.33$` },
        { q: R`A 0.750 g sample of a copper arsenite pigment decomposes to 0.0360 g $\mathrm{H_2O}$, 0.396 g $\mathrm{As_2O_3}$ and black CuO. What mass of CuO forms?`, a: 0.318, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mass is conserved.', w: R`$0.750 - 0.0360 - 0.396 = 0.318\ \mathrm{g}$. Moles: $\mathrm{H_2O}$ $2.00 \times 10^{-3}$, As $4.00 \times 10^{-3}$, Cu $4.00 \times 10^{-3}$: the pigment is $\mathrm{CuHAsO_3}$ (Cu +2, As +3).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'c3l624-2', n: 2, t: 'Forever chemicals', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 40, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-3-4', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Perfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) have every H replaced by F. The monomer of PTFE, $\mathrm{C_2F_4}$, is made by heating chlorodifluoromethane, $\mathrm{CHClF_2}$, which loses HCl to give difluorocarbene, $\mathrm{CF_2}$, which dimerises.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Write the overall equation for making $\mathrm{C_2F_4}$, and the equation for $\mathrm{C_2F_4}$ decomposing explosively to carbon and $\mathrm{CF_4}$. What happens to carbon in the second reaction?`, h: 'Carbon starts at +2.', w: R`$\mathrm{2CHClF_2 \rightarrow C_2F_4 + 2HCl}$; $\mathrm{C_2F_4 \rightarrow C + CF_4}$: carbon disproportionates (+2 → 0 and +4).` },
        { q: R`The relative density of PTFE follows $d = 2.612 - 0.058\log_{10}M$. Find $d$ for $M = 5.0 \times 10^6$.`, a: 2.223, u: '', tol: 0.002, h: 'log₁₀(5×10⁶) = 6.699.', w: R`$2.612 - 0.058 \times 6.699 = 2.223$` },
        { q: 'About how many carbon atoms are in one such chain?', a: 1.0e5, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Each CF₂ unit (50.01) holds one carbon.', w: R`$5.0 \times 10^6 \div 50.01 = 1.0 \times 10^5$ carbon atoms` },
        { q: 'What M gives a relative density of 2.150?', a: 9.24e7, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'log₁₀M = (2.612 − 2.150) ÷ 0.058.', w: R`$\log_{10}M = 7.966$; $M = 9.2 \times 10^7$` },
        { q: R`$\Delta_f H(\mathrm{C_2F_6, g}) = -1344.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$; $\Delta_{at}H(\mathrm{C}) = 716.7$; $E(\mathrm{F{-}F}) = 157.0$; $E(\mathrm{C{-}C}) = 325$. Find the average C–F bond enthalpy.`, a: 487, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Atomise the elements, then form 1 C–C and 6 C–F.', w: R`$6E(\mathrm{C{-}F}) = 2(716.7) + 3(157.0) + 1344.0 - 325 = 2923.4$; $E = 487\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`Perfluorodecalin, $\mathrm{C_{10}F_{18}}$ ($M = 462.08$, density $1.917\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$), dissolves $5.03 \times 10^{-3}$ mol $\mathrm{O_2}$ per mol. What volume of $\mathrm{O_2}$ (at 24 dm³ mol⁻¹) does 1 cm³ dissolve?`, a: 0.501, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of solvent in 1 cm³ first.', w: R`$1.917 \div 462.08 = 4.149 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 5.03 \times 10^{-3} \times 24\,000 = 0.501\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
        { q: R`A patient has 4.5 dm³ of blood carrying 19.5 cm³ of $\mathrm{O_2}$ per 100 cm³. What volume of perfluorodecalin carries the same oxygen?`, a: 1752, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Total O₂ ÷ 0.501.', w: R`$4500 \times 0.195 = 877.5\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ $\mathrm{O_2}$; $\div 0.501 = 1.75 \times 10^3\ \mathrm{cm^3}$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2026, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Identifying a liquid · nickel complexes · hydrogen storage in LaNi₅ · hydrolysis kinetics · net equations · a gold–silver–copper alloy · boranes · HBr addition', qs: [
    { id: 'us26-1', n: 1, t: 'An unknown liquid', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-4-1', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`Compound X contains only C, H and O. It melts at 11.8 °C and boils at 101.1 °C. $R = 0.08314\ \mathrm{L\ bar\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$; 1 atm = 1.01325 bar = 760 mmHg.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Burning 1.50 g of X gives 3.00 g $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and 1.227 g $\mathrm{H_2O}$. What is the empirical formula mass?`, a: 44.05, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Find C and H, then O by difference.', w: R`C $0.06817$, H $0.1362$, O $(1.50 - 0.8188 - 0.1373) \div 16.00 = 0.0340$ mol: ratio 2 : 4 : 1, $\mathrm{C_2H_4O}$ = 44.05` },
        { q: R`3.894 g of X just evaporates completely in a 3.00 L flask at 75.0 °C, where the pressure is 319.9 mmHg. Find its molar mass.`, a: 88.1, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'n = PV/RT in bar.', w: R`$P = 0.4265\ \mathrm{bar}$; $n = 0.4265 \times 3.00 \div (0.08314 \times 348.15) = 0.04420$; $M = 88.1$: $\mathrm{C_4H_8O_2}$` },
        { q: 'Taking 319.9 mmHg as the vapour pressure at 75.0 °C, find ΔH°vap.', a: 35.9, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Clausius–Clapeyron with the normal boiling point.', w: R`$\ln(760/319.9) = \dfrac{\Delta H}{8.314}\left(\dfrac{1}{348.15} - \dfrac{1}{374.25}\right)$, so $\Delta H = 35.9\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$ and $\Delta S = \Delta H/T_b = 96.0\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$. A molar mass of 88 with no O–H (a C₃ alcohol of mass 60 boils at 97 °C) points to dispersion and dipole forces only.` },
        { q: R`Solid X has density $1.211\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$ and a unit cell of $483.0\ \text{Å}^3$. How many molecules are in a unit cell?`, a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.05, h: '1 Å³ = 10⁻²⁴ cm³.', w: R`$1.211 \times 4.830 \times 10^{-22} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} \div 88.11 = 4.00$. The solid is denser than the liquid, so pressure raises the melting point.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us26-2', n: 2, t: 'Nickel complexes', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-28-1', 'ca-28-3', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{[Ni(H_2O)_6]^{2+}}$ is green, $\mathrm{[Ni(NH_3)_6]^{2+}}$ ($K_f = 2.0 \times 10^8$) is violet and square-planar $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}}$ is yellow. $K_{sp}(\mathrm{Ni(OH)_2}) = 2.0 \times 10^{-15}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many unpaired electrons does each complex have, and why do the colours change?', h: 'd⁸; square planar pairs them all.', w: 'Aqua 2, ammine 2, cyanide 0. The ligand field grows H₂O < NH₃ < CN⁻, so the absorbed light moves to higher energy (red → violet) and the colour seen shifts.' },
        { q: R`A solution is 0.0050 M in $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$. At what pH does $\mathrm{Ni(OH)_2}$ begin to precipitate?`, a: 7.80, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: '[OH⁻] = √(Ksp/[Ni²⁺]).', w: R`$[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{4.0 \times 10^{-13}} = 6.3 \times 10^{-7}$; pOH 6.20; pH 7.80` },
        { q: R`0.500 mol of $\mathrm{NH_3}$ is added to 1.00 L of 0.0050 M $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$. Find the free $[\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}]$ (ignore $\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ formation).`, a: 2.32e-9, u: 'M', tol: 0.02, h: 'Six NH₃ are bound per Ni.', w: R`Free $\mathrm{NH_3} = 0.500 - 0.030 = 0.470\ \mathrm{M}$; $[\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}] = 0.0050 \div (2.0 \times 10^8 \times 0.470^6) = 2.3 \times 10^{-9}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us26-3', n: 3, t: 'Hydrogen storage in LaNi₅', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-2'],
      s: R`LaNi₅ absorbs hydrogen. Data: $\Delta_f H$ ($\mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$) / $S^\circ$ ($\mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$): $\mathrm{H_2}$ 0 / 130.7; Ni 0 / 29.9; La 0 / 56.9; $\mathrm{LaNi_5}$ $-162$ / 217; α-$\mathrm{LaNi_5H_{0.43}}$ $-186$ / 223. $M(\mathrm{LaNi_5}) = 432.35$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Estimate $\Delta_f G$ of $\mathrm{LaNi_5}$ at 350 K.`, a: -165.7, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.002, h: 'ΔS_f = 217 − 56.9 − 5(29.9).', w: R`$\Delta S_f = 10.6\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $-162 - 350 \times 0.0106 = -165.7\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`An α-phase sample is 0.085% hydrogen by mass. Find x in $\mathrm{LaNi_5H_x}$.`, a: 0.365, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Per 100 g.', w: R`$0.08433\ \mathrm{mol\ H} \div (99.915 \div 432.35) = 0.365$` },
        { q: R`For $\mathrm{α\text{-}LaNi_5H_{0.43} + 2.98H_2 \rightarrow β\text{-}LaNi_5H_{6.39}}$, the equilibrium $\mathrm{H_2}$ pressure is 2.28 bar at 20.0 °C and 4.12 bar at 40.0 °C. Find ΔH° of the reaction.`, a: -67.3, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG° = 2.98RT ln(P/1 bar) at each T; then ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 5.99\ \mathrm{kJ}$ (293 K) and $10.98\ \mathrm{kJ}$ (313 K); $\Delta S^\circ = -250\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $\Delta H^\circ = -67.3\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: R`Hence find $\Delta_f H$ of β-$\mathrm{LaNi_5H_{6.39}}$.`, a: -253, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Add to the α value.', w: R`$-186 - 67.3 = -253\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$; $S^\circ = 223 + 2.98(130.7) - 250 = 362\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us26-4', n: 4, t: 'Hydrolysis of iodomethane', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-8-2'],
      s: R`Iodomethane hydrolyses by a water pathway (first order, $k_1$) and a hydroxide pathway ($k_2[\mathrm{OH^-}]$), so $k_{obs} = k_1 + k_2[\mathrm{OH^-}]$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`In neutral water at 89.9 °C, 0.0400 M $\mathrm{CH_3I}$ gives $[\mathrm{I^-}] = 0.0216\ \mathrm{M}$ after 1.20 h. Find $k_1$.`, a: 1.80e-4, u: 's⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: '[CH₃I] = 0.0400 − 0.0216.', w: R`$k_1 = \ln(0.0400/0.0184) \div 4320 = 1.80 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'What is [I⁻] after 2.50 h?', a: 0.0321, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'First-order decay.', w: R`$0.0400(1 - e^{-1.80 \times 10^{-4} \times 9000}) = 0.0321\ \mathrm{M}$` },
        { q: R`At 333 K, $k_1 = 8.7 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $k_{obs} = 1.10 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at pH 12.50. Find $k_2$.`, a: 3.2e-3, u: 'M⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: '[OH⁻] = 10^(−1.50).', w: R`$(1.10 \times 10^{-4} - 8.7 \times 10^{-6}) \div 0.0316 = 3.2 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{M^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. Ethyl iodide reacts more slowly by this S_N2 route because the extra methyl crowds the five-coordinate transition state.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us26-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-28-1', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) $\mathrm{H_2S}$ bubbled into lead(II) nitrate (b) $\mathrm{PCl_5}$ with a little water (c) copper(II) nitrate in concentrated HCl`, h: 'Precipitate; partial hydrolysis; a tetrahedral complex.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Pb^{2+} + H_2S \rightarrow PbS + 2H^+}$ (b) $\mathrm{PCl_5 + H_2O \rightarrow POCl_3 + 2HCl}$ (c) $\mathrm{Cu^{2+} + 4Cl^- \rightarrow [CuCl_4]^{2-}}$ (yellow-green)` },
        { q: R`(d) acidified $\mathrm{KMnO_4}$ with oxalic acid (e) methylbenzene with $\mathrm{Br_2}$ and $\mathrm{FeBr_3}$ (f) cyclohexanone with hydroxylamine`, h: 'CH₃ directs ortho/para.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + H_2C_2O_4 + H^+ \rightarrow Mn^{2+} + CO_2 + H_2O}$ (e) 2- and 4-bromomethylbenzene + HBr (f) cyclohexanone oxime, $\mathrm{C_6H_{10}{=}NOH}$, + $\mathrm{H_2O}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us26-6', n: 6, t: 'A coinage-metal alloy', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 14, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-24-1', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`A 0.1200 g alloy of Cu, Ag and Au is treated with nitric acid; 0.0300 g of gold is left. The solution is electrolysed at a constant 15.0 mA: silver plates out first (end point at 3220 s), then copper (end point at 10 510 s). $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What is the percentage of silver in the alloy?', a: 45.0, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Charge = It; 1 e⁻ per Ag.', w: R`$48.3\ \mathrm{C} \div 96\,500 = 5.006 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 107.87 = 0.0540\ \mathrm{g} = 45.0\%$` },
        { q: 'What is the percentage of copper?', a: 30.0, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '2 e⁻ per Cu, over 7290 s.', w: R`$0.0150 \times 7290 = 109.4\ \mathrm{C}$; $\div 2F = 5.67 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} \times 63.55 = 0.0360\ \mathrm{g} = 30.0\%$ (gold 25.0%)` },
        { q: R`Gold dissolves in bromide-containing oxidising mixtures. Given $E^\circ(\mathrm{Au^{3+}/Au}) = +1.52\ \mathrm{V}$ and $E^\circ(\mathrm{AuBr_4^-/Au}) = +0.854\ \mathrm{V}$, find $K_f$ of $\mathrm{AuBr_4^-}$ at 298 K.`, a: 6.3e33, u: '', tol: 0.05, h: 'ΔG° = −3F(1.52 − 0.854) = −RT ln Kf.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -192.8\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K_f = e^{77.8} = 6.3 \times 10^{33}$. Complexing lowers the potential of gold, which is why it dissolves.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us26-7', n: 7, t: 'Boranes', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-1-4'],
      s: R`Boron has a low-lying excited state ($\mathrm{1s^22s^12p^2}$) 3.55 eV above its ground state. $h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\ \mathrm{J\ s}$; $c = 2.998 \times 10^8\ \mathrm{m\ s^{-1}}$; 1 eV = $1.602 \times 10^{-19}$ J.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What wavelength of light corresponds to this excitation, in nm?', a: 349, u: 'nm', tol: 0.005, h: 'λ = hc/E.', w: R`$6.626 \times 10^{-34} \times 2.998 \times 10^8 \div 5.687 \times 10^{-19} = 349\ \mathrm{nm}$. Ground B has 1 unpaired electron; the excited state has 3, so B can form three bonds.` },
        { q: R`Describe the shapes and bonding of $\mathrm{BH_3}$, $\mathrm{B_2H_6}$ and $\mathrm{BH_4^-}$.`, h: 'Three-centre two-electron bonds.', w: R`$\mathrm{BH_3}$: trigonal planar, three 2c–2e bonds. $\mathrm{B_2H_6}$: two $\mathrm{BH_4}$ tetrahedra sharing an edge, with two B–H–B 3c–2e bridges. $\mathrm{BH_4^-}$: tetrahedral, formal charge −1 on B.` },
        { q: R`How many isomers does the icosahedral carborane $\mathrm{C_2B_{10}H_{12}}$ have?`, a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Place two C on an icosahedron.', w: 'Three: the carbons are adjacent (ortho), one apart (meta) or opposite (para).' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us26-8', n: 8, t: 'HBr addition', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-14-2', 'ca-13-4'],
      s: 'HBr adds to (E)-hex-2-ene through a carbocation.',
      p: [
        { q: 'How many stereoisomeric bromoalkane products can form?', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Br on C2 or C3, each chiral, each racemic.', w: '2-bromohexane (R and S) and 3-bromohexane (R and S): four. The 2- and 3-bromo compounds are constitutional isomers; each R/S pair are enantiomers.' },
        { q: '(E)-2-pentene melts 40 °C higher than (Z)-2-pentene, but they boil at the same temperature. Why? Then name a chiral C₆H₁₂ alkene.', h: 'Packing versus surface area.', w: 'The straighter E isomer packs better in the crystal; in the liquid both have similar surface area and dispersion forces. 3-Methylpent-1-ene is chiral.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2025, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Copper hydrates · calcium oxalate solubility · acid–base calorimetry · a Zn–Cd cell · net equations · oxygen species · perovskites · halogenation', qs: [
    { id: 'us25-1', n: 1, t: 'Copper hydrates', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 1, m: 12, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Blue $\mathrm{CuSO_4{\cdot}5H_2O}$ ($M = 249.70$) loses its water on heating to white $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$ ($M = 159.62$). Copper(II) oxidises iodide: the iodine formed is titrated with thiosulfate.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`What mass of anhydrous $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$ comes from 7.500 g of the pentahydrate?`, a: 4.794, u: 'g', tol: 0.002, h: 'Same moles.', w: R`$7.500 \div 249.70 \times 159.62 = 4.794\ \mathrm{g}$. Sulfate is a weaker-field ligand than water, so the absorption moves into the infrared and the solid looks white.` },
        { q: R`Write the net equation for $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ with excess iodide.`, h: 'Cu(I) iodide precipitates.', w: R`$\mathrm{2Cu^{2+} + 5I^- \rightarrow 2CuI + I_3^-}$; then $\mathrm{I_3^- + 2S_2O_3^{2-} \rightarrow 3I^- + S_4O_6^{2-}}$, so 1 Cu ↔ 1 thiosulfate.` },
        { q: R`0.1500 g of $\mathrm{Cu(NO_3)_2{\cdot}nH_2O}$ needs 21.50 mL of 0.0300 M thiosulfate. Find n.`, a: 2.50, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'M(Cu(NO₃)₂) = 187.57.', w: R`$6.45 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol}$ Cu → 0.1210 g anhydrous; water $0.0290\ \mathrm{g} = 1.61 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $n = 2.50$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us25-2', n: 2, t: 'Calcium oxalate', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1'],
      s: R`$K_{sp}(\mathrm{CaC_2O_4}) = 2.7 \times 10^{-9}$. Oxalic acid: $\mathrm{p}K_{a1} = 1.27$, $\mathrm{p}K_{a2} = 4.28$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the molar solubility of CaC₂O₄ in 0.0250 M CaCl₂.', a: 1.08e-7, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 's = Ksp/[Ca²⁺].', w: R`$2.7 \times 10^{-9} \div 0.0250 = 1.1 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{M}$ (in pure water: $\sqrt{K_{sp}} = 5.2 \times 10^{-5}$)` },
        { q: R`HCl gas is passed into 1.00 L of water over 0.0500 mol of $\mathrm{CaC_2O_4}$ until it all just dissolves. What is the pH?`, a: -0.20, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: '[C₂O₄²⁻] = Ksp/0.0500; total oxalate = [C₂O₄²⁻](1 + [H⁺]/Ka₂ + [H⁺]²/Ka₁Ka₂).', w: R`$[\mathrm{C_2O_4^{2-}}] = 5.4 \times 10^{-8}$; solving gives $[\mathrm{H^+}] = 1.59\ \mathrm{M}$, pH −0.20` },
        { q: 'How many moles of HCl were added?', a: 1.69, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'Free H⁺ plus the H⁺ now held by H₂C₂O₄ and HC₂O₄⁻.', w: R`$1.59 + 2(0.0484) + 0.0016 = 1.69\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us25-3', n: 3, t: 'Acid–base calorimetry', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-25-1', 'ca-5-1'],
      s: R`N,N-dimethylethanolamine (DMEA; $\mathrm{p}K_a$ of DMEAH⁺ = 9.22) reacts with propanoic acid ($\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.87$). 7.41 g of propanoic acid (0.1000 mol) in 100.0 g of water is titrated with DMEA in a Dewar; at the end point (8.91 g DMEA) the temperature has risen by 10.40 °C. $c = 4.184\ \mathrm{J\ g^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find ΔG° at 298 K for acid + DMEA → ions.', a: -24.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'K = 10^(9.22 − 4.87).', w: R`$K = 2.24 \times 10^4$; $\Delta G^\circ = -RT\ln K = -24.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔH°.', a: -50.6, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Total mass 116.32 g.', w: R`$q = 116.32 \times 4.184 \times 10.40 = 5061\ \mathrm{J}$; $\div 0.1000 = 50.6\ \mathrm{kJ}$, so $\Delta H^\circ = -50.6$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔS°.', a: -86.6, u: 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: '(ΔH − ΔG)/T.', w: R`$(-50\,615 + 24\,816) \div 298 = -86.6$: two neutral molecules become two ions that order the water around them.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us25-4', n: 4, t: 'A zinc–cadmium cell', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`A cell is built from Zn and Cd electrodes in 1.00 M $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ and 1.00 M $\mathrm{Cd^{2+}}$. Its voltage follows $E = 0.4033 - 1.487 \times 10^{-4}\,T$ (V, T in K). $E^\circ(\mathrm{Zn^{2+}/Zn}) = -0.762\ \mathrm{V}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $E^\circ(\mathrm{Cd^{2+}/Cd})$ at 298 K.`, a: -0.403, u: 'V', abs: 0.002, h: 'Zn is the anode.', w: R`$E_{cell} = 0.3590\ \mathrm{V}$; $E^\circ = 0.359 - 0.762 = -0.403\ \mathrm{V}$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔH° for Zn + Cd²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cd.', a: -77.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG = ΔH − TΔS matches E = a + bT: ΔH = −2Fa.', w: R`$\Delta H^\circ = -2 \times 96\,500 \times 0.4033 = -77.8\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $\Delta G^\circ = -69.3\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔS°.', a: -28.7, u: 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔS = 2F × slope.', w: R`$2 \times 96\,500 \times (-1.487 \times 10^{-4}) = -28.7$` },
        { q: R`What is the cell voltage at 298 K if $[\mathrm{Cd^{2+}}] = 0.100\ \mathrm{M}$?`, a: 0.329, u: 'V', abs: 0.002, h: 'Nernst: Q = [Zn²⁺]/[Cd²⁺].', w: R`$0.3590 - \dfrac{8.314 \times 298}{2 \times 96\,500}\ln 10 = 0.329\ \mathrm{V}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us25-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-28-1', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) $\mathrm{SO_2}$ through barium hydroxide (b) $\mathrm{K_2Cr_2O_7}$ with $\mathrm{FeSO_4}$ in dilute $\mathrm{H_2SO_4}$ (c) solid AgBr in sodium thiosulfate`, h: 'Sulfite precipitate; Cr(VI) → Cr(III); a soluble complex.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Ba^{2+} + 2OH^- + SO_2 \rightarrow BaSO_3 + H_2O}$ (b) $\mathrm{Cr_2O_7^{2-} + Fe^{2+} + H^+ \rightarrow Cr^{3+} + Fe^{3+} + H_2O}$ (c) $\mathrm{AgBr + 2S_2O_3^{2-} \rightarrow [Ag(S_2O_3)_2]^{3-} + Br^-}$` },
        { q: R`(d) $\mathrm{PCl_3}$ with chlorine (e) 1,4-dichlorobenzene with conc. $\mathrm{HNO_3/H_2SO_4}$ (f) carbon-11 emits a positron`, h: 'β⁺ lowers Z by one.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{PCl_3 + Cl_2 \rightarrow PCl_5}$ (e) 1,4-dichloro-2-nitrobenzene + $\mathrm{H_2O}$ (f) $\mathrm{^{11}_6C \rightarrow {}^{11}_5B + \beta^+}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us25-6', n: 6, t: 'Oxygen species', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-4', 'ca-1-3'],
      s: R`Oxygen has low-lying excited states; one emits at 762 nm when it falls back to the ground state. $h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\ \mathrm{J\ s}$; $c = 2.998 \times 10^8\ \mathrm{m\ s^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How far above the ground state is this excited state, in kJ mol⁻¹?', a: 157.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'E = hc/λ × N_A.', w: R`$2.607 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 157.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`What is the bond order of $\mathrm{O_2^+}$?`, a: 2.5, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Remove a π* electron from O₂.', w: R`2.5, so it has the shortest O–O bond. Order: $\mathrm{O_2^+}$ (2.5) < $\mathrm{O_2}$ (2) < $\mathrm{O_3}$ (1.5 each) < $\mathrm{O_2^-}$ (1.5) < $\mathrm{O_2^{2-}}$ (1) in length. $\mathrm{O_2}$ does not absorb IR (no dipole change) but bent $\mathrm{O_3}$ does.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us25-7', n: 7, t: 'Perovskites', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-4-2', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`Cubic $\mathrm{CsPbBr_3}$ has one formula unit per cell with edge 587 pm: Pb at the corners (octahedrally surrounded by Br), Br on the edges and Cs at the centre.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the coordination numbers of Pb, Cs and Br.', h: 'Count nearest neighbours of the other kind.', w: 'Pb 6 (Br); Cs 12 (Br); Br 2 (Pb).' },
        { q: R`Find the density of $\mathrm{CsPbBr_3}$.`, a: 4.76, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'M = 579.8; a³ in cm³.', w: R`$579.8 \div (6.022 \times 10^{23} \times (5.87 \times 10^{-8})^3) = 4.76\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: R`In $\mathrm{YBa_2Cu_3O_{6.5}}$, what is the average oxidation state of Cu?`, a: 2.00, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Y +3, Ba +2, O −2.', w: R`$(13 - 3 - 4) \div 3 = +2.00$ (in $\mathrm{YBa_2Cu_3O_7}$ it is +2.33).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us25-8', n: 8, t: 'Halogenation', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-14-1', 'ca-14-2', 'ca-17-1'],
      s: 'Halogens react with alkanes, alkenes, arenes and carbonyl compounds in different ways.',
      p: [
        { q: R`Give conditions and the main product for monobromination of 2-methylbutane, and the products of cyclopentene with $\mathrm{Br_2}$ in water and in $\mathrm{CCl_4}$.`, h: 'Radical bromination picks the tertiary H.', w: 'Light (or a peroxide): 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. In water: trans-2-bromocyclopentanol; in CCl₄: trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane (racemic).' },
        { q: 'Butanone is warmed with excess I₂ and NaOH. How many moles of I₂ react per mole of butanone, and what are the products?', a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'The CH₃ next to C=O is fully iodinated.', w: R`3. Products: yellow iodoform, $\mathrm{CHI_3}$, and sodium propanoate. Each iodination makes the remaining H more acidic, so it goes to completion.` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2024, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Identifying a Group 2 halide · fluoride and carbonate solubility · polymerising ethene · a copper–ammonia cell · net equations · Group 1 trends · azides and diazirinone · amine basicity', skipped: 'Parts that need the printed graphs (CaF₂ solubility data, cell voltage curves) are rebuilt with given constants instead.', qs: [
    { id: 'us24-1', n: 1, t: 'A Group 2 halide', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 11, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-25-1', 'ca-10-1'],
      s: R`An unknown salt $\mathrm{MX_2}$ is a Group 2 metal halide. Water: $K_f = 1.86\ \mathrm{°C\ kg\ mol^{-1}}$. Carbonic acid: $K_{a1} = 4.3 \times 10^{-7}$, $K_{a2} = 4.7 \times 10^{-11}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`8.00 g of $\mathrm{MX_2}$ in 40.0 g of water freezes at −5.36 °C. Find its molar mass (assume full dissociation).`, a: 208.2, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'i = 3.', w: R`$m = 5.36 \div (3 \times 1.86) = 0.9606\ \mathrm{mol\ kg^{-1}}$; $n = 0.03842$; $M = 208.2$` },
        { q: R`With excess $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$, 8.00 g gives 11.01 g of dry precipitate. Identify $\mathrm{MX_2}$.`, h: 'Try AgCl and AgBr.', w: R`$2 \times 0.03842 \times 143.32 = 11.01\ \mathrm{g}$ fits AgCl, so X = Cl and M = 208.2 − 70.9 = 137.3: $\mathrm{BaCl_2}$. Flame test: pale (apple) green. Adding $\mathrm{Na_2SO_4}$ precipitates $\mathrm{BaSO_4}$ until all Ba is used, then the mass levels off.` },
        { q: R`8.00 g $\mathrm{Na_2CO_3}$ and 8.00 g $\mathrm{BaCl_2}$ are shaken in 200.0 mL of water; $\mathrm{BaCO_3}$ precipitates. Find the pH of the solution.`, a: 11.79, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Excess carbonate is a weak base: Kb = Kw/Ka₂.', w: R`Excess $\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}} = (0.07548 - 0.03842) \div 0.2000 = 0.1853\ \mathrm{M}$; $K_b = 2.13 \times 10^{-4}$; $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 6.17 \times 10^{-3}$; pH 11.79` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us24-2', n: 2, t: 'Fluoride and carbonate solubility', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 13, lec: ['ca-25-1'],
      s: R`$K_{sp}(\mathrm{CaF_2}) = 3.9 \times 10^{-11}$; $K_a(\mathrm{HF}) = 6.8 \times 10^{-4}$; $K_{sp}(\mathrm{CaCO_3}) = 8.7 \times 10^{-9}$; carbonic acid $K_{a1} = 4.3 \times 10^{-7}$, $K_{a2} = 4.7 \times 10^{-11}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the solubility of $\mathrm{CaF_2}$ in pure water.`, a: 2.14e-4, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'Ksp = 4s³.', w: R`$s = (3.9 \times 10^{-11}/4)^{1/3} = 2.1 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
        { q: 'Find its solubility in a solution buffered at pH 2.50.', a: 6.78e-4, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'Only the fraction α = Ka/(Ka + [H⁺]) of the fluoride is F⁻.', w: R`$\alpha = 0.177$; $K_{sp} = s(2\alpha s)^2$; $s = 6.8 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{M}$: protonating fluoride pulls the equilibrium over.` },
        { q: R`Water under 1 atm $\mathrm{CO_2}$ (0.0345 M dissolved) has $[\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}] = 1.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{M}$. NaOH is added. At what pH does $\mathrm{CaCO_3}$ start to precipitate?`, a: 6.55, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: '[CO₃²⁻] = 0.0345·Ka₁Ka₂/[H⁺]².', w: R`Need $[\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}}] = 8.7 \times 10^{-6}$; $[\mathrm{H^+}]^2 = 0.0345 \times 2.02 \times 10^{-17} \div 8.7 \times 10^{-6}$; pH 6.55` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us24-3', n: 3, t: 'Polymerising ethene', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-2', 'ca-15-1'],
      s: R`Ethene polymerises: $\mathrm{C_2H_4(g) \rightarrow \tfrac{1}{n}(C_2H_4)_n}$, with $\Delta H^\circ = -95.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$ and $\Delta S^\circ = -150.0\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$ per mole of ethene; $K = 1/p(\mathrm{C_2H_4})$ (bar). Average C–C bond enthalpy 345 kJ mol⁻¹.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many chains are in 2.0 g of polymer with an average of 1500 monomer units per chain?', a: 2.86e19, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mass of one chain in g mol⁻¹: 1500 × 28.05.', w: R`$2.0 \div 42\,075 \times 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 2.9 \times 10^{19}$` },
        { q: 'Estimate the C=C bond enthalpy in ethene.', a: 595, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Each monomer breaks one C=C and makes two C–C (each shared).', w: R`$\Delta H = E(\mathrm{C{=}C}) - 2(345)$, so $E = 690 - 95 = 595\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Starting at 30.0 bar of ethene at 700 K, what percentage is converted at equilibrium (constant volume, polymer as a separate phase)?', a: 81.4, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG° at 700 K gives K; p(eq) = 1/K.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = +10.0\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = 0.179$; $p = 5.57\ \mathrm{bar}$; $(30.0 - 5.57)/30.0 = 81.4\%$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us24-4', n: 4, t: 'A copper–ammonia cell', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`Half-cell A: Cu in 1.00 M $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$. Half-cell B: Cu in 100 mL of 0.200 M $\mathrm{NH_3}$ containing $1.0 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{M}$ copper(II) in total. $K_f(\mathrm{Cu(NH_3)_4^{2+}}) = 1.7 \times 10^{13}$; 298 K.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the free $[\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}]$ in half-cell B.`, a: 3.7e-15, u: 'M', tol: 0.02, h: 'Almost all is complexed; free NH₃ ≈ 0.1996 M.', w: R`$1.0 \times 10^{-4} \div (1.7 \times 10^{13} \times 0.1996^4) = 3.7 \times 10^{-15}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
        { q: 'What is the cell voltage? Which electrode is the cathode?', a: 0.427, u: 'V', abs: 0.003, h: 'A concentration cell: E = (0.0592/2) log([Cu²⁺]A/[Cu²⁺]B).', w: R`$0.0296 \times \log(2.7 \times 10^{14}) = 0.427\ \mathrm{V}$. A (higher $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$) is the cathode. Adding $\mathrm{HNO_3}$ to B protonates $\mathrm{NH_3}$, frees $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ and makes the voltage fall, sharply near the equivalence point.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us24-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-18-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) aqueous ammonia with methanoic acid (b) potassium iodate with excess acidified iodide (c) lead(IV) oxide with concentrated HCl`, h: 'Comproportionation of iodine.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{NH_3 + HCOOH \rightarrow NH_4^+ + HCOO^-}$ (b) $\mathrm{IO_3^- + I^- + H^+ \rightarrow I_2 + H_2O}$ ($\mathrm{I_3^-}$ with excess $\mathrm{I^-}$) (c) $\mathrm{PbO_2 + H^+ + Cl^- \rightarrow PbCl_2 + Cl_2 + H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`(d) ethyl benzoate heated with NaOH(aq) (e) calcium oxide with coke at 2200 °C (f) iodine-125 decays by electron capture`, h: 'Saponification; a carbide.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{C_6H_5COO^- + C_2H_5OH}$ (e) $\mathrm{CaO + C \rightarrow CaC_2 + CO}$ (f) $\mathrm{^{125}I + e^- \rightarrow {}^{125}Te}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us24-6', n: 6, t: 'Group 1 trends', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-1-3', 'ca-1-4'],
      s: R`First ionisation energies (kJ mol⁻¹): H 1312, Li 520, Na 496, K 420, Rb 403, Cs 376. Exciting hydrogen’s electron from 1s to 2s costs 984 kJ mol⁻¹. $c = 2.998 \times 10^8\ \mathrm{m\ s^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`A hydrogen atom in its 2s state passes its electron to a $\mathrm{K^+}$ ion (giving $\mathrm{H^+}$ and K). What is ΔH, in kJ mol⁻¹?`, a: -92, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', abs: 1, h: 'Removing the 2s electron costs 1312 − 984.', w: R`$328 - 420 = -92\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$: energy is released.` },
        { q: R`Strontium-90 (89.9077276 u) β⁻-decays to yttrium-90 (89.9071437 u, atomic masses). How much energy is released, in kJ mol⁻¹?`, a: 5.25e7, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'E = Δm c², Δm in kg mol⁻¹.', w: R`$\Delta m = 5.839 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{kg\ mol^{-1}}$; $\times (2.998 \times 10^8)^2 = 5.25 \times 10^{10}\ \mathrm{J} = 5.25 \times 10^7\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us24-7', n: 7, t: 'Azides and diazirinone', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: R`Bond enthalpies (kJ mol⁻¹): C–N 290; C=O 741; N=N 418; C≡O 1080; N≡N 949. Diazirinone is a three-membered $\mathrm{N{=}N}$ ring joined to a $\mathrm{C{=}O}$; carbonyl diazide is $\mathrm{O{=}C(N{=}N{=}N)_2}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Estimate ΔH for diazirinone → CO + $\mathrm{N_2}$.`, a: -290, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Break C=O, 2 C–N, N=N; make C≡O, N≡N.', w: R`$(741 + 580 + 418) - (1080 + 949) = -290\ \mathrm{kJ}$. The measured value (−347) is more negative because bond enthalpies ignore the strain of the three-membered ring.` },
        { q: R`Estimate ΔH for $\mathrm{CO(N_3)_2 \rightarrow CO + 3N_2}$, treating each azide as N=N=N.`, a: -934, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Break C=O, 2 C–N and 4 N=N.', w: R`$(741 + 580 + 1672) - (1080 + 2847) = -934\ \mathrm{kJ}$. With 1 gas → 4 gases, ΔS > 0, so ΔG is even more negative than ΔH.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us24-8', n: 8, t: 'Basicity of nitrogen compounds', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-34-1', 'ca-34-4'],
      s: R`Compare isomers of $\mathrm{C_4H_9NO_2}$ such as an amino acid, an amine with an ester, and a nitroalkane, and the heterocycles pyridine and pyrrole.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Which is most basic: methyl 3-aminopropanoate, 1-nitrobutane or 2-aminobutanoic acid (as the neutral form drawn)? Which isomer is chiral?', h: 'A nitro N has no lone pair to give.', w: 'The amino ester (a free primary amine). Nitro N is not basic; the amino acid is a zwitterion already holding its proton. 2-Aminobutanoic acid is chiral.' },
        { q: 'Which is more basic, pyridine or pyrrole, and which reacts faster with Br₂?', h: 'Where is the lone pair?', w: 'Pyridine: its lone pair is outside the ring (conjugate acid pyridinium). Pyrrole’s lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet, which makes the ring electron-rich: it brominates much faster, at C2.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2023, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Identifying an ionic solid · zinc hydrolysis · burning hydrogen · freezing a salt solution · net equations · palladium electrochemistry · boiling points of AXₙ · two cyclic compounds', qs: [
    { id: 'us23-1', n: 1, t: 'An ionic compound of H, N and O', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-4-1', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: R`Compound A is ionic and contains only H, N and O. Its cation is a weak acid (titrates as monoprotic). $R = 0.08314\ \mathrm{L\ bar\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$; 1 bar = 750.06 mmHg.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`1.200 g of A needs 37.48 mL of 0.4000 M NaOH. Find the molar mass of A.`, a: 80.0, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Monoprotic: 1 : 1.', w: R`$0.014992\ \mathrm{mol}$; $1.200 \div 0.014992 = 80.0$` },
        { q: R`The same mass of A is heated to 250 °C in an evacuated 2.00 L vessel and decomposes completely into gases. The pressure is 733.7 mmHg. How many mol of gas form per mol of A?`, a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'n = PV/RT.', w: R`$0.9782 \times 2.00 \div (0.08314 \times 523.15) = 0.04498\ \mathrm{mol} = 3.00 \times 0.014992$` },
        { q: R`After drying, the gas fills 371.7 mL at 25 °C and 750.0 mmHg. What is the molar mass of A’s dry gaseous product, and what is A?`, h: 'One mole of gas per mole of A survives drying.', w: R`0.01499 mol, i.e. 1 per A; the other 2 are water. A is $\mathrm{NH_4NO_3}$ ($\mathrm{H_4N_2O_3}$, 80.04): $\mathrm{NH_4NO_3 \rightarrow N_2O + 2H_2O}$. N₂O is linear, $\mathrm{N{=}N^+{=}O^-} \leftrightarrow \mathrm{N{\equiv}N^+{-}O^-}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us23-2', n: 2, t: 'Zinc in water', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`Water bound to $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ is acidic: $\mathrm{p}K_a(\mathrm{Zn^{2+}(aq)}) = 8.96$. $K_{sp}(\mathrm{Zn(OH)_2}) = 4.5 \times 10^{-17}$; $K_f(\mathrm{Zn(OH)_4^{2-}}) = 5.0 \times 10^{14}$; $\mathrm{p}K_a(\mathrm{HCOOH}) = 3.75$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the pH of 0.050 M $\mathrm{Zn(NO_3)_2}$.`, a: 5.13, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Weak acid: [H⁺] = √(Ka c).', w: R`$\sqrt{1.10 \times 10^{-9} \times 0.050} = 7.4 \times 10^{-6}$; pH 5.13` },
        { q: R`Find the pH of zinc methanoate solution, $\mathrm{Zn(HCOO)_2}$ (it does not depend on concentration).`, a: 6.51, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Zn²⁺ + HCOO⁻ ⇌ ZnOH⁺ + HCOOH, K = 10^(3.75 − 8.96).', w: R`$K = 6.17 \times 10^{-6}$; $x^2 = K \cdot c \cdot 2c$ gives $[\mathrm{HCOO^-}]/[\mathrm{HCOOH}] = \sqrt{2/K} = 569$; pH $= 3.75 + 2.76 = 6.51$` },
        { q: R`What is the solubility of $\mathrm{Zn(OH)_2}$ at pH 12.50?`, a: 2.25e-5, u: 'mol dm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'Mostly zincate: [Zn(OH)₄²⁻] = Kf·Ksp·[OH⁻]².', w: R`$[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 0.0316$; $5.0 \times 10^{14} \times 4.5 \times 10^{-17} \times 1.0 \times 10^{-3} = 2.25 \times 10^{-5}$ (free $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ only $4.5 \times 10^{-14}$)` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us23-3', n: 3, t: 'Burning hydrogen', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-23-4', 'ca-5-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{H_2 + \tfrac{1}{2}O_2 \rightarrow H_2O(g)}$: $\Delta H^\circ = -241.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, $\Delta S^\circ = -44.5\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$. $S^\circ$: $\mathrm{H_2}$ 130.7, $\mathrm{O_2}$ 205.2. Bond enthalpies: O–H 463, H–H 436 kJ mol⁻¹. Specific heats (J g⁻¹ K⁻¹): $\mathrm{H_2O}$ 4.18, $\mathrm{O_2}$ 0.92, Al 0.89.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $S^\circ$ of $\mathrm{H_2O(g)}$.`, a: 188.8, u: 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'ΔS = S(H₂O) − S(H₂) − ½S(O₂).', w: R`$-44.5 + 130.7 + 102.6 = 188.8$` },
        { q: 'Estimate the O=O bond enthalpy.', a: 496, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Bonds broken − bonds made = ΔH.', w: R`$436 + \tfrac{1}{2}E - 2(463) = -241.8$; $E = 496\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`0.150 mol $\mathrm{H_2}$ and 0.100 mol $\mathrm{O_2}$ at 100 °C react in a sealed vessel inside an insulated 1.20 kg aluminium block also at 100 °C. Find the final temperature in K.`, a: 406.7, u: 'K', tol: 0.002, h: 'Heat 36.27 kJ; heat capacity of Al + H₂O formed + leftover O₂.', w: R`$C = 1068 + 2.703(4.18) + 0.80(0.92) = 1080\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $\Delta T = 36\,270 \div 1080 = 33.6\ \mathrm{K}$; $T = 406.7\ \mathrm{K}$` },
        { q: R`Air at 298 K holds 21% $\mathrm{O_2}$ and 2.5% $\mathrm{H_2O}$ (1 bar total). Above what partial pressure of $\mathrm{H_2}$ (bar) is burning thermodynamically favourable?`, a: 4.7e-42, u: 'bar', tol: 0.05, h: 'ΔG° = −228.5 kJ; need Q < K.', w: R`$K = e^{228\,500/(8.314 \times 298)} = 1.15 \times 10^{40}$; $p(\mathrm{H_2}) > 0.025 \div (K\sqrt{0.21}) = 4.7 \times 10^{-42}\ \mathrm{bar}$. Real mixtures need about 4% H₂: a flame also needs a fast enough rate.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us23-4', n: 4, t: 'Freezing a salt solution', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 13, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`Pure water and ice both have vapour pressure 611 Pa at 0.0 °C. $\Delta H_{sub}(\text{ice}) = 51.1$ and $\Delta H_{vap} = 45.1\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. A solution has 0.600 mol $\mathrm{CaCl_2}$ (fully dissociated) in 1.000 kg of water (55.51 mol).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the vapour pressure of the solution at 0.0 °C (Raoult’s law).', a: 591.8, u: 'Pa', tol: 0.003, h: 'Count every ion.', w: R`$x = 55.51 \div 57.31 = 0.9686$; $P = 591.8\ \mathrm{Pa}$` },
        { q: 'Pure ice has that vapour pressure a little below 0 °C. How many kelvin below 273.15 K?', a: 0.39, u: 'K', abs: 0.02, h: 'ln(P/611) = −(ΔH_sub/R)(1/T − 1/273.15).', w: R`$1/T = 1/273.15 + 8.314\ln(611/591.8)/51\,100$; $T = 272.76\ \mathrm{K}$` },
        { q: 'Find the freezing point of the solution, in °C, where ice and solution have equal vapour pressures.', a: -3.26, u: '°C', abs: 0.02, h: 'ln x = −(ΔH_fus/R)(1/T − 1/273.15), ΔH_fus = 6.0 kJ.', w: R`$1/T = 1/273.15 - 8.314\ln(0.9686)/6000$; $T = 269.89\ \mathrm{K} = -3.26\ \mathrm{°C}$. As ice forms, the solution gets more concentrated, so the temperature keeps falling.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us23-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-28-1', 'ca-17-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) concentrated HCl on $\mathrm{KMnO_4}$ (b) zinc in concentrated NaOH (c) potassium in liquid ammonia with a trace of iron(III)`, h: 'Amphoteric zinc; amide formation.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + H^+ + Cl^- \rightarrow Mn^{2+} + Cl_2 + H_2O}$ (b) $\mathrm{Zn + OH^- + H_2O \rightarrow [Zn(OH)_4]^{2-} + H_2}$ (c) $\mathrm{K + NH_3 \rightarrow K^+ + NH_2^- + H_2}$` },
        { q: R`(d) $\mathrm{[Pt(NH_3)_4]^{2+}}$ heated with 2 equivalents of chloride (e) potassium tert-butoxide with 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (f) iron-55 decays by electron capture`, h: 'Trans effect; a bulky base gives the less substituted alkene.', w: R`(d) trans-$\mathrm{[PtCl_2(NH_3)_2]}$ (e) 2-methylbut-1-ene (Hofmann product) (f) $\mathrm{^{55}Fe + e^- \rightarrow {}^{55}Mn}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us23-6', n: 6, t: 'Palladium electrochemistry', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`$E^\circ$ at 298 K: $\mathrm{Pd^{2+}/Pd}$ +0.951 V; $\mathrm{PdCl_4^{2-}/Pd}$ (+ 4Cl⁻) +0.591 V; $\mathrm{PdCl_6^{2-}/PdCl_4^{2-}}$ (+ 2Cl⁻) +1.288 V.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $K_f$ for $\mathrm{PdCl_4^{2-}}$.`, a: 1.5e12, u: '', tol: 0.05, h: 'ΔG° = −2F(0.951 − 0.591).', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -69.5\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K_f = 1.5 \times 10^{12}$` },
        { q: R`Find $E^\circ$ for $\mathrm{PdCl_6^{2-} + 4e^- \rightarrow Pd + 6Cl^-}$.`, a: 0.940, u: 'V', abs: 0.002, h: 'Add ΔG values, not potentials.', w: R`$(2 \times 0.591 + 2 \times 1.288) \div 4 = 0.940\ \mathrm{V}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us23-7', n: 7, t: 'Boiling points of AXₙ', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 8, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-3-6'],
      s: R`Compare the fluorides $\mathrm{AF_n}$ and methyls $\mathrm{A(CH_3)_n}$ of the 2p elements A = B, C, N, O.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the shapes of $\mathrm{BF_3}$ and $\mathrm{NF_3}$, and explain why each fluoride boils lower than the matching methyl compound.`, h: 'Polarizability.', w: R`$\mathrm{BF_3}$ trigonal planar; $\mathrm{NF_3}$ trigonal pyramidal. F holds its electrons tightly (low polarizability), so dispersion forces are weaker than for $\mathrm{CH_3}$ groups.` },
        { q: R`Why does $\mathrm{BF_3}$ boil higher than $\mathrm{CF_4}$, though $\mathrm{B(CH_3)_3}$ boils lower than $\mathrm{C(CH_3)_4}$?`, h: 'Lewis acidity.', w: R`Boron’s empty p orbital accepts electron density from F on neighbouring molecules: an extra attraction that $\mathrm{CF_4}$ (and the methyl compounds) cannot make.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us23-8', n: 8, t: 'Two cyclic compounds', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-14-2', 'ca-17-1'],
      s: 'Compare cyclohexanone (bp 156 °C) with 1-methylcyclohexene (bp 110 °C).',
      p: [
        { q: 'Which decolourises bromine, and which reacts with CH₃MgBr? Give the products.', h: 'C=C versus C=O.', w: 'The alkene adds Br₂ to give trans-1,2-dibromo-1-methylcyclohexane (racemic). The ketone gives 1-methylcyclohexanol with CH₃MgBr then acid; heating that with H₂SO₄ dehydrates it to 1-methylcyclohexene.' },
        { q: 'Why does the ketone boil higher?', h: 'Similar mass.', w: 'Its polar C=O adds dipole–dipole attraction to the dispersion forces.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2022, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Identifying a CFC · an ammonia buffer and silver chloride · chlorine atoms and Cl₂⁺ · an iodide clock · net equations · a silver–copper cell · amides versus diketones · C₄H₈O₂ isomers', qs: [
    { id: 'us22-1', n: 1, t: 'A chlorofluorocarbon', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 11, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-2-4', 'ca-37-3'],
      s: R`Compound A contains only C, F and Cl. Its vapour has a density of 7.559 g L⁻¹ at 1.00 bar and 25.0 °C. $R = 0.08314\ \mathrm{L\ bar\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the molar mass of A.', a: 187.4, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'M = ρRT/P.', w: R`$7.559 \times 0.08314 \times 298.15 \div 1.00 = 187.4$` },
        { q: R`150.0 mL of the vapour (same conditions) is decomposed and the chloride titrated: 45.38 mL of 0.4000 M $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$. How many Cl atoms are in each molecule?`, a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'Moles of A = PV/RT.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{A}) = 6.051 \times 10^{-3}$; $n(\mathrm{Cl^-}) = 0.01815$; ratio 3.00` },
        { q: 'Give the formula of A, and structures of the isomer with one ¹⁹F environment and the one with two.', h: '187.4 − 106.4 (3 Cl) = 81.0.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_2F_3Cl_3}$ (24.0 + 57.0 = 81.0 ✓). One F environment: $\mathrm{CF_3{-}CCl_3}$. Two: $\mathrm{CF_2Cl{-}CFCl_2}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us22-2', n: 2, t: 'An ammonia buffer', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`$K_a(\mathrm{NH_4^+}) = 5.6 \times 10^{-10}$; $K_{sp}(\mathrm{AgCl}) = 1.8 \times 10^{-10}$; $K_f(\mathrm{Ag(NH_3)_2^+}) = 1.6 \times 10^7$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the pH of 0.50 M NH₃.', a: 11.47, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Kb = Kw/Ka.', w: R`$K_b = 1.79 \times 10^{-5}$; $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 2.98 \times 10^{-3}$; pH 11.47` },
        { q: R`What $[\mathrm{NH_3}]/[\mathrm{NH_4^+}]$ ratio gives pH 9.40?`, a: 1.41, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Ka/[H⁺].', w: R`$5.6 \times 10^{-10} \div 3.98 \times 10^{-10} = 1.41$` },
        { q: R`250 mL of this buffer must have total nitrogen 0.60 M, made from 0.50 M $\mathrm{NH_3}$ and solid $\mathrm{NH_4Cl}$. What mass of $\mathrm{NH_4Cl}$ is needed?`, a: 3.33, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: '0.150 mol N in total, split 1.41 : 1.', w: R`$\mathrm{NH_3} = 0.0877\ \mathrm{mol}$ (175 mL of 0.50 M); $\mathrm{NH_4^+} = 0.0623\ \mathrm{mol} \times 53.49 = 3.33\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`5.0 mL of 0.10 M $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$ is added. Free $[\mathrm{Ag^+}]$ would be $1.06 \times 10^{-9}$ M without precipitation, so AgCl forms ($Q = 2.6 \times 10^{-10} > K_{sp}$). What mass of AgCl precipitates?`, a: 0.0214, u: 'g', tol: 0.02, h: 'At equilibrium [Ag⁺] = Ksp/[Cl⁻]; the rest of the silver stays as the ammine complex.', w: R`$[\mathrm{Ag^+}] = 7.4 \times 10^{-10}$; complex $= 7.4 \times 10^{-10} \times 1.6 \times 10^7 \times 0.340^2 \times 0.255\ \mathrm{L} = 3.5 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol}$; AgCl $= 1.5 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} = 0.021\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us22-3', n: 3, t: 'Chlorine atoms', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 14, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-2', 'ca-1-3'],
      s: R`Cl(g): $\Delta_f H = 121.3\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, $S^\circ = 165.2$; $\mathrm{Cl_2(g)}$: $S^\circ = 223.1\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$. Ionisation energies: Cl 1251.2, $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ 1107.7 kJ mol⁻¹.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the Cl–Cl bond enthalpy.', a: 242.6, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.002, h: '2ΔfH(Cl).', w: R`$2 \times 121.3 = 242.6\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`A 1.00 L flask holds $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ at 0.100 bar and 298 K. About how many Cl atoms are present at equilibrium?`, a: 2.7e3, u: 'atoms', tol: 0.05, h: 'ΔG° = 242.6 − 298(0.1073); K = p(Cl)²/p(Cl₂).', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 210.6\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = 1.2 \times 10^{-37}$; $p(\mathrm{Cl}) = 1.1 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{bar}$; $n = 4.4 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{mol} \approx 2.7 \times 10^3$ atoms` },
        { q: R`Find the bond enthalpy of $\mathrm{Cl_2^+}$ (dissociating to Cl + Cl⁺).`, a: 386, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Hess cycle through the two ionisation energies.', w: R`$242.6 + 1251.2 - 1107.7 = 386\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$: stronger, because the electron removed came from a π* orbital (bond order 1 → 1.5).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us22-4', n: 4, t: 'An iodide clock', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{H_2O_2 + 2I^- + 2H^+ \rightarrow I_2 + 2H_2O}$, timed by thiosulfate and starch. Each 10.0 mL run has 1.0 mL of 0.020 M $\mathrm{Na_2S_2O_3}$, so blue appears when $\Delta[\mathrm{H_2O_2}] = 0.0010\ \mathrm{M}$. Stocks: 0.20 M KI, 0.20 M $\mathrm{H_2O_2}$, 1.0 M $\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$, 1.0 M NaOH. Runs (mL acid / NaOH / KI / $\mathrm{H_2O_2}$; time): A 2/1/2/2, 50.0 s; B 4/1/2/2, 50.6 s; C 2/1/4/2, 25.2 s; D 2/1/2/4, 24.9 s.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the initial $[\mathrm{H^+}]$ in run A ($K_a = 1.8 \times 10^{-5}$).`, a: 1.8e-5, u: 'M', tol: 0.02, h: 'Acid 0.20 M, NaOH 0.10 M after dilution: a 1 : 1 buffer.', w: R`$[\mathrm{H^+}] = K_a = 1.8 \times 10^{-5}$ (run B: $5.4 \times 10^{-5}$, yet the time barely changes)` },
        { q: 'Give the orders in I⁻, H₂O₂ and H⁺ (as m, n, p).', h: 'Compare runs.', w: 'm = 1, n = 1, p = 0: doubling I⁻ or H₂O₂ halves the time; tripling [H⁺] does nothing.' },
        { q: 'Find k from run A.', a: 0.0125, u: 'M⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Rate = 0.0010 M ÷ t; [I⁻] = [H₂O₂] = 0.040 M.', w: R`$2.0 \times 10^{-5} \div (0.040 \times 0.040) = 1.25 \times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{M^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$ (the other runs give 1.24–1.26). A mechanism with $\mathrm{H^+}$ in its slow step would not fit.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us22-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-24-1', 'ca-18-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) $\mathrm{P_4O_{10}}$ added to barium hydroxide (b) $\mathrm{H_2S}$ bubbled into acidified $\mathrm{KMnO_4}$ (c) CuO heated in hydrogen`, h: 'Phosphate precipitate; S²⁻ → S.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{P_4O_{10} + Ba^{2+} + OH^- \rightarrow Ba_3(PO_4)_2 + H_2O}$ (b) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + H_2S + H^+ \rightarrow Mn^{2+} + S + H_2O}$ (c) $\mathrm{CuO + H_2 \rightarrow Cu + H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`(d) aqueous KI electrolysed with Pt electrodes (e) propan-1-ol heated under reflux with excess acidified dichromate (f) α-decay of polonium-210`, h: 'Iodine at the anode; full oxidation.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{I^- + H_2O \rightarrow I_2 + H_2 + OH^-}$ (e) $\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CH_2OH + Cr_2O_7^{2-} + H^+ \rightarrow CH_3CH_2COOH + Cr^{3+} + H_2O}$ (f) $\mathrm{^{210}Po \rightarrow {}^{206}Pb + {}^4He}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us22-6', n: 6, t: 'A silver–copper cell', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-24-1', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`A 12.00 g Ag wire dips into 1.00 L of 0.0500 M $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$; a 15.00 g Cu plate into 1.00 L of 0.100 M $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$. $E^\circ(\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag}) = 0.800$, $E^\circ(\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu}) = 0.337\ \mathrm{V}$; $K_{sp}(\mathrm{Ag_2C_2O_4}) = 3.5 \times 10^{-11}$; 298 K.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the cell voltage.', a: 0.416, u: 'V', abs: 0.002, h: 'Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag; Q = [Cu²⁺]/[Ag⁺]².', w: R`$0.463 - 0.0296\log(0.100/0.0025) = 0.416\ \mathrm{V}$` },
        { q: 'At 0.100 A, how long until the two electrodes have equal mass, in hours?', a: 5.76, u: 'h', tol: 0.01, h: 'Per mol Cu lost, Ag gains 2 × 107.87 g.', w: R`$12.00 + 215.74x = 15.00 - 63.55x$; $x = 0.01074\ \mathrm{mol}$; $q = 2xF = 2073\ \mathrm{C}$; $20\,730\ \mathrm{s} = 5.76\ \mathrm{h}$` },
        { q: R`To drop the fresh cell to 0.200 V, sodium oxalate is added to the silver half-cell. What mass of $\mathrm{Na_2C_2O_4}$ is needed?`, a: 39.9, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Find the [Ag⁺] for 0.200 V, then the free oxalate, then add what precipitated.', w: R`$[\mathrm{Ag^+}] = 1.13 \times 10^{-5}$; free $\mathrm{C_2O_4^{2-}} = 0.272\ \mathrm{M}$; precipitated $0.0250\ \mathrm{mol}$; total $0.297\ \mathrm{mol} \times 134.0 = 39.9\ \mathrm{g}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us22-7', n: 7, t: 'Amides versus diketones', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-3-6', 'ca-3-5'],
      s: 'Ethanamide (CH₃CONH₂) and butanedione (CH₃COCOCH₃) have similar sizes; one boils at 221 °C and the other at 88 °C.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Assign the boiling points and explain.', h: 'Which has N–H?', w: 'Ethanamide 221 °C: its N–H groups hydrogen-bond to C=O on neighbours. Butanedione 88 °C has only dipole and dispersion forces.' },
        { q: 'Why is the H–N–H angle in an amide near 120°, and why is rotation about its C–N bond hindered?', h: 'Resonance.', w: 'The N lone pair delocalises into C=O, giving C–N partial double-bond character: N is sp² (planar, ~120°), and twisting would break that π overlap. The C–C bond in butanedione is σ only, so it turns easily.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us22-8', n: 8, t: 'C₄H₈O₂ isomers', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-18-1', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: 'Compare butanoic acid, ethyl ethanoate and methyl propanoate.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Rank them by boiling point, and draw a chiral C₄H₈O₂ isomer.', h: 'Only one can H-bond to itself.', w: 'Ethyl ethanoate (77 °C) ≈ methyl propanoate (80 °C) < butanoic acid (164 °C, H-bonded dimers). Chiral: 2-hydroxybutanal, CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CHO (or 3-hydroxybutanal).' },
        { q: 'Why is ester hydrolysis reversible in acid but goes to completion in NaOH?', h: 'What happens to the acid formed?', w: 'In base the carboxylic acid is deprotonated to a carboxylate, which cannot be attacked by the alcohol: K becomes enormous. In acid every step is an equilibrium with K near 1.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2021, mins: 105,
    topics: 'A volatile oxide · calcium hydroxide and sulfate · the vanadium flow battery · NO₂Cl equilibrium · net equations · magnetism and colour of complexes · boiling points of hydrides · brominating hydrocarbons', qs: [
    { id: 'us21-1', n: 1, t: 'A volatile oxide', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-24-1', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`A white, slightly volatile oxide $\mathrm{XO_n}$ gives a white precipitate with barium hydroxide. $R = 0.08314\ \mathrm{L\ bar\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$; 760 mmHg = 1.01325 bar; $F = 96\,485\ \mathrm{C\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: '104.3 mg vaporises completely in a 1.50 L flask at 250.0 °C, giving 20.4 mmHg. Find its molar mass.', a: 111, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Convert to bar first.', w: R`$P = 0.0272\ \mathrm{bar}$; $n = 9.40 \times 10^{-4}$; $M = 111$` },
        { q: R`Electrolysis reduces 1.50 g of the oxide to the element X in 58.0 min at 1.50 A. How many electrons per formula unit?`, a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.05, h: 'Charge ÷ F, then ÷ moles of oxide.', w: R`$5220\ \mathrm{C} = 0.0541\ \mathrm{mol}\ e^-$; oxide $= 0.0135\ \mathrm{mol}$; 4.0 per unit, so X is +4 and n = 2: X = 111 − 32 = 79, selenium: $\mathrm{SeO_2}$. With $\mathrm{Ba(OH)_2}$: $\mathrm{SeO_2 + Ba^{2+} + 2OH^- \rightarrow BaSeO_3 + H_2O}$.` },
        { q: R`Its vapour pressure is 0.1111 bar at 270.0 °C and 0.392 bar at 300.0 °C. Find $\Delta H_{sub}$.`, a: 108.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Clausius–Clapeyron.', w: R`$8.314\ln(0.392/0.1111) \div (1/543.15 - 1/573.15) = 108.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us21-2', n: 2, t: 'Calcium hydroxide and sulfate', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-25-1'],
      s: R`A saturated $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$ solution has pH 12.35. $K_{sp}(\mathrm{CaSO_4}) = 2.4 \times 10^{-5}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $K_{sp}$ of $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$.`, a: 5.6e-6, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: '[Ca²⁺] = ½[OH⁻].', w: R`$[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 0.0224$; $K_{sp} = 0.0112 \times 0.0224^2 = 5.6 \times 10^{-6}$` },
        { q: R`What is the solubility of $\mathrm{CaSO_4}$ in 0.0100 M $\mathrm{Na_2SO_4}$?`, a: 2.0e-3, u: 'M', tol: 0.02, h: 's(s + 0.0100) = Ksp: solve the quadratic.', w: R`$s = 2.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{M}$ (in pure water $4.9 \times 10^{-3}$): the common ion cuts it.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us21-3', n: 3, t: 'The vanadium flow battery', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 13, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`$E^\circ$: $\mathrm{VO_2^+/VO^{2+}}$ +1.00; $\mathrm{VO^{2+}/V^{3+}}$ +0.34; $\mathrm{V^{3+}/V^{2+}}$ −0.26 V. The battery: $\mathrm{VO_2^+ + V^{2+} + 2H^+ \rightarrow VO^{2+} + V^{3+} + H_2O}$, $E^\circ = 1.26\ \mathrm{V}$, $dE^\circ/dT = +2.10 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{V\ K^{-1}}$. Each half-cell is 100.0 mL at pH 1.00 (buffered); reactants start at 0.50 M and products at 0.10 M.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $E^\circ$ for $\mathrm{VO_2^+ + 4H^+ + 2e^- \rightarrow V^{3+} + 2H_2O}$.`, a: 0.67, u: 'V', abs: 0.005, h: 'Average weighted by electrons.', w: R`$(1 \times 1.00 + 1 \times 0.34) \div 2 = 0.67\ \mathrm{V}$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔH° of the battery reaction.', a: -115.5, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔS = F dE/dT; ΔH = ΔG + TΔS.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -121.6\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $\Delta S^\circ = 20.3\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $\Delta H^\circ = -115.5\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Discharging at 10.0 A, how long until the voltage reaches 1.10 V, in seconds?', a: 304, u: 's', tol: 0.01, h: 'Q = (x/y)² ÷ [H⁺]² with x products, y reactants.', w: R`$Q = 10^{0.16/0.05916} = 506$; $x/y = 2.25$; $0.10 + d = 2.25(0.50 - d)$, $d = 0.315\ \mathrm{M}$ ([V³⁺] = 0.415 M); $0.0315\ \mathrm{mol} \times F \div 10.0 = 304\ \mathrm{s}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us21-4', n: 4, t: 'NO₂Cl and NOCl', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-22-2'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{NO_2Cl + NO \rightleftharpoons NO_2 + NOCl}$ has $K = 1.12 \times 10^4$ at 298 K and $6.90 \times 10^3$ at 320 K.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find ΔG° at 320 K.', a: -23.52, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: '−RT ln K.', w: R`$-8.314 \times 320 \ln 6900 = -23.52\ \mathrm{kJ}$ (at 298 K: −23.10)` },
        { q: 'Find ΔH°, assuming ΔH° and ΔS° are constant.', a: -17.5, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'Two equations ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.', w: R`$\Delta S^\circ = (-23.10 + 23.52) \div 22 = 18.9\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $\Delta H^\circ = -23.10 + 298(0.0189) = -17.5\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'NO₂ also dimerises to N₂O₄. If the volume is doubled, does the amount of NOCl rise or fall?', h: 'Lower pressure favours more gas moles in the dimerisation.', w: 'Doubling the volume shifts 2NO₂ ⇌ N₂O₄ towards NO₂; extra NO₂ then pushes the first equilibrium left, so NOCl falls.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us21-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-15-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) BaO added to water (b) acidified dichromate with iron(II) sulfate (c) gaseous $\mathrm{(CH_3)_3N}$ and $\mathrm{BF_3}$`, h: 'A Lewis acid–base adduct.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{BaO + H_2O \rightarrow Ba^{2+} + 2OH^-}$ (b) $\mathrm{Cr_2O_7^{2-} + Fe^{2+} + H^+ \rightarrow Cr^{3+} + Fe^{3+} + H_2O}$ (c) $\mathrm{(CH_3)_3N{\rightarrow}BF_3}$` },
        { q: R`(d) $\mathrm{SO_3}$ into excess sodium carbonate solution (e) NaOH with 1-bromopropane in DMSO (f) carbon-11 emits a positron`, h: 'S_N2 in a polar aprotic solvent.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{SO_3 + CO_3^{2-} \rightarrow SO_4^{2-} + CO_2}$ (or $\mathrm{HCO_3^-}$ with excess carbonate) (e) $\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CH_2OH + Br^-}$ (f) $\mathrm{^{11}C \rightarrow {}^{11}B + \beta^+}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us21-6', n: 6, t: 'Magnetism and colour', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-28-3', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: 'Explain each pair using d-electron counts, ligand-field strength and geometry.',
      p: [
        { q: R`How many unpaired electrons do $\mathrm{[Fe(H_2O)_6]^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_6]^{4-}}$ have?`, h: 'Fe(II) is d⁶; weak versus strong field.', w: 'High-spin aqua: 4. Low-spin cyanide: 0 (all six in t₂g).' },
        { q: R`Explain: $\mathrm{[Ti(H_2O)_6]^{3+}}$ is coloured but $\mathrm{[Sc(H_2O)_6]^{3+}}$ is not; $\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}$ is yellow though Cr(VI) is d⁰.`, h: 'd–d versus charge transfer.', w: 'Ti(III) is d¹, so a d–d transition absorbs visible light; Sc(III) is d⁰ with none. Chromate’s colour is ligand-to-metal charge transfer (O → Cr), which falls in the visible.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us21-7', n: 7, t: 'Boiling points of hydrides', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-3-6', 'ca-3-1'],
      s: 'Boiling points: NaH decomposes above 800 °C; CH₄ −164; NH₃ −33; H₂O 100; HF 19; Ne −246 °C.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Why does water boil higher than both NH₃ and HF, and CH₄ higher than Ne?', h: 'Count donors and acceptors; polarizability.', w: 'H₂O has two H-bond donors and two acceptor lone pairs, so it builds a full network; NH₃ is short of acceptors and HF of donors. CH₄ and Ne both have 10 electrons, but Ne holds its electrons tightly, so CH₄ has larger dispersion forces. NaH is ionic.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us21-8', n: 8, t: 'Brominating hydrocarbons', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-14-2', 'ca-30-1'],
      s: 'Bromine reacts differently with cyclopentene, cyclopentane and arenes.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the products with cyclopentene (dark, room temperature) and with cyclopentane (light). What does the light do?', h: 'Addition versus radical substitution.', w: 'trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane (racemic); bromocyclopentane. Light splits Br₂ into Br• radicals that carry the chain.' },
        { q: 'Rank phenol, methylbenzene and chlorobenzene for reaction with Br₂/FeBr₃, and give the role of FeBr₃.', h: 'Resonance donation beats induction; Cl withdraws.', w: 'Phenol > methylbenzene > chlorobenzene (phenol needs no catalyst at all). FeBr₃ polarises Br₂ into a stronger electrophile (Br⁺ equivalent).' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2020, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Oxalyl bromide · a fluorescent pH indicator and phosphate buffers · iron carbonyl · BrO kinetics · net equations · electrolysing copper sulfate · silicon versus carbon · C₆H₁₂ isomers', qs: [
    { id: 'us20-1', n: 1, t: 'An unknown halogen compound', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`Gas X is 11.13% C, 14.83% O and 74.04% of one halogen by mass. Its vapour density is 6.603 g L⁻¹ at 120 °C and 1.00 bar. $R = 0.08314\ \mathrm{L\ bar\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$; 1 bar = 750.06 mmHg.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the molar mass of X.', a: 215.8, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'M = ρRT/P.', w: R`$6.603 \times 0.08314 \times 393.15 = 215.8$` },
        { q: 'Find the formula of X.', h: 'Masses of each element in one mole.', w: R`C 24.0 (2), O 32.0 (2), halogen 159.8: two Br. $\mathrm{C_2O_2Br_2}$, oxalyl bromide, $\mathrm{BrOC{-}COBr}$.` },
        { q: R`1.50 g of X reacts with excess NaOH and releases a gas, collected over water at 22 °C and 1.00 bar (water vapour pressure 19.8 mmHg). The gas fills 175.2 mL. How many mol of gas per mol of X?`, a: 1.00, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Dry gas pressure = 1.00 bar − water vapour.', w: R`$P = 0.9736\ \mathrm{bar}$; $n = 6.95 \times 10^{-3}$, the same as the moles of X. $\mathrm{C_2O_2Br_2 + 4OH^- \rightarrow 2Br^- + CO_3^{2-} + CO + 2H_2O}$ (the gas is CO; carbonate gives $\mathrm{BaCO_3}$ with Ba²⁺).` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us20-2', n: 2, t: 'A fluorescent indicator', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-1-3'],
      s: R`The base form of fluorescein absorbs at 490 nm and emits at 515 nm. Phosphoric acid: $\mathrm{p}K_1 = 2.12$, $\mathrm{p}K_2 = 7.21$, $\mathrm{p}K_3 = 12.32$. $h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\ \mathrm{J\ s}$, $c = 2.998 \times 10^8\ \mathrm{m\ s^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How much energy is lost as heat per mole of photons absorbed and re-emitted, in kJ mol⁻¹?', a: 11.85, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Difference of the two photon energies × N_A.', w: R`$4.054 \times 10^{-19} - 3.857 \times 10^{-19} = 1.97 \times 10^{-20}\ \mathrm{J}$; $\times N_A = 11.9\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`How many mol of NaOH must be added to 100 mL of 0.100 M $\mathrm{H_3PO_4}$ to make a pH 6.80 buffer?`, a: 0.0128, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'First proton fully, then the HPO₄²⁻ fraction.', w: R`$[\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}]/[\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}] = 10^{-0.41} = 0.389$; fraction 0.280; NaOH $= 0.0100 + 0.0028 = 0.0128\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'And for pH 12.80 (count the free OH⁻ too)?', a: 0.0338, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'Two protons fully; PO₄³⁻ fraction; plus 0.0631 M OH⁻.', w: R`$0.0200 + 0.0100 \times 0.751 + 0.0063 = 0.0338\ \mathrm{mol}$. At pH 10 there is no good buffer: almost everything is $\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}$, with no partner in useful amounts.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us20-3', n: 3, t: 'Iron pentacarbonyl', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 13, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-2', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`$\Delta_f H$ (kJ mol⁻¹) / $S^\circ$ (J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹): Fe(s) 0 / 27.3; Fe(g) 416.3 / –; CO(g) −110.5 / 197.7; $\mathrm{Fe(CO)_5(g)}$ −733.8 / 445.2.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the average Fe–C bond enthalpy.', a: 119.5, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Fe(CO)₅(g) → Fe(g) + 5CO(g).', w: R`$416.3 + 5(-110.5) + 733.8 = 597.6\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $\div 5 = 119.5\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Fe(CO)₅ is trigonal bipyramidal with linear Fe–C–O.` },
        { q: R`For $\mathrm{Fe(s) + 5CO(g) \rightarrow Fe(CO)_5(g)}$, find K at 298 K.`, a: 94, u: '', tol: 0.03, h: 'ΔH = −181.3 kJ; ΔS = −570.6 J K⁻¹.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -181\,300 + 298(570.6) = -11.3\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = e^{4.545} = 94$` },
        { q: 'At 200 °C with CO at 2.0 bar, what equilibrium pressure of Fe(CO)₅ (bar) forms over iron?', a: 5.3e-9, u: 'bar', tol: 0.05, h: 'p(Fe(CO)₅) = K · p(CO)⁵.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = +88.6\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = 1.6 \times 10^{-10}$; $\times 2.0^5 = 5.3 \times 10^{-9}\ \mathrm{bar}$: the carbonyl forms cool and breaks up hot, which is how the carbonyl process purifies the metal.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us20-4', n: 4, t: 'BrO kinetics', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`BrO radicals decay by $\mathrm{2BrO \rightarrow 2Br + O_2}$ ($k_a$) and $\mathrm{2BrO \rightarrow Br_2 + O_2}$ ($k_b$). Plots of $1/[\mathrm{BrO}]$ against t are straight. At 298 K the slope is $6.40 \times 10^{-12}\ \mathrm{cm^3\ molecule^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$ without ozone and $7.00 \times 10^{-13}$ with excess $\mathrm{O_3}$ (which turns Br straight back into BrO). The slope equals $2(k_a + k_b)$ without $\mathrm{O_3}$ and $2k_b$ with it.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Without O₃, how long does BrO take to fall from 2.5 × 10¹⁴ to half that, in s?', a: 6.25e-4, u: 's', tol: 0.01, h: 'Second order: t½ = 1/(slope × [A]₀).', w: R`$1 \div (6.40 \times 10^{-12} \times 2.5 \times 10^{14}) = 6.25 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{s}$` },
        { q: R`Find $k_a$.`, a: 2.85e-12, u: 'cm³ molecule⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k_b = 3.50 × 10⁻¹³.', w: R`$k_a = 3.20 \times 10^{-12} - 3.50 \times 10^{-13} = 2.85 \times 10^{-12}$` },
        { q: R`With $\mathrm{O_3}$, the slope at 267 K is $9.10 \times 10^{-13}$. Find the activation energy, in kJ mol⁻¹.`, a: -5.6, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', abs: 0.15, h: 'Arrhenius with the two temperatures.', w: R`$8.314\ln(7.00/9.10) \div (1/267 - 1/298) = -5.6\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$: negative, so the rate falls as T rises, a sign of a loosely bound intermediate.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us20-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-28-1', 'ca-18-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) chlorine through hot concentrated NaOH (b) heating solid $\mathrm{MgCO_3}$ (c) iron(III) nitrate with potassium thiocyanate`, h: 'Hot alkali gives chlorate.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Cl_2 + OH^- \rightarrow ClO_3^- + Cl^- + H_2O}$ (b) $\mathrm{MgCO_3 \rightarrow MgO + CO_2}$ (c) $\mathrm{Fe^{3+} + SCN^- \rightarrow [Fe(SCN)]^{2+}}$ (blood red)` },
        { q: R`(d) methyl ethanoate heated with KOH(aq) (e) $\mathrm{N_2O}$ with sodium amide (f) lithium-6 captures a neutron and emits an α particle`, h: 'Count nucleons.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{CH_3COO^- + CH_3OH}$ (e) $\mathrm{N_2O + NaNH_2 \rightarrow NaN_3 + NaOH + NH_3}$ (f) $\mathrm{^6Li + {}^1n \rightarrow {}^4He + {}^3H}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us20-6', n: 6, t: 'Electrolysing copper sulfate', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 13, lec: ['ca-24-1', 'ca-24-2'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{CuSO_4{\cdot}5H_2O}$ is dissolved in 0.500 L of pH 1.00 sulfuric acid and electrolysed with Pt electrodes at 0.150 A. Only $\mathrm{O_2}$ forms at the anode; after 12.0 min, $\mathrm{H_2}$ starts forming at the cathode. $E^\circ$: $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu}$ 0.337 V; $\mathrm{O_2/H_2O}$ 1.229 V. $F = 96\,500$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What mass of CuSO₄·5H₂O was dissolved?', a: 0.1397, u: 'g', tol: 0.005, h: 'All the Cu is plated in 12.0 min.', w: R`$108\ \mathrm{C} = 1.119 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}\ e^-$; Cu $5.60 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} \times 249.69 = 0.140\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`Find the starting cell potential at 298 K with $p(\mathrm{O_2}) = 0.20$ bar.`, a: -0.910, u: 'V', abs: 0.003, h: '2Cu²⁺ + 2H₂O → 2Cu + O₂ + 4H⁺, n = 4.', w: R`$[\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}] = 1.12 \times 10^{-3}$; $E = -0.892 - \tfrac{0.05916}{4}\log\dfrac{0.20 \times 10^{-4}}{(1.12 \times 10^{-3})^2} = -0.910\ \mathrm{V}$` },
        { q: 'After how many minutes are the volumes of O₂ and H₂ made equal?', a: 24.0, u: 'min', tol: 0.005, h: 'O₂ takes 4 e⁻ from the start; H₂ takes 2 e⁻ after 12 min.', w: R`$q/4 = (q - q_1)/2$ gives $q = 2q_1$: 24.0 min` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us20-7', n: 7, t: 'Silicon versus carbon', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-1-4'],
      s: 'Silicon sits below carbon and beside phosphorus.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Why does silicon have only a diamond-like form, and why does it conduct better than diamond?', h: 'π overlap and band gaps.', w: 'Si–Si π bonds are weak (3p orbitals overlap poorly), so no graphite analogue. Its band gap is much smaller than diamond’s, so heat promotes some electrons into the conduction band.' },
        { q: R`Why does $\mathrm{SiF_4}$ add fluoride (to $\mathrm{SiF_5^-}$, trigonal bipyramidal, and $\mathrm{SiF_6^{2-}}$, octahedral) while $\mathrm{CF_4}$ and $\mathrm{Si(CH_3)_4}$ do not?`, h: 'Size and electron-poor Si.', w: 'Si is big enough for 5–6 neighbours, and the electronegative F make it a strong Lewis acid (a low-lying σ* acceptor). Carbon is too small; methyl groups do not make Si electron-poor.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us20-8', n: 8, t: 'Cycloalkane versus alkene', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-14-2', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: R`Methylcyclopentane and 2-methylpent-2-ene are both $\mathrm{C_6H_{12}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many constitutional isomers of C₆H₁₁Cl can come from monochlorinating methylcyclopentane?', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Count distinct carbons.', w: 'Four: on C1 (tertiary), C2, C3 or the CH₃. (Counting stereoisomers gives 10.)' },
        { q: 'Which reacts with water and acid, and what forms? Which compound is more stable, and why?', h: 'Markovnikov.', w: '2-Methylpent-2-ene gives 2-methylpentan-2-ol. The cycloalkane is more stable: a C=C bond is weaker than two C–C σ bonds. A chiral C₆H₁₂: 3-methylpent-1-ene.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2019, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Studtite, a uranium peroxide · iodate solubility · making ammonia by electrolysis · ascorbic acid kinetics · net equations · beryllium versus barium · borazine · a Grignard route', qs: [
    { id: 'us19-1', n: 1, t: 'Studtite', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-2-3', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Studtite, $\mathrm{MO_x(H_2O)_y}$, decomposes at 520 °C into $\mathrm{O_2}$, water vapour and solid $\mathrm{MO_3}$. A 1.2000 g sample is heated in a sealed 1.000 L vessel. At 200.0 °C the pressure is 425.9 mmHg; at 25.0 °C it is 53.62 mmHg (water’s vapour pressure is 23.80 mmHg; condensed water has negligible volume). $R = 62.36\ \mathrm{L\ mmHg\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many moles of O₂ formed?', a: 1.604e-3, u: 'mol', tol: 0.005, h: 'At 25 °C, the O₂ partial pressure is 53.62 − 23.80.', w: R`$29.82 \div (62.36 \times 298.15) = 1.604 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'How many moles of H₂O formed?', a: 0.01283, u: 'mol', tol: 0.005, h: 'Total gas at 200 °C minus O₂.', w: R`$425.9 \div (62.36 \times 473.15) = 0.01443$; $- 0.00160 = 0.01283\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: R`Find the molar mass of $\mathrm{MO_3}$, assuming y = 4.`, a: 286.0, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Mass of solid = 1.2000 − gases; moles = n(H₂O)/4.', w: R`$1.2000 - 0.0513 - 0.2311 = 0.9175\ \mathrm{g}$; $\div 3.208 \times 10^{-3} = 286.0$; M = 238.0: uranium. x = 4, so studtite is $\mathrm{UO_2(O_2)(H_2O)_4}$: U is +6 with one peroxide.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us19-2', n: 2, t: 'Iodate solubility', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-23-4', 'ca-6-1'],
      s: R`Saturated barium iodate is acidified with excess KI ($\mathrm{IO_3^- + 8I^- + 6H^+ \rightarrow 3I_3^- + 3H_2O}$) and the triiodide titrated with thiosulfate.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`A 25.00 mL aliquot needs 15.00 mL of 0.0200 M $\mathrm{Na_2S_2O_3}$. Find $K_{sp}$ of $\mathrm{Ba(IO_3)_2}$.`, a: 4.0e-9, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: '1 IO₃⁻ ↔ 3 I₃⁻ ↔ 6 S₂O₃²⁻.', w: R`$3.00 \times 10^{-4} \div 6 = 5.00 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $[\mathrm{IO_3^-}] = 2.00 \times 10^{-3}$, $[\mathrm{Ba^{2+}}] = 1.00 \times 10^{-3}$; $K_{sp} = 4.0 \times 10^{-9}$` },
        { q: R`$K_{sp}$ of calcium iodate hexahydrate is $1.15 \times 10^{-7}$ at 10.0 °C and $1.64 \times 10^{-6}$ at 30.0 °C. Find ΔH° of dissolution.`, a: 94.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'van ’t Hoff.', w: R`$8.314\ln(1.64/0.115) \div (1/283.15 - 1/303.15) = 94.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔS° of dissolution.', a: 202, u: 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG = −RT ln K = ΔH − TΔS at 283.15 K.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ(283) = 37.6\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $\Delta S^\circ = (94.8 - 37.6) \div 283.15 = 202$: the six waters of the hydrate are released.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us19-3', n: 3, t: 'Ammonia by electrolysis', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-24-1', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{N_2(g) + 3H_2O(l) \rightarrow 2NH_3(g) + 1.5O_2(g)}$. $\Delta_f G$: $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ −237.1, $\mathrm{NH_3(g)}$ −16.4 kJ mol⁻¹. $F = 96\,500$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find E° of the cell at 298 K.', a: -1.17, u: 'V', abs: 0.005, h: 'ΔG° = 2(−16.4) − 3(−237.1); 6 electrons.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 678.5\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $E^\circ = -678\,500 \div (6 \times 96\,500) = -1.17\ \mathrm{V}$. NH₃ forms at the cathode (N is reduced); with ΔS° > 0, |E°| shrinks as T rises.` },
        { q: 'A run at 1.50 A for 2500 s gives 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol NH₃. What is the Faradaic yield, in %?', a: 4.63, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '3 electrons per NH₃.', w: R`$3750\ \mathrm{C} = 0.0389\ \mathrm{mol}\ e^-$; max NH₃ $= 0.0130\ \mathrm{mol}$; $6.0 \times 10^{-4} \div 0.0130 = 4.6\%$` },
        { q: R`In base, $E^\circ(\mathrm{O_2 + 2H_2O + 4e^- \rightarrow 4OH^-}) = +0.40\ \mathrm{V}$. Find E° for $\mathrm{N_2 + 6H_2O + 6e^- \rightarrow 2NH_3 + 6OH^-}$.`, a: -0.77, u: 'V', abs: 0.01, h: 'The cell voltage is the same in acid or base.', w: R`$E^\circ_{cell} = E^\circ(\mathrm{N_2}) - E^\circ(\mathrm{O_2})$, so $-1.17 + 0.40 = -0.77\ \mathrm{V}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us19-4', n: 4, t: 'Ascorbic acid and ferricyanide', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 11, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`Ascorbic acid (HAsc, $\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.10$) reduces $\mathrm{Fe(CN)_6^{3-}}$, followed at 416 nm with HAsc in large excess at $[\mathrm{H^+}] = 0.06\ \mathrm{M}$. Plots of $\ln[\mathrm{Fe(CN)_6^{3-}}]$ against time are straight, with slope $-0.01049\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $[\mathrm{HAsc}] = 0.0120\ \mathrm{M}$ and $-0.003496\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at 0.00400 M.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the overall equation, and give the order in each reactant.', h: 'Two Fe(III) per ascorbic acid.', w: R`$\mathrm{C_6H_8O_6 + 2Fe(CN)_6^{3-} \rightarrow C_6H_6O_6 + 2Fe(CN)_6^{4-} + 2H^+}$. First order in ferricyanide (straight ln plot) and in HAsc (3× concentration, 3× slope).` },
        { q: 'Find the second-order rate constant.', a: 0.874, u: 'M⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k = k_obs/[HAsc].', w: R`$0.01049 \div 0.0120 = 0.874\ \mathrm{M^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. Lower [H⁺] speeds it up because the anion Asc⁻ reacts much faster than HAsc: $k_{obs} = k_1 + k_2/[\mathrm{H^+}]$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us19-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-28-1', 'ca-30-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) ethanoic acid with aqueous NaCN (b) solid AgBr with concentrated aqueous ammonia (excess) (c) sodium sulfite boiled with sulfur`, h: 'HCN is weaker than ethanoic acid.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{CH_3COOH + CN^- \rightarrow CH_3COO^- + HCN}$ (b) $\mathrm{AgBr + NH_3 \rightarrow [Ag(NH_3)_2]^+ + Br^-}$ (partly; AgBr is less soluble than AgCl) (c) $\mathrm{SO_3^{2-} + S_8 \rightarrow S_2O_3^{2-}}$` },
        { q: R`(d) copper(II) sulfate added to KI solution (e) 4-methylaniline with cold $\mathrm{NaNO_2/HCl}$ (f) U-235 absorbs a neutron and splits into Cs-140 and Rb-93`, h: 'Count the neutrons released.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{Cu^{2+} + I^- \rightarrow CuI + I_3^-}$ (e) the diazonium ion $\mathrm{CH_3C_6H_4N_2^+}$ + $\mathrm{H_2O}$ (f) $\mathrm{^{235}U + n \rightarrow {}^{140}Cs + {}^{93}Rb + 3n}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us19-6', n: 6, t: 'Beryllium versus barium', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-1-4', 'ca-3-5', 'ca-22-2'],
      s: R`Gaseous beryllium chloride dimerises: $\mathrm{2BeCl_2 \rightleftharpoons Be_2Cl_4}$, $K_p = 2.9$ at 800 K.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Why is the ionisation energy of Be much higher than that of Ba, and why does Ba (but not Be) release energy on gaining an electron?', h: '2s versus 6s; where would the extra electron go?', w: 'Be loses a 2s electron close to the nucleus; Ba a far 6s one. Both are ns²; the added electron goes to 2p for Be (much higher) but to the nearby 5d for Ba.' },
        { q: 'At 0.250 bar total and 800 K, what is the mole fraction of dimer?', a: 0.328, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'p_D = Kp·p_M²; p_M + p_D = 0.250.', w: R`$2.9p_M^2 + p_M - 0.250 = 0$: $p_M = 0.168$, $p_D = 0.082$; $x_D = 0.33$. Monomer linear; dimer has two bridging Cl, each Be trigonal planar.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us19-7', n: 7, t: 'Borazine', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-3-4', 'ca-3-6'],
      s: R`B–N distances: 156.4, 142.9 and 139.1 pm belong to $\mathrm{BH_3NH_3}$, borazine $\mathrm{B_3N_3H_6}$ and $\mathrm{BH_2NH_2}$ (in some order).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Match the distances, and explain why borazine (bp 55 °C) boils higher when N-methylated (84 °C) while piperazine boils lower when N-methylated.', h: 'Bond orders; N lone pairs.', w: 'BH₃NH₃ 156.4 (single dative), borazine 142.9 (order ~1.5), BH₂NH₂ 139.1 (double). Piperazine loses an N–H hydrogen-bond donor when methylated. Borazine’s N lone pairs are tied up in ring π bonding, so it barely H-bonds anyway; methylation just adds dispersion.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us19-8', n: 8, t: 'A Grignard route', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-17-1', 'ca-15-1'],
      s: 'Ethylmagnesium bromide is made in dry ether, added to ethanal, worked up with acid, and the alcohol dehydrated.',
      p: [
        { q: 'What forms if the ether is wet? What alcohol forms, and which alkenes come from it?', h: 'Grignards are strong bases.', w: 'Ethane (the Grignard is protonated). Butan-2-ol forms; dehydration gives but-1-ene, cis- and trans-but-2-ene, with trans-but-2-ene the major product (most substituted, least strained).' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2018, mins: 105,
    topics: 'An unknown chiral acid · dissolving potassium iodate · a potentiometric chloride titration · an iridium oxidation · net equations · lithium halide crystals · nitric oxide · amides', qs: [
    { id: 'us18-1', n: 1, t: 'An unknown acid', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-25-1', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: R`A monoprotic acid contains only C, H and O. Burning 1.200 g gives 1.759 g $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and 0.7200 g $\mathrm{H_2O}$. Another 1.200 g, in 50.0 mL of water, is titrated with 0.2500 M NaOH: the end point is at 53.29 mL, and the pH after 15.00 mL is 3.45.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the molar mass of the acid.', a: 90.1, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'Monoprotic.', w: R`$n = 0.01332\ \mathrm{mol}$; $1.200 \div 0.01332 = 90.1$. Empirical formula: C 0.03997, H 0.0799, O by difference 0.0400 mol: $\mathrm{CH_2O}$, so $\mathrm{C_3H_6O_3}$.` },
        { q: 'Find the pKa.', a: 3.86, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Henderson–Hasselbalch with base added / acid left.', w: R`$3.75 \times 10^{-3} \div 9.57 \times 10^{-3} = 0.392$; $\mathrm{p}K_a = 3.45 - \log 0.392 = 3.86$` },
        { q: 'What is the pH after 60.00 mL of NaOH?', a: 12.18, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Past the end point, excess OH⁻ sets the pH.', w: R`Excess $6.71\ \mathrm{mL} \times 0.2500 = 1.68 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$ in 110 mL; $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 0.0153$; pH 12.18. The acid rotates polarised light, so it is chiral: lactic acid, $\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)COOH}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us18-2', n: 2, t: 'Dissolving potassium iodate', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-1', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{KIO_3}$ ($M = 214.0$) is added in portions to 150.0 g of water at 20.50 °C in an insulated cup. The solution saturates after 9.95 g has dissolved (solution 159.95 g, taken as 159.95 mL), by which point the temperature has fallen by 4.02 °C. $c = 4.184\ \mathrm{J\ g^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$; take the average temperature as 291 K.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $K_{sp}$ of $\mathrm{KIO_3}$.`, a: 0.0845, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Concentration of each ion squared.', w: R`$0.04650\ \mathrm{mol} \div 0.15995\ \mathrm{L} = 0.291\ \mathrm{M}$; $K_{sp} = 0.0845$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔH° of dissolution.', a: 57.9, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'q = mcΔT, per mole dissolved.', w: R`$159.95 \times 4.184 \times 4.02 = 2690\ \mathrm{J}$; $\div 0.04650 = +57.9\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔS°.', a: 178, u: 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG° = −RT ln Ksp.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 5.98\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $(57.9 - 5.98) \div 291 = 178$. Dissolving ions often has a small ΔS because water orders itself round them.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us18-3', n: 3, t: 'A potentiometric titration', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`80.0 g of saline (density 1.00 g mL⁻¹) is titrated with 0.150 M $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$. A silver electrode in the sample is joined to a silver electrode in 1.00 M $\mathrm{Ag^+}$; the end point is at 21.6 mL. 298 K.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the mass percentage of NaCl.', a: 0.237, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of Ag⁺ at the end point.', w: R`$3.24 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol} \times 58.44 = 0.189\ \mathrm{g}$; $0.237\%$. The sample electrode is the anode (lower [Ag⁺]).` },
        { q: R`After 10.00 mL of titrant, the cell reads 0.475 V. Find $[\mathrm{Ag^+}]$ in the sample.`, a: 9.35e-9, u: 'M', tol: 0.03, h: 'E = 0.05916 log(1.00/[Ag⁺]).', w: R`$[\mathrm{Ag^+}] = 10^{-0.475/0.05916} = 9.4 \times 10^{-9}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
        { q: R`Hence find $K_{sp}$ of AgCl.`, a: 1.8e-10, u: '', tol: 0.03, h: 'Remaining Cl⁻ ÷ 90.0 mL.', w: R`$[\mathrm{Cl^-}] = (3.24 - 1.50) \times 10^{-3} \div 0.0900 = 0.0193$; $K_{sp} = 1.8 \times 10^{-10}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us18-4', n: 4, t: 'An iridium oxidation', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`A trimesityliridium complex (Ir) reacts with excess $\mathrm{O_2}$. With $[\mathrm{O_2}] = 1.25 \times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{M}$ and $[\mathrm{Ir}]_0 = 1.30 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{M}$, a plot of $1/[\mathrm{Ir}]$ against time is straight with slope $350\ \mathrm{M^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$; halving $[\mathrm{O_2}]$ halves the slope.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the rate law and find [Ir] after 100 s.', a: 2.34e-5, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: '1/[Ir] = 1/[Ir]₀ + k_obs t.', w: R`Rate $= k[\mathrm{Ir}]^2[\mathrm{O_2}]$. $1/[\mathrm{Ir}] = 7692 + 35\,000 = 42\,692$; $[\mathrm{Ir}] = 2.34 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
        { q: 'Find the third-order rate constant.', a: 2.8e4, u: 'M⁻² s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k = k_obs/[O₂].', w: R`$350 \div 0.0125 = 2.8 \times 10^4\ \mathrm{M^{-2}\ s^{-1}}$. A fast pre-equilibrium Ir + O₂ ⇌ IrO₂ followed by slow IrO₂ + Ir fits this.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us18-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-30-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) ethanoic acid on solid $\mathrm{Zn(OH)_2}$ (b) $\mathrm{PCl_3}$ with water (c) $\mathrm{H_2S}$ into copper(II) sulfate solution`, h: 'Phosphonic acid; an insoluble sulfide.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Zn(OH)_2 + CH_3COOH \rightarrow Zn^{2+} + CH_3COO^- + H_2O}$ (b) $\mathrm{PCl_3 + H_2O \rightarrow H_3PO_3 + H^+ + Cl^-}$ (c) $\mathrm{Cu^{2+} + H_2S \rightarrow CuS + H^+}$` },
        { q: R`(d) $\mathrm{KMnO_4}$ with concentrated HCl (e) $\mathrm{MgCO_3}$ with aqueous HF (f) methylbenzene with $\mathrm{HNO_3/H_2SO_4}$`, h: 'MgF₂ is insoluble.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + H^+ + Cl^- \rightarrow Mn^{2+} + Cl_2 + H_2O}$ (e) $\mathrm{MgCO_3 + HF \rightarrow MgF_2 + CO_2 + H_2O}$ (f) 2- and 4-nitromethylbenzene + $\mathrm{H_2O}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us18-6', n: 6, t: 'Lithium halide crystals', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-4-2', 'ca-3-1'],
      s: R`The lithium halides have the rock-salt structure (4 formula units per cell). Cell edges (pm): LiH 408.3, LiF 403.5, LiCl 513.6, LiBr 548.9, LiI 601.9.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the density of LiBr.', a: 3.49, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'M = 86.85.', w: R`$4 \times 86.85 \div (6.022 \times 10^{23} \times (5.489 \times 10^{-8})^3) = 3.49\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: 'Why is the LiCl → LiBr step in a smaller than LiF → LiCl, and why does a smaller cell mean a higher melting point (except LiH)?', h: 'The d-block contraction; Coulomb’s law.', w: 'Br follows the 3d metals, whose poorly shielding d electrons contract it, so Br⁻ is only a little bigger than Cl⁻. Attraction goes as 1/r, so smaller cells hold together more strongly. LiH is much less ionic (H is far less electronegative), so it melts low.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us18-7', n: 7, t: 'Nitric oxide', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-3-4', 'ca-1-4'],
      s: 'NO has 11 valence electrons. Its ionisation energy is 9.26 eV (1 eV = 96.485 kJ mol⁻¹); that of the N atom is 1400 kJ mol⁻¹.',
      p: [
        { q: R`Give the bond orders of NO, $\mathrm{NO^+}$ and $\mathrm{NO^-}$.`, h: 'The 11th electron is in π*.', w: R`NO 2.5; $\mathrm{NO^+}$ 3 (isoelectronic with N₂, shortest bond); $\mathrm{NO^-}$ 2.` },
        { q: 'Convert the ionisation energy of NO to kJ mol⁻¹.', a: 893, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: '× 96.485.', w: R`$9.26 \times 96.485 = 893\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, less than N: the electron leaves a high π* orbital, which lies closer to N 2p, so the unpaired electron sits mostly on N.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us18-8', n: 8, t: 'Amides', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-34-1', 'ca-18-1'],
      s: 'Ethanoic acid and diethylamine first form a salt, which loses water on strong heating to give N,N-diethylethanamide.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Why is ethanoic acid more acidic than ethanol, and diethylamine more basic than diphenylamine?', h: 'Delocalisation.', w: 'Ethanoate spreads its charge over two O by resonance; ethoxide cannot. Diphenylamine’s lone pair is delocalised into the rings, and protonation would cost that.' },
        { q: 'What is the first-formed salt, and why do the two N-ethyl groups of the amide give separate NMR signals?', h: 'Partial C–N double bond.', w: R`Diethylammonium ethanoate, $\mathrm{[(C_2H_5)_2NH_2]^+[CH_3COO]^-}$. The amide C–N bond has π character, so it is planar and rotation is slow: one ethyl is cis to O and the other trans.` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2017, mins: 105,
    topics: 'A fertiliser salt · copper(I) iodide equilibria · ozone kinetics · NO₂ dimerisation · net equations · germanium crystals · sulfur fluorides · Friedel–Crafts and nitration', qs: [
    { id: 'us17-1', n: 1, t: 'A fertiliser', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`A fertiliser contains only C, H, N and O. Burning 1.000 g gives 0.6979 g $\mathrm{CO_2}$, 0.7142 g $\mathrm{H_2O}$ and 0.2221 g $\mathrm{N_2}$. 1.000 g dissolved in 20.00 g of water freezes at −2.95 °C ($K_f = 1.86\ \mathrm{°C\ kg\ mol^{-1}}$).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the percentage of carbon by mass.', a: 19.05, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Mass of C in the CO₂.', w: R`$0.6979 \times 12.011/44.01 = 0.1905\ \mathrm{g}$: 19.05% (H 7.99%, N 22.21%, O 50.75%), giving $\mathrm{CH_5NO_2}$, formula mass 63.06` },
        { q: 'What is the apparent molar mass from the freezing point?', a: 31.5, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'm = ΔT/K_f, then moles of particles in 20.00 g water.', w: R`$1.586\ \mathrm{m} \times 0.02000 = 0.03172\ \mathrm{mol}$; $1.000 \div 0.03172 = 31.5$: half of 63.06, so each unit gives two particles. It is a salt: ammonium methanoate, $\mathrm{NH_4^+HCOO^-}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us17-2', n: 2, t: 'Copper(I) iodide', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`$K_{sp}(\mathrm{CuI}) = 1.2 \times 10^{-12}$; $K_f(\mathrm{CuI_2^-}) = 7.1 \times 10^8$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the total dissolved copper in a saturated CuI solution that is 0.0100 M in NaI.`, a: 8.5e-6, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'CuI + I⁻ ⇌ CuI₂⁻ has K = Ksp·Kf.', w: R`$K = 8.5 \times 10^{-4}$; $[\mathrm{CuI_2^-}] = 8.5 \times 10^{-6}$; $[\mathrm{Cu^+}]$ is only $1.2 \times 10^{-10}$` },
        { q: R`How many moles of NaI must be added to 1.00 L to dissolve $2.00 \times 10^{-3}$ mol of CuI completely?`, a: 2.35, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'Free I⁻ = [CuI₂⁻]/K, plus the I⁻ taken into the complex.', w: R`$[\mathrm{I^-}] = 2.00 \times 10^{-3} \div 8.52 \times 10^{-4} = 2.35\ \mathrm{M}$, plus $0.002$ mol bound: 2.35 mol` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us17-3', n: 3, t: 'Ozone decomposition', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 3, m: 13, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{2O_3 \rightarrow 3O_2}$ is followed by the total pressure in a fixed volume. $R = 62.4\ \mathrm{L\ mmHg\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$. At 363 K with 200 mmHg $\mathrm{O_2}$: at $p(\mathrm{O_3}) = 9.0$ mmHg the pressure rises at $1.50 \times 10^{-3}$ mmHg s⁻¹; at 18.0 mmHg, $5.94 \times 10^{-3}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the rate of $\mathrm{O_3}$ loss at 9.0 mmHg in mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹.`, a: 1.32e-7, u: 'mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Each 2 O₃ lost raises the gas count by 1.', w: R`O₃ falls at $3.00 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mmHg\ s^{-1}}$; $\div (62.4 \times 363) = 1.32 \times 10^{-7}$` },
        { q: 'What is the order in O₃?', a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.05, h: 'Doubling p(O₃) multiplies the rate by 3.96.', w: R`$\log 3.96 / \log 2 = 1.99$: second order. Doubling $\mathrm{O_2}$ halves the rate (order −1), so rate $= k[\mathrm{O_3}]^2/[\mathrm{O_2}]$: a fast O₃ ⇌ O₂ + O pre-equilibrium, then slow O + O₃.` },
        { q: R`At the same pressures the rate is $2.80 \times 10^{-3}$ at 363 K and $6.60 \times 10^{-3}$ mmHg s⁻¹ at 375 K. Estimate $E_a$.`, a: 80.9, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Arrhenius.', w: R`$8.314\ln(2.357) \div (1/363 - 1/375) = 80.9\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us17-4', n: 4, t: 'NO₂ dimerisation', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-22-2'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{2NO_2(g) \rightleftharpoons N_2O_4(g)}$. $\Delta_f H$: $\mathrm{NO_2}$ 33.2, $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$ 11.1 kJ mol⁻¹; $S^\circ$: 240.1 and 304.4 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. $\Delta H_{vap}$: $\mathrm{NO_2}$ 27.7, $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$ 38.1 kJ mol⁻¹.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find ΔG° at 288 K.', a: -4.67, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔH = −55.3 kJ; ΔS = −175.8 J K⁻¹.', w: R`$-55\,300 + 288(175.8) = -4.67\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Find K at 288 K.', a: 7.03, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'e^(−ΔG/RT).', w: R`$e^{1.950} = 7.0$` },
        { q: 'Above what temperature does K fall below 1?', a: 315, u: 'K', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔG = 0.', w: R`$55\,300 \div 175.8 = 315\ \mathrm{K}$. In the liquid phase ΔH is less negative: $-55.3 + 2(27.7) - 38.1 = -38.0\ \mathrm{kJ}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us17-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) sodium chromate with barium nitrate (b) zinc in concentrated NaOH (c) heating silver(I) oxide`, h: 'A yellow precipitate; amphoteric zinc; a noble-metal oxide.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Ba^{2+} + CrO_4^{2-} \rightarrow BaCrO_4}$ (b) $\mathrm{Zn + OH^- + H_2O \rightarrow [Zn(OH)_4]^{2-} + H_2}$ (c) $\mathrm{Ag_2O \rightarrow Ag + O_2}$` },
        { q: R`(d) potassium with propan-2-ol (e) $\mathrm{ClF_3}$ with $\mathrm{AsF_5}$ (f) carbon-11 emits a positron`, h: 'A fluoride-ion acceptor.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{K + (CH_3)_2CHOH \rightarrow K^+ + (CH_3)_2CHO^- + H_2}$ (e) $\mathrm{ClF_3 + AsF_5 \rightarrow [ClF_2]^+[AsF_6]^-}$ (fluoride-ion transfer) (f) $\mathrm{^{11}C \rightarrow {}^{11}B + e^+}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us17-6', n: 6, t: 'Germanium crystals', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-4-2', 'ca-3-1'],
      s: 'Germanium has the diamond structure: 8 atoms per cubic cell, edge 0.5658 nm.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the density of germanium.', a: 5.33, u: 'g cm⁻³', tol: 0.01, h: 'M = 72.63.', w: R`$8 \times 72.63 \div (6.022 \times 10^{23} \times (5.658 \times 10^{-8})^3) = 5.33\ \mathrm{g\ cm^{-3}}$` },
        { q: 'Why do band gaps fall C ≫ Si > Ge, and why does a trace of gallium raise germanium’s conductivity?', h: 'Bond strength; holes.', w: 'Bigger atoms form longer, weaker bonds, so bonding and antibonding bands lie closer. Ga has one fewer valence electron than Ge, so each Ga leaves a hole in the valence band that carries current.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us17-7', n: 7, t: 'Sulfur fluorides', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-3-6'],
      s: 'SF₄ boils at −38 °C and SF₆ at −64 °C.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the shapes of SF₄ and SF₆, and explain why SF₄ boils higher despite being lighter.', h: 'Dipoles.', w: 'SF₄ is see-saw (one lone pair) and polar; SF₆ is octahedral and non-polar. SF₄ adds dipole–dipole attraction, and SF₆’s tightly held F electrons are not very polarisable.' },
        { q: 'In SF₄, why are the axial and equatorial F different, and which S–F bonds are longer?', h: 'Three-centre axial bonding.', w: 'The lone pair sits equatorial. The two axial bonds are longer and weaker (a 3-centre 4-electron F–S–F unit); the two equatorial bonds are shorter.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us17-8', n: 8, t: 'Friedel–Crafts and nitration', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-30-1', 'ca-34-1'],
      s: 'Isopropylbenzene is nitrated, reduced to the amine and acetylated.',
      p: [
        { q: 'How is isopropylbenzene made from benzene, and why is nitration mostly para with little meta or ortho?', h: 'Electron donation and bulk.', w: 'Friedel–Crafts alkylation with 2-chloropropane/AlCl₃ (or propene/H⁺). The alkyl group donates electrons, stabilising ortho/para attack more than meta; its bulk blocks ortho, so para wins.' },
        { q: 'Give the reagents to turn the nitro compound into 4-isopropylacetanilide.', h: 'Reduce, then acylate.', w: 'Sn/HCl (then NaOH) or H₂/Pd to give 4-isopropylaniline; then ethanoic anhydride (or ethanoyl chloride).' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2016, mins: 105,
    topics: 'Diphosgene · ethanoate buffers · the lead–acid cell · aspirin hydrolysis kinetics · net equations · breaking Cl₂ with light · Group 15 halides · radical versus ionic HBr addition', qs: [
    { id: 'us16-1', n: 1, t: 'A chlorine compound of C and O', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`Liquid A contains only C, O and Cl and is hydrolysed by water to $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and HCl. $R = 0.08206\ \mathrm{L\ atm\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: '3.00 g of A vaporises completely in a 1.00 L flask at 150.0 °C, giving 0.527 atm. Find its molar mass.', a: 197.8, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'n = PV/RT.', w: R`$n = 0.527 \div (0.08206 \times 423.15) = 0.01517$; $M = 197.8$` },
        { q: R`0.300 g of A is hydrolysed and the HCl titrated: 40.44 mL of 0.1500 M NaOH. Find the mass percentage of Cl.`, a: 71.7, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Moles of NaOH = moles of Cl.', w: R`$6.066 \times 10^{-3} \times 35.45 = 0.2150\ \mathrm{g}$; $71.7\%$` },
        { q: 'How many Cl atoms per molecule, and what is A?', a: 4, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: '0.717 × 197.8 ÷ 35.45.', w: R`4 Cl (141.8); the rest, 56.0, is $\mathrm{C_2O_2}$. A is $\mathrm{ClC(O)OCCl_3}$ (“diphosgene”): $\mathrm{C_2O_2Cl_4 + 2H_2O \rightarrow 2CO_2 + 4HCl}$. Its % Cl equals phosgene’s because its formula is exactly $\mathrm{(COCl_2)_2}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us16-2', n: 2, t: 'An ethanoate buffer', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-25-1'],
      s: R`1.00 L of 0.0800 M ethanoic acid ($K_a = 1.8 \times 10^{-5}$, $\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.745$) has 4.10 g of sodium ethanoate (82.03 g mol⁻¹) dissolved in it.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the pH.', a: 4.54, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Henderson–Hasselbalch.', w: R`$0.04998\ \mathrm{mol}$; $4.745 + \log(0.0500/0.0800) = 4.54$` },
        { q: 'Find the pH after adding 2.00 mL of 1.00 M HCl.', a: 4.51, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Ethanoate + H⁺ → ethanoic acid.', w: R`$4.745 + \log(0.0480/0.0820) = 4.51$` },
        { q: 'What mass of NaOH added to 1.00 L of 0.0800 M ethanoic acid gives the same pH as in the first part (4.54)?', a: 1.23, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'x/(0.0800 − x) = 10^(4.54 − 4.745).', w: R`Ratio 0.624; $x = 0.0308\ \mathrm{mol} \times 40.00 = 1.23\ \mathrm{g}$. Chloroethanoic acid is stronger, so the same recipe would give a lower pH.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us16-3', n: 3, t: 'The lead–acid cell', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{Pb + PbO_2 + 4H^+ + 2SO_4^{2-} \rightarrow 2PbSO_4 + 2H_2O}$ (n = 2). Take $E^\circ = 1.64 + 0.00138\,T$ (V, T in K). $F = 96\,500$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find ΔG° at 318 K.', a: -401, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'E° first.', w: R`$E^\circ = 2.079\ \mathrm{V}$; $\Delta G^\circ = -2F E^\circ = -401\ \mathrm{kJ}$. PbO₂ is the cathode, Pb the anode.` },
        { q: 'Find ΔH° and ΔS° (enter ΔS°).', a: 266, u: 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔS = nF dE/dT; ΔH = −nF × 1.64.', w: R`$\Delta S^\circ = 2 \times 96\,500 \times 0.00138 = +266$; $\Delta H^\circ = -317\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Find the cell voltage at 15 °C, pH 4.0 and 0.20 M sulfate.', a: 1.54, u: 'V', abs: 0.005, h: 'Q = 1/([H⁺]⁴[SO₄²⁻]²).', w: R`$E^\circ(288) = 2.037$; $\ln Q = \ln(2.5 \times 10^{17}) = 40.1$; $E = 2.037 - 0.01241 \times 40.1 = 1.54\ \mathrm{V}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us16-4', n: 4, t: 'Aspirin hydrolysis', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`Acetylsalicylate (X) is hydrolysed by hydroxide in buffers at 60 °C. Run 1, pH 10.30: $[\mathrm{X}]$ falls from $3.50 \times 10^{-4}$ to $2.07 \times 10^{-4}$ M in 900 s. Run 2, pH 10.80: from $3.55 \times 10^{-4}$ to $1.70 \times 10^{-4}$ M in 400 s. Take $K_w = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the pseudo-first-order rate constant for run 1.', a: 5.84e-4, u: 's⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k′ = ln([X]₀/[X])/t.', w: R`$\ln(3.50/2.07) \div 900 = 5.84 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ (run 2: $1.84 \times 10^{-3}$)` },
        { q: R`What is the order in $\mathrm{OH^-}$, and the second-order constant k?`, a: 2.92, u: 'M⁻¹ s⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: '[OH⁻] rises 3.16×; compare k′.', w: R`k′ rises 3.15×: first order. $k = 5.84 \times 10^{-4} \div 2.00 \times 10^{-4} = 2.92\ \mathrm{M^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$. Rate $= k[\mathrm{OH^-}][\mathrm{X}]$; any mechanism must have OH⁻ before or in the slow step.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us16-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-14-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) silver heated with concentrated nitric acid (b) $\mathrm{SO_3}$ added to water (c) $\mathrm{PbCO_3}$ added to hydriodic acid`, h: 'NO₂ forms; PbI₂ is insoluble.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Ag + H^+ + NO_3^- \rightarrow Ag^+ + NO_2 + H_2O}$ (b) $\mathrm{SO_3 + H_2O \rightarrow H^+ + HSO_4^-}$ (c) $\mathrm{PbCO_3 + H^+ + I^- \rightarrow PbI_2 + CO_2 + H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`(d) $\mathrm{H_2O_2}$ with a little catalase or $\mathrm{MnO_2}$ (e) $\mathrm{Br_2}$ with ethylbenzene in UV light (f) carbon-14 decays`, h: 'Benzylic radical; β⁻.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{H_2O_2 \rightarrow H_2O + O_2}$ (e) $\mathrm{C_6H_5CHBrCH_3 + HBr}$ (f) $\mathrm{^{14}C \rightarrow {}^{14}N + e^-}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us16-6', n: 6, t: 'Breaking Cl₂ with light', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-1-3', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: R`The Cl–Cl bond enthalpy is 242 kJ mol⁻¹; $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ vibrates at $1.68 \times 10^{13}$ Hz. $h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\ \mathrm{J\ s}$, $c = 2.998 \times 10^8\ \mathrm{m\ s^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What is the longest wavelength that can break a Cl–Cl bond, in nm?', a: 494, u: 'nm', tol: 0.005, h: 'Energy per molecule first.', w: R`$242\,000 \div 6.022 \times 10^{23} = 4.02 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$; $\lambda = hc/E = 494\ \mathrm{nm}$ (visible light can start chlorination).` },
        { q: 'One vibrational quantum (hf) is what percentage of the bond enthalpy?', a: 2.77, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'E = hf.', w: R`$1.113 \times 10^{-20} \div 4.02 \times 10^{-19} = 2.77\%$. $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ does not absorb IR: its vibration does not change a dipole. The energy falls as atoms approach (electron–nucleus attraction) until nuclear repulsion takes over at short range.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us16-7', n: 7, t: 'Group 15 halides', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-3-1'],
      s: 'PCl₅ has two P–Cl lengths (three at 202 pm, two at 214 pm); F–N–F in NF₃ is 102.5° but F–P–F in PF₃ is 96.3°.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Explain the two bond lengths in PCl₅, and why solid PCl₅ is ionic.', h: 'Trigonal bipyramid.', w: 'The two axial bonds form a 3-centre 4-electron unit and are longer than the three equatorial bonds. In the solid, PCl₅ transfers Cl⁻ to give [PCl₄]⁺[PCl₆]⁻, which pack well.' },
        { q: 'Why is the angle larger in NF₃, and why is SbF₃ non-volatile while AsF₃ boils at 60 °C?', h: 's-character; ionicity.', w: 'N’s compact 2s and 2p mix more, so its bonds have more s-character than P’s near-pure p bonds (angles near 90°). Sb–F is more ionic, so Sb bridges to F on neighbours and builds a network.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us16-8', n: 8, t: 'Radical versus ionic HBr addition', f: 'Organic', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-14-2', 'ca-15-1', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: 'HBr is added to 3-methylhex-2-ene in the dark (product A) and with peroxides (product B).',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give A and B, and say whether A has stereoisomers.', h: 'Tertiary cation versus tertiary radical.', w: 'A: 3-bromo-3-methylhexane (Markovnikov, via the tertiary cation); it is chiral, so a racemic pair forms. B: 2-bromo-3-methylhexane, because Br• adds first to give the tertiary radical.' },
        { q: 'A is heated with a strong base. How many alkenes (counting E/Z isomers) can form?', a: 5, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'H can be lost from three different carbons.', w: '3-Methylhex-2-ene (E and Z), 3-methylhex-3-ene (E and Z) and 2-ethylpent-1-ene: five. The trisubstituted ones dominate.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2015, mins: 105,
    topics: 'A chromium chloride hydrate · titrating an amino acid salt · thermodynamics of water · net equations · Fe(phen)₃²⁺ kinetics · H₂O versus H₂S · chlorine · C₃H₆O isomers', qs: [
    { id: 'us15-1', n: 1, t: 'A chromium chloride hydrate', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-24-1', 'ca-2-3', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`A green salt has formula $\mathrm{CrCl_n(H_2O)_m}$. $F = 96\,485\ \mathrm{C\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Electrolysing a solution at 1.50 A for 1600 s deposits 0.4312 g of Cr. How many electrons per Cr?', a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'Moles of e⁻ ÷ moles of Cr.', w: R`$2400 \div 96\,485 = 0.02487$; Cr $0.008292$; ratio 3.00, so n = 3` },
        { q: R`Heating 2.500 g to constant mass leaves 1.486 g of $\mathrm{CrCl_3}$. Find m.`, a: 6, u: '', abs: 0.05, h: 'Water lost ÷ moles CrCl₃.', w: R`$1.014\ \mathrm{g} = 0.05627\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{H_2O}$; $\mathrm{CrCl_3} = 0.009384$; m = 6.00` },
        { q: R`0.400 g of the salt (266.47 g mol⁻¹), titrated at once with 0.250 M $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$ (chromate indicator), needs 6.00 mL. How many Cl⁻ per Cr react?`, a: 1, u: '', abs: 0.03, h: 'Moles AgNO₃ ÷ moles salt.', w: R`$1.50 \times 10^{-3} \div 1.501 \times 10^{-3} = 1.00$: only one Cl⁻ is free. The others are bonded to Cr(III), which swaps ligands slowly: $\mathrm{[CrCl_2(H_2O)_4]Cl{\cdot}2H_2O}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us15-2', n: 2, t: 'Titrating leucine hydrochloride', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-34-4'],
      s: R`40.0 mL of 0.0800 M leucine hydrochloride (diprotic: $\mathrm{p}K_{a1} = 2.36$, $\mathrm{p}K_{a2} = 9.60$) is titrated with 0.400 M NaOH.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the starting pH (do not neglect ionisation).', a: 1.78, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'x²/(0.0800 − x) = 10^(−2.36).', w: R`$x = 0.0166\ \mathrm{M}$; pH 1.78` },
        { q: 'What volume of NaOH brings the pH to 3.00?', a: 6.51, u: 'mL', tol: 0.01, h: '[A]/[HA] = 10^(0.64).', w: R`Ratio 4.37; fraction 0.814 of $3.20 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$ = $2.60 \times 10^{-3}$ mol; $6.51\ \mathrm{mL}$` },
        { q: 'What is the highest concentration the zwitterion reaches?', a: 0.0667, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'At the first equivalence point (8.00 mL added).', w: R`$3.20 \times 10^{-3} \div 0.0480\ \mathrm{L} = 0.0667\ \mathrm{M}$. Methyl red changes sharply at this first end point; phenolphthalein, sitting in the second buffer region, changes gradually.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us15-3', n: 3, t: 'Thermodynamics of water', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{H_2O(l) \rightleftharpoons H^+(aq) + OH^-(aq)}$, $K_w = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}$ at 298 K. $\Delta_f H$: $\mathrm{OH^-}$ −229.9, $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ −285.83 kJ mol⁻¹; $S^\circ(\mathrm{H_2O, l}) = 69.95$; for $\mathrm{H^+}$ both are 0.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find ΔS° for the ionisation.', a: -80.3, u: 'J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔH° = +55.93 kJ; ΔG° = −RT ln Kw.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 79.87\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $(55.93 - 79.87) \div 298 = -80.3$: the ions order the water round them. So $S^\circ(\mathrm{OH^-}) = -10.4$.` },
        { q: 'Find Kw at 40 °C.', a: 2.95e-14, u: '', tol: 0.03, h: 'ΔG at 313 K from ΔH and ΔS.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 55\,930 + 313(80.3) = 81.1\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K_w = 2.9 \times 10^{-14}$` },
        { q: 'What is the pH of pure water at 40 °C?', a: 6.77, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: '[H⁺] = √Kw.', w: R`$-\log\sqrt{2.95 \times 10^{-14}} = 6.77$: still neutral, since [H⁺] = [OH⁻].` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us15-4', n: 4, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-14-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) KI with lead(II) nitrate (b) $\mathrm{SO_2}$ into water (c) sodium dichromate with NaOH`, h: 'The chromate–dichromate equilibrium.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Pb^{2+} + I^- \rightarrow PbI_2}$ (b) $\mathrm{SO_2 + H_2O \rightleftharpoons H^+ + HSO_3^-}$ (c) $\mathrm{Cr_2O_7^{2-} + OH^- \rightarrow CrO_4^{2-} + H_2O}$` },
        { q: R`(d) acidified $\mathrm{H_2O_2}$ with iron(II) (e) 2-methylpropan-2-ol heated with conc. $\mathrm{H_3PO_4}$ (f) iodine-131 β⁻ decay`, h: 'Dehydration via a tertiary cation.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{H_2O_2 + Fe^{2+} + H^+ \rightarrow Fe^{3+} + H_2O}$ (e) 2-methylpropene + $\mathrm{H_2O}$ (f) $\mathrm{^{131}I \rightarrow {}^{131}Xe + e^-}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us15-5', n: 5, t: 'Fe(phen)₃²⁺ kinetics', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`The red complex $\mathrm{[Fe(phen)_3]^{2+}}$ falls apart in acid. ln[complex] against time is straight, and its slope does not change when $[\mathrm{H^+}]$ is changed. $k = 1.70 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at 40.0 °C and $6.05 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at 50.0 °C.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the orders, and the half-life at 40.0 °C in seconds.', a: 408, u: 's', tol: 0.01, h: 'First order: t½ = ln 2/k.', w: R`First order in complex, zero order in H⁺. $0.693 \div 1.70 \times 10^{-3} = 408\ \mathrm{s}$` },
        { q: 'Find the activation energy.', a: 107, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Arrhenius.', w: R`$8.314\ln(3.56) \div (1/313.15 - 1/323.15) = 107\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Fe²⁺ is the Lewis acid, each phen a bidentate Lewis base; the ion is octahedral.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us15-6', n: 6, t: 'H₂O versus H₂S', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-3-6', 'ca-3-5'],
      s: 'Compare water and hydrogen sulfide.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Which has the larger enthalpy of fusion, the larger bond angle, the larger Ka, and the higher O–H/S–H stretching frequency? Explain each.', h: 'H-bonding; s-character; anion size; bond strength.', w: 'H₂O: hydrogen bonding (fusion); its 2s orbital mixes more, so the angle is ~105° against ~92°; H₂S is the stronger acid because the big S holds the negative charge more easily and the S–H bond is weaker; H₂O stretches at higher frequency (stronger bond).' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us15-7', n: 7, t: 'Chlorine', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-1-4', 'ca-3-4'],
      s: 'Chlorine (Z = 17) forms many oxoanions up to ClO₄⁻.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the configuration of Cl, say which has the higher ionisation energy (Cl or Cl₂) and which is larger (Cl or Cl⁻), and explain why ClO₅⁻ is unknown.', h: 'π* electrons; oxidation state +9.', w: R`$\mathrm{[Ne]3s^23p^5}$. Atomic Cl ionises harder: Cl₂’s top electrons are in π*. Cl⁻ is larger (extra electron, same nuclear charge). ClO₅⁻ would need Cl(+9): more than its 7 valence electrons.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us15-8', n: 8, t: 'C₃H₆O isomers', f: 'Organic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-13-1', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: 'There are three ethers of formula C₃H₆O, and several non-ether isomers.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Name the three ethers, and say which has stereoisomers.', h: 'One open-chain, two rings.', w: 'Methoxyethene, methyloxirane (propylene oxide) and oxetane. Only methyloxirane is chiral (a pair of enantiomers).' },
        { q: 'Name two non-ether isomers and compare their boiling points with the ethers.', h: 'Alcohols hydrogen-bond.', w: 'Propanone or propanal (carbonyl; boil a little above most of the ethers) and prop-2-en-1-ol or cyclopropanol (alcohols; boil near 100 °C thanks to H-bonding).' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2014, mins: 105,
    topics: 'A carbonate–oxide mixture · nitrogen oxides in smog · fluoride solubility · amines · NO + O₂ kinetics · net equations · Group 13 trends · acid strength', qs: [
    { id: 'us14-1', n: 1, t: 'A carbonate–oxide mixture', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`A 0.8000 g mixture of $\mathrm{MCO_3}$ and MO is heated until only MO (0.5800 g) remains. The $\mathrm{CO_2}$ fills 0.1291 L at 25.0 °C and 720.0 mmHg. The MO dissolves in exactly 41.37 mL of 0.500 M HCl. $R = 62.36\ \mathrm{L\ mmHg\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many moles of CO₂ formed?', a: 5.00e-3, u: 'mol', tol: 0.005, h: 'n = PV/RT.', w: R`$720.0 \times 0.1291 \div (62.36 \times 298.15) = 5.00 \times 10^{-3}$` },
        { q: 'Find the molar mass of MO, and identify M.', a: 56.08, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'MO + 2HCl.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{MO}) = 0.02068 \div 2 = 0.01034$; $0.5800 \div 0.01034 = 56.08$; M = 40.08: calcium.` },
        { q: 'What mole percentage of the original mixture was CaCO₃?', a: 48.3, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles CaCO₃ = moles CO₂.', w: R`$0.00500 \div 0.01034 = 48.3\%$ (CaO 51.7%). Of CO₂’s vibrations, the bend and the asymmetric stretch are IR active; the symmetric stretch is not.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us14-2', n: 2, t: 'Nitrogen oxides in smog', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-22-2'],
      s: R`$\Delta_f H$ / $\Delta_f G$ (kJ mol⁻¹): $\mathrm{NO_2}$ 33.85 / 51.84; $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$ 9.66 / 98.29. 1 ppb of a gas at 1 atm means a partial pressure of $10^{-9}$ atm. $R = 0.08206\ \mathrm{L\ atm\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $K_p$ for $\mathrm{2NO_2 \rightleftharpoons N_2O_4}$ at 298 K.`, a: 8.81, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG° = 98.29 − 2(51.84).', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -5.39\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K_p = e^{2.175} = 8.81$` },
        { q: R`With 40 ppb $\mathrm{NO_2}$ in air at 298 K, what is the equilibrium concentration of $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$, in mol L⁻¹?`, a: 5.76e-16, u: 'mol L⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'p(N₂O₄) = Kp·p(NO₂)², then n/V = p/RT.', w: R`$8.81 \times (4.0 \times 10^{-8})^2 = 1.41 \times 10^{-14}\ \mathrm{atm}$; $\div (0.08206 \times 298) = 5.8 \times 10^{-16}\ \mathrm{M}$: negligible.` },
        { q: R`Find $K_p$ at 35.0 °C.`, a: 4.07, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'ΔH = −58.04 kJ, ΔS = (ΔH − ΔG)/298.', w: R`$\Delta S^\circ = -176.7\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $\Delta G^\circ(308) = -3.59\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = 4.07$. Although $\Delta_f G(\mathrm{NO_2}) > 0$, NO₂ does not fall apart in air: no fast pathway exists.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us14-3', n: 3, t: 'Fluoride solubility', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-25-1'],
      s: R`$K_{sp}$: $\mathrm{SrF_2}$ $4.3 \times 10^{-9}$; $\mathrm{BaF_2}$ $1.5 \times 10^{-6}$; $\mathrm{PbF_2}$ $4.0 \times 10^{-8}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the molar solubility of $\mathrm{SrF_2}$ in water.`, a: 1.02e-3, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'Ksp = 4s³.', w: R`$(4.3 \times 10^{-9}/4)^{1/3} = 1.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{M}$. Adding $\mathrm{Sr^{2+}}$ lowers it (common ion); adding nitric acid raises it (F⁻ → HF).` },
        { q: R`NaF is added slowly to a solution 0.020 M in both $\mathrm{Ba^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Pb^{2+}}$. Which fluoride precipitates first, and what $[\mathrm{F^-}]$ starts the second?`, a: 8.66e-3, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: '[F⁻] = √(Ksp/0.020).', w: R`$\mathrm{PbF_2}$ first ($1.41 \times 10^{-3}$ M F⁻); $\mathrm{BaF_2}$ at $8.66 \times 10^{-3}$ M` },
        { q: R`What $[\mathrm{Pb^{2+}}]$ is left when $\mathrm{BaF_2}$ starts to precipitate?`, a: 5.33e-4, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'Ksp(PbF₂)/[F⁻]².', w: R`$4.0 \times 10^{-8} \div 7.5 \times 10^{-5} = 5.3 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{M}$: the separation is incomplete.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us14-4', n: 4, t: 'Amines', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-34-1', 'ca-13-1'],
      s: 'Ethylamine is a primary amine; its conjugate acid is the ethylammonium ion.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the shape at N in ethylamine and ethylammonium, and explain why ethylamine is a much stronger base than phenylamine.', h: 'Lone-pair delocalisation.', w: 'Trigonal pyramidal and tetrahedral (both sp³). Phenylamine’s lone pair is delocalised into the ring, a stabilisation lost on protonation.' },
        { q: 'How many amines (all classes) have the formula C₄H₁₁N?', a: 8, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Primary 4, secondary 3, tertiary 1.', w: 'Primary: butan-1-amine, butan-2-amine, 2-methylpropan-1-amine, 2-methylpropan-2-amine. Secondary: N-methylpropan-1-amine, N-methylpropan-2-amine, diethylamine. Tertiary: N,N-dimethylethylamine. Eight.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us14-5', n: 5, t: 'NO + O₂ kinetics', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 13, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`For $\mathrm{2NO + O_2 \rightarrow 2NO_2}$, the initial rate of $\mathrm{NO_2}$ formation is 0.0213 M s⁻¹ at [NO] = 0.010, $[\mathrm{O_2}]$ = 0.030 M; 0.0852 at 0.020 and 0.030; and 0.1704 at 0.020 and 0.060.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Give the orders and find k.', a: 7.1e3, u: 'M⁻² s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Doubling NO gives ×4; doubling O₂ gives ×2.', w: R`Second order in NO, first in $\mathrm{O_2}$. $k = 0.0213 \div (0.010^2 \times 0.030) = 7.1 \times 10^3\ \mathrm{M^{-2}\ s^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'In the third run, how fast is O₂ used up?', a: 0.0852, u: 'M s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'One O₂ per two NO₂.', w: R`$0.1704 \div 2 = 0.0852\ \mathrm{M\ s^{-1}}$. A fast $\mathrm{2NO \rightleftharpoons N_2O_2}$ followed by slow $\mathrm{N_2O_2 + O_2 \rightarrow 2NO_2}$ fits.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us14-6', n: 6, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-28-1', 'ca-14-2'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) silver nitrate with sodium phosphate (b) excess KCN with iron(III) nitrate (c) $\mathrm{Br_2}$ with cyclohexene in $\mathrm{CCl_4}$ (d) U-235 + n gives Ba-140, a second nucleus and 3 neutrons`, h: 'Count protons and nucleons.', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{Ag^+ + PO_4^{3-} \rightarrow Ag_3PO_4}$ (yellow) (b) $\mathrm{Fe^{3+} + CN^- \rightarrow [Fe(CN)_6]^{3-}}$ (c) trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane (d) $\mathrm{^{235}_{92}U + n \rightarrow {}^{140}_{56}Ba + {}^{93}_{36}Kr + 3n}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us14-7', n: 7, t: 'Group 13 trends', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-1-4', 'ca-9-2'],
      s: 'First ionisation energies (kJ mol⁻¹): B 801, Al 578, Ga 579, In 558, Tl 589.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Why is Ga’s IE no lower than Al’s, and Tl’s higher than In’s? Why is Tl³⁺ a strong oxidant while B and Al are only +3?', h: 'Poor d/f shielding; the inert pair.', w: 'Filled 3d (for Ga) and 4f (for Tl) shield poorly, raising Z_eff; relativistic contraction adds for Tl. Down the group the ns² pair gets harder to use, so Tl prefers +1 and Tl³⁺ readily takes electrons back.' },
        { q: R`Write $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$ acting as a Lewis acid, and explain why the trichlorides get weaker as Lewis acids down the group.`, h: 'Overlap with the incoming lone pair.', w: R`$\mathrm{AlCl_3 + Cl^- \rightarrow AlCl_4^-}$ (or with $\mathrm{NH_3}$). Larger central atoms overlap less well with a donor’s lone pair, so the new bond is weaker.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us14-8', n: 8, t: 'Acid strength', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-3-1'],
      s: R`$K_{a1}/K_{a2}$ is about $2 \times 10^5$ for $\mathrm{H_2SO_3}$ but about $10^{12}$ for $\mathrm{H_2S}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Why do HCl and HI look equally strong in water but not in pure ethanoic acid? Why do the two Ka ratios differ so much?', h: 'Levelling; where the second proton sits.', w: 'Water is basic enough to ionise both completely (levelling). Ethanoic acid is a weaker base, so HI (the stronger acid) ionises more. In H₂SO₃ the two H are on different O, far from each other, so the first negative charge holds the second proton only weakly; in H₂S both are on the same S.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'usnco', comp: 'US National Chemistry Olympiad (USNCO)', round: 'National exam, Part II', y: 2013, mins: 105,
    topics: 'A copper carboxylate · benzene thermodynamics · peroxynitric acid kinetics · a gas-phase dissociation · net equations · SF₄ and NMR · solution vapour pressures · butanols', qs: [
    { id: 'us13-1', n: 1, t: 'A copper compound', f: 'Stoichiometry', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-6-1', 'ca-4-1'],
      s: R`A compound contains only C, H, O and Cu. Burning 0.400 g gives 0.8060 g $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and 0.1178 g $\mathrm{H_2O}$. 0.200 g, treated with excess KI, releases iodine needing 26.16 mL of 0.0250 M thiosulfate (1 Cu ↔ 1 $\mathrm{S_2O_3^{2-}}$). 0.150 g in 12.00 g of benzene lowers its freezing point by 0.100 °C ($K_f = 4.90\ \mathrm{°C\ kg\ mol^{-1}}$).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the percentage of carbon.', a: 55.0, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Mass of C in the CO₂.', w: R`$0.8060 \times 12.011/44.01 = 0.2200\ \mathrm{g}$: 55.0% (H 3.30%)` },
        { q: 'Find the percentage of copper.', a: 20.8, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of thiosulfate = moles of Cu.', w: R`$6.54 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} \times 63.55 = 0.04157\ \mathrm{g}$: 20.8%. O by difference 20.9%, giving $\mathrm{C_{14}H_{10}O_4Cu}$ (copper benzoate). Starch: blue to colourless at the end point.` },
        { q: 'Find the molar mass in benzene.', a: 612, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'm = ΔT/K_f.', w: R`$m = 0.0204$; $n = 2.45 \times 10^{-4}$; $M = 612$: twice the formula mass, a dimer $\mathrm{Cu_2(O_2CC_6H_5)_4}$.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us13-2', n: 2, t: 'Benzene thermodynamics', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 14, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-5-1'],
      s: R`Benzene: liquid $\Delta_f H = 48.7$, $S^\circ = 173.3$; gas $\Delta_f H = 82.9\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, $S^\circ = 269.0\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$. $\Delta_c H(\text{liquid}) = -3273\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Estimate the vapour pressure of benzene at 40 °C, in mmHg.', a: 149, u: 'mmHg', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG_vap = ΔH − TΔS; K = p/1 atm.', w: R`$\Delta G = 34\,200 - 313(95.7) = 4.25\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = 0.196\ \mathrm{atm} = 149\ \mathrm{mmHg}$` },
        { q: 'Burning 2.00 g of benzene in a bomb calorimeter (6.20 kJ °C⁻¹) inside 1.20 kg of water at 21.00 °C: what is the final temperature?', a: 28.47, u: '°C', abs: 0.03, h: 'Total heat capacity = 6.20 + 1.20 × 4.18.', w: R`$q = 0.02561 \times 3273 = 83.8\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $C = 11.22\ \mathrm{kJ\ °C^{-1}}$; $\Delta T = 7.47$; 28.47 °C` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us13-3', n: 3, t: 'Peroxynitric acid', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{HO_2NO_2}$ decomposes by first-order kinetics: $k = 0.21\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at 331 K and $0.452\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at 336 K.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the half-life at 331 K.', a: 3.30, u: 's', tol: 0.01, h: 'ln 2/k.', w: R`$0.693 \div 0.21 = 3.30\ \mathrm{s}$; after 5 half-lives, $(1/2)^5 = 3.1\%$ remains.` },
        { q: R`Starting at $5.0 \times 10^{-8}$ M at 331 K, what is left after 15.0 s?`, a: 2.14e-9, u: 'M', tol: 0.02, h: 'C = C₀e^(−kt).', w: R`$5.0 \times 10^{-8} \times e^{-3.15} = 2.1 \times 10^{-9}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
        { q: 'Find the activation energy.', a: 142, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Arrhenius.', w: R`$8.314\ln(2.152) \div (1/331 - 1/336) = 142\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us13-4', n: 4, t: 'A gas-phase dissociation', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 14, lec: ['ca-22-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`0.0400 mol of $\mathrm{XCl_3}$ is sealed in a 1.50 L bulb at 100.0 °C, where $\mathrm{2XCl_3(g) \rightleftharpoons 2X(g) + 3Cl_2(g)}$ reaches equilibrium at a total pressure of 0.9000 atm. $R = 0.08206\ \mathrm{L\ atm\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`What would the pressure of $\mathrm{XCl_3}$ be with no reaction?`, a: 0.8166, u: 'atm', tol: 0.002, h: 'P = nRT/V.', w: R`$0.0400 \times 0.08206 \times 373.15 \div 1.50 = 0.8166\ \mathrm{atm}$` },
        { q: R`Find the equilibrium partial pressure of $\mathrm{Cl_2}$.`, a: 0.0834, u: 'atm', tol: 0.01, h: 'Total = P₀ − 2y + 2y + 3y.', w: R`$3y = 0.0834$, so $p(\mathrm{Cl_2}) = 0.0834$, $p(\mathrm{X}) = 0.0556$, $p(\mathrm{XCl_3}) = 0.7609\ \mathrm{atm}$` },
        { q: R`Find $K_p$.`, a: 3.1e-6, u: '', tol: 0.03, h: 'p(X)²p(Cl₂)³/p(XCl₃)².', w: R`$0.0556^2 \times 0.0834^3 \div 0.7609^2 = 3.1 \times 10^{-6}$; $\Delta G^\circ = +39.3\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K_c = K_p/(RT)^3 = 1.1 \times 10^{-10}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us13-5', n: 5, t: 'Net equations', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-30-1'],
      s: 'Write a net equation for each reaction (balancing not needed).',
      p: [
        { q: R`(a) magnesium carbonate heated strongly (b) acidified $\mathrm{KMnO_4}$ with hydrogen peroxide (c) zinc sulfate with barium hydroxide`, h: 'Two precipitates in (c).', w: R`(a) $\mathrm{MgCO_3 \rightarrow MgO + CO_2}$ (b) $\mathrm{MnO_4^- + H_2O_2 + H^+ \rightarrow Mn^{2+} + O_2 + H_2O}$ (c) $\mathrm{Zn^{2+} + SO_4^{2-} + Ba^{2+} + OH^- \rightarrow Zn(OH)_2 + BaSO_4}$` },
        { q: R`(d) trimethylamine gas with HBr gas (e) methylbenzene with $\mathrm{Br_2/FeBr_3}$ (f) nitrogen-13 emits a positron`, h: 'Ring substitution ortho/para.', w: R`(d) $\mathrm{(CH_3)_3N + HBr \rightarrow (CH_3)_3NH^+Br^-}$ (e) 2- and 4-bromomethylbenzene + HBr (f) $\mathrm{^{13}N \rightarrow {}^{13}C + e^+}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us13-6', n: 6, t: 'SF₄ and NMR', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-3-5', 'ca-37-3'],
      s: 'SF₄ has a lone pair on S and is fluxional at room temperature.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Where is the lone pair, and how many ¹⁹F signals are seen at −100 °C?', a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Lone pairs go equatorial.', w: 'Equatorial (see-saw shape). Two signals, 2 : 2 (axial and equatorial F). At room temperature they swap quickly (Berry pseudorotation), giving one averaged signal.' },
        { q: 'Why is OF₄ unknown when SF₄ exists?', h: 'Size and valence orbitals.', w: 'O has no low-lying orbitals for more than an octet and is too small for four F plus lone pairs; S, Se and Te are larger and can be hypervalent.' },
      ] },
    { id: 'us13-7', n: 7, t: 'Vapour pressure of solutions', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-3-6'],
      s: 'At 25 °C: CCl₄ 98.8 torr, CHCl₃ 172.5 torr. Their mixture behaves ideally.',
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the vapour pressure when $x(\mathrm{CHCl_3}) = 0.40$.`, a: 128.3, u: 'torr', tol: 0.003, h: 'Raoult’s law for each.', w: R`$0.40(172.5) + 0.60(98.8) = 128.3\ \mathrm{torr}$` },
        { q: R`What is the mole fraction of $\mathrm{CHCl_3}$ in the vapour?`, a: 0.538, u: '', tol: 0.005, h: 'Partial ÷ total.', w: R`$69.0 \div 128.3 = 0.538$: richer than the liquid in the more volatile part. Propanone + chloroform mixes exothermically (C–H···O=C hydrogen bonds), so its vapour pressure falls below the ideal line.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'us13-8', n: 8, t: 'Butanols', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 10, lec: ['ca-16-1', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: R`There are four alcohols of formula $\mathrm{C_4H_{10}O}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many C₄H₁₀O ethers are there, and which alcohol is chiral?', a: 3, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Ethers: split the four carbons across the O.', w: 'Three ethers: methoxypropane, 2-methoxypropane and ethoxyethane. Butan-2-ol is chiral; its enantiomers rotate light oppositely but react identically except with other chiral substances.' },
        { q: 'Which alcohol resists acidified dichromate, and what do the other three give?', h: 'Count H on the C–OH carbon.', w: '2-Methylpropan-2-ol (tertiary, no H on the carbinol C). Butan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-1-ol give aldehydes (then acids); butan-2-ol gives butanone.' },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'icho', comp: 'International Chemistry Olympiad (IChO)', round: 'Theory exam', y: 2026, mins: 300,
    topics: 'A mineral puzzle for Avicenna · covalent organic frameworks · uranium and fission · fatty acids · carbon nanorings · nitrides · photocatalysts for CO₂ · cyclodextrins', skipped: 'Problem 2 (Belousov–Zhabotinsky oscillations) needs its printed data and phase portrait, and the drawn-structure parts of problems 3, 5, 6, 8 and 9 are left out.', qs: [
    { id: 'ic26-1', n: 1, t: 'A mineral puzzle', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 25, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-1-2', 'ca-37-3'],
      s: 'Ancient minerals and plant oils are identified from mass spectra, compositions and heating curves. (H 1.008, C 12.011, O 15.999, F 18.998, Al 26.982, S 32.06, K 39.098.)',
      p: [
        { q: 'A hydrocarbon Z is monochlorinated; the product shows molecular ions at m/z 168 and 170 (3 : 1). What is the molar mass of Z (integer masses)?', a: 134, u: 'g mol⁻¹', abs: 0.01, h: 'Cl replaces one H.', w: R`$168 - 35 + 1 = 134$: $\mathrm{C_{10}H_{14}}$` },
        { q: 'A plant compound W shows [M+1]⁺ at 9.9% of M⁺. How many carbons does it have?', a: 9, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: '≈ 1.1% per carbon.', w: R`$9.9 \div 1.1 = 9$` },
        { q: R`A hydrated aluminium sulfate, $\mathrm{Al_2(SO_4)_3{\cdot}nH_2O}$, is 48.66% water by mass. Find n.`, a: 18, u: '', abs: 0.05, h: 'M(anhydrous) = 342.15.', w: R`$18.015n \div (342.15 + 18.015n) = 0.4866$ gives $n = 18.0$` },
        { q: R`A salt $\mathrm{K_3QF_6}$ contains 10.45% Q. Find the molar mass of Q and name it.`, a: 27.0, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'x ÷ (117.29 + 113.99 + x) = 0.1045.', w: R`$x = 27.0$: aluminium, $\mathrm{K_3AlF_6}$ (a cryolite analogue)` },
        { q: R`12.00 g of a stone, $\mathrm{Al_2(C_{12}O_{12}){\cdot}nH_2O}$ (an aluminium mellitate), weighs 6.90 g after heating to 200 °C and 1.804 g of $\mathrm{Al_2O_3}$ at 400 °C. Find n.`, a: 16, u: '', abs: 0.1, h: 'Moles of Al₂O₃ = moles of formula units.', w: R`$1.804 \div 101.96 = 0.01769\ \mathrm{mol}$; $M = 678.3$; anhydrous 390.1; water 288.2 = 16 H₂O. (Check: anhydrous mass $0.01769 \times 390.1 = 6.90$ g ✓.)` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic26-3', n: 3, t: 'Covalent organic frameworks', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`COF-1 is built from boroxine rings joined by benzene rings; its empirical formula is $\mathrm{C_9H_4BO_2}$. π-Stacked layers hold the framework together.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What is the boron mass percentage of COF-1?', a: 6.98, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'M = 154.94.', w: R`$10.81 \div 154.94 = 6.98\%$ (carbon 69.77%)` },
        { q: 'Each stacked repeat unit gains −49.8 kJ mol⁻¹ from benzene–boroxine contacts, −7.9 from benzene–benzene and −6.7 from boroxine–boroxine. Sum them for the eclipsed arrangement.', a: -64.4, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Add the three contributions.', w: R`$-49.8 - 7.9 - 6.7 = -64.4\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$: stacking is a major source of the framework’s stability.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic26-4', n: 4, t: 'Uranium and fission', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 3, m: 30, lec: ['ca-1-2', 'ca-5-2', 'ca-1-3'],
      s: R`$^{235}\mathrm{U}$ = 235.04 u, $^{238}\mathrm{U}$ = 238.05 u. Take the binding energy per nucleon as 7.60 MeV in U-235 and 8.50 MeV in the fission products. 1 MeV = $1.602 \times 10^{-13}$ J; 1 t TNT = $4.184 \times 10^9$ J.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Depleted uranium has an average atomic mass of 238.04 u. What percentage is U-235?', a: 0.33, u: '%', tol: 0.02, h: 'Two-isotope weighted mean.', w: R`$(238.05 - 238.04) \div (238.05 - 235.04) = 0.33\%$ (natural uranium is about 0.72%)` },
        { q: 'U-235 captures a neutron and splits into two nuclei and 2 neutrons. Estimate the energy released, in MeV.', a: 203, u: 'MeV', tol: 0.005, h: 'The products hold 234 nucleons.', w: R`$234(8.50) - 235(7.60) = 203\ \mathrm{MeV}$` },
        { q: 'A 2 MeV neutron is slowed to 0.025 eV in graphite, losing on average ξ = 0.158 of ln E per collision. About how many collisions are needed?', a: 115, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'n = ln(E₀/E)/ξ.', w: R`$\ln(8 \times 10^7) \div 0.158 = 115$ (water, ξ ≈ 0.95, needs only ~19)` },
        { q: 'ΔH°₂₉₈ for CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O(g) is −802.3 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔCp = +12 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Estimate ΔH° at 1500 K.', a: -787.9, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.002, h: 'Kirchhoff: ΔH(T₂) = ΔH(T₁) + ΔCp ΔT.', w: R`$-802.3 + 0.012 \times 1202 = -787.9\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'A 20 kt device releases 203 MeV per fission. What mass of 85% enriched uranium is needed if only 25% of it fissions, in kg?', a: 4.73, u: 'kg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Energy ÷ energy per fission = fissions.', w: R`$8.37 \times 10^{13}\ \mathrm{J} \div 3.25 \times 10^{-11} = 2.57 \times 10^{24}$ fissions = 1.00 kg U-235; $\div 0.25 \div 0.85 = 4.73\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic26-5', n: 5, t: 'Fatty acids', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-14-2', 'ca-18-1'],
      s: 'The iodine number is the mass of I₂ (g) that adds to 100 g of fat; each C=C adds one I₂ (253.81 g mol⁻¹).',
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the iodine number of linolenic acid, C₁₈H₃₀O₂ (three C=C).', a: 273.5, u: '', tol: 0.005, h: '3 × 253.81 per 278.43 g.', w: R`$761.4 \div 278.43 \times 100 = 273.5$ (linoleic acid, two C=C: 181)` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic26-6', n: 6, t: 'Carbon nanorings', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-13-3'],
      s: 'A tip voltage breaks carbon–halogen bonds on a surface. Bond enthalpies (kJ mol⁻¹): C–F 467, C–Cl 346, C–Br 290, C–I 228. 1 eV = 96.485 kJ mol⁻¹.',
      p: [
        { q: 'What energy per mole does a 3.2 eV pulse correspond to, and which C–X bonds can it break?', a: 308.8, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: 'eV × 96.485.', w: R`$3.2 \times 96.485 = 308.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$: enough for C–Br and C–I, not C–Cl or C–F.` },
        { q: 'A cyclic π-system with 4n + 2 electrons is aromatic. How many π electrons does the n = 20 case have?', a: 82, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Hückel.', w: R`$4(20) + 2 = 82$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic26-7', n: 7, t: 'Nitrides', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-9-2'],
      s: 'Metal nitrides are made by heating metals in nitrogen.',
      p: [
        { q: R`A nitride $\mathrm{M_3N_2}$ is 27.76% nitrogen. Find M’s molar mass and identify it.`, a: 24.3, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: '28.014 ÷ 0.2776 = formula mass.', w: R`$100.9 - 28.0 = 72.9$; $\div 3 = 24.3$: magnesium, $\mathrm{Mg_3N_2}$. (LaN is 9.16% N, Si₃N₄ 39.94%.)` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic26-8', n: 8, t: 'Photocatalysts for CO₂', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-1-3', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`An iron complex ($M = 557.21$) is loaded at 2.5 wt% onto carbon nitride with a surface area of 25.0 m² g⁻¹. It is lit by a 405 nm, 80 mW LED. $h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}$, $c = 2.998 \times 10^8$, $N_A = 6.022 \times 10^{23}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many complex molecules sit on each nm² of surface?', a: 1.08, u: 'nm⁻²', tol: 0.01, h: 'Per gram: molecules ÷ area (1 m² = 10¹⁸ nm²).', w: R`$0.025 \div 557.21 \times N_A = 2.70 \times 10^{19}$; $\div 2.50 \times 10^{19}\ \mathrm{nm^2} = 1.08$` },
        { q: 'What is the photon flux, in mol s⁻¹?', a: 2.71e-7, u: 'mol s⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Power ÷ photon energy ÷ N_A.', w: R`$E = 4.90 \times 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$; $0.080 \div E \div N_A = 2.71 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{mol\ s^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic26-9', n: 9, t: 'Cyclodextrins', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-34-4', 'ca-29-4'],
      s: 'Cyclodextrins are rings of glucose units (180.16 g mol⁻¹), each link losing one water.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the molar mass of γ-cyclodextrin (8 units).', a: 1297.2, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.003, h: '8 × (180.16 − 18.015).', w: R`$8 \times 162.14 = 1297.2$ (β, 7 units: 1135.0)` },
        { q: 'Seven different groups are put one on each glucose of β-cyclodextrin. Counting rotations of the ring as the same, how many arrangements are possible?', a: 720, u: '', abs: 0.5, h: 'Circular arrangements of n distinct items: n!/n (the ring is directional, so no reflection).', w: R`$7!/7 = 720$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'icho', comp: 'International Chemistry Olympiad (IChO)', round: 'Theory exam', y: 2025, mins: 300,
    topics: 'Tennis balls and gas leakage · solar desalination · splitting CO₂ with sunlight · analysing crude oil · CO on metal catalysts', skipped: 'Problems 1, 2, 3 and 9 (terpene and rapamycin syntheses, molecular cages, enzyme mechanisms) are answered almost entirely with drawn structures, so they are not recreated here.', qs: [
    { id: 'ic25-4', n: 4, t: 'The life of tennis balls', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 25, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2'],
      s: R`A tennis ball is a hollow rubber sphere, inner radius 3.1 cm, at 25 °C. Air is 80% $\mathrm{N_2}$ and 20% $\mathrm{O_2}$ (M = 28.82 g mol⁻¹). $R = 8.314\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$; 1 atm = 101 325 Pa.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'What mass of air does the ball hold at 1.00 atm?', a: 0.147, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'V = (4/3)πr³ in m³.', w: R`$V = 1.248 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m^3}$; $n = 5.10 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $m = 0.147\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: R`Extra gas is made inside the ball by $\mathrm{NaNO_2 + NH_4Cl \rightarrow N_2 + NaCl + 2H_2O}$, raising the pressure from 1.00 to 1.60 atm. What mass of $\mathrm{NaNO_2}$ is needed?`, a: 0.211, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of N₂ for 0.60 atm.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{N_2}) = 3.06 \times 10^{-3}$; $\times 69.00 = 0.211\ \mathrm{g}$` },
        { q: 'The excess pressure in a nitrogen-filled ball falls by first-order kinetics from 0.90 atm to 0.55 atm in 180 h. Find k.', a: 2.74e-3, u: 'h⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k = ln(p₀/p)/t.', w: R`$\ln(0.90/0.55) \div 180 = 2.74 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{h^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'How long until the excess pressure is 0.30 atm?', a: 402, u: 'h', tol: 0.01, h: 'Same k.', w: R`$\ln 3 \div 2.74 \times 10^{-3} = 402\ \mathrm{h}$` },
        { q: 'An air-filled ball loses pressure 1.16 times as fast as a nitrogen ball. Find k for O₂.', a: 4.92e-3, u: 'h⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'k(air) = 0.8k(N₂) + 0.2k(O₂).', w: R`$0.8 + 0.2x = 1.16$, $x = 1.8$; $k(\mathrm{O_2}) = 4.92 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{h^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'With Ea = 50.0 kJ mol⁻¹ for the leakage, by what factor is it faster at 35 °C than at 25 °C?', a: 1.92, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Arrhenius ratio.', w: R`$\exp\left[\dfrac{50\,000}{8.314}\left(\dfrac{1}{298.15} - \dfrac{1}{308.15}\right)\right] = 1.92$` },
        { q: 'Each ball’s rubber shell runs from r = 3.1 to 3.3 cm, density 1.30 g cm⁻³. What mass of rubber is recovered from 1500 balls at 90% efficiency, in kg?', a: 45.2, u: 'kg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Shell volume = (4/3)π(3.3³ − 3.1³).', w: R`$25.74\ \mathrm{cm^3} \times 1.30 = 33.5\ \mathrm{g}$; $\times 1500 \times 0.90 = 45.2\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic25-5', n: 5, t: 'Solar desalination', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 25, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-23-4', 'ca-5-1'],
      s: R`Model seawater: 100 g contains 3.10 g NaCl and 96.90 g water. Water: $K_b = 0.512\ \mathrm{K\ kg\ mol^{-1}}$, $\Delta H_{vap} = 40.716\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$; take i = 2 for NaCl.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the boiling point of this seawater at 1 atm, in K.', a: 373.71, u: 'K', abs: 0.02, h: 'ΔT = i K_b m.', w: R`$m = 0.547\ \mathrm{mol\ kg^{-1}}$; $\Delta T = 0.561\ \mathrm{K}$; $T_b = 373.71\ \mathrm{K}$` },
        { q: 'Brine is concentrated until it boils at 377.00 K. What is its NaCl mass percentage?', a: 18.0, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔT = 3.85 K.', w: R`$m = 3.85 \div 1.024 = 3.76\ \mathrm{mol\ kg^{-1}}$ = 219.7 g per kg water; $219.7 \div 1219.7 = 18.0\%$` },
        { q: 'At what temperature does the original seawater boil under 2.0 atm?', a: 394.6, u: 'K', abs: 0.6, h: 'Clausius–Clapeyron from 373.71 K at 1 atm.', w: R`$1/T_2 = 1/373.71 - 8.314\ln 2 \div 40\,716$; $T_2 = 394.6\ \mathrm{K}$` },
        { q: 'In a flash stage, 3.0 kg of brine (c = 3850 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) cools by 2.5 K as water evaporates. How many moles of water evaporate?', a: 0.709, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'Heat released = heat of vaporisation used.', w: R`$3.0 \times 3850 \times 2.5 = 28\,875\ \mathrm{J}$; $\div 40\,716 = 0.709\ \mathrm{mol}$` },
        { q: 'How much energy (kWh) heats 1 kg of seawater by 75 K (c = 3.85 kJ kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)?', a: 0.0802, u: 'kWh', tol: 0.01, h: '1 kWh = 3600 kJ.', w: R`$288.75\ \mathrm{kJ} \div 3600 = 0.0802\ \mathrm{kWh}$` },
        { q: 'A plant makes 4.0 × 10⁴ m³ of fresh water a day, recovering 80% of the water in the seawater it takes in (seawater is 96.90% water). What mass of seawater does it take in, in kg?', a: 5.16e7, u: 'kg', tol: 0.01, h: 'Fresh water = 4.0 × 10⁷ kg.', w: R`$4.0 \times 10^7 \div 0.80 \div 0.9690 = 5.16 \times 10^7\ \mathrm{kg}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic25-6', n: 6, t: 'Splitting CO₂ with sunlight', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 3, m: 25, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-22-2', 'ca-1-3'],
      s: R`For $\mathrm{CO_2 \rightleftharpoons CO + \tfrac{1}{2}O_2}$: $\Delta H^\circ = 283\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, $\Delta S^\circ = 86.45\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$ (take both as constant). A zinc cycle does the same job in two steps: $\mathrm{Zn(l) + CO_2 \rightarrow ZnO + CO}$ ($\Delta H^\circ = -74$ kJ, $\Delta S^\circ = -23.2$ J K⁻¹) and $\mathrm{ZnO(s) \rightarrow Zn(g) + \tfrac{1}{2}O_2}$ ($\Delta H^\circ = 480.9$ kJ, $\Delta S^\circ = 219.85$ J K⁻¹). Total pressure 1 bar.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`At what temperature is 30% of $\mathrm{CO_2}$ dissociated at equilibrium? First find $K_p$.`, a: 0.155, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'From 1 mol: 0.70 CO₂, 0.30 CO, 0.15 O₂ (1.15 mol in all).', w: R`$K_p = 0.261 \times \sqrt{0.130} \div 0.609 = 0.155$` },
        { q: 'Now find that temperature.', a: 2776, u: 'K', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔH − TΔS = −RT ln K, so T = ΔH/(ΔS − R ln K).', w: R`$283\,000 \div (86.45 + 15.51) = 2776\ \mathrm{K}$: far too hot for a practical reactor.` },
        { q: R`For $\mathrm{Zn(l) + CO_2 \rightarrow ZnO + CO}$ at 973 K, find K and the equilibrium fraction of $\mathrm{CO_2}$ converted.`, a: 0.998, u: '', tol: 0.001, h: 'K = x/(1 − x).', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -51.4\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = 577$; $x = 0.998$` },
        { q: R`For $\mathrm{ZnO \rightarrow Zn(g) + \tfrac{1}{2}O_2}$ at 2100 K, find the equilibrium $p(\mathrm{O_2})$ in bar.`, a: 0.302, u: 'bar', tol: 0.01, h: 'p(Zn) = 2p(O₂); K = p(Zn)·p(O₂)^½ = 2p^1.5.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 19.2\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K_p = 0.333$; $p = (0.166)^{2/3} = 0.302\ \mathrm{bar}$` },
        { q: 'Which semiconductors can absorb 450 nm light? Give the photon energy in eV.', a: 2.76, u: 'eV', tol: 0.005, h: 'E = 1239.8/λ(nm).', w: R`$2.76\ \mathrm{eV}$: any material with a band gap below 2.76 eV (e.g. CdS, Si, Ge, WO₃; not TiO₂ or ZnO).` },
        { q: R`A 400 W lamp at 380 nm shines for 5.0 h on a photocatalyst in 0.200 L of water, which ends up 1.5 mM in HCHO (4 e⁻ from CO₂) and 4.0 mM in $\mathrm{CH_3OH}$ (6 e⁻). Find the apparent quantum yield (electrons per photon).`, a: 2.62e-4, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'Photons = energy ÷ (hc/λ).', w: R`Electrons $= 0.200(0.006 + 0.024) = 6.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$; photons $= 7.2 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{J} \div 5.23 \times 10^{-19} = 22.9\ \mathrm{mol}$; $2.6 \times 10^{-4}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic25-7', n: 7, t: 'Analysing crude oil', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-37-3', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: 'A crude oil is analysed by mass spectrometry and by converting its sulfur to barium sulfate.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Four C₈ components give molecular ions at m/z 112, 114, 112 and 106. The first loses 29 (C₂H₅) to give m/z 83; the third has a strong peak at 70 and decolourises bromine; the fourth loses 15 to give m/z 91. Suggest all four.', h: '114 is saturated; 106 is aromatic.', w: 'Ethylcyclohexane (112), octane (114), oct-1-ene (112) and ethylbenzene (106; m/z 91 is the benzyl/tropylium ion).' },
        { q: 'An alkane gives [M+1]⁺ at 7.7% of M⁺. How many carbon atoms does it contain (1.1% ¹³C per C)?', a: 7, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: 'Ratio ÷ 1.1%.', w: R`$7.7 \div 1.1 = 7$: a heptane, $\mathrm{C_7H_{16}}$ (M = 100).` },
        { q: 'A 120 μL oil sample (density 865 g dm⁻³) yields 0.0172 g of BaSO₄. What is its sulfur content in wt%? (Oil above 0.5% S is “sour”.)', a: 2.28, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'n(S) = n(BaSO₄); M = 233.39.', w: R`Sample $0.1038\ \mathrm{g}$; $n(\mathrm{S}) = 7.37 \times 10^{-5}$; $2.36 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{g}$ S; $2.28\%$: sour` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic25-8', n: 8, t: 'CO on metal catalysts', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-2-4', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`CO is oxidised on supported palladium, and its chemisorption is used to count surface metal atoms. Molar volume at 273.15 K and 101 325 Pa: 22.414 L mol⁻¹. $N_A = 6.022 \times 10^{23}$; Pd 106.42, Ni 58.69.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'CO flows at 12 mL min⁻¹ (273.15 K, 1 atm) for 45 min over 0.20 g of catalyst (5.0% Pd by mass, 20% of the Pd atoms on the surface); 75% of the CO becomes CO₂. How many CO₂ molecules form per surface Pd atom?', a: 961, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of CO, then surface Pd atoms.', w: R`$0.540\ \mathrm{L} \div 22.414 \times 0.75 = 0.01807\ \mathrm{mol} = 1.09 \times 10^{22}$; surface Pd $= 0.20 \times 0.05 \times 0.20 \div 106.42 \times N_A = 1.13 \times 10^{19}$; ratio 961` },
        { q: 'A 0.020 g sample with 10% Ni takes up 20.0 μL of CO (100 000 Pa, 298 K), one CO per surface Ni. What percentage of the Ni atoms are on the surface?', a: 2.37, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'n = pV/RT for the CO.', w: R`$4.86 \times 10^{17}$ CO; Ni atoms $= 2.05 \times 10^{19}$; $2.37\%$. In IR, terminal CO absorbs near 2050–2100 cm⁻¹ and bridging CO lower (1850–1930), since back-donation weakens C≡O; free CO is at 2143 cm⁻¹.` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'icho', comp: 'International Chemistry Olympiad (IChO)', round: 'Theory exam', y: 2024, mins: 300,
    topics: 'Ammonia and hydrogen’s carbon cost · a spinel gas sensor · tyrosinase kinetics · potassium and an oxocarbon acid · a copper ammine permanganate · penicillin', skipped: 'Problems 7 (oligomers and vitrimers), 8 (safflower natural products) and 9 (porphyrin complexes) are answered mainly with structures, so they are left out; so are the structural parts of the others.', qs: [
    { id: 'ic24-1', n: 1, t: 'Ammonia', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 30, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-22-2', 'ca-2-4'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{N_2 + 3H_2 \rightleftharpoons 2NH_3}$: $\Delta H^\circ = -91.8\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, $\Delta S^\circ = -198.1\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$ (constant). Feed $\mathrm{N_2 : H_2} = 1 : 3$; $p^\circ = 1$ bar.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find K at 450 °C.', a: 1.92e-4, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS°.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = 51.5\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = e^{-8.56} = 1.92 \times 10^{-4}$` },
        { q: 'At 450 °C and 200 bar, what fraction of the N₂ is converted at equilibrium?', a: 0.534, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'From 1 N₂ : 3 H₂, x converted gives 2x NH₃ in 4 − 2x mol; K = y(NH₃)²/(y(N₂)y(H₂)³P²). Solve numerically.', w: R`$x = 0.534$ (53%)` },
        { q: 'Hydrogen from coal gasification, C + 2H₂O → CO₂ + 2H₂, releases how many kg of CO₂ per kg of H₂?', a: 10.9, u: 'kg', tol: 0.005, h: 'Molar masses.', w: R`$44.01 \div 4.032 = 10.9\ \mathrm{kg}$ (methane reforming, CH₄ + 2H₂O → CO₂ + 4H₂: 5.46 kg)` },
        { q: 'How much CO₂ (kg) must be captured to make 25 000 kg of “blue” ammonia from methane-reformed hydrogen (stoichiometric only)?', a: 24230, u: 'kg', tol: 0.005, h: 'H₂ needed, then × 5.46.', w: R`$25\,000 \div 17.031 \times 1.5 \times 2.016 = 4439\ \mathrm{kg}\ \mathrm{H_2}$; $\times 5.457 = 2.42 \times 10^4\ \mathrm{kg}\ \mathrm{CO_2}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic24-2', n: 2, t: 'A spinel gas sensor', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 2, m: 20, lec: ['ca-2-3', 'ca-4-2', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`A metal oxalate hydrate $\mathrm{ZC_2O_4{\cdot}kH_2O}$ loses 20.13% of its mass at 140 °C. Heated in air at 500 °C, it gives 3.432 g of a black oxide $\mathrm{Z_3O_4}$ and 5.71 dm³ of $\mathrm{CO_2}$ (measured at 773 K and 101 325 Pa). $R = 8.314$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the molar mass of Z and identify it.', a: 54.9, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Each oxalate gives 2 CO₂; three Z per oxide.', w: R`$n(\mathrm{CO_2}) = 0.0900$; Z $= 0.0450$, oxide $= 0.0150\ \mathrm{mol}$; $M(\mathrm{Z_3O_4}) = 228.8$; Z $= 54.9$: Mn, $\mathrm{Mn_3O_4}$` },
        { q: 'Find k.', a: 2, u: '', abs: 0.05, h: '20.13% of (142.96 + 18.015k) is water.', w: R`$k = 2.00$: $\mathrm{MnC_2O_4{\cdot}2H_2O}$` },
        { q: 'In a normal spinel AB₂O₄ (oxide ions in a cubic close-packed array), what percentage of the octahedral holes is filled?', a: 50, u: '%', abs: 0.5, h: 'Per 4 O²⁻: 4 octahedral and 8 tetrahedral holes.', w: R`2 of 4 octahedral holes: 50% (and 1 of 8 tetrahedral: 12.5%). Fe₃O₄ is an inverse spinel.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic24-3', n: 3, t: 'Tyrosinase', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 3, m: 20, lec: ['ca-26-1', 'ca-26-2', 'ca-34-4'],
      s: R`Tyrosinase oxidises catechol to o-quinone ($\mathrm{C_6H_6O_2 + O_2 \rightarrow C_6H_4O_2 + H_2O_2}$). Michaelis–Menten: $r = k[\mathrm{E}][\mathrm{S}]/(K_M + [\mathrm{S}])$, $k = 250\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$, $K_M = 0.49\ \mathrm{mmol\ dm^{-3}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'With [E] = 6.0 nmol dm⁻³ and [S] = 2.00 mmol dm⁻³, find the initial rate in μmol dm⁻³ min⁻¹.', a: 1.20, u: 'μmol dm⁻³ min⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Keep units: nM × min⁻¹.', w: R`$250 \times 6.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mu\mathrm{M} \times 2.00/2.49 = 1.20\ \mu\mathrm{M\ min^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'Without enzyme, catechol reacts slowly: 2.0 mmol dm⁻³ gives 1.50 and 5.0 mmol dm⁻³ gives 3.75 μmol dm⁻³ min⁻¹. Find the first-order constant.', a: 7.5e-4, u: 'min⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Rate ∝ [catechol].', w: R`$1.50 \times 10^{-3} \div 2.0 = 7.5 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'The enzyme is inactivated by catechol + o-quinone with k = 7.1 dm³ mol⁻¹ min⁻¹, and t½ = 1/(k[catechol]₀). Find t½ at 5.00 mmol dm⁻³ catechol.', a: 28.2, u: 'min', tol: 0.01, h: 'Convert to mol dm⁻³.', w: R`$1 \div (7.1 \times 0.00500) = 28.2\ \mathrm{min}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic24-4', n: 4, t: 'Potassium and an oxocarbon acid', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 15, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-24-1'],
      s: R`Potassium is made by electrolysing molten KOH. A diprotic oxocarbon acid $\mathrm{H_2Y}$ ($\mathrm{H_2C_5O_5}$) has $\mathrm{p}K_{a1} = 0.8$, $\mathrm{p}K_{a2} = 2.2$; its salt X is $\mathrm{K_2C_5O_5{\cdot}2H_2O}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Write the electrode reactions for molten KOH electrolysis, then find the pH at which 90% of Y is in the fully protonated form.', a: -0.15, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: '[H₂Y]/[HY⁻] = 9 = [H⁺]/Ka₁ (ignore Y²⁻).', w: R`Cathode $\mathrm{K^+ + e^- \rightarrow K}$; anode $\mathrm{4OH^- \rightarrow O_2 + 2H_2O + 4e^-}$. $\mathrm{pH} = 0.8 - \log 9 = -0.15$` },
        { q: 'Find the pH of 0.050 mol dm⁻³ X in water (include water’s own ionisation).', a: 7.48, u: '', abs: 0.02, h: 'Kb = 10^(−14 + 2.2); [OH⁻]² = Kb·c + Kw.', w: R`$[\mathrm{OH^-}]^2 = 1.58 \times 10^{-12} \times 0.050 + 10^{-14}$; $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 2.99 \times 10^{-7}$; pH 7.48: the dianion is barely basic.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic24-5', n: 5, t: 'A copper ammine permanganate', f: 'Redox', lv: 3, m: 20, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-28-1'],
      s: R`Salt A is $\mathrm{[Cu(NH_3)_4](MnO_4)_2}$, an explosive purple solid. It is analysed iodometrically with 0.0400 mol dm⁻³ thiosulfate. (Cu 63.546, Mn 54.938, N 14.007, O 15.999, H 1.008.)`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find the molar mass of A.', a: 369.5, u: 'g mol⁻¹', tol: 0.002, h: '[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ + 2MnO₄⁻.', w: R`$63.55 + 68.12 + 237.87 = 369.5$` },
        { q: 'In titration II only Cu²⁺ is measured (2Cu²⁺ + 4I⁻ → 2CuI + I₂). What volume of thiosulfate does 150.0 mg of A need?', a: 10.15, u: 'cm³', tol: 0.005, h: '1 Cu²⁺ ↔ 1 S₂O₃²⁻.', w: R`$4.059 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol} \div 0.0400 = 10.15\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ (titration I, the permanganate, takes 10 times as much: 10 e⁻ from 2 MnO₄⁻)` },
        { q: R`A decomposes: $\mathrm{[Cu(NH_3)_4](MnO_4)_2 \rightarrow CuMn_2O_4 + NH_4NO_3 + 2NH_3 + H_2O}$. What percentage of the original mass remains as $\mathrm{CuMn_2O_4}$?`, a: 64.2, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'M(CuMn₂O₄) = 237.4.', w: R`$237.4 \div 369.5 = 64.2\%$ (it is 27.0% O by mass)` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic24-6', n: 6, t: 'Penicillin', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 5, lec: ['ca-29-4'],
      s: 'The penicillin core has three stereocentres.',
      p: [
        { q: 'How many stereoisomers are possible in principle?', a: 8, u: '', abs: 0.01, h: '2ⁿ.', w: R`$2^3 = 8$; nature makes only one.` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'icho', comp: 'International Chemistry Olympiad (IChO)', round: 'Theory exam', y: 2023, mins: 300,
    topics: 'Technetium-99m imaging · turning CO₂ into alcohols · artificial photosynthesis · hypervalent compounds · hydrodesulfurisation isotopes · methane to methanol on copper · enzyme kinetics · a peptide drug', skipped: 'Problems 8 (Nazarov reaction) and 9 (organic electrolysis) are answered with structures and mechanisms, so they are left out, as are the drawn parts of the others.', qs: [
    { id: 'ic23-1', n: 1, t: 'Technetium-99m imaging', f: 'Atomic structure', lv: 2, m: 15, lec: ['ca-1-2', 'ca-26-1'],
      s: R`$^{99m}\mathrm{Tc}$ ($t_{1/2} = 6.0$ h) is milked from a generator, where it forms by β⁻ decay of $^{99}\mathrm{Mo}$. Activity $A = \lambda N$. $N_A = 6.022 \times 10^{23}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many moles of ⁹⁹ᵐTc give an activity of 8.0 GBq?', a: 4.14e-10, u: 'mol', tol: 0.01, h: 'λ = ln 2 / (21 600 s).', w: R`$\lambda = 3.21 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$; $N = 8.0 \times 10^9 \div \lambda = 2.49 \times 10^{14}$; $4.1 \times 10^{-10}\ \mathrm{mol}$: imaging needs astonishingly little material.` },
        { q: 'After injection (no excretion), how many hours until the activity falls to 0.5% of the dose?', a: 45.9, u: 'h', tol: 0.005, h: 't = ln(200)/λ.', w: R`$\ln 200 \div 3.21 \times 10^{-5} = 1.65 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{s} = 45.9\ \mathrm{h}$ (the starting dose does not matter)` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic23-2', n: 2, t: 'Turning CO₂ into alcohols', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 30, lec: ['ca-24-1', 'ca-24-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`$\Delta_f H$ (kJ mol⁻¹) / $S^\circ$ (J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹): $\mathrm{H_2O(l)}$ −285.83 / 69.95; $\mathrm{H_2}$ 0 / 130.68; $\mathrm{CO_2}$ −393.52 / 213.79; propan-1-ol(l) −302.54 / 192.80. $F = 96\,485$. CO₂ → ethanol takes 12 e⁻; CO₂ → propan-1-ol takes 18 e⁻.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`For $\mathrm{3CO_2 + 9H_2 \rightarrow C_3H_7OH(l) + 5H_2O(l)}$, find ΔG° at 298.15 K.`, a: -171.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'ΔH = −551.13 kJ; find ΔS.', w: R`$\Delta S^\circ = -1274.9\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $\Delta G^\circ = -551.13 + 298.15(1.2749) = -171.0\ \mathrm{kJ}$` },
        { q: 'Hence find E° for the CO₂/propan-1-ol cathode against a hydrogen anode (n = 18).', a: 0.0985, u: 'V', tol: 0.01, h: 'E = −ΔG/(nF).', w: R`$171\,000 \div (18 \times 96\,485) = 0.0985\ \mathrm{V}$` },
        { q: 'A Cu–Ag foam (7.2 mg, 85% Cu, 15% Ag) is plated in 30 s at 2.5 A. What is the Faradaic efficiency of plating (the rest makes H₂)?', a: 26.1, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Cu²⁺ takes 2 e⁻, Ag⁺ 1 e⁻; total charge 75 C.', w: R`Cu $9.63 \times 10^{-5}$ mol → 18.6 C; Ag $1.00 \times 10^{-5}$ → 0.97 C; $19.55 \div 75 = 26.1\%$` },
        { q: R`The foam then electrolyses CO₂ for 1 h at 25 mA (1 cm²) in 40 mL of electrolyte, which ends up with 38.0 mg L⁻¹ ethanol. What is the Faradaic efficiency for ethanol?`, a: 42.4, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Q(total) = 90 C.', w: R`$1.52\ \mathrm{mg} = 3.30 \times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $\times 12F = 38.2\ \mathrm{C}$; 42.4% (partial current 10.6 mA cm⁻²)` },
        { q: 'The electrolyte also holds 6.0 mg L⁻¹ propan-1-ol, and the rest of the charge makes H₂. What volume of H₂ forms at 298.15 K and 1 bar, in mL?', a: 5.76, u: 'mL', tol: 0.01, h: 'Propanol: 18 e⁻ each; H₂: 2 e⁻.', w: R`Propanol $3.99 \times 10^{-6}$ mol → 6.94 C (7.7%); H₂ charge $90 - 38.2 - 6.9 = 44.9\ \mathrm{C}$; $n = 2.33 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $V = 5.76\ \mathrm{mL}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic23-3', n: 3, t: 'Artificial photosynthesis', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 15, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-1-3', 'ca-5-2'],
      s: R`For $\mathrm{H_2O(l) \rightarrow H_2 + \tfrac{1}{2}O_2}$: $\Delta H^\circ = +285\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, $\Delta G^\circ = +237\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$. Chlorophyll: ε = 8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹ cm⁻¹ at 680 nm.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find ΔS° and the thermoneutral voltage ΔH°/(2F).', a: 1.48, u: 'V', tol: 0.005, h: 'Electrolysis is endothermic beyond ΔG.', w: R`$\Delta S^\circ = (285 - 237) \div 298 = 161\ \mathrm{J\ K^{-1}}$; $285\,000 \div (2 \times 96\,485) = 1.48\ \mathrm{V}$ (versus 1.23 V reversible)` },
        { q: 'A cell should make 2.0 nmol H₂ per second; each absorbed photon moves one electron, with 25% efficiency. How many photons per second must be absorbed?', a: 9.6e15, u: 's⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: '2 e⁻ per H₂.', w: R`$4.0 \times 10^{-9} \times N_A \div 0.25 = 9.6 \times 10^{15}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'What chlorophyll concentration gives an absorbance of 0.060 in a 1 cm cell?', a: 7.5e-7, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'A = εcl.', w: R`$0.060 \div 8 \times 10^4 = 7.5 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic23-4', n: 4, t: 'Hypervalent compounds', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 8, lec: ['ca-26-2', 'ca-3-5'],
      s: 'A group in a hypervalent tellurium compound rotates with an activation energy of 25 kJ mol⁻¹; its rate constant at 213 K is 1800 s⁻¹ (measured by variable-temperature NMR).',
      p: [
        { q: 'Estimate the rotation rate constant at 298 K.', a: 1.01e5, u: 's⁻¹', tol: 0.02, h: 'Arrhenius between 213 and 298 K.', w: R`$1800 \times \exp\left[\dfrac{25\,000}{8.314}\left(\dfrac{1}{213} - \dfrac{1}{298}\right)\right] = 1.0 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$: too fast to see separate NMR signals at room temperature.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic23-5', n: 5, t: 'Hydrodesulfurisation isotopes', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 10, lec: ['ca-1-2'],
      s: R`Natural abundances: ³²S 94.8%, ³³S 0.75%, ³⁴S 4.37%; ¹H 99.986%, ²H 0.014%.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`What percentage of $\mathrm{H_2S}$ molecules is $^1\mathrm{H_2}{}^{33}\mathrm{S}$?`, a: 0.750, u: '%', tol: 0.005, h: 'Multiply independent abundances.', w: R`$0.99986^2 \times 0.0075 = 0.750\%$` },
        { q: R`And $^1\mathrm{H}{}^2\mathrm{H}{}^{32}\mathrm{S}$?`, a: 0.0265, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Two ways to place the D.', w: R`$2 \times 0.99986 \times 0.00014 \times 0.948 = 2.65 \times 10^{-4} = 0.0265\%$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic23-6', n: 6, t: 'Methane to methanol on copper', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-26-1'],
      s: 'Copper-exchanged zeolites oxidise methane at copper-oxo sites; two Cu atoms are consumed per CH₄.',
      p: [
        { q: 'In a 1.00 L autoclave at 500 K, the methane pressure over 300 mg of zeolite (3.5% Cu) falls from 1050 to 1038 Pa. What percentage of the Cu reacted?', a: 3.49, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Δn(CH₄) = ΔpV/RT.', w: R`$\Delta n = 2.89 \times 10^{-6}$; Cu $= 1.65 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol}$; $2 \times 2.89 \div 165 = 3.49\%$` },
        { q: 'One type of site reacts with first-order k = 3.10 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. What is its half-life?', a: 224, u: 's', tol: 0.01, h: 'ln 2/k.', w: R`$0.693 \div 3.10 \times 10^{-3} = 224\ \mathrm{s}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic23-7', n: 7, t: 'Enzyme kinetics', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 8, lec: ['ca-26-2', 'ca-34-4'],
      s: 'An enzyme site turns over 10 200 times per minute. Two of its steps differ in activation energy by 1500 J mol⁻¹ (same pre-exponential factor), at 310 K.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Express the turnover number in s⁻¹.', a: 170, u: 's⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: '÷ 60.', w: R`$10\,200 \div 60 = 170\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'By what factor is the lower-barrier step faster?', a: 1.79, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'exp(ΔEa/RT).', w: R`$e^{1500/(8.314 \times 310)} = 1.79$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic23-10', n: 10, t: 'A peptide drug', f: 'Organic', lv: 1, m: 4, lec: ['ca-29-4'],
      s: 'A cyclic peptide drug has 9 stereogenic centres.',
      p: [
        { q: 'How many stereoisomers are possible in principle?', a: 512, u: '', abs: 0.5, h: '2ⁿ.', w: R`$2^9 = 512$; solid-phase synthesis from single-enantiomer amino acids makes just one.` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'icho', comp: 'International Chemistry Olympiad (IChO)', round: 'Theory exam', y: 2022, mins: 300,
    topics: 'Gold nanoparticle tests · chromium in spinels · capturing CO₂ · lithium–sulfur batteries · NO oxidation equilibria', skipped: 'Problems 6–9 (phosphines, biomolecules, a chiral catalyst, a total synthesis) are answered with structures, so they are left out.', qs: [
    { id: 'ic22-1', n: 1, t: 'Gold nanoparticle tests', f: 'Analysis', lv: 2, m: 15, lec: ['ca-4-2', 'ca-37-2'],
      s: R`Colour tests for viral RNA use spherical gold nanoparticles of diameter 20.0 nm. Gold: ρ = 19.3 g cm⁻³, M = 197.0 g mol⁻¹; $N_A = 6.022 \times 10^{23}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many gold atoms are in one nanoparticle?', a: 2.47e5, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'V = (4/3)πr³ with r = 1.00 × 10⁻⁶ cm.', w: R`$V = 4.19 \times 10^{-18}\ \mathrm{cm^3}$; $m = 8.08 \times 10^{-17}\ \mathrm{g}$; $N = 2.47 \times 10^5$` },
        { q: R`Nanoparticles are made from 4.0 mg of $\mathrm{HAuCl_4{\cdot}3H_2O}$ (394 g mol⁻¹) in 100 mL. Find the nanoparticle concentration in mol L⁻¹.`, a: 4.11e-10, u: 'mol L⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Gold atoms ÷ atoms per particle.', w: R`$6.11 \times 10^{18}$ Au atoms $\div 2.47 \times 10^5 = 2.47 \times 10^{13}$ particles; $4.1 \times 10^{-10}\ \mathrm{M}$` },
        { q: 'The suspension’s absorbance is 0.650 in a 1 cm cell. Find the molar extinction coefficient.', a: 1.58e9, u: 'L mol⁻¹ cm⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'ε = A/(cl).', w: R`$0.650 \div 4.11 \times 10^{-10} = 1.6 \times 10^9$: enormous, which is why tiny amounts are visible.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic22-2', n: 2, t: 'Chromium in spinels', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 1, m: 8, lec: ['ca-28-3'],
      s: R`In chromite, $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ sits in octahedral sites, with $\Delta_O = 17\,400\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the CFSE of $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ in cm⁻¹ (as a positive number).`, a: 20880, u: 'cm⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'd³: three electrons in t₂g at −0.4Δ₀ each.', w: R`$1.2 \times 17\,400 = 20\,880\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$: the large CFSE is why Cr³⁺ insists on octahedral sites.` },
        { q: R`Find its spin-only magnetic moment.`, a: 3.87, u: 'μB', tol: 0.005, h: 'μ = √(n(n + 2)).', w: R`$\sqrt{15} = 3.87\ \mu_B$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic22-3', n: 3, t: 'Capturing CO₂', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 20, lec: ['ca-24-1', 'ca-4-2', 'ca-25-1'],
      s: R`An electrochemical cell makes $\mathrm{H^+}$ (one per electron) to release CO₂ from a carbonate capture solution flowing at 12.0 mL min⁻¹ with 0.10 M $\mathrm{HCO_3^-}$ and 0.040 M $\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}}$. The outflow stays saturated with CO₂ at 0.033 M. $F = 96\,485$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'At 3.00 A, how many mmol of H⁺ are made per minute?', a: 1.87, u: 'mmol min⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'I/F × 60.', w: R`$3.00 \div 96\,485 \times 60 = 1.87\ \mathrm{mmol\ min^{-1}}$` },
        { q: 'How many mmol of CO₂ gas are released per minute?', a: 0.990, u: 'mmol min⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'H⁺ first turns CO₃²⁻ into HCO₃⁻; the rest makes CO₂; some CO₂ leaves dissolved.', w: R`$1.866 - 0.48 = 1.386$ mmol CO₂ formed; dissolved outflow $0.033 \times 12.0 = 0.396$; gas 0.990 mmol min⁻¹` },
        { q: R`A ZIF framework has two spherical cages (diameter 1.08 nm) per cubic cell of edge 1.70 nm, and the cell contains 12 units of $\mathrm{Zn(C_4H_5N_2)_2}$ (227.6 g mol⁻¹). Find its pore volume in cm³ g⁻¹.`, a: 0.291, u: 'cm³ g⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Cage volume × 2 ÷ cell mass.', w: R`$V_{cage} = 0.660\ \mathrm{nm^3}$; cell mass $= 4.54 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{g}$; $1.32 \times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{cm^3} \div 4.54 \times 10^{-21} = 0.291$ (27% of the cell is cage)` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic22-4', n: 4, t: 'Lithium–sulfur batteries', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 3, m: 25, lec: ['ca-6-1', 'ca-24-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`Sulfur is measured iodometrically and used in Li–S batteries. $F = 96\,485$; $R = 8.314$; S = 32.06.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`A 15.0 g sample is dissolved and diluted; 1/20 of it reacts with 50.00 mL of 0.05000 M $\mathrm{I_2}$ (1 S²⁻ per I₂). The leftover iodine needs 20.10 mL of 0.1000 M thiosulfate. Find the sulfur percentage.`, a: 6.39, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: 'Iodine used = 2.500 − 1.005 mmol.', w: R`$1.495 \times 20 = 29.9\ \mathrm{mmol}$ S $= 0.959\ \mathrm{g}$; $6.39\%$` },
        { q: 'A phone battery (3.85 V, 4.000 Ah) lasts 25 h. A Li–S pack holds 30 g of sulfur (2 e⁻ per S) at 2.2 V. How long would the phone run on it?', a: 179, u: 'h', tol: 0.01, h: 'Energy = charge × voltage.', w: R`Phone uses $55\,440 \div 25 = 2218\ \mathrm{J\ h^{-1}}$; Li–S: $1.806 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{C} \times 2.2 = 3.97 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{J}$; 179 h` },
        { q: 'Two polysulfide isomers I and II have ΔG of formation differing by 2.10 kJ mol⁻¹ (II less stable). Find K for I ⇌ II at 298.15 K.', a: 0.429, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'K = e^(−ΔG/RT).', w: R`$e^{-2100/(8.314 \times 298.15)} = 0.429$` },
        { q: R`$E^\circ(\mathrm{Li^+(aq)/Li}) = -3.040\ \mathrm{V}$. Li⁺ is solvated by water with ΔG = −120.0 kJ mol⁻¹ and by DME with −116.5 kJ mol⁻¹. Find $E^\circ(\mathrm{Li^+(DME)/Li})$.`, a: -3.004, u: 'V', abs: 0.002, h: 'Weaker solvation makes Li⁺ easier to reduce.', w: R`$-3.040 + 3.5 \div 96.485 = -3.004\ \mathrm{V}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic22-5', n: 5, t: 'NO oxidation equilibria', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 2, m: 15, lec: ['ca-23-4', 'ca-22-2', 'ca-26-1'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{2NO + O_2 \rightleftharpoons 2NO_2}$: $\Delta H^\circ = -116.4\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$, $\Delta S^\circ = -146.6\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$ (constant).`,
      p: [
        { q: 'Find K°p at 650 K.', a: 49.7, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS°.', w: R`$\Delta G^\circ = -21.11\ \mathrm{kJ}$; $K = e^{3.906} = 49.7$` },
        { q: 'Find ΔU° at 650 K.', a: -111.0, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.002, h: 'ΔH = ΔU + Δn(gas)RT, Δn = −1.', w: R`$-116.4 + 8.314 \times 650 / 1000 = -111.0\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`The forward rate constant is $3.20 \times 10^5\ \mathrm{L^2\ mol^{-2}\ min^{-1}}$ at 650 K. Find the reverse rate constant.`, a: 119, u: 'L mol⁻¹ min⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Kc = Kp(RT/p°) with R = 0.08314 L bar.', w: R`$K_c = 49.7 \times 0.08314 \times 650 = 2687\ \mathrm{L\ mol^{-1}}$; $k_- = 3.20 \times 10^5 \div 2687 = 119$` },
      ] },
  ] },

  { c: 'icho', comp: 'International Chemistry Olympiad (IChO)', round: 'Theory exam', y: 2021, mins: 300,
    topics: 'Hydrogen soaking into a metal · isotopes and zero-point energy · Beer–Lambert with an equilibrium · zinc redox and batteries · silicon aromaticity · solid-state transition-metal chemistry and MOFs', skipped: 'Problems 7, 8 and 9 (non-benzenoid aromatics, helicene chirality and molecular capsules) are answered with structures, so they are left out.', qs: [
    { id: 'ic21-1', n: 1, t: 'Hydrogen soaking into a metal', f: 'Kinetics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-4-1', 'ca-26-1'],
      s: R`A metal sheet of area $1.0 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m^2}$ sits in a 1.0 L vessel of $\mathrm{H_2}$ at 450 K. The $\mathrm{H_2}$ pressure falls at $6.0 \times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{Pa\ s^{-1}}$ as H atoms dissolve. The surface has $1.3 \times 10^{18}$ sites m⁻². $R = 8.31$, $N_A = 6.022 \times 10^{23}$.`,
      p: [
        { q: 'How many moles of H atoms enter each m² per second?', a: 3.21e-7, u: 'mol s⁻¹ m⁻²', tol: 0.01, h: 'Two H per H₂; n = pV/RT.', w: R`$2 \times 6.0 \times 10^{-4} \times 10^{-3} \div (8.31 \times 450 \times 10^{-3}) = 3.2 \times 10^{-7}$` },
        { q: 'If the surface is nearly saturated, the absorption rate constant equals this flux divided by the site density. Find it.', a: 0.149, u: 's⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Convert moles to atoms.', w: R`$3.21 \times 10^{-7} \times N_A \div 1.3 \times 10^{18} = 0.15\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic21-2', n: 2, t: 'Isotopes and zero-point energy', f: 'Bonding and structure', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-1-2', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`$\mathrm{H_2}$ vibrates at 4161.0 cm⁻¹; the wavenumber scales as $1/\sqrt{\mu}$ (reduced mass; take H = 1, T = 3). The zero-point energy is $\tfrac{1}{2}hc\tilde{\nu}$; 1 cm⁻¹ = 11.96 J mol⁻¹.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find the vibrational wavenumber of HT.`, a: 3397, u: 'cm⁻¹', tol: 0.002, h: 'μ(H₂) = 1/2; μ(HT) = 3/4.', w: R`$4161.0 \times \sqrt{0.5/0.75} = 3397\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (T₂: 2402)` },
        { q: R`Find ΔH at 0 K for $\mathrm{H_2 + T_2 \rightarrow 2HT}$, in J mol⁻¹.`, a: 1385, u: 'J mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'Only zero-point energies change.', w: R`$\tfrac{1}{2}(2 \times 3397.4 - 4161.0 - 2402.4) = 115.8\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$; $\times 11.96 = 1385\ \mathrm{J\ mol^{-1}}$: at 0 K the mixed isotopologue is disfavoured.` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic21-3', n: 3, t: 'Beer–Lambert with an equilibrium', f: 'Equilibrium', lv: 3, m: 12, lec: ['ca-25-1', 'ca-37-2'],
      s: R`A solution contains $[\mathrm{A^-}] = 8.00 \times 10^{-3}$ M and $[\mathrm{HA}] = 2.00 \times 10^{-3}$ M with $K_a = 1.00 \times 10^{-3}$. It is diluted twofold with HCl solution of pH 2.300, and the absorbance at λ₁ (same cell) does not change.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $[\mathrm{A^-}]$ after dilution.`, a: 2.43e-3, u: 'M', tol: 0.01, h: 'Total A = 5.00 × 10⁻³; total acidic H = [HA] + [H⁺] = 3.63 × 10⁻³ M; solve for [H⁺].', w: R`$[\mathrm{H^+}] = 1.06 \times 10^{-3}$ (pH 2.98); $[\mathrm{A^-}] = 2.43 \times 10^{-3}$, $[\mathrm{HA}] = 2.57 \times 10^{-3}$` },
        { q: 'Find ε(HA)/ε(A⁻) at λ₁.', a: 9.74, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'ε_A(8.00) + ε_HA(2.00) = ε_A(2.43) + ε_HA(2.57), in mM.', w: R`$\varepsilon_{HA}/\varepsilon_{A^-} = (8.00 - 2.43) \div (2.57 - 2.00) = 9.7$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic21-4', n: 4, t: 'Zinc redox and batteries', f: 'Electrochemistry', lv: 2, m: 15, lec: ['ca-24-2', 'ca-24-1', 'ca-23-4'],
      s: R`Zinc metal and its oxide interconvert in batteries and in metallurgy. $F = 96\,485$; $R = 8.314$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`For $\mathrm{ZnO + H_2 \rightarrow Zn + H_2O}$ at 400 °C, ΔG° = +62 kJ mol⁻¹. Below what water pressure (bar) does the reduction go, with $p(\mathrm{H_2}) = 1$ bar?`, a: 1.54e-5, u: 'bar', tol: 0.02, h: 'Q < K.', w: R`$K = e^{-62\,000/(8.314 \times 673.15)} = 1.5 \times 10^{-5}$: water must be removed continuously.` },
        { q: 'A zinc–air cell runs at 15 mA for 36 h; its zinc anode turns to ZnO. By how much does the anode’s mass change, in g?', a: 0.161, u: 'g', tol: 0.01, h: 'Q/2F mol of Zn each gain one O.', w: R`$1944\ \mathrm{C} = 0.01007\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{Zn}$; $+0.161\ \mathrm{g}$ (oxygen gained)` },
        { q: R`The cell reaction $\mathrm{2Zn + O_2 \rightarrow 2ZnO}$ (n = 4) has E° = 1.65 V. Find E at 25 °C on a mountain where $p(\mathrm{O_2}) = 0.13$ bar.`, a: 1.637, u: 'V', abs: 0.002, h: 'E = E° + (RT/4F) ln p(O₂).', w: R`$1.65 + 0.006424 \ln 0.13 = 1.637\ \mathrm{V}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic21-5', n: 5, t: 'Silicon and aromaticity', f: 'Thermodynamics', lv: 2, m: 12, lec: ['ca-5-2', 'ca-23-4', 'ca-30-1'],
      s: 'Aromatic stabilisation can be measured from hydrogenation enthalpies.',
      p: [
        { q: 'Hydrogenating cyclohexene releases 120 kJ mol⁻¹ and benzene 208 kJ mol⁻¹. Estimate the aromatic stabilisation energy of benzene.', a: 152, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.005, h: 'Compare with three isolated C=C.', w: R`$3 \times 120 - 208 = 152\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
        { q: R`A silicon compound D isomerises to E. The equilibrium ratio D : E is 1 : 30 at 40.0 °C and 1 : 12 at 140.0 °C. Find ΔH for D → E.`, a: -9.86, u: 'kJ mol⁻¹', tol: 0.01, h: 'van ’t Hoff with K = [E]/[D].', w: R`$8.314\ln(12/30) \div (1/313.15 - 1/413.15) = -9.86\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}$` },
      ] },
    { id: 'ic21-6', n: 6, t: 'Solid-state transition-metal chemistry', f: 'Inorganic', lv: 3, m: 25, lec: ['ca-28-3', 'ca-4-2'],
      s: R`Crystal-field stabilisation: octahedral t₂g −0.4Δ₀, e_g +0.6Δ₀; tetrahedral e −0.6Δ_T, t₂ +0.4Δ_T, with $\Delta_T = \tfrac{4}{9}\Delta_O$. For distributing $\mathrm{M^{2+}}$ between sites, $\ln D = 4.46\,|\Delta\text{CFSE}|/\Delta_O + 0.100$.`,
      p: [
        { q: R`Find $|\Delta\text{CFSE}|$ (octahedral minus tetrahedral) for high-spin $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$, in units of Δ₀.`, a: 0.844, u: 'Δ₀', tol: 0.005, h: 'd⁸: octahedral 1.2Δ₀; tetrahedral 0.8Δ_T.', w: R`$1.2 - \tfrac{4}{9}(0.8) = 0.844\Delta_O$` },
        { q: 'Estimate D for Ni²⁺.', a: 47.8, u: '', tol: 0.02, h: 'Use the line.', w: R`$e^{4.46 \times 0.844 + 0.100} = 48$: Ni²⁺ strongly prefers octahedral sites.` },
        { q: R`A tetragonal $\mathrm{La_2CuO_4}$-type oxide has a = 0.3780 nm and c = 1.3150 nm. The in-plane Cu–O bond is a/2; the apical bond is (c − 2 × 0.2500)/4. Find the apical Cu–O length, in nm.`, a: 0.2038, u: 'nm', tol: 0.003, h: 'Jahn–Teller elongation.', w: R`$(1.3150 - 0.5000) \div 4 = 0.204\ \mathrm{nm}$, longer than the in-plane 0.189 nm: Cu²⁺ (d⁹) is Jahn–Teller elongated.` },
        { q: R`Replacing La³⁺ by Sr²⁺ creates one hole each. The cell (volume a²c, 4 La sites) has $1.80 \times 10^{27}$ holes m⁻³. What percentage of La is replaced?`, a: 8.46, u: '%', tol: 0.01, h: '1 nm³ = 10⁻²⁷ m³.', w: R`$V = 0.1879\ \mathrm{nm^3}$; holes per cell $= 0.338$; $\div 4 = 8.5\%$` },
        { q: R`A cubic MOF, $\mathrm{Zn_4O(L)_3}$ (M = 806 per formula), has 8 formula units per cell and density 0.65 g cm⁻³. Find the cell edge, in cm.`, a: 2.54e-7, u: 'cm', tol: 0.01, h: 'a³ = 8M/(N_A ρ).', w: R`$a = (6448 \div (6.022 \times 10^{23} \times 0.65))^{1/3} = 2.54 \times 10^{-7}\ \mathrm{cm}$ (2.54 nm)` },
        { q: 'The MOF takes up 250 mL of CO₂ per gram at 1 bar and 25 °C, with one pore per formula unit. How many CO₂ molecules sit in each pore?', a: 8.13, u: '', tol: 0.01, h: 'Moles of CO₂ per g ÷ moles of pores per g.', w: R`$0.01009 \div (1/806) = 8.1$` },
      ] },
  ] },
  ];
})();
